  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1FFSR8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CE1FFSR8005",
    "text": "**Depth-legibility marker: option B (`'│ '` per level) over option A (full box-drawing art)**: option A doesn't actually fix the underlying problem (depth is still `prefixWidth / perLevelWidth`, just spread across a wider glyph unit) and needs a full sibling-lookahead pass first; option B is self-describing (count the marker glyph) with no lookahead cost, at the same 2-characters-per-level width as the plain-space indent it replaces.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1DDF58000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1FFSR8006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1FFSR8006:links:Link:00CGM0RSP0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638004",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1FFSR8006:links:Link:00CGM0RSP0000:props:StringProp:00CGM0RSP0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1239"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Stale `TreeView.unfolds` references: an audit/prune sweep (option 2), not just write-time validation (option 1)**",
    "text": "**Stale `TreeView.unfolds` references: an audit/prune sweep (option 2), not just write-time validation (option 1)**: write-time validation only catches a ref that was already dead *at unfold time* — the actually-observed failure mode is a ref going stale *afterward*, through an unrelated edit or GC pass. Only a sweep that revisits existing `unfolds` entries on some cadence closes that; write-time validation is worth adding too, but as a cheap complementary check, not a substitute. **Both built**: `TreeView.unfold(ref)` now checks `nodeExists` before appending (silent no-op on a ref with zero quads, matching `unfold`'s own idempotent-no-op-on-repeat posture); `pruneStaleUnfolds` (`node.ts`) walks every `TreeView`'s `unfolds` set and strips any entry with no quads at all, run at the same three explicit-sweep call sites `pruneUnreachableTombstones` already uses (`kg:reload`, `kg:flush --clobber`, service shutdown). A tombstoned-but-present target is deliberately left alone by both — legitimate, revealed via `--tombstoned` — only a fully-gone id counts as stale. Verified live in `verify.ts` (a never-existed ref refused at write time; an injected already-stale entry swept while a genuinely live sibling entry survived) — see [Issues](../issues/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CE1GW638004).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1DDF58000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1FFSR8007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CE1FFSR8007",
    "text": "**Upward-pointing-link gap: stop recursion at a container in the chain (option 1), not re-root to the maximal ancestor (option 2)**: option 2 is structurally cleaner in isolation but silently changes what the query is even about — `aperas tree` promising \"show me the tree from *this* apex\" would get violated the moment some deep node happens to link back to an ancestor. Option 1 preserves that contract exactly, degrading the link to a flat, non-recursing reference instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1DDF58000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
