    ],
    "title": "**GFM task-list items round-trip broken**",
    "text": "**GFM task-list items round-trip broken**: originally found here: [archive-migration discussion](../discussion/archive-migration.md#id/BlockNode:00CE0HD0HG007) — `- [ ] text` reportedly stored the literal `[ ] ` marker inside the block's own `.text` alongside a separate `checked: false` prop, so `aperas project` would re-emit `- [ ] ` on top of the leftover `[ ] `, doubling to `- [ ] [ ] text`. Re-tested live during the list-consumption migration (both a direct `parseMarkdownTree`/`serializeBlock` round trip and a real `aperas insert` against the live corpus): does not reproduce, renders correctly. `git log` shows the checkbox-handling code in `astParser.ts` has never changed since it was first written. No corroborating evidence anywhere in the graph that this was ever actually observed broken in a real projected artifact — awaiting a decision on whether to close as not-reproducible or keep open pending further investigation. See [list-consumption discussion](../discussion/list-consumption.md#id/BlockNode:00CE8J339R001) for the re-test note. **Correction: it does reproduce, narrower than either re-test tried.** Confirmed live on this session's own discussion plan checkboxes, then isolated and traced to the exact upstream line — full account in [discussion/treeview.md](../discussion/treeview.md#id/BlockNode:00CGJHDK68001). The real trigger is narrower than \"bold\" and broader than either re-test tried: `mdast-util-gfm-task-list-item`'s `exitParagraphWithTaskListItem` only trims the checkbox's mandatory leftover space, and re-bases the paragraph's own position past it, when the paragraph's first inline child is a `text` node — `- [x] Plain: rest.` qualifies, `- [x] **Bold**:`/`*Italic*:`/`` `code`: ``/`[link](url):` do not, since CommonMark's inline tokenizer never materializes a lone leading space with nothing before an inline span as its own text node. `astParser.ts` has no checkbox-marker-stripping logic of its own — it only reads `node.checked` into a prop — so it faithfully trusts the un-rebased position for `rawSlice`, including the leftover marker as literal text. Both earlier re-tests happened to use plain-text task items, which is exactly why neither one reproduced it. **Resolved**: the real fix lives upstream (`mdast-util-gfm-task-list-item`), out of reach here, so `astParser.ts` compensates defensively — a listItem already known to be a checkbox (`node.checked` boolean) has a leading `/^\\[[ xX]\\]\\s*/` stripped from both its extracted `text` and `title` after the fact, regardless of what leaked or why. Verified live end-to-end: a bold-lead checkbox item pushed through the real service now stores clean text with `checked` set correctly, and projects to a single, correct `- [x]` marker — confirmed by re-checking this session's own six plan checkboxes, which had actually rendered doubled (`[ ] [x]`) before this fix and render correctly now.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1GW63800A",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638008:links:Link:00CGRWGM78000",
