    "title": "00CF0GH9P8000",
    "text": "**`grep`ping the raw `Apeiron/*.jsonld` mirror directly is a legitimate full-text-search step — `grep` the store, `aperas` the nodes**: confirmed live, `grep -n -C3 'keyword' Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld` surfaces every matching node's `@id`, `type`, `parent` and complete (untruncated) `text` in one command, across the whole corpus. This is not the shadow-grepping anti-pattern from [Settled](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA56A0G003) — that rule is about `artifacts/*.md`, the rendered projection with anchors stripped and `props`/`tombstonedAt` invisible. `Apeiron/*.jsonld` is the on-disk mirror of the graph itself, full fidelity, the same file `scripts/show_node.py` reads — grepping it is reading the source, not the shadow.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8001",
      "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8002",
      "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8001:props:StringProp:00CF0GHB0R002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8001:props:StringProp:00CF0GHB0R003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Versus `show_node.py --grep`**",
    "text": "**Versus `show_node.py --grep`**: raw `grep` is a single external command with no wrapper needed, shows the full, untruncated `.text` (the script's own preview caps at 90 chars), and works over any `.jsonld` file — including `ArtifactNode.jsonld` for an artifact's own title/abstract, which `show_node.py --grep` never scans (it only iterates `BlockNode.jsonld`). In exchange, the script auto-resolves the owning artifact's path and marks `(tombstoned)` on every hit, which raw `grep` does not.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The real caution, confirmed by inspecting the schema directly**",
    "text": "**The real caution, confirmed by inspecting the schema directly**: a node's field order is `@id, @type, [links], [props], [tombstonedAt], [holder], title, [text], parent, type, children` for a `BlockNode` (the bracketed fields are omitted when unset or empty; the order is that of the class shape in `shape.ts`, which `serializeDoc` follows, and `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` add `path` and their hash fields), and `links` and `props` are variable-length (0 to several entries depending on the node) — so `tombstonedAt`'s distance from a `.text` match shifts per node and can't be relied on to fall inside any fixed `-C<n>` window. A `grep` hit is therefore only a candidate id, never proof of live status, current parent, or actual link structure — those still require handing the id to `aperas` (`unfold`, `tree`, `backlinks --text`), which is what correctly resolves tombstones and relationships instead of trusting eyeballed JSON.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The resulting two-step workflow**",
    "text": "**The resulting two-step workflow**: `grep` the raw store to find candidate node ids fast, in one shot, with full-length text — then hand each id to the `aperas` CLI to actually work with it (confirm it's live, see its real children/links, edit it). Search and structure are different tools now, same as `unfold` vs `backlinks` already split content from context.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF0GH9P8000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
