    "title": "**`aperas update` never re-derives a non-heading block's title**",
    "text": "**`aperas update` never re-derives a non-heading block's title**: replacing a paragraph's `.text` leaves `.title` frozen at whatever ingest originally derived from the lead-in term, even when the new text no longer has that lead-in at all — so the block goes on previewing under a title for content it no longer holds. Root cause confirmed directly in `kgUpdate.ts`: `runUpdate`'s local `title` variable is assigned only in the heading-target/heading-input branch — for a paragraph/listItem target it stays `undefined` for the entire function, so the old title is carried forward unconditionally, every time, not merely left stale by a parsing quirk. Also confirmed worse than a stale-title problem: verifying it by re-pushing a node's own `--text` output (assumed complete) tombstoned that node's 4 real children, since `--text` never shows children and a non-`--text-only` update reconciles the piped (empty) overflow against what's really there. Recovered via the staged `git` checkpoint; see [discussion/linking.md](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CGGA9J68000) for the full incident. Invisible in the rendered projection, since only a heading's title is ever written back to disk (`kg:title`'s removal made the lead-in term the one and only title mechanism, with `update` compensating for heading targets only) — but `tree`, `unfold` and `backlinks --text` all display the stale title in the graph itself, with the summary line's `0 changed` giving no hint either way. No CLI route yet to repair an already-stale title short of tombstoning the block. Found live while rewriting `design/aperas-skill.md`'s Topology paragraph for the `aperas` skill's v2; full account in [discussion/linking.md](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CGF83D4G001). **Resolved**: `runUpdate`'s paragraph branch now reads `title = firstChild.title` too (already computed by the parser for every paragraph, nothing new to derive), skipped only when `target` itself is a heading (where `firstChild` is the adopted leading paragraph, a different piece of text from the heading's own title). Decoupled from `props`, which must stay untouched for a non-heading re-title — a new `retitling` flag (true only for an actual heading retitle) now gates the two prop-replacement sites that used to key off `title !== undefined` alone. Verified live: a scratch paragraph's title updates on content change and a scratch listItem's `orderedList`/`startIndex`/`checked` props survive a title-changing update untouched; a real heading retitle still replaces its own props (tree-anchor) as before.",
