    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CGM5V098001 --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGM5V098001",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A4FT0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:17.567Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A4FT0002",
    "text": "**A second casualty of the non-heading title re-derivation fix above: `issues/linking.md`'s own `ArtifactNode` title was corrupted to a bare block-id string, breaking its own path resolution** — the fix in [the item above](#id/BlockNode:00CGFE4SV0002) never excluded `ArtifactNode` targets, so a push to this artifact's own leading-summary text (found while auditing every `ArtifactNode.title` corpus-wide for a basename mismatch) silently overwrote its title. Full root cause, the `kgUpdate.ts` fix, and a second sibling case (`issues/core.md`) are recorded in [issues/core.md's Resolved section](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CGM54FX0001); manually corrected back to `\"linking.md\"` here, its own `.text` summary was already accurate.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGM5V098001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A4W08001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:39.926Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A4W08001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CGGHVBF8001 --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGGHVBF8001",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A4W08002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:40.693Z",
    "title": "**`extractAnchorNames` treats a quoted example anchor as a real claimed name — no code-span awareness**",
    "text": "**`extractAnchorNames` treats a quoted example anchor as a real claimed name — no code-span awareness**: `astParser.ts`'s `extractAnchorNames(text)` is a bare regex over a block's raw text, with no AST/markdown awareness, so an anchor tag written *inside backticks as an illustration* is indistinguishable from one the projector actually spliced in. `rejectSlugPathCollisions` then enforces the illustration as a real claimed name. Confirmed live: the `aperas` skill's own item 2 (SKILL.md) quotes a complete anchor tag to warn readers off pasting projected files back in, and that quoting alone made the block claim the address as its own — `BlockNode:00CE95MVP8008` holds it today. Only one claimant exists so far, so nothing has collided yet, but `design/linking.md` (the canonical anchor-syntax spec) is exactly the kind of document that must quote the same example, and would collide the moment it does. Not a judgement call: a code span is literal display text by definition, so an anchor inside one is never a real claim — the regex needs to skip backtick-delimited spans, mirroring how `findLeadInColonOffset` already excludes `inlineCode`. Full account in [discussion/aperas-skill.md](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CECCBKNG001). **Resolved**, and widened while fixing it: a shared `anchorMatchesOutsideCode` helper now filters out any `ANCHOR_NAME_RE` match sitting inside a backtick span before either function sees it. Found designing the fix, not by this issue: `stripInlineAnchors` — this session's own new storage-time call included — had the identical gap in the opposite direction, and would *strip* a legitimately-quoted anchor example as if it were real projection markup, corrupting genuine prose. Both functions fixed from the one shared helper. Verified in isolation: a real anchor is still extracted/stripped, a quoted example is preserved untouched in both directions, and a mixed case (one real, one quoted) resolves each correctly.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGGHVBF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A5850001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:23.657Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A5850001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CGGG6N5G001 --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGGG6N5G001",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A5850002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:24.432Z",
    "title": "**Graph-wide sweep for the pre-fix \"wikilink never resolved\" residue — 9 real cases found and fixed, not the 20 first estimated**",
    "text": "**Graph-wide sweep for the pre-fix \"wikilink never resolved\" residue — 9 real cases found and fixed, not the 20 first estimated**: `resolveBlockLinks` used to run only from `kg:ingest`, never `kg:update`/`kg:insest` ([this doc's own account above](#id/BlockNode:00CF1276D0001)) — already fixed in the current code, but every node last touched before that fix kept a permanently-empty `.links` regardless of what its text actually cited. A first pass (any stored `text` containing `](...#...)`with an empty `.links`) found 20 candidates graph-wide; most turned out to be false positives once checked by hand — a fenced code-block example in `design/documentation.md` with placeholder ids (`00C0000000001`, all zeros), and half a dozen copies of `SKILL.md`'s own wikilink-syntax documentation quoting the pattern with a literal `<ID>`/`...` placeholder inside backticks, none of them real citations at all. Re-run comparing each node's *actual* `#id/BlockNode:...` fragments (code fences and inline-code spans excluded from the count) against its resolved `.links` targets found exactly 9 genuine cases, spread across `planning/linking.md`, `issues/packaging.md`, `history/aperas-skill.md` and `discussion/aperas-skill.md` — all resolvable (every target confirmed live, none tombstoned or missing). Fixed by re-touching each with `aperas update <id> --text-only` (piping the node's own unchanged text) — chosen over a plain `update` specifically because a plain reconcile risks tombstoning real children on a node whose full shape isn't known up front (the near-miss recorded just above this entry); `--text-only` never reconciles children at all, so it was safe regardless. Confirmed by re-running the same precise sweep afterward: zero remaining. A companion check for the opposite failure — a resolved `Link` whose *target* is now dead or missing — also came back empty, so this concern's link integrity is otherwise clean as of this pass.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGGG6N5G001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A5N48001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:14.715Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A5N48001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CGFE4SV0002 --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGFE4SV0002",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A5N48002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:15.574Z",
    "title": "**`aperas update` never re-derives a non-heading block's title**",
    "text": "**`aperas update` never re-derives a non-heading block's title**: replacing a paragraph's `.text` leaves `.title` frozen at whatever ingest originally derived from the lead-in term, even when the new text no longer has that lead-in at all — so the block goes on previewing under a title for content it no longer holds. Root cause confirmed directly in `kgUpdate.ts`: `runUpdate`'s local `title` variable is assigned only in the heading-target/heading-input branch — for a paragraph/listItem target it stays `undefined` for the entire function, so the old title is carried forward unconditionally, every time, not merely left stale by a parsing quirk. Also confirmed worse than a stale-title problem: verifying it by re-pushing a node's own `--text` output (assumed complete) tombstoned that node's 4 real children, since `--text` never shows children and a non-`--text-only` update reconciles the piped (empty) overflow against what's really there. Recovered via the staged `git` checkpoint; see [discussion/linking.md](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CGGA9J68000) for the full incident. Invisible in the rendered projection, since only a heading's title is ever written back to disk (`kg:title`'s removal made the lead-in term the one and only title mechanism, with `update` compensating for heading targets only) — but `tree`, `unfold` and `backlinks --text` all display the stale title in the graph itself, with the summary line's `0 changed` giving no hint either way. No CLI route yet to repair an already-stale title short of tombstoning the block. Found live while rewriting `design/aperas-skill.md`'s Topology paragraph for the `aperas` skill's v2; full account in [discussion/linking.md](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CGF83D4G001). **Resolved**: `runUpdate`'s paragraph branch now reads `title = firstChild.title` too (already computed by the parser for every paragraph, nothing new to derive), skipped only when `target` itself is a heading (where `firstChild` is the adopted leading paragraph, a different piece of text from the heading's own title). Decoupled from `props`, which must stay untouched for a non-heading re-title — a new `retitling` flag (true only for an actual heading retitle) now gates the two prop-replacement sites that used to key off `title !== undefined` alone. Verified live: a scratch paragraph's title updates on content change and a scratch listItem's `orderedList`/`startIndex`/`checked` props survive a title-changing update untouched; a real heading retitle still replaces its own props (tree-anchor) as before.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CGFE4SV0002",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A6178001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:26.879Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A6178001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800K --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800K",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A6178002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:27.650Z",
    "title": "**Collision check only covered a block's current slug-path**",
    "text": "**Collision check only covered a block's current slug-path**: resolved — Slice 2's collision check (`apeironNgn/node.ts`'s `rejectSlugPathCollisions`) checks every anchor `name` still embedded in a block's `treeAnchor` prop or raw text too, not just its current `toPath()`, since [Anchor-Matching Requirement for Resolution](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG005) already treats either as a live resolvable target. See [Planning](../planning/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ77F000X).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800K",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A6CZ0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:28.563Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A6CZ0001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800J --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800J",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A6CZ0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:29.309Z",
    "title": "**A lead-in colon followed only by nested list children got no title**",
    "text": "**A lead-in colon followed only by nested list children got no title**: resolved — `findLeadInColonOffset`'s space-after-colon check read the next character from the colon's own mdast text node, which simply ends at the colon when nothing inline follows it on the same line (e.g. `- **Heading**:` before a nested list), and treated that the same as a genuinely ambiguous no-space case. Fixed by accepting \"nothing left in this text run\" the same as \"a space follows\" — the inline-code and bold/italic exclusions run first and are unaffected. See [Lead-In Term Detection](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG000) and [Discussion](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR00E).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800J",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A6T88001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:25.240Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A6T88001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800H --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800H",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A6T88002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:25.982Z",
    "title": "**Cross-artifact link staleness on ingestion**",
    "text": "**Cross-artifact link staleness on ingestion**: resolved — an artifact whose own text doesn't change is never re-ingested on its own, so a link it couldn't resolve stayed stale even after its target came into existence elsewhere in the corpus, with no way to fix it short of hand-editing `ingestedHash` to force a re-ingest. `kg:ingest` now stashes every code a block fails to resolve on its own referring artifact, and retries every stashed code against the current graph after its main work is done, force-reingesting (without discarding reconciliation identity — see [Discussion](../discussion/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ76TR008) for the `hadContent`/`bypassUnchangedCheck` distinction) any artifact where at least one now resolves. See [Workflows](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG007).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800H",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A7610001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:33.462Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A7610001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800G --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800G",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A7610002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:34.195Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A7610002",
    "text": "**Every `<block-slug-path>` written so far omitted its own H1 segment.** `toPath()` (`apeironNgn/node.ts:455-471`) walks every `BlockNode` ancestor — the H1 heading included — contributing a slug segment for each, stopping only at the owning `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`. Every anchor and cross-reference in this concern's own docs had been computed without that leading segment (traced back to the very first `test-slugifed-path.md` validation, which never included its own H1 either) and has been corrected corpus-wide. See [Topology](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T8007).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800G",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A7JDR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:35.122Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A7JDR001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800F --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800F",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A7JDR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:35.864Z",
    "title": "**\"Duplicate-slug collisions\"**",
    "text": "**\"Duplicate-slug collisions\"**: misdiagnosed — a collision only happens when two blocks share an identical *full* slug-path (same heading text at the exact same tree position), a genuine authoring error, correctly a hard reject, not a case needing disambiguation logic. Reusing the same heading title at different tree positions is ordinary and never collides, since the full path includes the distinguishing ancestor segments. See [Topology](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T8007).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800F",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A810G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:30.246Z",
    "title": "00CJ3A810G001",
    "text": "> tsx packages/cli/src/aperas.ts show BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800E --text",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800E",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ3A810G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T15:34:30.994Z",
    "title": "**\"No automated rename repair\"**",
    "text": "**\"No automated rename repair\"**: misdiagnosed — a rename adds an anchor rather than replacing one, so an old reference doesn't break at rename time; there's nothing to repair, only a later cleanup once it's actually safe. See [Architecture](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T8002).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A800E",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
