        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`aperas retype` — an identity-preserving block type-conversion channel, for refactoring/migration only**",
    "text": "**`aperas retype` — an identity-preserving block type-conversion channel, for refactoring/migration only**: agreed, built and verified 2026-09-17; the entries below are this design's own working record, in the order it actually went. Today a block's `type` is pinned everywhere a write could touch it: `kgUpdate.ts` builds both its `--text-only` and reconcile shapes with `type: target.type`, so a piped input's own type is never adopted; `reconcileTree` could not pair the two sides anyway, since containers match partitioned `byType` (a `listItem` and a `heading` land in different buckets and never meet) and leaves match on `leafKey`, which keys a heading on `.title` and a paragraph on `.text` — unequal by construction, in the cross-parent salvage stage too. `kg:update` separately refuses a heading depth change outright (`oldDepth !== newDepth` throws). Each guard is correct on its own: an ordinary edit must never silently restructure. Together they leave no in-place route at all, so the only way to change a block's type today is `remove` + `insert`, which tombstones the id and every citation pointing at it — precisely what *Preserve identity* exists to prevent.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CJ50DWW0001",
      "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8002",
      "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8003",
      "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8004",
      "BlockNode:00CJ4XG0RG001",
      "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8005",
      "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8006"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "The data model has no objection",
    "text": "The data model has no objection: `reconcile.ts`'s `carryForwardFields` carries `blockId` and `links` onto the **new** node and pointedly does *not* carry `type`, so \"same identity, different type\" is already expressible. Only the CLI pin and the matcher stand in the way, which is what makes this a narrow addition rather than an architectural one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Shape**",
    "text": "**Shape**: `aperas retype <ref> --to <type>`, where `<type>` is `h1`..`h6` (heading, depth carried in the type itself) or any non-heading block type (`paragraph`, `listItem`, `code`, `blockquote`, …). Heading depth change is therefore the same operation as any other conversion (`h2` → `h3`), not a special case — one verb covers both pinned structural fields. Preserves id, `links`, children, parent and position; `.text` is never touched. Title is re-derived for the new type: an `h*` target rebuilds the heading line at the requested depth from the node's existing title words; a non-heading target re-derives via the ordinary lead-in-term rule.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Deliberately type-only, not a content channel**",
    "text": "**Deliberately type-only, not a content channel**: changing a block's text stays `aperas update`'s job, run separately afterwards. The cost is a real one to design around — converting a heading away from `heading` re-derives its title from `.text`, which for a heading is its *body* (the consumed leading paragraph) and normally carries no lead-in term, so the title falls back to the raw block id and the heading's own words survive only in whatever the operator captured first. The verb must therefore report the outgoing title in its own result line, and warn when a conversion is about to leave a node titled by id fallback, so a migration can compose the follow-up `update --text-only` from it rather than recovering it from `git`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Props**",
    "text": "**Props**: a type-specific prop is meaningless on the new type (a heading's `treeAnchor`; a `listItem`'s `orderedList`/`startIndex`/`checked`), so a conversion drops the ones belonging to the old type rather than carrying stale state forward. Not a concern for the migration that motivated this — none of the 16 nodes involved carries an anchor prop — but it is the kind of leftover that turns into a rendering bug later.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
