    ],
    "title": "00CJ4H8ZJ0001",
    "text": "**This doc's own Freeflow grouping does not round-trip, and that is what makes the heading→lead-in-term refactor structural rather than cosmetic.** The 16 entries below `## Freeflow` were moved under it as real children, but they are themselves `##` headings, and markdown nests headings by `#` count alone — so a `##` under a `##` is a sibling on re-read, not a child. Confirmed by parsing this file's own projection: `## Freeflow` comes back with **zero** children and all 16 entries re-parse as its siblings. A corpus-wide scan finds exactly these 16 and nothing else, so it is not a pre-existing pattern. Nothing is lost today (the graph is the source, and the `id/` anchors mean every id survives a re-read), but any actual re-ingest of this path would silently flatten the grouping back out. The fix is converting them to lead-in-term `listItem`s, which nest by indentation and so carry unbounded depth — verified live that a three-level nested list round-trips intact. Blocked on an identity-preserving type-conversion channel, planned in [discussion/core.md](../discussion/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8001); `remove`+`insert` is the only route today and would tombstone 19 blocks carrying 34 incoming citations.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ37GPRR009",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ4X8VB0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-17T17:22:37.082Z",
    "title": "00CJ4X8VB0001",
    "text": "**Automatic title migration across the heading boundary — built, not left to a manual follow-up.** Reconsidered from the item below: a heading and a non-heading store their title in genuinely different places (a field vs. a lead-in folded into `.text`), so crossing that boundary now migrates the title *with* the type, in whichever direction applies, instead of leaving the operator to compose a follow-up `update`. Heading → non-heading folds the heading's own words into `.text` as a canonical `**words**: <body>` lead-in — the always-uncapped shape, since the whole span before the colon sits inside one bold node — then derives the new title from that text via the real parser, not a hand-built guess. Non-heading → heading does the exact inverse: cuts the existing lead-in back out using `findLeadInSpliceOffset` (`astParser.ts`), the identical colon `project.ts`'s own anchor splice already relies on, so the cut lands exactly where the title was actually derived from. Verified live on the corpus's hardest real case (`kg:update`/`kg:insert` never resolve wikilinks — a title with embedded backtick colons and an em-dash): converts to a `listItem` with both links intact, and converting back reproduces the original title and text byte-for-byte. A depth-only heading change (`h2` → `h3`) and a non-heading → non-heading retype still never touch `.text` at all — only actually crossing the boundary does. The one remaining gap is a lead-in that isn't a single, fully-bold span, or missing entirely: that degrades to the old id-fallback behavior, `.text` left alone, `untitled` reported — the same graceful failure the parser already uses everywhere else.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJ4XG0RG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Automatic title migration across the heading boundary, built in rather than left to a manual follow-up**",
    "text": "**Automatic title migration across the heading boundary, built in rather than left to a manual follow-up**: reconsidered from the item above — a heading and a non-heading store their title in genuinely different places (a field vs. a lead-in folded into `.text`), so crossing that boundary now migrates the title *with* the type, in whichever direction applies. Heading → non-heading folds the heading's own words into `.text` as a canonical `**words**: <body>` lead-in — the always-uncapped shape, since the whole span before the colon sits inside one bold node — then derives the new title from that text via the real parser, not a hand-built guess. Non-heading → heading does the exact inverse: cuts the existing lead-in back out using `findLeadInSpliceOffset` (`astParser.ts`), the identical colon `project.ts`'s own anchor splice already relies on, so the cut lands exactly where the title was actually derived from. Verified live on the corpus's hardest real case (`kg:update`/`kg:insert` never resolve wikilinks — a title with embedded backtick colons and an em-dash): converts to a `listItem` with both links intact, and converting back reproduces the original title and text byte-for-byte. A depth-only heading change (`h2` → `h3`) and a non-heading → non-heading retype still never touch `.text` at all — only actually crossing the boundary does. The one remaining gap is a lead-in that isn't a single, fully-bold span, or missing entirely: that degrades to the old id-fallback behavior, `.text` left alone, `untitled` reported — the same graceful failure the parser already uses everywhere else.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ4H44J8001",
