        "@id": "BlockNode:00CJE11FRR000:props:StringProp:00CJE11K7R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Body text renders at full brightness, not dimmed by default**",
    "text": "**Body text renders at full brightness, not dimmed by default**: `.fd-abstract` and the link-preview popover's own abstract used to sit at reduced opacity or a muted color (`0.75`, `var(--fg-muted)`) for no reason tied to actual state. Dimming is reserved for things that are genuinely secondary — a tombstoned tag, a truncation ellipsis, the poll status line — not for a node's own stored text. A link's title is the one exception in the other direction: it's link-highlighted (the same blue as an inline wikilink) precisely because it's the part that's clickable, while its abstract stays at that same full brightness rather than sharing the highlight.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJE14W0R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A link row shows the target's own kind glyph next to the link glyph, and its title/abstract split like a normal node's**",
    "text": "**A link row shows the target's own kind glyph next to the link glyph, and its title/abstract split like a normal node's**: `RenderLinkItem.targetDisplayLabel` (added alongside `targetTitle`, populated in every mode but `no-target`) is what makes the first half possible — no second lookup, same reasoning as `RenderNodeItemFound.displayLabel`. The two used to run together as one `\"title — abstract\"` string inside a single highlighted span; now only the title carries the link highlight, and the abstract renders as an ordinary `.fd-abstract` line beneath it, so a link preview reads as \"a title, then its actual body text\" the same way an unfolded node does, rather than one long highlighted run.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJE94F88001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Backlinks surfaced in the tree UI, 2026-09-18**",
    "text": "**Backlinks surfaced in the tree UI, 2026-09-18**: `FolderDiv` should show, on each node's own title line, a small badge with its backlink count, floating right; clicking it opens a popover above the line listing each backlink as a folded-node-style entry (kind glyph, title, abstract); clicking one of those entries should navigate to it — zoom the apex to the backlink's *source* (the node containing the citing `Link`) with that link pre-unfolded, so the reader lands in the citing context already expanded rather than just at a bare id. Plumbing: `backlinks(store, id, 'target')` (`node.ts`) already gives an indexed, non-scanning count per node — add `backlinkCount: number` to `RenderNodeItemFound` in `buildNodeItem`, gated the same as its other found-node fields, and mirror it into `render.ts`. The popover's actual list reuses the CLI's own `runBacklinks`/`BacklinkEntry` (`kgBacklinks.ts`) via the existing service `'backlinks'` op — no new core computation — fetched lazily on click through a new `/api/backlinks?id=` dev-bridge route, matching `/api/tree`'s own on-demand-fetch pattern in `Inline.tsx`'s hover popover. `BacklinkEntry` needs one new field, `ownerId`, since a `Link`'s id encodes its owner only by string convention (`ownerOfLink`'s own doc comment says querying, not parsing, is how every other lookup here resolves it) — `runBacklinks` already computes `ownerId` internally, so this is exposing an existing value, not new computation.",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJE97N2G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Backlinks surfaced in the tree UI, 2026-09-18**",
    "text": "**Backlinks surfaced in the tree UI, 2026-09-18**: `FolderDiv` should show, on each node's own title line, a small badge with its backlink count, floating right; clicking it opens a popover above the line listing each backlink as a folded-node-style entry (kind glyph, title, abstract); clicking one of those entries should navigate to it — zoom the apex to the backlink's *source* (the node containing the citing `Link`) with that link pre-unfolded, so the reader lands in the citing context already expanded rather than just at a bare id. Plumbing: `backlinks(store, id, 'target')` (`node.ts`) already gives an indexed, non-scanning count per node — add `backlinkCount: number` to `RenderNodeItemFound` in `buildNodeItem`, gated the same as its other found-node fields, and mirror it into `render.ts`. The popover's actual list reuses the CLI's own `runBacklinks`/`BacklinkEntry` (`kgBacklinks.ts`) via the existing service `'backlinks'` op — no new core computation — fetched lazily on click through a new `/api/backlinks?id=` dev-bridge route, matching `/api/tree`'s own on-demand-fetch pattern in `Inline.tsx`'s hover popover. `BacklinkEntry` needs one new field, `ownerId`, since a `Link`'s id encodes its owner only by string convention (`ownerOfLink`'s own doc comment says querying, not parsing, is how every other lookup here resolves it) — `runBacklinks` already computes `ownerId` internally, so this is exposing an existing value, not new computation.",
