    ],
    "title": "**A node's title line carries its own backlink count, and a click reveals them**",
    "text": "**A node's title line carries its own backlink count, and a click reveals them**: `RenderNodeItemFound.backlinkCount` (`backlinks(store, id, 'target').length` in `buildNodeItem`, an indexed store match, not a corpus scan — `0` while `hidden`, like this item's sibling found-node fields) drives a small badge floating right on the title line, shown only once there's something to show. Clicking it fetches the actual list lazily (the dev bridge's `/api/backlinks`, a thin wrapper over the CLI's own `backlinks` service op) and opens a popover above the line (or below, clamped into the viewport, when there isn't room above), each entry rendered like a folded node's own preview — kind glyph, title, abstract — since that's what a backlink is: another node's title-and-text, citing this one. A plain click on an entry unfolds both the citing `Link` and its owning node *without changing the apex*, then scrolls to wherever the owner actually renders — the same canonical-position machinery [both sites already obey](#id/BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR003) decides whether that's inline content, a pointer elsewhere in the current render, or nowhere at all if the owner isn't reachable from the current apex (in which case the scroll silently finds nothing, same as any anchor to an absent id). Ctrl-click is the stronger action — it also zooms the apex to the owner — matching ctrl-click's meaning everywhere else in this app rather than introducing a third gesture. Revealing the link needs both unfolds, not just the link's own: a node's own links only render at all once the node itself is in the view's `unfolds` set (`buildNodeItem`'s `linksToShow`) — the link's own unfold alone leaves it invisible until its owner is unfolded too.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CJFDGBVR001",
