    ],
    "title": "**A link row's click toggles its own fold state; ctrl-click zooms**",
    "text": "**A link row's click toggles its own fold state; ctrl-click zooms**: `preview` and `expanded` share one row-level `onClick` — plain click unfolds (`preview`) or folds (`expanded`) that link, ctrl/meta-click zooms to its target instead, mirroring the same modifier-key convention this UX pass gave the [backlinks popover](#id/BlockNode:00CJEDTR40001). The `Gutter`'s own arrow/stem click still works too (`e.stopPropagation()` keeps the two from double-toggling on the same click) — this just extends the same fold/unfold gesture to the rest of the row instead of leaving it as the arrow's own private affordance. `pointer`/`outside-view` rows are unchanged: they have no fold state of their own to toggle, so their plain click still jumps directly, same as before.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CKE3TGD0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-19T17:23:29.263Z",
    "title": "**Frontmatter-backed `description` replaces the abstract dupe, 2026-09-20**",
    "text": "**Frontmatter-backed `description` replaces the abstract dupe, 2026-09-20**: found while chasing why a `../discussion/linking.md` link inside `linking.md`'s own `**Discussion**:` line still rendered `inline-link-inert` in a folder listing, even after the bare-relative-path resolver fix and the `bakeResolvedLinkIds` render-time fix ([history/linking.md](../history/linking.md), [history/webapp.md](../history/webapp.md), both 2026-09-18) were verified working for the direct case. Root cause: a folder/artifact listing's abstract reads `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.text`, which is a *copy* of the first content-bearing descendant's text (`extractAbstract`), made once at hydration time — the descendant's own `Link` (confirmed already correctly resolved via `aperas show`) is never duplicated onto the container, so `bakeResolvedLinkIds(node.text, node.links)` finds `node.links` empty and has nothing to bake with.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CKE3TGD0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-19T17:23:30.505Z",
    "title": "A narrower fix was considered first",
    "text": "A narrower fix was considered first: a persisted `abstractSourceId` pointer riding alongside the existing `.text` copy, set at the same hydration moment, so render time could cheaply look up the real source's links. That fell apart on one question: how does the copy get refreshed when a descendant changes? Checked directly against `kgUpdate.ts` — it doesn't. `target.text = text` there never touches an ancestor's own `.text`; only a full re-hydration (`hydrateFromParsed`) or the one-time holder-promotion path (`kgProject.ts`) ever recomputes it. So the abstract is a stale-until-next-full-reingest snapshot *by design* — a reasonable feature when the main direction was ingestion (Phase 0: capture whatever's on disk), but a flaw now that the main direction is projection/live editing: once captured, it's stuck there forever, and a pointer riding on top of a frozen copy would only add a second thing that's frozen alongside it, not fix the freezing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CKE3TGD8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-19T17:23:31.676Z",
    "title": "Chosen direction instead",
    "text": "Chosen direction instead: retire the copy-and-heuristic mechanism (`extractAbstract`/`deriveAbstractFromLiveChildren`) for `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode` entirely. Give both a real, directly-authored `description` field living in the artifact/folder's own YAML frontmatter — already parsed out separately by `astParser.ts` via `remark-frontmatter`, but today kept fully opaque past one narrow `lang:` regex read. `description` may contain markdown links, extracted into real `Link`s owned by the container itself, so the original baking bug dissolves rather than needing a workaround: the container legitimately owns its own abstract's links now. Storage: stop treating the whole frontmatter block as one opaque `StringProp` — parse it into one `StringProp` per key found, known or not, so a hand-authored key the engine doesn't yet recognize still round-trips through projection instead of silently vanishing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CKE3TGD8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-19T17:23:32.806Z",
    "title": "Edit path settled without inventing anything new",
    "text": "Edit path settled without inventing anything new: this is the project's own long-standing \"update as a scoped reconciliation\" philosophy, making direct use of the ingestion mechanism — `aperas update <id>` already re-parses piped markdown and reconciles it exactly like a real re-ingest would. It just has two gaps to close: it rejects `FolderNode` targets outright today, and it never consumes the `frontmatter` that `parseMarkdownTree` already extracts from the piped input. Closing both means editing `description` is just piping a doc with a `description:` frontmatter line at the exact same call site every other edit already uses — no bespoke flag, no separate command.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CKE3TGD8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-19T17:23:34.135Z",
    "title": "Migration still needed",
    "text": "Migration still needed: every already-tracked artifact/folder holds the old opaque `frontmatter` prop and a stale `.text` copy; a one-time pass has to split existing frontmatter into per-key props and seed `description` from the current `.text` wherever `description` isn't already set. Filed as an Open Issue ([issues/core.md](../issues/core.md)) rather than a Design entry — `design/core.md` doesn't exist yet, and this corpus's own convention reserves Design for crystallized/built architecture, not a plan agreed in conversation but not yet implemented.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
