    ],
    "title": "**`reconcile.ts`'s `carryForwardFields` corrupts `title` on any fallback-titled block it reconciles, breaking its own path resolution**",
    "text": "**`reconcile.ts`'s `carryForwardFields` corrupts `title` on any fallback-titled block it reconciles, breaking its own path resolution**: found live via [kg:migrate-frontmatter's corpus-wide re-ingestion run](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CKSAK04R001) (2026-09-20). `astParser.ts`'s fallback title for a block with no extractable lead-in term (`let title = blockId`) is the block's own *fresh, scratch* id, assigned during that parse, before reconciliation ever runs — not a function of its text the way every other title is. `carryForwardFields` carries the matched pair's *old* `blockId` forward (correctly preserving identity) but never re-synced `title` to match, on the explicit — and, for this one case, false — assumption in its own doc comment that a matched block's freshly-computed title is always already identical to what carrying the old one forward would have produced. Confirmed live: 37 blocks across the corpus had `title` silently overwritten with a fresh scratch id from a discarded parse the moment they were reconciled — the same family as [the `ArtifactNode`-title corruption already fixed above](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CGM54FX0001), one level down, at the ordinary-block scale, and with the same downstream break: `toPath()`'s slug-based addressing uses `title` directly, so a corrupted one makes the block unreachable by its own path. **Resolved**: `carryForwardFields` now captures the fresh node's own about-to-be-discarded `blockId` before overwriting it, and re-points `title` at the id it's actually keeping whenever `title` was only ever mirroring that discarded value. Not yet re-verified against a live corpus-wide reconciliation pass — the run that surfaced it was reverted, corruption and all, rather than repaired in place.",
