    ],
    "title": "**The stem is the fold control; a passive guide marks path**",
    "text": "**The stem is the fold control; a passive guide marks path**: a solid, hoverable stem appears only where a node is pinned *and* its children render as content, so clicking it always folds and never promises an action that isn't there. A row showing a child only because something deeper is pinned — a breadcrumb — gets a non-interactive sparse dotted guide instead (`repeating-linear-gradient`, roughly 2px on / 5px off; `╎` on the console), which is what tells a path stairway apart from real content. This restores the rule the [arrow and its stem are one control](#id/BlockNode:00CJDHYES8001) already implied and the Solid port lost by keying the stem on \"has children\". On the console the prefix has to be built from a per-level stack rather than `'│ '.repeat(depth)`, which cannot vary per column. [Rationale](../discussion/webapp.md#id/BlockNode:00CMWMMRGG001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMX5G968001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CMX5G968001:links:Link:00CMX5GBM0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CMWMVMV0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CMX5G968001:links:Link:00CMX5GBM0000:props:StringProp:00CMX5GBM0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "469"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The webapp's depth limit is derived from available width**",
    "text": "**The webapp's depth limit is derived from available width**: `floor((width − buffer) / gutter)` against a fixed `1.2rem` gutter, recomputed and refetched (debounced) on resize, and kept as `maxDepth` on the server render rather than truncated client-side so that `truncated` keeps a single meaning. A legibility backstop rather than a performance cap — at a typical viewport it lands far past anything this corpus nests to, so the `…` control is rare by construction. [Rationale](../discussion/webapp.md#id/BlockNode:00CMWMVMV0001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMX5GNB0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CMX5GNB0001:links:Link:00CMX5GQQ0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CMWMRX88001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CMX5GNB0001:links:Link:00CMX5GQQ0000:props:StringProp:00CMX5GQQ0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "539"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`TreeView` carries the child-display level as two orthogonal subset fields**",
    "text": "**`TreeView` carries the child-display level as two orthogonal subset fields**: `unfolds` keeps membership alone, so every existing sweep over it is untouched; `unfoldsCollapsed` and `unfoldsNoPreview` are subsets of it, and the default level (previews) is the absence of both, costing no storage. Orthogonal rather than one ordered scalar so that no mutual-exclusion invariant has to be enforced in code, and so that stashing remembers the level to return to. Both new fields have to join `removeDanglingUnfolds` and `pruneStaleUnfolds`. [The four sub-states, and why not a reified entry](../discussion/webapp.md#id/BlockNode:00CMWMRX88001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYG005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXB0X6R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-22T00:24:15.420Z",
    "title": "00CMXB0X6R001",
    "text": "scratch move-test node",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6TGSYR000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF4WTR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF4WTR001:props:StringProp:00CMXF4ZAR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "checked",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Slice 9 — the level fields on `TreeView`**",
    "text": "**Slice 9 — the level fields on `TreeView`**: add `unfoldsCollapsed` and `unfoldsNoPreview` as `reference`-kind subsets of `unfolds`, extend `removeDanglingUnfolds` and `pruneStaleUnfolds` to sweep both, and correct the former's doc comment where it claims `unfolds` is the only such field. Storage only — nothing reads the fields yet — so it lands and flushes safely on its own, and every existing view stays valid with both sets empty (the default level).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF5K98001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF5K98001:props:StringProp:00CMXF5NPG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "checked",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Slice 10 — level-aware rendering in core**",
    "text": "**Slice 10 — level-aware rendering in core**: split the double duty `isGenuinelyUnfolded` currently does in `buildNodeItem` (which children to walk *and* what tier they render at) so the titles level shows children title-only, and make the stashed level prune `cone.neededChildren` so a stashed node's pinned descendants stop forcing themselves into view. The pruning is the only part that touches cone *discovery* rather than tier assignment, and it deliberately breaks the standing guarantee that a pinned node is always reachable — with the knock-on that a hidden home lets a `Link` elsewhere win canonical position, which is intended. Carry the resolved level onto `RenderNodeItem` so both renderers can pick a glyph without recomputing it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF6AD8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF6AD8001:props:StringProp:00CMXF6D9G000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "checked",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Slice 11 — the verbs**",
    "text": "**Slice 11 — the verbs**: `fold` stops cascading by default, gaining `--recursive` for today's behaviour and `--stash` for the collapse that keeps the entry; `unfold` gains `--recursive`, `--no-preview` and `--unstash`. Service protocol ops and CLI flags together, since the webapp reaches them through the same ops. `fold`'s default changes for every existing caller, so `verify.ts`'s fold expectations have to be re-read rather than assumed — this is the one slice that alters behaviour already relied on.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF727R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF727R001:props:StringProp:00CMXF74T0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "checked",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Slice 12 — stem and guide on the console**",
    "text": "**Slice 12 — stem and guide on the console**: rebuild the line prefix from a per-level stack of ancestor states in both `toText` and `renderTreeLines`, replacing `'│ '.repeat(depth)`, which cannot vary per column — solid `│` where an ancestor is pinned with content, `╎` where it is only a breadcrumb. `toText`'s \"byte-identical to the old output by construction\" doc comment stops being true and is updated rather than left to quietly go false; `verify.ts`'s tree assertions are substring-based, so they do not trip.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF7RG0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CMXF7RG0001:props:StringProp:00CMXF7V0R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "checked",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Slice 13 — glyph and stem vocabulary in the UI**",
    "text": "**Slice 13 — glyph and stem vocabulary in the UI**: `▶`/`▷` rotated 0°/45°/90° off the level rather than the current open/closed boolean, `●` for a pinned leaf, `…` promoted from a marker beneath the row to the control itself at a depth cut-off (with the arrow disabled there, since nothing about the node can be toggled), and `hasStem` keyed on the level instead of on `hasChildren()` — which is what removes today's inert full-height stems. The passive guide is a `repeating-linear-gradient` rather than `dotted`/`dashed`, both of which read as a merely-lighter solid line at 1px.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
