    ],
    "title": "**`design/aperas-skill.md`'s Citation direction sat thirteen versions behind its own projection, and none of the three existing instruments could have caught it**",
    "text": "**`design/aperas-skill.md`'s Citation direction sat thirteen versions behind its own projection, and none of the three existing instruments could have caught it**: v1.1 generalized the sanctioned exception from \"a designated index\" to a **normative, singular** pointer, tested by whether removing the link leaves the block incomplete — cardinality is the real criterion and direction only its usual symptom. That landed in SKILL.md and was recorded as a delta ([v1.1](#id/BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR002)), but the design block it revised ([Citation direction](../design/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00H)) was never updated, and still asserted the pre-v1.1 wording at v2.7 — read top-down and quoted back as current before the gap was noticed. Measured while finding it: of design's 50 live blocks, 43 carry v1-authoring ids, 6 are v1.x-era and 1 is v2-era, with none after — additions land, revisions strand. The cause is structural rather than disciplinary: the edit loop's deep-write step answers \"what did this change make stale?\" by backlinks, and SKILL.md is a generative projection with no node and therefore no backlinks, so on the design ↔ SKILL.md edge that step has nothing to traverse. All three instruments sit on the other edge — `skill_drift.py` compares SKILL.md against the snapshot/delta thread, the [vertical thread](#id/BlockNode:00CF4D9XT0001) wires principles to their realizers *inside* the v1 snapshot and was never rebuilt past v1.2, and the snapshot/delta record is SKILL.md's own history rather than design's. `aperas backlinks` on the drifted block returned nothing at all. Remedy adopted: a version delta that revises a rule links **up** to the design block it revises — upward and so legal by citation direction, singular and constitutive and so sanctioned by that same rule's own test — which makes a design block's backlinks its status check, a backlink from a delta newer than the block's last edit being the staleness signal, and no backlinks at all meaning no version has yet revised it. Promoted to [issues/aperas-skill.md](../issues/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CN7G6D0G001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE8XWZK0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CN6SMSX0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CN6SMSX0001:links:Link:00CN6VN9N8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR004",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CN6SMSX0001:links:Link:00CN6VN9N8000:props:StringProp:00CN6VN9N8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "475"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CN6VN62G001",
    "text": "The test is whether removing the link leaves the block **incomplete**. A milestone whose content is \"this snapshot was taken\" no longer says what it exists to say once its pointer is gone, while \"here is a discussion that also touched on this\" loses only enrichment and stays forbidden. What the rule is actually against is a *list of children* — unbounded, discretionary, accumulating — so cardinality is the real criterion and direction is only its usual symptom. Reflects [v1.1](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CF5GK5BR004).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00G",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
