    ],
    "title": "**The frontmatter-`description` retirement of `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.text` landed on only one of the two preview paths, so `unfold` and `backlinks` still show the stale heuristic copy**",
    "text": "**The frontmatter-`description` retirement of `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode.text` landed on only one of the two preview paths, so `unfold` and `backlinks` still show the stale heuristic copy**: the direction agreed in [the parent issue](#id/BlockNode:00CKE4R93R001) shipped in `e66996b` — `description` became a real frontmatter-owned prop carrying its own bakeable `Link`s — and `buildNodeItem` (`node.ts:1799-1801`) was updated to read `description ?? .text` for `ArtifactNode`/`FolderNode`, which is what `aperas tree` and the webapp render through `emitNode`. `kgUnfold.ts`'s `nodeAbstract` (lines 32-46) was not: it reads `node.text` unconditionally, for every node kind, with no `description` check at all — and it feeds the unfolded target's own line, every structural child line, every link-target preview, and `aperas backlinks --text`, which is the reason that function was extracted in the first place. So for any artifact or folder whose authored `description` differs from the derived `.text`, the two surfaces disagree, and `aperas unfold` — the orientation command the skill puts first — renders precisely the heuristic copy the redesign set out to retire. Confirmed live 2026-09-22 against `issues/aperas-skill.md`, whose two values currently differ: `tree` and the webapp show the description, `unfold` shows `.text` both when the artifact is the target and when it appears as a child of its folder. It stayed invisible because the two values normally paraphrase each other — the corpus-wide frontmatter sweep seeded most descriptions from text that already said much the same thing — so only an artifact whose description was authored to differ exposes it. `aperas show` is not part of the defect: it prints the stored node raw, which is what made the divergence legible in the first place. Notably this is a re-run of a defect this same file already records in its own doc comment — \"this file has its own, separate preview-printing path (not `emitNode`), so it had its own separate instance of the same bug\" — the duplicated preview path diverging a second time, on the next change that crossed it. Fix direction: give `nodeAbstract` the same `description ?? .text` precedence `buildNodeItem` uses, or better, have both call one shared helper, since the recorded history of these two paths is that they diverge on every change that touches only one. Not yet implemented.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CN7XM9TG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "### Entry route — both directions, and the gap where they meet",
    "text": "The gradient that constrains citation is also the route for reading along it, and both directions are legitimate. Top-down — `design`, then `issues`/`history`, then `discussion`, then the code — narrows like a binary search and yields the frame: what the corpus claims about an area, and which facet the work is actually in. Bottom-up — a concrete identifier grepped against the store, landing on the exact node, then climbing outward — yields the pinpoint, which a top-down descent cannot supply without first choosing among dozens of sibling entries. Neither is a fallback for the other.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5Y5JG00D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CN7XM9TG002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CN7XM9TG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CN7XM9TG002:links:Link:00CN7YM2NR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CN7YEND0002",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CN7XM9TG002:links:Link:00CN7YM2NR000:props:StringProp:00CN7YM2P0000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "893"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00CN7YKYH0001",
    "text": "Drift between a tier and what it governs is detectable only where the two directions meet, because it is an absence as often as a contradiction — the abstract tier silent where the concrete has a rule, or asserting one the concrete has since replaced — and in both forms it is indistinguishable from agreement until something has been compared against it. Descending alone confirms the abstract; ascending alone confirms the concrete. Which axis carries the climb follows from citation direction: a lower block cites the higher one it realizes, so forward links usually run up the gradient, while a higher block points down only where it speaks normatively; backlinks are that same relation from the other end, and are how an abstract block's realizers below are found; ancestors climb within one artifact only, recovering the surrounding thread rather than the tier that governs it. Reflects [v2.8](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CN7YEND0002).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CN7XM9TG001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
