        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CN89ERE8000:props:StringProp:00CN89EWCG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`skill_drift.py` only ever checks that SKILL.md is contained in the record, never that the record is contained in SKILL.md, so text appended to a recorded entry is undetectable**",
    "text": "**`skill_drift.py` only ever checks that SKILL.md is contained in the record, never that the record is contained in SKILL.md, so text appended to a recorded entry is undetectable**: the ADDED pass asks `b not in haystack` against every recorded section joined into one string, and the DROPPED pass fingerprints each recorded entry by its first 120 characters (`b[:FINGERPRINT_CHARS] not in filetext`). Appending to the *tail* of a verbatim entry therefore leaves the original text intact as a substring and leaves the fingerprint prefix unchanged, and both passes report clean; only a prepend or an interior rewrite would be caught. Simulated against the live corpus on an in-scope v2.x delta entry (`BlockNode:00CGMY04AG003`), appending one sentence: ADDED does not flag, DROPPED does not flag. Found the hard way — two v1.1 delta paragraphs were contaminated exactly this way while the check reported `ADDED but unrecorded — 0` and `DROPPED, unaccounted — 0`, and the contamination surfaced only because a reader asked whether the appended sentence broke the verbatim rule. The reason a blanket \"record ⊆ file\" comparison cannot simply be added: the record deliberately mixes verbatim entries with meta scaffolding — a delta section's own framing text, a `Supersedes:` line, a pointer to where a rule was folded into design — and that split is carried by *convention* (meta on the entry heading, verbatim in its child paragraphs) rather than by any marked property the script can read. So the fix has to make the distinction explicit before it can enforce it: mark verbatim blocks as such and require exact equality for those, leaving heading/meta blocks exempt. Not yet implemented.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CN89NQKG001",
