        "value": "true"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Slice 16 — packaging**",
    "text": "**Slice 16 — packaging**: static asset serving on the same listener (the webapp's built `dist/`, with the current auth token injected into `index.html` server-side so the page's own same-origin JS can read it), a shared `apiFetch()` helper that attaches `X-Aperas-Token` on every call once the token is present, a new `aperas serve` subcommand that resolves the graph from cwd and ensures a service is running for it, and a `build.mjs` step that also builds the webapp and bundles its static output alongside the CLI binary — turning Slices 2/3 into one self-contained, distributable artifact rather than something only reachable from a source checkout.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNS7K630001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Slice 16 is verified end-to-end against a second graph**",
    "text": "**Slice 16 is verified end-to-end against a second graph**: the packaged binary, run from a throwaway graph's own directory with the AperasKG-bound service stopped first (the service is a single machine-wide singleton — `serviceLock.ts` — so this is sequential, not concurrent), must show that graph's own name in the UI and support fold/unfold/zoom/edit against it, exactly as it does against AperasKG from source. Slice 3's own adversarial checks (no token, wrong token, foreign `Origin`, non-loopback `Host`, an op outside the allowlist) are re-run against the packaged listener specifically, not assumed to still hold from testing the source checkout.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH8007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNS7TBTR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-09-23T09:21:25.398Z",
    "title": "**Package the webapp as a self-contained, distributable artifact (Slice 16)**",
    "text": "**Package the webapp as a self-contained, distributable artifact (Slice 16)**: static asset serving on the same in-service listener, an `apiFetch()` helper on the webapp side that attaches the per-run token, a new `aperas serve` subcommand, and a `build.mjs` step bundling the webapp's build output alongside the CLI binary — see [planning/webapp.md](../planning/webapp.md#id/BlockNode:00CNS7EXQ8000). Sequenced immediately after the HTTP listener, ahead of the tick channel — see [the revised ordering rationale](../planning/webapp.md#id/BlockNode:00CJ6V6CH0005).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6370005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
