        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Resolved: Slice 2 — per-run auth token**",
    "text": "**Resolved: Slice 2 — per-run auth token**: `randomBytes(32).toString('hex')`, generated once at service startup (`service.ts#main`) and written to the run directory (`serviceLock.ts#writeToken`) at mode 0600, alongside the lock and socket it already trusts. Verified per plan: file exists at 0600, and a service restart rotates it (a request bearing the old value is rejected once the new service is up).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V637000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNSME9Z8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Resolved: Slice 3 — HTTP listener**",
    "text": "**Resolved: Slice 3 — HTTP listener**: the route table itself (`apiRoutes.ts`) is shared with the dev-only bridge (`packages/web/devApiServer.ts`) rather than duplicated — one op allowlist, dispatched in-process via `handle()` (`service.ts`) for the production listener, or over the unix socket via `request()` for the dev bridge. Auth (`listenerAuth.ts`): loopback-only `Host`, an `Origin` allowlist, and `X-Aperas-Token` compared with `timingSafeEqual`; a restrictive `OPTIONS` preflight response (no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` for anything but the listener's own origin) is what actually stops a hostile cross-origin page's real request from ever being sent. Verified adversarially: no token, wrong token, foreign `Origin`, and non-loopback `Host` each rejected (401/401/403/400); an op outside the allowlist 404s even with a valid token; a same-origin call with a valid token and matching `Origin` succeeds and returns real graph content.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V637000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNSME9ZG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Resolved: Slice 16 — packaging**",
    "text": "**Resolved: Slice 16 — packaging**: static asset serving added to the same listener (`staticServe.ts`), `index.html` getting the current token injected server-side so the page's own same-origin JS can read it (`window.__APERAS_TOKEN__`); the webapp's `apiFetch()` helper attaches it to every `/api/*` call, a no-op in dev where the token is undefined. New `aperas serve` subcommand (`kgServe.ts`) resolves the graph from cwd, ensures a service is running for it, and prints the listener's URL. `build.mjs` now also runs `vite build` in `packages/web` and copies its `dist/` to `dist/web/`, sibling to the CLI bundle, so the packaged binary is self-contained. Verified end-to-end against a throwaway second graph (its own `aperas.config.json`, distinct name and content) with the AperasKG-bound service stopped first: the packaged `aperas serve` showed the throwaway graph's own name and content, fold/unfold/edit worked against it, an external CLI edit was picked up by the existing poll through the new listener, and every Slice 3 adversarial check was re-run against the packaged instance specifically rather than assumed from source-checkout testing. Surfaced one real bug along the way — `resolveEffectiveGraphName()` (`graphConfig.ts`) resolved from the *service process's own* cwd, which for a packaged binary is wherever it's installed, never the graph's directory; it silently reported \"AperasKG\" while genuinely serving the throwaway graph's data. Fixed by adding the same `APERAS_GRAPH_NAME` env-var passthrough `APERAS_APEIRON_ROOT`/`APERAS_ARTIFACTS_ROOT` already had, resolved once by `bindAndSpawn` (`kgService.ts`) from *its own* correct cwd and threaded through `spawnService`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V637000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CNSMR03R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CNSMR03R001:links:Link:00CNSMY860000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CNSME9ZG000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CNSMR03R001:links:Link:00CNSMY860000:props:StringProp:00CNSMY868000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1363"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`resolveEffectiveGraphName()` resolved from the wrong process's cwd, 2026-09-23**",
    "text": "**`resolveEffectiveGraphName()` resolved from the wrong process's cwd, 2026-09-23**: undetected until Slices 2/3/16 were verified against a genuine second graph, since this repo's own dev setup always runs the service with `cwd: monorepoRoot` (`vite.config.ts`'s spawn), where the ancestor-walk-then-fallback happened to land on AperasKG either way. The production listener calls `resolveEffectiveGraphName()` with no `startDir` from *inside* the long-lived service process — whose cwd, for a packaged binary, is wherever it's installed, never the graph's directory. Confirmed live: `aperas serve` from a throwaway graph's own directory reported `{\"name\":\"AperasKG\"}` over `/api/graph` while genuinely serving the throwaway graph's tree content. Fixed by giving name resolution the same `APERAS_GRAPH_NAME` env-var passthrough `APERAS_APEIRON_ROOT`/`APERAS_ARTIFACTS_ROOT` already had — resolved once, correctly, by `bindAndSpawn` (`kgService.ts`) from *its own* cwd (the same place the other two roots are resolved from) and threaded through `spawnService` into the child's env. The doc comment this session wrote for `resolveEffectiveGraphName` when the graph-name feature first landed claimed \"No env-var override... nothing to pass between a spawning and a spawned process\" — wrong, corrected in place rather than left to mislead the next reader. Folded into [Resolved: Slice 16 — packaging](../issues/webapp.md#id/BlockNode:00CNSME9ZG000).",
