        "target": "BlockNode:00CP9QYPNG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CP9R475R002:links:Link:00CP9R4BPG000:props:StringProp:00CP9R4BPG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "445"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "No skill counterpart for an installed `aperas`",
    "text": "No skill counterpart for an installed `aperas`: this project's own `aperas`/`kg-doc-ingest` skills only ever lived in this repo's `.claude/skills/`, so an agent working against a plain `npm install -g aperas` had the CLI with none of the graph-first discipline the skill encodes. Fixed by bundling both skills into the published package (`dist/skills/`) and adding `aperas skill install` to copy them into an agent's own `.claude/skills/` — see [history](../history/packaging.md#id/BlockNode:00CP9QYPNG001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDDVC8N0007",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CP9R7P50001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**2026-09-24: production listener's port fixed to follow the project's own naming convention**",
    "text": "**2026-09-24: production listener's port fixed to follow the project's own naming convention**: `aperas serve`'s HTTP+auth listener (Slices 2-3) defaulted to `4173` — Vite's own `vite preview` port, carried over incidentally rather than derived from anything project-specific, unlike the dev bridge's `2737`/`2734`. Changed to `2736`, continuing the same phone-keypad-spelling scheme: `apeo` (2=A, 7=P, 3=E, 6=O), from Ape[ras] + [Apei]o[n] — deliberately distinct from the dev ports so a dev server and a served instance can run side by side against the same graph without colliding. `serviceLock.ts#resolveHttpPort`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6N9R003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CP9RSEW0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CP9RSEW0001",
    "text": "> **Aperas-repo insiders**: `aperas` ships as a real published package (`npm install -g aperas`) as of `0.2.2`, but this repo still develops it from source — running the bare `aperas <verb> ...` commands below here would invoke whatever's globally installed, not your local changes. Run them as `npm run aperas -- <verb> ...` from `Aperas/monorepo/` instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNW5Y57G001",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CPYRQN40001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Resolved: the production webapp's hover link-preview never resolved, stuck on \"Loading…\"**",
    "text": "**Resolved: the production webapp's hover link-preview never resolved, stuck on \"Loading…\"**: `Inline.tsx`'s `LinkPopover` fetched `/api/tree` with a plain `fetch()`, never `apiFetch()` — unauthenticated against the production HTTP+auth listener (Slice 3), so it got a 401 with a JSON error body. `fetch()` doesn't reject on HTTP error statuses, and this call never checked `res.ok`, so the 401's `{error: ...}` body was parsed as if it were a real `TreeResponse`; `body.tree` was silently `undefined`, `preview()` never settled and `error()` never got set, leaving the loading guard (`preview() === undefined && !error()`) true forever. Predates Slice 3's auth work entirely — never exercised end-to-end against the real production listener until now. Fixed to match every other fetch in the app: `apiFetch()` plus a `res.ok`/`body.error` check. Verified live against the built package's own listener (not dev): dispatched a real `mouseenter` on a corpus link and confirmed the popover rendered its actual title/abstract instead of hanging.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V637000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CPYRTRA0001",
