    "title": "**A table/code block's own row title is a raw snowflake, not a readable name**",
    "text": "**A table/code block's own row title is a raw snowflake, not a readable name**: `table`/`code` blocks have no extractable lead-in term, so `astParser.ts`'s fallback title (the block's own snowflake id) is what the UI prints as the row's name. Split out from [the block-rendering bug it was originally filed under](#id/BlockNode:00CMX4NCW8001) once that bug's other two causes were fixed — a separate, server-side naming concern with no clear fix identified yet, not the same shape of problem.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6370002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CQ0VT1NR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Resolved: every markdown construct the webapp renders now goes through one shared `remark-gfm` AST parser, not four independent hand-rolled implementations**",
    "text": "**Resolved: every markdown construct the webapp renders now goes through one shared `remark-gfm` AST parser, not four independent hand-rolled implementations**: `MarkdownTable.tsx`, `MarkdownQuote.tsx`, `CodeBlock.tsx`'s fence detection, and `Inline.tsx`'s own inline tokenizer each parsed a different piece of the same spec by hand, free to disagree with `core`'s own `astParser.ts` (and with each other) on any edge case none of them happened to think of. Fixed with one new shared module (`markdown.ts`): `unified`+`remark-parse`+`remark-gfm` (same versions `core` already depends on) parses a block's own text into a real mdast AST once, and `classifyBlock` reads its top-level structure to decide whether it's a table/blockquote/code-fence/Mermaid-diagram/plain prose — replacing `MarkdownTable`'s hand-rolled `splitTableRow`/`isDelimiterRow`, `MarkdownQuote`'s regex check, `CodeBlock`'s fence-boundary parser, and `mermaidDetect.ts`'s diagram-type-keyword regex (now just `code.lang === 'mermaid'`, which also fixed a real gap: the old keyword list didn't know about every Mermaid diagram type). `Inline.tsx` now renders the AST's own inline nodes recursively (`renderInline`, exported so `MarkdownTable`/`MarkdownQuote` render an already-parsed cell/paragraph directly rather than re-serializing and re-parsing), which is also a real correctness gain: GFM strikethrough, nested formatting inside a link's own text, and autolinks all render correctly now, none of which the old regex tokenizer recognized at all. Along the way, `CodeBlock.tsx`'s own hand-rolled 5-language syntax highlighter was swapped for `highlight.js` (core + 21 explicitly registered languages, not the full ~190-language barrel — the 6 confirmed live in this corpus (`bash`/`javascript`/`typescript`/`json`/`markdown`/`rust`) plus 15 more common ones added ahead of need: `python`/`yaml`/`css`/`scss`/`xml`/`sql`/`go`/`java`/`c`/`cpp`/`csharp`/`dockerfile`/`ini`/`diff`/`graphql`), since the old keyword sets didn't even cover most of the corpus's own usage (no `json`, `rust`, `markdown`, or `text` entries existed). A fenced language outside that registered set degrades to plain, unhighlighted text rather than a guess — `highlightAuto` was tried first, but it only ever guesses *among the registered languages*, and was confirmed live to confidently mislabel a Python snippet as `bash` before `python` was added, which is worse than no highlighting at all. Verified live against real corpus content: a table renders identically (6 real `<table>` rows), a real `bash` fence gets correct `hljs-*` syntax classes, a scratch `python` fence highlights correctly too, and a blockquote with a bold lead-in plus multiple inline-code spans renders its nested formatting correctly. Both `MarkdownTable` and `CodeBlock` also gained a Copy button (`CodeBlock` had none before); the table's is view-aware — TSV in the rendered \"Table\" view (pastes into a spreadsheet), the original Markdown in \"Source\" view.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V637000F",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
