    ],
    "title": "**2026-09-25: inline `$…$` and display `$$…$$` math, rendered lazily via KaTeX**",
    "text": "**2026-09-25: inline `$…$` and display `$$…$$` math, rendered lazily via KaTeX**: `remark-math` joins `remark-gfm` in the shared `markdown.ts` processor (building on [the shared-parser rewrite](#id/BlockNode:00CQ1BGR2R001)), so `inlineMath`/`math` are real AST nodes. `Inline.tsx` renders both through a `lazy(() => import('./KatexMath'))` component: KaTeX and its CSS and fonts load only on a page that actually contains math, and the KaTeX chunk is the same one Mermaid already ships (not duplicated). The entry chunk grew ~6KB (the micromark math extension). A display block stays an ordinary `paragraph` node at ingestion (`core` doesn't parse math), and `Inline.tsx` renders its `math` child as display math. Single-`$` was chosen knowingly: prose like \"$5 to $10\" parses as math, and the fix is escaping (`\\$`). Same change: `vite.config.ts`'s `chunkSizeWarningLimit` raised to 1500kB, since only lazy-loaded Mermaid internals (elk ~1.46MB, one GWT-compiled file, not splittable) exceeded the 500kB default.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CJ6V6N9R003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CQ2WETBG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CQ2WETBG001:links:Link:00CQ2WEY70000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CQ2N1Z1R000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CQ2WETBG001:links:Link:00CQ2WEY70000:props:StringProp:00CQ2WEY88000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "496"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**2026-09-25: single `~` no longer strikes through**",
    "text": "**2026-09-25: single `~` no longer strikes through**: GFM's default `singleTilde: true` turned `range 5~10 and 20~30` into \"5<del>10 and 20</del>30\" (hit in real use). The shared processor in `markdown.ts` now passes `remarkGfm` `{ singleTilde: false }`, so only `~~x~~` strikes and a single `~` (approximately, ranges, `~/` paths) renders literally. A corpus-wide parse beforehand found no existing strikethrough of either form, so nothing depended on the single-tilde form. Web-only, alongside [inline/display math](#id/BlockNode:00CQ2N1Z1R000): `core` uses `remark-gfm` only for block boundaries, which strikethrough never affects.",
