    "title": "**The ApeironNgn service has died silently twice in one session, and a stuck prior process can survive `service stop`/`start`**",
    "text": "**The ApeironNgn service has died silently twice in one session, and a stuck prior process can survive `service stop`/`start`**: found 2026-09-27 during the skill-v3.1 work. First: `service restart`/`service start` timed out (\"did not become ready in time\") once while a prior instance was still mid-rehydration under load; a retry a few seconds later succeeded. Second, worse: the service died mid-turn with no error until the next call, and the stuck old process (90+ minutes old, no lock file, invisible to `service stop`) sat consuming growing CPU/memory; `service start` failed twice with the same \"did not become ready\" message before it was killed by PID and a fresh start came up clean in about a minute. No data was lost either time — every write had been flushed and survived on disk — but the failure mode itself (a dead process with no lock file, not visible to `service stop`, and a readiness check that may be too tight for this corpus's current size) is new and undiagnosed. Promoted to [issues/core.md's Open Issues](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CRBJ0VYG001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZE18004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CRBJ0VYG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The ApeironNgn service can die silently, and a stuck prior process can survive `service stop`/`start`**",
    "text": "**The ApeironNgn service can die silently, and a stuck prior process can survive `service stop`/`start`**: found 2026-09-27, twice in one session. First: `service restart`/`service start` timed out (\"did not become ready in time\") once while a prior instance was still mid-rehydration under load; a retry a few seconds later succeeded. Second, worse: the service died mid-turn with no error until the next call, and the stuck old process (90+ minutes old, no lock file, invisible to `service stop`) sat consuming growing CPU/memory; `service start` failed twice with the same \"did not become ready\" message before the stuck process was killed by PID and a fresh start came up clean in about a minute. No data was lost either time — every write had been flushed and survived on disk. Undiagnosed: why the process dies silently, why it leaves no lock file and is invisible to `service stop`, and whether the readiness check is too tight for this corpus's current size.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CRBW8248001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CRBW8248001:links:Link:00CRCHQQ3G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CRCH1DBR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00CRBW8248001:links:Link:00CRCHQQ3G000:props:StringProp:00CRCHQQ3G001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "618"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**`aperas update --text-only` always reports \"overflow prepended, no reconciliation\", even with zero overflow**",
    "text": "**`aperas update --text-only` always reports \"overflow prepended, no reconciliation\", even with zero overflow**: `kgUpdate.ts:444` prints that message unconditionally whenever `--text-only` is passed, not gated on whether any content actually followed the leading paragraph. Confirmed live: a bare single-line `--text-only` update against a disposable throwaway node printed the same message with nothing to prepend. Behavior is correct — nothing gets reconciled away either way — only the message is misleading. First noticed in Turn 4 of the skill-v3.1 recording task, confirmed reproducible 2026-09-27. Promoted to [issues/cli.md](../issues/cli.md#id/BlockNode:00CRCH1DBR001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CR81TAV8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CRBWA180001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
