    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CV2CFX70006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CV2CFX70006",
    "text": "```bash\ngrep -n -C3 '<identifier>' AperasKG/Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld\n```",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CV2CFX70005",
    "type": "code",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CV2CFX70007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CV2CFX70007",
    "text": "`-C3` context is essential to capture the `@id` line and surrounding fields (which vary in position).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CV2CFX70005",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CV2CFX70008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CV2CFX70008",
    "text": "> [!WARNING]\n> **Grep on symlinks (`git grep` and `grep -r` skip `AperasKG`)**:\n> `git grep` and standard `grep -r` (lowercase `-r`) do **not** follow symlinks and will silently skip `AperasKG` when run from the code repo root! Always use `grep -R` (uppercase `-R`) to follow symlinks when searching across repos, or target `AperasKG/Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld` directly. When running commands from `monorepo/`, target `../AperasKG/Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CV2CFX70005",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CV2CFX70009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00CV2CFX70009",
    "text": "> [!WARNING]\n> `git grep` and `grep -r` skip symlinked directories like `AperasKG -> ../AperasKG`. Always use `grep -R` to follow symlinks, or specify the path directly (e.g. `grep -n -C3 '<pattern>' AperasKG/Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld` from repo root, or `../AperasKG/Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld` from `monorepo/`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CV2CFX70005",
    "type": "blockquote",
    "children": []
  },
