        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Verification — the v4.3 delta, design, History and four issues confirmed**",
    "text": "**Verification — the v4.3 delta, design, History and four issues confirmed**: The orchestrator re-ran `skill_drift.py` from `Aperas/` bare, with `--added` and with `--dropped`: all exit 0, with 0 Status, 0 ADDED unrecorded, 0 DROPPED unaccounted and 192 superseded and accounted for. `aperas check-links` is a clean pass. The four filed tool gaps exist as issue entries: the unhandled `EPIPE` and the `aperas --help` skill-count mismatch in `issues/cli.md`, the spurious \"No ApeironNgn service running\" in `issues/core.md`, and the `skill_drift.py` truncation with no `--full` flag in `issues/aperas-skill.md`. History carries v4.2 and v4.3 milestones. The grapher also reported writing two wrong claims into long-lived docs before verifying them (an unfold-truncation claim, and guessed dates and counts in an issue) and correcting both.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVMS4YMR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Decision — file the missing way to read a node's full descendant text, and leave the `--text` flag mistake alone**",
    "text": "**Decision — file the missing way to read a node's full descendant text, and leave the `--text` flag mistake alone**: (orchestrator, applying the user's \"promote all issues\") The grapher's Turn 1 and 2 Reports needed `aperas show --text` per node, 51 calls in one case, because `unfold` gives abstracts and nothing prints a subtree's full text; that is a real tool gap and is filed as an issue. Passing `--text` to `aperas tree`, which has no such flag, was the grapher's own mistake and stays in Freeflow.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CVN8JG28001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVMS4YMR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Decision — History's Current Status gains a sentence pointing at the v4 milestones**",
    "text": "**Decision — History's Current Status gains a sentence pointing at the v4 milestones**: (orchestrator, not yet confirmed by the user) It stops at v3.1 and is stale.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVMSSQ68001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Decision — verify each finished task, then prune**",
    "text": "**Decision — verify each finished task, then prune**: (user, answer to a direct question) The grapher checks each finished task's outcome against the graph and tombstones only those whose content is fully promoted, in `.threads/grapher.md` and `.threads/coder.md`. This session's tasks (`standard-aperas-command-in-skill-bodies`, `document-dev-shim-in-packaging`, `file-check-links-dangling-target-issue` and `skill-wording-update-v4-3`) stay until the user closes the task.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVMY43DR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**No command prints the full text of a node's descendants in one call**",
    "text": "**No command prints the full text of a node's descendants in one call**: `aperas show <ref> --text` prints one node's exact stored text; `aperas unfold <ref>` (the read-only peek) prints the target's text and the text of each immediate child and link target, in full since the rendering fix, but only that one level; `aperas tree` prints titles only, and `aperas tree --view <view>` adds text only for nodes the view has unfolded, one `unfold` call per node. Reading a Report, a Freeflow section or any subtree whose children carry the content therefore takes a loop of `aperas show --text` over the ids that `tree` lists, or one `unfold` per parent. Observed twice in the v4.3 skill task: the grapher's Turn 1 and 2 Reports needed 51 `show --text` calls to read one long Report, and the filing sweep over five Freeflow headings needed about 95 (roughly 15 seconds per 30 calls, each one a separate process start). `tree`'s title-only output does not help choose what to read, because a bullet whose lead-in is too long to become a title is listed by its block id (see the lead-in title issue in `issues/linking.md`). Fix: a depth flag on the read-only `unfold` peek, as `tree` already has `--depth`, printing each descendant's id and full text down to that depth. Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CE1GW638002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D7V5DRZ8001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVMYB5W0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A bold lead-in followed by a parenthetical qualifier loses its title when the visible text before the colon passes 10 words**",
    "text": "**A bold lead-in followed by a parenthetical qualifier loses its title when the visible text before the colon passes 10 words**: reproduced 2026-10-02 on the parser alone (`parseMarkdownTree`), so it does not depend on `aperas insert`, on multi-line bullets or on ingest. The bullet `- **Decision — History's Current Status gains a sentence pointing at the v4 milestones** (orchestrator, not yet confirmed by the user): It stops.` stores its block id as its title, while the same bullet without the parenthetical gets its bold lead-in. Cause, read from `findLeadInColonOffset` in `packages/core/src/astParser.ts`: the canonical `**Term**:` shape is exempt from the 10-word cap, but once plain text (the parenthetical) sits between the bold span and the colon, the cap applies to all the visible text before the colon, bold words included, and a long bold lead plus a qualifier passes 10 words, so no title is derived and the block falls back to its id. That is what `design/linking.md`'s Lead-In Term Detection states (at most 10 words of visible, non-code text) working as written, not a regression; the gap is that the Freeflow convention `**Decision — <what>** (<who>, <how>): <body>` lands on the wrong side of it. A second effect of the same rule: when the qualifier itself holds a colon, as in `(user: \"...\")`, that colon is accepted as the delimiter if the words before it fit the cap, so the title is cut mid-parenthesis, as in `**Decision — promote all issues under the new rule** (user`. Impact as filed: under the five Freeflow headings of the v4.3 skill task in `discussion/aperas-skill.md`, 40 of the 87 bold-led bullets are titled by their id, every one with this shape, and 4 more carry a cut-off title, so `aperas tree` and `aperas unfold` list a Freeflow section as a column of ids that cannot be read without a `show` call each. Fix options: (a) count only the text outside the bold span against the cap when a bullet starts with a bold span, or (b) keep the rule and have Freeflow writers put the attribution after the colon. Convention chosen 2026-10-02: option (b); a Freeflow bullet is written `**Term**: (qualifier) body`, or with the qualifier inside the bold span, and the 53 existing entries of the old shape under the five headings were rewritten with `aperas update --text-only`, so their titles now derive. The engine option (a) stays open: the parser still gives the old shape no title, and nothing stops a writer from using it again. Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDBYV5A8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVMYE11G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**No skill states who moves the `Status:` lines or at which step**",
    "text": "**No skill states who moves the `Status:` lines or at which step**: each of the four `SKILL.md` files carries a `Status: **vN**` line, `skill_drift.py` fails when the newest delta's version differs from them, and the Grapher skill's drift section says a delta titled vN needs one `Status` entry per skill whose line moved and that a mismatch is a Question to the orchestrator. What no skill says is who moves the lines and when. Decided 2026-10-02, by the user from how v4.3 went: the orchestrator, with the user, decides at the start of the skill work that the version moves and names it in the Coder's Ask; the Coder moves the four `Status:` lines in the same edit as its changes, since the files are code; the Grapher records the delta with one `Status` entry per skill at the Filing and Finishing turns; and the `Status` guard in `skill_drift.py` fails if any of the three is missed. It is not the Grapher who moves the version. What is not done: no skill states the rule, so a Coder skill-edit turn has no instruction to bump, and the Grapher's only recourse on a mismatch is to ask. The issue stays open only for the skill wording that says this: most naturally one statement in the Coder skill, to move the four `Status:` lines in the same edit when the Ask names the version. Filed 2026-10-02.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5KW4G002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
