    ],
    "title": "**Observation — the monitor's two memory series, and what they say about the wedged service**",
    "text": "**Observation — the monitor's two memory series, and what they say about the wedged service**: (user, from the system memory monitor, twice: \"the mem just dropped, check the service!\", and later a screenshot) The monitor draws two series. The light-cyan \"Active\" memory dropped many times before the service was stopped and did not move at the kill; only the dark-cyan \"Allocated\" memory dropped at the kill. The orchestrator's first reading of the screenshot, that Active dropped once before and again at the kill, was wrong. The orchestrator's samples of the service's resident size rose the whole time (4.74 GB at 19:20, 5.01 at 19:26, 5.66 at 19:40, 5.76 at 19:42, just before the stop), with a gap in sampling between 19:28 and 19:40, and after the kill available memory rose from 3.3 GB to 7.9 GB, the freed 5.8 GB less the fresh service's 1.1 GB. Reading: because Active did not fall when the 5.8 GB service was killed, its memory was not in Active, which counts pages recently touched. So the service held about 5.8 GB it was not touching while its CPU stayed at 100% on two threads: a loop on a small working set, not work across the whole heap, which weakens the garbage-collection explanation. The repeated drops in Active before the kill were then something else, with the short-lived `aperas` client processes (each command starts its own node process) the obvious candidate; not tested. Both readings are untested.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVK45ZQ0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
