    "title": "**Finding — the canonical address cannot be used for authoring, so every cross-reference is hand-written Markdown**",
    "text": "**Finding — the canonical address cannot be used for authoring, so every cross-reference is hand-written Markdown**: (user: \"I did not want to create that friction ... I designed [the canon address] to be *used* for all cases regarding the graph, then the MD-compatible syntax is meant to be automatically generated from the canon address. But the impl has not caught that design ... That fix has been staying in my waiting list for weeks!\") The design already says the writer addresses a target by [`id/<ID>` or `aperas://id/<ID>` after ingestion](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4TG009) and that [the canonical syntax uses the `aperas://` scheme](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T8009), and the [history of the linking work](../history/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBZ7698002) records that the target design replaces the older `[[code]]` wrapper with it. The implementation does not follow through: the Report and Ask templates in the skills teach a relative-path link, `[title](../design/x.md#id/BlockNode:…)`, so the writer works out the artifact path and the anchor form by hand for every reference, which is the friction that led the orchestrator to cite bare ids instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CVQK150G001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAKWMR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Observation — what the parser does with each spelling**",
    "text": "**Observation — what the parser does with each spelling**: (orchestrator, offline, `collectLinkCodesFromText` in `astParser.ts`, nothing written to the graph) `[label](aperas://id/BlockNode:X)`, `[label](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:X)` and `[label](#id/BlockNode:X)` are each taken as a link; `[[BlockNode:X]]`, a bare `aperas://id/BlockNode:X` and a code-spanned `BlockNode:X` are not, so the form `tree` and `unfold` print, and the form the orchestrator pasted, creates no link. Not tested: whether an `aperas://id/` link persists through `insert`, what the projection writes for it, and whether `check-links` accepts it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CVQK28HR001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAKWMR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Open — what the authoring fix should be**",
    "text": "**Open — what the authoring fix should be**: (orchestrator, the user's design stated, not decided) Accept a canonical address wherever a reference is expected in written text, bare or with an optional label, and have ingest and projection produce the MD-compatible link (relative path, anchor, and a label taken from the target's title). It is promoted to an issue at the next Filing turn; its place in the tools queue is the user's call, since it has waited weeks.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00CVQK3EZG001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAY51G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAY51G001:props:StringProp:00CVQAYDC0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAY51G001:props:StringProp:00CVQAYDC0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Finding — the grapher's survey of the slow-service record**",
    "text": "**Finding — the grapher's survey of the slow-service record**: (orchestrator, read from the grapher's Turn 1 Report in `.threads/grapher.md`; a thread node, so not linked) Of eight symptoms, only three have a mechanism established by code reading or reproduction: the migration blocking (a synchronous ingest loop blocks the event loop), the thread-flush routing gap and the tombstone leak. The verify slowdown has two causes ruled out by experiment and its cause open; the removal stall, the memory growth, the slow start and the spurious \"not running\" rest on untested hypotheses. The service has no timing or memory instrumentation of its own and `service.log` holds only startup lines. Block count does not predict the removal stall: removals of 39 and 50 blocks were fast and of 42 and 31 slow. The grapher's own reading, labelled as such: one long synchronous operation on the single-threaded service would explain the removal stall, the spurious \"not running\", the SIGTERM that never completed and the timers that never fire. `design/` has no document for the service lifecycle.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAY51G002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Open — the first measurements, in order**",
    "text": "**Open — the first measurements, in order**: (the grapher's list, none of them changes behaviour) 1, event-loop lag and `process.memoryUsage()` logged by the service; 2, per-operation timing split into pre-check, operation, post-check and flush; 3, one controlled `remove` on a fresh service against the same after a long uptime, with the link sweep timed alone; 4, a CPU profile during a stall; 5, a heap snapshot at idle and when large; 6, a timestamped boot log and ping timing; 7, sweep cost against tombstone count; 8, dehydrate time at current node counts, before the thread-flush fix adds work to the 3-second timer.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00CVQAY51G003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Default — the external sampler moves into the repo as the outside half of measurements 1 to 4, and the design home waits**",
    "text": "**Default — the external sampler moves into the repo as the outside half of measurements 1 to 4, and the design home waits**: (orchestrator, answering the grapher's two questions; not yet confirmed by the user) The sampler is a session scratchpad script that will disappear, so the coder takes it into the repo as investigation tooling and extends it; the in-process half (event-loop lag, memory, per-operation timing, profile, heap snapshot) needs changes in the service. The grapher opens a `design/` document for the service lifecycle once the investigation has settled what the store, reload, sweeps and flush timers do, not before.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVKQDJ2G001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
