    "title": "**Decision — the Orchestrator decides the bump first, the Coder moves `Status:` with its changes, and the Grapher records the delta**",
    "text": "**Decision — the Orchestrator decides the bump first, the Coder moves `Status:` with its changes, and the Grapher records the delta**: (user, first as a question, \"Isn't it the Grapher at Finishing step?\", then correcting it: \"the Coder moved the four `Status:` lines first, on my Ask, and the Grapher then recorded the delta. Wait... this actually reflects the existing behaviour: we choose to bump the version first, don't we? ... the Orchestrator decides to bump and ask the Coder to update Status together with its changes\") This is how v4.3 actually went and it is the rule: the orchestrator, with the user, decides at the start of the skill work that the version moves, and puts it in the Coder's Ask; the Coder moves the four `Status:` lines in the same edit as the changes, since the files are code; the Grapher records the delta with one `Status` entry per skill, at the Filing and Finishing turns; the `Status` guard in `skill_drift.py` fails if any of the three is missed. An earlier version of this entry recorded the Grapher as the one who moves the version, which came from reading the user's question as a decision; it is replaced. [The Status-lines issue](../issues/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00CVMYE11G001) therefore stays open only for the skill wording that says this.",
