    "title": "**`aperas service start` prints \"did not become ready in time\" at 5 s although the lock turns ready at 5.3 to 5.8 s, and every command is then refused for about 20 s**",
    "text": "**`aperas service start` prints \"did not become ready in time\" at 5 s although the lock turns ready at 5.3 to 5.8 s, and every command is then refused for about 20 s**: measured 2026-10-10 by the Coder in the store-size investigation, scratch service as in [the restart entry](#id/BlockNode:00D0W044DG001) above, cold `service start` 6 times, load average 2 to 3.5. The lock turned ready at 5.28 to 5.77 s and the CLI exited 1 with \"ApeironNgn service did not become ready in time\" at 5.42 to 5.76 s in all 6 runs, because `READY_TIMEOUT_MS = 5_000` in `serviceClient.ts` (lines 24 and 25) starts counting after the CLI has loaded, while the service came up 0 to 60 ms later; the ready time breaks down as about 1 s of node and `tsx` start, 1.1 s of `rehydrateStore` and 3.2 s of boot sweeps (`Rehydrated` is logged about 3.3 s before the lock turns ready). After ready, every `aperas` command in the first 19.5 to 23.0 s failed with \"No ApeironNgn service running\" (6 of 6 starts; `usable_minus_ready` 19.5, 20.2, 20.9, 21.4, 22.0 and 23.0 s, measured through a probe that costs about 1 s of CLI start, so accurate to about 1 s), because the startup link sweep is enqueued on the event loop right after `markReady` (`service.ts` lines 818 to 826) and `ping` has a 300 ms timeout (`serviceClient.ts` line 23). A service that is already settled answers a `profile list` in 0.55 s wall, CLI start included. This measures two points already in the graph: the 5 s ready wait that [the boot and shutdown issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHCEW8001) put close to the real boot time, and the 300 ms ping that [the spurious \"not running\" issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVMPABY8001) names as the reason a busy service reads as down; both are extended with these figures and link here, and this entry holds the start-side measurement. Not measured: a start on the live service (it would need the user's approval to stop it). No fix has been tried. Filed 2026-10-10.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D3YH0QGR001",
      "BlockNode:00D3Z1FX4R001",
      "BlockNode:00D4198VGG001",
      "BlockNode:00D457N7XR001",
      "BlockNode:00D7T5W9Z8001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0W07DRR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**`design/` has no document for the store, hydration or the service lifecycle**",
    "text": "**`design/` has no document for the store, hydration or the service lifecycle**: noted 2026-10-10 in the store-size investigation. The graph holds the early ApeironNgn design only in the archive (`archive/Aperas-design.md`, where `rehydrateStore` is a quoted 256 ms at 1,594 BlockNodes), and nothing in `design/` states the quad shape the store holds, when and how the mirrors are hydrated and dehydrated, what the boot, the flush timers, the shutdown and the reload each do and in what order, or what the client's waits (300 ms ping, 5 s ready, 10 s stop) are allowed to assume of them. The boot and shutdown issue already says there is no document for the service lifecycle; this entry records the same gap for the store and hydration too, so the figures in the issues above have no design to be read against. Recorded only; no design is written here. Filed 2026-10-10.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG00J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D21860Y8001",
      "BlockNode:00D3X3MHF0001"
