    "title": "**Count corrected by the orchestrator**",
    "text": "**Count corrected by the orchestrator**: the scope the user chose lists ten issues of 2.6 KB or more, so with the pilot done there are nine others, not eight: boot and shutdown, memory, no coverage, spurious \"not running\", full-store scans, reload, thread flush, whole-artifact re-parse, and the `service restart` error. The user's \"eight\" was taken from the orchestrator's own miscount in the question; the scope itself is unchanged. The 1.9 KB entry on the CLI's 5 s ready wait is under the 2.6 KB line and stays as it is unless the user asks.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0WYXHXG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0WYXHXR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Pilot fixes**",
    "text": "**Pilot fixes**: reword the \"explains\" line to the old strength (\"consistent with\"); find and link the leak issue in `issues/treeview.md`; move the established mechanism to the front of Root cause, with the suspected causes after it under their own heading.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0WYXHXG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0WYXHXR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**How it runs**",
    "text": "**How it runs**: in three Grapher turns of two to four issues, sequentially (they all write `issues/core.md`), with a lossless comparison of numbers, dates, code spans and link targets after each issue and the orchestrator's read of each batch before the next. Why: the pilot took one turn and about 80k tokens for one 8.8 KB issue, so nine at once would not fit one turn.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0WYXHXG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
