  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XB8098001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D0XB8098001",
    "text": "**Question — elaborate on \"stop creating the wrappers at all\"** (2026-10-10, user; answered by the orchestrator from `dehydrate.ts` line 49, `node.ts` lines 251 to 258, 2089, 2196 to 2210 and 2242, `folders.ts` line 21 and `artifacts.ts` line 200, read and not yet checked by the Coder): there are three shapes of full scan, each with a cheaper form. (1) \"All ids of kind K\" (`allIdsOfKind`, a `store.match` over every quad that reads only `q.subject`): ask the store for the kind's type quads alone, or keep a kind-to-ids index. (2) \"Which views unfold this node\" (`removeDanglingUnfolds`, which scans all quads to list the views and then matches each): one object-bound match, `store.match(null, unfolds, deletedIri, null)`. (3) Per-node field reads (`wrap`, `readField`, one match per field): one subject-bound match per node. Beyond scans, rehydrate adds quads one at a time, which the bare-engine run showed costs far more garbage than a bulk `store.load` (4.7 s of Mark-Compact at 100k quads against 0.29 s). Untested and to check first: that every node has a type quad matching the kind that `nodeKindFromId` derives from the id, so the result sets are identical on a copy of the real store; and the cost of each bound form against the full scan. Nothing is implemented; the user has not chosen a fix.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XN8CAR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D0XN8CAR001",
    "text": "**Correction — \"stop creating the wrappers at all\" overstated it; the wrappers cannot be avoided, only made proportional to what is asked for** (2026-10-10, orchestrator, after the user asked whether a bound match's results are not wrapped too, and how JavaScript could access WASM without wrapping): the user is right. A bound `store.match` returns `Quad` objects as well. Read in `node_modules/oxigraph/node.js` by the orchestrator: `Quad`, `NamedNode`, `BlankNode`, `Literal` and the rest are wasm-bindgen classes whose instance holds only an integer pointer into wasm memory (`__wrap(ptr)` creates an object with `__wbg_ptr = ptr` and registers it with a `FinalizationRegistry` that calls `wasm.__wbg_quad_free(ptr)`), and `store.match` returns an array of them; each has a `free()` method that releases the pointer at once. So the cost scales with the number of results, and a full scan makes 41.7k Quad objects (plus term objects) where a bound match makes only the few it matches. Ways to avoid wrappers for a given need: ask for fewer results (bound match), keep the answer on the JavaScript side (an index), or cross the boundary once with a string (`store.dump`, `store.load`), which carries no per-quad object. Releasing at once with `free()` is also available and untested.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D0XVRHER001",
    "text": "**Question — how can a JavaScript-side index reflect changes made on the wasm side, and is whole-store I/O for large operations or for any operation?** (2026-10-10, user, after accepting the bound match as the first step: \"not to litter every time\"; answered by the orchestrator from `node.ts` and `store.ts`, read and not yet checked by the Coder):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER002",
      "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER003",
      "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER002:props:StringProp:00D0XVRW1R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER002:props:StringProp:00D0XVRW1R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The index needs no view of the wasm side, because nothing else writes to it**",
    "text": "**The index needs no view of the wasm side, because nothing else writes to it**: the store is in-process and every mutation is a `store.add` or `store.delete` at 27 call sites, 22 in `node.ts` and 5 in `store.ts` (the rehydrate encoder); no `store.update`, `store.load` or `store.clear` is used. The index is kept in step by JavaScript at the same points. The risk is drift, not observation; guards: route the 27 sites through two helpers, rebuild the index at every rehydrate, and have the verify run compare the index with a full scan after each operation. The subject set changes only at creation (first quad) and at hard delete (`node.ts` line 237), because detaching a child deletes only its parent and sibling-index quads.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Whole-store I/O is for large operations only**",
    "text": "**Whole-store I/O is for large operations only**: rehydrate (boot and reload) adds 46,519 quads one by one in `encodeDoc`, then scans the whole store once more to find dangling references; a bulk `store.load` of an N-Quads string and a JavaScript-side set of referenced ids collected during encoding would avoid both. A single insert, update or remove touches a handful of quads and keeps using `add`, `delete` and bound matches. The flush is a separate question: the mirror is stable-id JSON-LD, so a bulk `store.dump` does not produce it directly.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Order the orchestrator recommends**",
    "text": "**Order the orchestrator recommends**: bound match first (the user agreed); the bulk load and the dangling-reference set for rehydrate second; the index only if the measurements show the bound match still leaves too many wrappers.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0XVRHER001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0XZZE0R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D0XZZE0R001",
    "text": "**Question — the index is kind to ids, so how is it used?** (2026-10-10, user, correcting the orchestrator's wording \"subject set\"; answered by the orchestrator, read and not yet checked by the Coder): the index is a `Map<kind, Set<id>>` attached to the store (for example a `WeakMap<Store, …>`), and it replaces only the body of `allIdsOfKind(store, kind)`, which returns a snapshot array of `index.get(kind)`; its callers (`node.ts` lines 253, 984, 2089, 2196, 2204 and 2242, `dehydrate.ts` lines 136, 161 and 168, `folders.ts`, `artifacts.ts`) do not change, and the snapshot keeps loops that delete while iterating safe, as the scan's array does today. It is built from `seenIds` at rehydrate and updated by the two write helpers. What it does not remove is the per-node cost after the ids are known: callers then `wrap` each id and read fields such as `tombstonedAt`, one `store.match` per field read, so `pruneUnreachableTombstones` still wraps every node; that is the next layer to measure.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0Y4Q7B8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D0Y4Q7B8001",
    "text": "**Question — why can a wasm quad not have exactly one `Quad` object on the JavaScript side?** (2026-10-10, user, stating the ideal interface; answered by the orchestrator, partly from the binding read earlier and partly from the orchestrator's understanding of Oxigraph's storage, which was not verified this session): the reasons are that the store holds quads as encoded index entries and no quad object exists on the wasm side to be one-to-one with, so each `match` decodes a fresh Rust value and hands JavaScript a new pointer (two matches of the same stored quad return different pointers, and `__wrap(ptr)` makes a new object for each); that a JavaScript identity map would need the quad's content as its key, which is what the wrapper exists to carry, and would be a copy of the store if strong or need the same finalization if weak; and that the RDF/JS interface the binding follows gives quads value semantics (`equals`), not identity. The ideal is reachable one level up, in Aperas: one plain JavaScript record per node (not per quad), filled by a single subject-bound match with the quads freed at once, invalidated at the write helpers. That is the same machinery as the kind index and would make repeated field reads cross the boundary zero times.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0Y8S87R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D0Y8S87R001",
    "text": "**Noted — the layers of the full-store-scan fix, as discussed with the user; none decided, none tried** (2026-10-10, user: \"Yeah, hearing 'index' i thought of this fine-grained index. Let's note down these layers.\"): the user asked for the layers to be written down, which is a request to record, not a decision to implement. The layers, in the order to try them, each only if the previous leaves the cost in place:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
