      }
    ],
    "title": "00D0Y8S87R002",
    "text": "**Layer 1 — bound matches in place of unbounded ones** (the user agreed to this one as the first step, \"not to litter every time\"): `allIdsOfKind` asks for a kind's type quads instead of every quad; `removeDanglingUnfolds` uses one object-bound `store.match(null, unfolds, deletedIri, null)`; `wrap` and `readField` read a node with one subject-bound match. To check first on a copy of the real store: that every node has a type quad agreeing with the kind `nodeKindFromId` derives from its id, by comparing both result sets; and the cost of each bound form against the full scan (the twelve-fold saving is an estimate).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0Y8S87R001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0Y8S880000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Layer 2 — a kind-to-ids index**",
    "text": "**Layer 2 — a kind-to-ids index**: a `Map<kind, Set<id>>` attached to the store, replacing only the body of `allIdsOfKind` (callers unchanged, a snapshot array returned); built from `seenIds` at rehydrate and kept by two write helpers through which the 27 `store.add` and `store.delete` sites are routed; the verify run compares it with a full scan after each operation. Only if layer 1 still leaves too many wrappers.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0Y8S87R001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D0Y8S880001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Layer 3 — a per-node record cache, the fine-grained index**",
    "text": "**Layer 3 — a per-node record cache, the fine-grained index**: one plain JavaScript record per node (strings and numbers, no wasm handle), filled by a single subject-bound match with the quads freed at once and invalidated by the write helpers, so repeated reads of a node's fields cross into wasm zero times; a set of tombstoned ids is the smallest form. The user's ideal of one object per thing, built one level above the library because a wasm quad has no object to be one-to-one with. Only if per-node reads still dominate after layers 1 and 2.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0Y8S87R001",
