  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1AD215R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1AD215R001",
    "text": "**Question — what makes the event loop count as a \"turn\", an empty stack?** (2026-10-11, user; answered by the orchestrator from general knowledge of Node, not from this codebase): an empty call stack is necessary and not sufficient. When a task ends the stack is empty, but Node then drains the `process.nextTick` queue and the promise (microtask) queue to the end, including microtasks that enqueue more microtasks, and only when both are empty does the loop move on to the next task (a due timer, a ready socket, a `setImmediate`, a platform task such as a `FinalizationRegistry` cleanup). So a continuation scheduled as a microtask (an `await` on a resolved promise, a `.then`) gives a new stack but not a turn, and a chain that always has the next op queued as a microtask never lets the loop go round; a continuation scheduled as a task (`setImmediate`, `setTimeout`, an I/O callback) does. Garbage collection itself can happen inside synchronous code; it is the cleanup callback that frees the wasm side which needs the loop to reach it. Whether a zero-delay `setImmediate` is enough to run that cleanup in the same turn is the untested point recorded earlier.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1ARDXHG000",
