      }
    ],
    "title": "**Why the stop comes first**",
    "text": "**Why the stop comes first**: a write to a thread node reaches `.state/` only at a view operation or at shutdown (the filed thread-flush gap), and the five bad targets sit in the thread partition. Editing the files while the service runs would be overwritten by its unflushed memory, so the sweep waits for the graceful stop, which flushes both mirrors.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1AYPMDG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1AYPMDG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A hazard in a text edit**",
    "text": "**A hazard in a text edit**: the `position` prop of each later link in a block is an offset into the block's text, so removing a repeated anchor from the text shifts every later position in that block (block `00D0VY04SG00F` holds three bad links among others). The script must shift them, and `check-links` after the restart is the check that it did.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1AYPMDG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1AYPMDG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The sweep plan, with the user already having asked for it**",
    "text": "**The sweep plan, with the user already having asked for it**: (1) the Coder writes the script under `/tmp` and tries it on a copy of the current mirror (dry-run prints the exact changes and writes nothing); (2) the orchestrator stops the service gracefully, copies the whole `Apeiron/` directory (including `.state/`) to `/tmp` as the backup, runs the dry-run on the real files and shows the user the change list; (3) it is applied; (4) the service is started on the new code, `aperas check-links` must be clean with no `unresolvedTargets`, and the stray block must be gone. The fallback, if the offline result is wrong in any way, is to restore the backup and make the corrections through `aperas update --text-only` on the new code.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1AYPMDG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1B662PR001",
    "text": "**Decision — a verify emergency: the flag lives in `issues/core.md` and a gate lives in the `verify` command itself; skills v4.4 follow** (2026-10-11, user, answering two direct questions after \"The severe issue of the core has made `verified` counterproductive, (it has been refactored once, but has grown up again). So let's update the skill to set threshold to `verify`: if it's longer than 1min, don't run it as a batch but the required tests only, and turn on a flag (i don't know how to call it ... maybe 'emergency') so that later turns won't run `verify`. The emergency flag prompts for a fix of the core. Only when fixed and brought down to under 1 min, will the flag be turned off and the `verify` be run as normal.\"):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1B662PR002",
      "BlockNode:00D1B662PR003",
      "BlockNode:00D1B662PR004",
      "BlockNode:00D1B662PR005",
      "BlockNode:00D1B662PR006",
      "BlockNode:00D1B662PR007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR002:props:StringProp:00D1B66DW8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR002:props:StringProp:00D1B66DW8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Where the flag lives, the user's answer**",
    "text": "**Where the flag lives, the user's answer**: \"issues/core.md + gate in `verify` command. We should make the command to accept args: which test cases to be run, toggle emergency mode, option to force running the full suite despite emergency mode. In emergency mode, it refuses to run full suite. The command also has a buit-in timer to warn user when it runs over a minute.\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The threshold rule, confirmed (\"Yes, that rule\")**",
    "text": "**The threshold rule, confirmed (\"Yes, that rule\")**: at Step 7 a verify is tried once as a run capped at one minute; if it finishes green within the minute, Step 7 is normal; if not, it is stopped, the emergency is turned on, and the change is verified by the required tests only (its own tests, `tsc`, targeted passes); while the emergency is on nobody runs verify, not even the trial; it is turned off when a core fix lets one run finish green within a minute. The user's \"built-in timer\" in the command warns at one minute.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The name**",
    "text": "**The name**: \"verify emergency\", the user's own word. The orchestrator decides the version bump first, as the Status rule has it: **v4.4** (a new protocol rule, not a wording patch).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The emergency is on now**",
    "text": "**The emergency is on now**: the last full run took 1,434 s, so the flag is on from this decision; the Grapher opens the issue \"Verify emergency\" in `issues/core.md` as the record, and agents already skip the full verify under the standing rule from earlier today.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1BBTPB0001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Open design points, not decided, for the Coder's survey**",
    "text": "**Open design points, not decided, for the Coder's survey**: (1) the command's \"which test cases to be run\": `verify.ts` is one long function whose later steps use the store built by earlier ones (step 9c needs what steps 1 to 9b made), so running a named case alone needs either a registry with declared prerequisites or the standalone-script form already used for the new tests; the survey says what is feasible; (2) where the command keeps the emergency state it reads and toggles: the issue is the record the agents see, the command needs a flag it can check without the service, so a small tracked file in the code repo is the likely home and must agree with the issue; (3) what the timer does at one minute: warn only, or also turn the emergency on; (4) what `--force-full` prints and whether it needs a reason. Skill text waits for the settled command, because the skills must describe how it behaves.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Sequence**",
    "text": "**Sequence**: first the offline sweep that is ready (service stop, backup, dry-run shown to the user, apply, start); then the Grapher opens the emergency issue and the Coder surveys the command; the user settles the design points; then the Coder builds the command and the skill change, and the Grapher records the v4.4 delta.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1B662PR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
