  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1BCFPQ8001",
    "text": "**Finding — `verify` runs as one synchronous task, so it has the same cause as the service's slowness; the Coder's design and five questions for the user** (2026-10-11, orchestrator, from the Coder's survey `verify-command-survey`; no verify was run, no source changed):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8002",
      "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8003",
      "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8004",
      "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8005",
      "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8006",
      "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8002:props:StringProp:00D1BCG350000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8002:props:StringProp:00D1BCG350001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The map**",
    "text": "**The map**: `npm run verify` is `tsx packages/cli/src/verify.ts`, 26 step headers in 13 numbered groups inside one function, and there is no `await` anywhere in it, so the whole run is one synchronous task and the event loop never turns. `verify:slow` is `verifyExtended.ts`. Steps 1 to 1e are pure; step 12 needs only the store; 3 to 5f share the demo artifact; 7, 8, 9, 9b, 10, 11 and 13 depend on earlier values; the new `verifyLinkRefusals.ts` (steps 5g and 13) and `verifyDanglingUnfolds.ts` (step 9c) already have standalone forms.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The durations** (logs in `/tmp/aperas-scratch/out2/`)",
    "text": "**The durations** (logs in `/tmp/aperas-scratch/out2/`): `verify2.txt` 1,431 s (exit 0): step 7 649 s, 9c 203 s, 11 202 s, 10 188 s, together 87% of the run; `verify.txt` 809 s, failed at step 7. Steps 5g and 13 have no timing (both logs predate them), nor does `verify:slow`. One `pruneUnreachableTombstones` took 188 to 202 s inside the run against 2.2 s in a fresh process, about 85 times more.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The cause, as far as shown**",
    "text": "**The cause, as far as shown**: the same pattern as the service: six rehydrates in one process with no turn went from 1.2 s to 23 s and stayed near 1 s with a turn between them; a forced `gc()` without a turn did not help; a reload as one request took 88 s against 8 s with pauses. This is shown in scratch processes and not for `verify.ts` itself: nobody has run it with turns in it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Design recommended by the Coder, none decided**",
    "text": "**Design recommended by the Coder, none decided**: `--case <ids>` and `--list` with a static prerequisites table that runs the needed earlier steps first (shared values hoisted, each step guarded; no registry rewrite, no ranges); the emergency state in a tracked file `monorepo/verify-emergency.json` written only by the command and cited by the issue (a gitignored `.state` file, an environment variable, or reading the issue through the graph each have a worse failure mode); a timer that cannot fire in the same process (the run never yields), so a real one-minute cap needs a launcher that re-runs `verify.ts` as a child with the timer in the parent (not tried under `tsx`); at 60 s a normal full run warns, stops, turns the emergency on and exits with code 3, while `--force-full` and `--case` runs only warn; `--force-full` needs a reason string, prints a banner, and never turns the emergency off.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Measurement proposed, not run**",
    "text": "**Measurement proposed, not run**: steps 2 to 5 on a scratch copy, three runs capped at 60 s: as it is, with a `setImmediate` yield at each step header, and with a 50 ms timer; it executes verify steps, so it waits for the user's go.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Questions for the user**",
    "text": "**Questions for the user**: (1) run that turn measurement before building the command; (2) whether the 60 s timer stops the run and turns the emergency on, or only warns; (3) whether `--emergency off` is unconditional or a trial that flips only on a green full run under 60 s; (4) whether the tracked flag file is acceptable and the gate covers `verify:slow` too; (5) whether the static-table form of `--case` is the first version wanted, given that steps 7, 9c, 10 and 11 cannot fit a minute today whatever is selected.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BCFPQ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1BDQ3TR001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's filing of the anchor-fix result and the \"Verify emergency\" issue read, with answers to its three questions** (2026-10-11, orchestrator): `aperas check-links` is clean. The new open issue \"Verify emergency\" (`issues/core.md`, `BlockNode:00D1BAT6Q0001`) has Symptom, Root cause, The rule, Fix directions, Status and Related, states that the issue is the flag (opening it turned the flag on, resolving it turns the flag off), and records the user's design for the command with the open points marked \"open and not decided\". The consequence issue, the tree-view doubling issue and the `check-links` gap each carry a Resolved child and sit under their file's `## Resolved`; the collector issue stays open with the removal of the stray block recorded; the 80 stored link positions are a new open issue in `issues/linking.md` (`BlockNode:00D1BAWJ58001`); \"Promoted to\" children were added at the origins.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR002",
      "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR002:props:StringProp:00D1BDQE5R000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR002:props:StringProp:00D1BDQE5R001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The quote — the Grapher's attribution is right**",
    "text": "**The quote — the Grapher's attribution is right**: \"has been refactored once, but has grown up again\" is the user's, said on 2026-10-11 in the verify-emergency request; the orchestrator's Ask wrongly said the old entries \"said\" it. The issue records the old entry's own facts (about 5 minutes, then about 42 s after splitting `verify.ts`) and quotes the user as the user. A useful fact for the fix: `verify` was once under a minute.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1BDQ3TR003",
    "text": "**Decision — retitle the collector issue to the blind spot alone** (orchestrator): its title still says \"so the stray block survives every restart\", which is no longer true; it goes in the next Grapher turn, with the Sub-Parts count in `issues/core.md` (\"`treeview` — 4 open\", stale before this and still stale) left alone as out of scope.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BDQ3TR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1BN9DYR001",
    "text": "**Decision — the user's answers on the verify command, and the long-run question** (2026-10-11, user, answering the Coder's five questions as relayed by the orchestrator):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR002",
      "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR003",
      "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR004",
      "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR005",
      "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR006",
      "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR007"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR002:props:StringProp:00D1BN9RVR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR002:props:StringProp:00D1BN9RVR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The flag file**",
    "text": "**The flag file**: \"emergency is the status of the codebase, not a transient state! So verify-emergency.json is nice.\" A tracked file in the code repo, `monorepo/verify-emergency.json`, is approved. Whether the gate covers `verify:slow` is tied to the long-run question below.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1BN9DYR003",
    "text": "**The timer is instrumentation between cases, warning only** (replaces the Coder's launcher that stops the run at 60 s): \"After each case, check time, if over 1min, warn, that's all.\" No child process, no stop, no automatic turning on of the emergency; the command checks elapsed time after each case and prints a warning when it is over a minute. The emergency is turned on by the command's toggle, by a decision.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Turning the emergency off is a trial**",
    "text": "**Turning the emergency off is a trial**: \"Flip emergency off only on a green full run under 60 s.\" `--emergency off` runs the full suite and flips the file only when it finishes green within 60 s.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The turn measurement is a go**",
    "text": "**The turn measurement is a go**: \"Do the turn measurement.\" The Coder runs the experiment it proposed, with the limits in the next Ask.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The first version of `--case`**",
    "text": "**The first version of `--case`**: the user did not understand \"the static-table form\" and asked for it to be explained; the orchestrator's explanation is in the chat: `verify.ts` is one function in which later steps use state built by earlier ones, so a case run alone needs to know what to run first; a static table lists, per case, the earlier cases it needs, written by hand beside the steps; the alternatives are a rewrite so every case builds its own fixtures (a registry), or one standalone script per case (the form already used for the new tests). No decision yet.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The long-run question, open**",
    "text": "**The long-run question, open**: \"The test suite will grow forever, won't it? While this refactor is only an emergency tactic now, how should we deal with it in the long run? Should we run all cases each turn? What's the normal practice in the industry?\" The orchestrator's answer is in the chat: the usual practice is tiers by speed and by cause: a change runs only the tests it can affect (seconds), a fast tier under a minute or two runs before each commit, and the slow tiers (integration and end-to-end, the full suite) run on merge or on a schedule, with a time budget per tier that fails or warns when exceeded so growth forces a split. The user has not chosen a shape for this project.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1BN9DYR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BQN1HG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1BQN1HG001",
    "text": "**Result — the turn measurement: a bare `setImmediate` yield does not cut the cost, a 50 ms timer does; one sample per variant, steps 2 to 5 only** (2026-10-11, orchestrator, from the Coder's Turn 2 of `verify-command-survey`; no repository source changed, nothing staged, the real mirror untouched): three runs on a scratch copy (`/tmp/aperas-yield/verify.ts`, own copies of `Apeiron` and `artifacts` per run), each capped at 60 s, all exit 0, step times in ms:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1BQN1HG002",
      "BlockNode:00D1BQN1HG003",
      "BlockNode:00D1BQN1HG004",
      "BlockNode:00D1BQN1HG005",
      "BlockNode:00D1BQN1HG006"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BQN1HG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1BQN1HG002:props:StringProp:00D1BQNEA0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
