    ],
    "title": "**The runs**",
    "text": "**The runs**: both exited 124 at the cap, wall 180.13 s, on fresh scratch copies. (d), no yield: user CPU 290.8 s, max RSS 1.39 GB, in step 5f at the cap. (e), a 50 ms timer at each header: user CPU 294.8 s, max RSS 1.29 GB, finished 5f in 9.5 s and then spent at least 124.6 s in step 5g without finishing it. Steps 6, 6b and 7 were not reached in either.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Step times in ms (d against e)**",
    "text": "**Step times in ms (d against e)**: step 3 15,534 and 13,559; step 5 30,251 and 1,784; 5c 42,485 and 25,294; 5d 54,607 and 1,915; 5f at least 34,000 (cut off) and 9,464.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The garbage-collection reading is supported**",
    "text": "**The garbage-collection reading is supported**: in every slow step Mark-Compact time is 70 to 87% of the step time and fast steps have 0 or 1 Mark-Compacts. The timer lowers it in step 5 (24,985 ms to 0), 5d (47,103 ms to 3 ms), 5c (36,052 ms to 20,787 ms) and 5f (29,538 ms to 6,614 ms, but (d)'s 5f is cut off); it does not lower it in step 3 (11,349 ms to 9,694 ms) and does not help 5g (107.7 s of Mark-Compact).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**What the collections look like**",
    "text": "**What the collections look like**: 849 of 895 Mark-Compacts in (d) and 1,041 of 1,088 in (e) freed 5 MB or less from a heap of about 230 to 450 MB, each pause about 150 to 290 ms. So the slow steps are repeated full collections that find almost nothing to free, not a pile of garbage being cleared; the Coder did not test why a 50 ms pause stops this in steps 5 and 5d. The orchestrator's reading, not tested here: V8 starts a full collection when external (wasm) memory has grown past its limit, and the collection cannot lower it because the wasm side is released by a later callback; a pause lets the callbacks run and the external figure fall, which stops the collections; `free()` on the quads, which the earlier scan test showed leaves external memory flat, would remove the growth itself.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A new cost found: the step 5g added with the link-refusal tests is itself slow inside `verify`**",
    "text": "**A new cost found: the step 5g added with the link-refusal tests is itself slow inside `verify`**: it ran at least 125 s without finishing in (e), against 1 s as the standalone script on a tiny store; inside `verify.ts` it uses the shared store, which by then has the accumulated external memory. The steps 9c and 13 added the same way share that fate. They were added in this session's A, C and D and the dangling-unfolds change, and they make the full suite slower.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The Coder's question — one more run, and which**",
    "text": "**The Coder's question — one more run, and which**: a run starting at step 5g or 6, a run with a V8 old-space or semi-space setting, or neither. Not decided; the orchestrator recommends neither, below.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C1W5CG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1C88WS8001",
    "text": "**Decision (direction) and Question — standalone scripts in groups under `src/tests/`, and whether `verify` is still needed** (2026-10-11, user, after the results of runs (d) and (e): \"I prefer standalone scripts organized into groups under src/tests/. But then do we need `verify` anymore?!\"): the preference is recorded as the direction for the `verify` redesign: one process per case, cases as standalone scripts, grouped by directory under `src/tests/`, replacing the one-function `verify.ts`. It is not yet a go to build, and the survey that would settle the shape has not been sent.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8002",
      "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8003",
      "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8002:props:StringProp:00D1C899PR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8002:props:StringProp:00D1C899PR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The orchestrator's answer to \"do we need `verify` anymore?\"**",
    "text": "**The orchestrator's answer to \"do we need `verify` anymore?\"**: not as a monolith, and a thin runner stays for four jobs: choosing what to run (a group, a tier, one case), running each case in a fresh process so the build-up cannot accumulate, timing each case and warning past a minute, and holding the emergency gate. Node's built-in test runner (`node --test`) runs each matched test file in its own child process, supports name filters and prints per-test timing, so the runner may be an existing tool and not code written here; whether the repository's Node version and its current test setup allow it has not been checked. The name can stay (`npm run verify` runs the runner), so the skills change little.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Tiers and groups are two axes**",
    "text": "**Tiers and groups are two axes**: a group is an area (links, store, ingest, tree), a tier is a speed class; the tiers the user chose (per change, per commit, on demand) could be a subdirectory, a naming rule or a header in each script; the survey proposes one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Migration**",
    "text": "**Migration**: the old steps share hidden state, so each one moved needs its own small fixture (a shared helper that builds a tiny store); the corpus-scale checks against the real mirror (a `check-links` pass, a dehydrate and rehydrate round trip) belong to the on-demand tier. The monolith shrinks until `verify.ts` is empty and the emergency file can be retired or kept as the budget guard for the commit tier. The new steps 5g, 9c and 13 become scripts of their own first.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1C88WS8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CDABSR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1CDABSR001",
    "text": "**Decision — the runner is `node --test` if it fits; the emergency retires with the monolith and becomes the three tiers; groups are subdirectories and tiers are metadata** (2026-10-11, user, on the orchestrator's answer: \"Yep, that runner is exactly what i'm thinking. Let's check `node --test` to see how we can make use of it. The emergency retires together with the monolithic suite, and is transformed to the 3 tier architecture. Group = subdirectory, and tiers are managed as metadata.\"):",
