  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CGCP68001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1CGCP68001",
    "text": "**Question — why does the 50 ms timer help?** (2026-10-11, user; answered by the orchestrator: what is measured, what is hypothesis): measured only that a 50 ms timer at each step header cut step 5 from 17 to 23 s to 1.6 s and 5d from 54.6 s to 1.9 s, did not help step 3 or 5g, while `setImmediate` and no yield did not help; the Mark-Compact time fell in step 5 (24,985 ms to 0) and 5d (47,103 ms to 3 ms). Why a pause of 50 ms and not a pass of the loop is not tested. Hypotheses, none checked: (1) V8 runs parts of a collection on background threads (user CPU was about twice wall time), and the cleanup callback that frees the wasm side is posted only when a collection has finished, so a zero-delay yield returns before it and 50 ms of wall time lets it run; (2) V8 starts a full collection each time external (wasm) memory has grown by a fixed step since the last one, so while the cleanup has not run every further step of growth costs another full collection (150 to 290 ms at this heap), and a pause that lets the cleanup run lets the wasm space be reused, so the growth and the collections stop; (3) a timer lets the loop block in its wait for the first time, which an immediate never does. The orchestrator's view: the pause length is a tuning that depends on the machine and its load, so it is a workaround; the deterministic remedies are fewer wrappers (bound matches) and `free()` on the quads, which the scan test showed keeps external memory flat. A short experiment on the bare engine could separate the hypotheses and find the shortest pause that works: scans in a loop with a yield of each kind (`setImmediate`, `setTimeout` 0, 1, 5, 20 and 50 ms, a forced `gc()` then a yield), logging `process.memoryUsage().external` right after each yield. Not sent.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1CJMZHG001",
    "text": "**Result — `node --test` fits, and the old suite's slowness comes from the real mirror in its store, not from its steps; the Coder's design and the orchestrator's answers to its four questions** (2026-10-11, orchestrator, from the Coder's Turn 1 of `test-runner-design`; no repository source changed, nothing staged):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG002",
      "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG003",
      "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG004",
      "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG005",
      "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG006",
      "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG007",
      "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG008"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG002:props:StringProp:00D1CJNBHR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG002:props:StringProp:00D1CJNBHR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Fit**",
    "text": "**Fit**: Node v22.22.1 has no type stripping, so TypeScript tests run as `node --import tsx --test <explicit globs>` (a directory argument fails); each file runs in its own child process (two files printed different pids and an 8 MB heap each); start-up costs 0.3 to 0.7 s a file on a tiny store; concurrency 2 is the recommended default (six copies of the heavier file took 3.62 s at 1, 2.56 s at 2 and 2.50 s at 3); `--test-timeout` kills a stuck file, a synchronous spin and a live handle included, while a per-test `{ timeout }` option alone does not; `--case` with no match exits 0, so the wrapper must catch that; a custom reporter module can carry the one-minute warning and do nothing else.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The finding that matters**",
    "text": "**The finding that matters**: `verifyLinkRefusals.ts` rewritten as a `node:test` file on a tiny store passes (steps 5g and 13) in 0.70 s for the whole run, and copies of steps 1 to 5f and 8 to 9c of `verify.ts` pass on an empty scratch store in 232 ms of step time (the extended steps 13 and 14 in 78 ms). So the steps are cheap; what costs 24 minutes is that the suite runs them against a store loaded with the real mirror, 48k quads, in one process. The coverage that this gave on real-size data is what the three corpus checks in the on-demand tier keep.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Design recommended**",
    "text": "**Design recommended**: the tier is a `// tier: change|commit|demand` comment in the first eight lines, read by the wrapper (17 files scanned in 3.3 ms; a missing or unknown tier stops the run with exit 2); the wrapper takes `--tier` (inclusive), `--group`, `--case`, `--list` (a static scan, since `node --test` has no list mode), `--concurrency` and `--timeout`; the legacy `verify.ts` and `verifyExtended.ts` move under `src/tests/legacy/` and run only with `npm run verify:legacy`; about 18 to 22 files would hold the fast tiers, in roughly 10 to 15 s of wall time at concurrency 2; migration order: 5g and 13, then 9c, then `parse`, `ingest`, `folder`, `store`, `gc` and 12, then the extended steps, the three corpus checks last. The full inventory of the 26 `verify.ts` headers and the extended steps is in the Report.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The flag file does not exist**",
    "text": "**The flag file does not exist**: `monorepo/verify-emergency.json` was never created; nothing reads it. It is dropped.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Answers to the Coder's four questions (the orchestrator's; the user may veto any)**",
    "text": "**Answers to the Coder's four questions (the orchestrator's; the user may veto any)**: (1) `src/tests/` lives at `monorepo/packages/cli/src/tests/`, beside the existing `verify*.ts` scripts; (2) a tier is inclusive (`--tier commit` runs `change` and `commit`), `npm run verify` with no flag is `commit`, and the Coder's split of tests across the three tiers is the starting point and moves with measured time; (3) a chain of steps that pass state to the next (3 to 5f, 5g with 13, 8 to 9c, 10 with 11) is one ordered file per chain at first, as recommended, and is split into self-contained cases later where it pays; (4) the flag file is dropped and the layout rule is the gate: nothing under `src/tests/legacy/` is run by `npm run verify` in any tier, and the graph's \"Verify emergency\" issue stays open until the legacy suite is gone.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Not covered by the user's earlier go**",
    "text": "**Not covered by the user's earlier go**: the Coder ran copies of parts of `verify.ts` and `verifyExtended.ts` in `/tmp` against an empty scratch store, each under `timeout 55`, which the Ask excluded in letter; they took milliseconds and read nothing from the real mirror, so no harm, noted here.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Not yet a go to build**",
    "text": "**Not yet a go to build**: the build (the runner and wrapper, the reporter, the fixture helper, the first scripts and the move of the legacy suite) is waiting for the user.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CJMZHG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1CWJKVG001",
    "text": "**Decision (direction) — tiers and other sets are compound scripts that call other scripts, not a header line in each test and not a manifest** (2026-10-11, user, answering a direct question on where a tier lives, after asking \"It's for each script?! How about the sets of cases, write a script for each set?\"): \"So, there are some chains must be tested together due to their deps, aren't they? They will be natural sets, but we may need other aggregate set like a group of function, or essentiality. Manifest seems overkill, so can we make compound test scripts just to call other scripts?\"",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG002",
      "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG003",
      "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVR000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG002:props:StringProp:00D1CWJZYR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG002:props:StringProp:00D1CWJZYR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The orchestrator's reading, put to the user to confirm**",
    "text": "**The orchestrator's reading, put to the user to confirm**: a test file stays the unit that runs in one process, and a chain with dependencies is one file (a natural set). A **suite** is a small compound script that lists test files and other suites and does nothing else; nesting gives the tiers (`commit` lists `change` and more files; `demand` lists `commit` and more), and other aggregates (by function group, by essentiality) are further suites. A suite run on its own runs its members, each in its own process; it never imports a member, because importing would put the members in one process and bring the build-up back. Test files carry no tier line and there is no central manifest; \"group\" is still a subdirectory and `--group` runs the files in it. `npm run verify` with no flag runs the `commit` suite; the timer warns after each file when the run is over a minute.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**What changes in the Coder's design**",
    "text": "**What changes in the Coder's design**: the `// tier:` header, its 8-line scanner and the exit-2 on a missing tier are dropped; `--tier` becomes `--suite <name>`; `--list` prints a suite's resolved files by reading the suite scripts; a file in no suite is found by comparing the files on disk with the members of an `all` suite, as a warning.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1CWJKVR000",
    "text": "**Not yet a go to build**; the earlier question on building phase 1 and on the pause experiment is still open.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1CWJKVG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1D00390000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1D00390000",
    "text": "**Question — is `change` a suite, and what does it contain?** (2026-10-11, user, on \"`commit` lists `change` plus more files\"; answered by the orchestrator, proposal open): as the orchestrator put it, `change` was loose. \"Per change, in seconds\" is a selection that depends on what the change touched, and a static suite cannot know that; the selection is `--group <area>` (the files of the subdirectory the change is in) or named files. What a static suite can hold is a small fast set that is worth running after every edit whatever the area. Proposed: rename that suite `essential` (the user's word for an aggregate by essentiality): the pure-function tests and the few cheapest checks of each area, a few seconds in all, which `commit` lists first; the per-change tier is then `--group` for the area touched plus `essential`. The actual member list comes from the Coder's inventory (its rough tier column) at build time and has not been drawn up. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1D2MKVR001",
    "text": "**Decision — the test redesign is settled; document it and build phase 1** (2026-10-11, user, answering whether `essential` plus `--group` is the per-change tier and whether to build: \"Yep, this design is reasonable. Let's document and implement it.\"):",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR002",
      "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR003",
      "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR004",
      "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR002:props:StringProp:00D1D2N0Y0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR002:props:StringProp:00D1D2N0Y0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The settled design**",
    "text": "**The settled design**: tests are standalone scripts (a chain with dependencies is one ordered file) in groups, each group a subdirectory under `monorepo/packages/cli/src/tests/`; a **suite** is a small compound script that lists test files and other suites and runs each member in its own process (never importing a member); the tiers are suites: per change = `--group <area>` plus the `essential` suite (seconds), per commit = the `commit` suite (under a minute; it lists `essential`), on demand = the `demand` suite (it lists `commit`, the large sets and the corpus checks on the real mirror); the runner is `node --import tsx --test` with explicit globs, concurrency 2 by default, a per-file `--test-timeout`, and a reporter that warns after a case when the run is over a minute and does no more; `npm run verify` with no flag runs `commit`; the legacy suite moves to `src/tests/legacy/` and runs only with `npm run verify:legacy`; there is no emergency flag file, and the graph's \"Verify emergency\" issue stays open until the legacy suite is gone; fixtures are tiny stores from a shared `makeFixtureStore()` helper; the corpus-scale checks on the real mirror stay in the on-demand tier.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Phase 1, the build**",
    "text": "**Phase 1, the build**: the runner and suite mechanism, the reporter, the fixture helper, the first scripts (the link-refusal chain 5g and 13, then the dangling-unfolds case 9c), the move of the legacy suite, and the `verify` and `verify:legacy` scripts, staged and not committed; the later phases migrate the remaining steps in the Coder's order. The skills' v4.4 text follows the built command (a Coder edit, then the Grapher's delta).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Document**",
    "text": "**Document**: the Grapher writes the settled design into `design/` (a new document if no existing one covers verification), keeps what is measured apart from what is not, and points the \"Verify emergency\" issue at it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1D5X340001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Still open, not part of this go**",
    "text": "**Still open, not part of this go**: the bare-engine pause experiment for the service remedy, and the choice of the service remedy itself.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1D2MKVR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
