    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1DMKT60001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1DMKT60001",
    "text": "**Question — did `free()` have no effect, and why was it put low? The answer is no, and the record was stale** (2026-10-11, user: \"> The scan test showed `free()` keeps external memory flat — But i recall you said that `free` has no effect or something like that. That's why you put its priority way down, isn't it?!\"): what had no effect was a forced `gc()` without a turn, not `free()`. The measurement of `free()` is in the Coder's Turn 2 of `bound-matches-layer-1` (ten scans in one tick, baseline external 90.7 MB): left to the finalizer 223 ms a scan and 253.9 MB external; `q.free()` only 162 ms and 115.8 MB; `q.free()` plus the subject term freed 109 ms and 101.3 MB with no growth after the first scan (rss 499, 351 and 289 MB). The orchestrator reported it then as the most useful number and recommended `free()` first. The low placement came from the layers recorded earlier, written before the measurement: the scans issue's Fix directions still says `free()` is \"untested\" in two places (the Layer 3 check and \"Smaller measures\", `BlockNode:00D0Y9HG3000B` and `00D0Y9HG3000F`), and the measurement was never copied into the issue, only into this Freeflow. The record contradicts the measurement and the orchestrator left it so. Correction: the issue is updated to state the measurement, `free()` becomes the first remedy for the scans in the service (before the bound matches, the index and the pause), and the pause stays the safety net. Caveats that stay: one sample, ten scans in one tick, the first scan's +10.6 MB of wasm memory is never returned, a term read from a quad (`q.subject`) is a separate object with its own handle that must be freed too, and `allIdsOfKind` reads the subject twice per quad.",
