  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1DS0GRG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1DS0GRG001",
    "text": "**Question — what is the difference between `free()` and `gc()`?** (2026-10-11, user; answered by the orchestrator from the binding read in `node_modules/oxigraph/node.js` and the Coder's measurements): `gc()` (the global `gc`, available with `--expose-gc`) forces V8 to collect the unreachable objects of the JavaScript heap now; for a wrapper that means the small JavaScript object holding the pointer is reclaimed and the cleanup callback registered for it is queued, and that callback frees the wasm side only when the event loop runs it later, so `gc()` without a turn frees nothing of the wasm memory. `free()` is a method of the wrapper that frees the wasm side at once and synchronously (`__destroy_into_raw` clears the pointer and unregisters the finalizer, then `wasm.__wbg_quad_free(ptr)` runs), with no collection and no turn; the object cannot be used afterwards. Neither shrinks the wasm linear memory, which only grows; both make the freed space reusable, `free()` immediately and `gc()` plus a turn later. A freed object used again, and a double `free()`, are not tested.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1DX75H8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1DX75H8001",
    "text": "**Question — can `free()` solve the issue to its root?** (2026-10-11, user; answered by the orchestrator, not yet tested end to end): it removes the link of the chain that turns wrappers into repeated full collections, and it is not yet shown to remove the problem. The chain as measured and read: the code makes very many wasm-bound wrappers (full scans, one match per field read, a quad object per added quad); the wasm side of each is freed only by a callback that needs a turn; the service and the old suite run in long synchronous bursts that never give one; external memory then grows and V8 starts a full collection each time it has grown by a step, which frees little. `free()` frees the wasm side at once, so it cuts the middle link without needing the turns, which the scan test supports (external memory flat after the first scan). What it does not do: it applies only where it is called, so every site that creates and drops wrappers must call it (the scans in `allIdsOfKind`, the matches in `wrap`, `readField` and `childrenOf`, the two scans in boot and flush, the per-quad `quad(...)` and `store.add` in rehydrate, the term read from each quad), and a missed site keeps leaking; it does not cut the CPU of creating the wrappers (109 ms a scan with both freed against 140 ms measured earlier), so fewer scans still help; the wasm memory it frees is reusable and never returned; a freed object used again is an error; and it is measured only on a scan micro-benchmark, one sample, not on boot, reload, flush or a suite. The 50 ms pause did not help step 3 or step 5g of the old suite, so part of the cost may have another source that `free()` does not address, which is untested. A safe way to apply it is a helper that reads the plain values out of a match and frees every object it made, used at the sites above, so that no raw wrapper escapes. The next step is a measurement in a scratch copy, before any production edit.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1E2NV00001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1E2NV00001",
    "text": "**Question — free at the root, right after each match from the store?** (2026-10-11, user: \"So, to be safe, we should `free` at the root right after each match from the store, shouldn't we?\"; answered by the orchestrator, design not decided): yes, at one boundary and not at each call site. Counted by the orchestrator outside tests: 33 `store.match` call sites in six files (`node.ts` 23, `dehydrate.ts` 5, `threadScope.ts` 2, and one each in `vocab.ts`, `tree.ts`, `store.ts`), 27 `store.add` and `store.delete` sites, and 51 calls that build a term (`nodeIri`, `predIri`). The shape that follows from the user's point: one store-access module through which every read, delete and add goes, so that no raw wasm-bound object leaves it: reads return plain values (strings and term types) after freeing the quad and each term read from it; a `delete matching` does the match, the deletes and the frees inside, because a quad must be deleted before it is freed; the terms built for a query are made and freed inside the module, or cached when they are constants such as the predicate IRIs; a test fails if any file outside the module calls `store.match`, `store.add` or `store.delete` directly, so a missed site cannot exist. Points to check first, not yet checked: whether `match` and `add` accept plain RDF/JS term objects (`{termType, value}`) as arguments, which would remove the wrappers created for the arguments; what a freed quad does if it is used afterwards; and the cost of the extra calls on a scan (the Coder measured 109 ms a scan with quad and subject term freed). Not decided; not a go to edit the files named earlier.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1E46B40000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1E46B40000",
    "text": "**Verification — migration batch 3 read and run by the orchestrator; the legacy suite is retired; decisions on the Coder's four questions** (2026-10-11, orchestrator; the `demand` suite was not run by the orchestrator, to spare the CPU): run by the orchestrator, `npm run verify -- --suite essential` passes 25 cases in 7 files in 2.8 s wall and `npm run verify` passes 47 cases in 15 files in 8.3 s wall (the Coder 8.7 s), `tsc --noEmit` is clean on core and cli, `legacy/` no longer exists, `package.json` has only `verify` (the `verify:legacy` scripts are gone), and `--list --suite demand` shows 19 files; no `/tmp/aperas-corpus-*` directory is left. The Coder reports: steps 13 to 22 as four files in `linking/` (`anchors`, `collisions`, `integrity`, `edits`; all 105 old checks carried over as 102 `assert` calls, 3 compounds becoming one assert each, 2 added); four corpus files (`copy`, `dangling-unfolds`, `link-integrity`, `roundtrip`) on a copy of the mirror made by `makeCorpusCopy()` in `support/corpus.ts`, in `demand` only, alone at 3.4 s, 19 s, 22 s and 87 s; `demand` at 116 s for 52 cases in 19 files at concurrency 2; 22 negative controls of which 21 fail the intended case (the one not caught, `hasIdAnchor` always false, was a blind spot of the old step too); `verifyLinkRefusals.ts` and `verifyDanglingUnfolds.ts` kept because test files import them. **The legacy suite of 26 steps that took 1,434 s is replaced by 47 cases that take 8 s, plus a 116 s on-demand tier on a copy of the real mirror.**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1E46B40001",
      "BlockNode:00D1E46B40002",
