        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The finding**",
    "text": "**The finding**: the round trip moved into one fresh child process (`support/roundtripChild.ts`) still takes 90.4 s alone: in that one child the first rehydrate is 3.0 s, the dehydrate of content and `.state/` 26.2 s and the second rehydrate 37.8 s, with 55,714 quads before and after and 0 dangling references. So the second rehydrate is slow in a process that has already rehydrated and dehydrated, and the earlier reading, that it was slow because the parent held the mirror's store, was only part of it: the same build-up happens inside one process whichever process it is. The Coder asks whether the slowness could be in the dehydrated directory itself; the first rehydrate of the original directory was 3.0 s, and a fresh rehydrate of the dehydrated directory has not been timed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1E7VPB0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1E7VPB0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1E7VPB0003",
    "text": "**Decision — split the round trip into two fresh child processes** (orchestrator): the first rehydrates the copy and dehydrates into a scratch directory; the second, a new process, rehydrates that directory and compares the quad sets; the file's time then shows the second rehydrate in a fresh process, which settles the Coder's question without a third run for its own sake. The expected time is the 3.0 s and 26.2 s of the first child plus a few seconds for the second.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1E7VPB0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1E7VPB0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1E7VPB0004",
    "text": "**Decision — the 900 s timeout stays keyed on \"the suite declares no budget\"** (orchestrator): any budgetless suite is a long one by its own declaration.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1E7VPB0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EAZSRG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1EAZSRG001",
    "text": "**Result — the corpus round trip in two fresh child processes: the second rehydrate takes 2.9 s, against 37.8 s when it follows a rehydrate and a dehydrate in the same process; the slowness is state built up in the process, not the data** (2026-10-11, orchestrator, from the Coder's Turn 2 of `test-runner-final-adjustments`; one run, the children run by hand on a corpus copy, 55,968 quads and 0 dangling references): child 1 rehydrates the copy in 2.8 s and dehydrates content and `.state/` in 24.3 s; child 2, a new process, rehydrates the dehydrated directory in 2.9 s. The corpus file, which runs the two children, passed in 42.8 s (2 passed). So the same directory costs 2.9 s in a fresh process and 37.8 s in a process that has already rehydrated and dehydrated, about thirteen times: the clearest single measurement so far that the cost is process state, and it fits the garbage-collection reading (the dehydrate, which scans the store, leaves the wrappers that slow the next rehydrate). It also leaves the 24.3 s dehydrate: in a fresh process the first dehydrate still costs that, so the scans inside the flush are slow by themselves, which `free()` or fewer wrappers would address and a pause between processes does not. Corrected by the Coder along the way: the dehydrate does not create its target directory (the child now makes it), and quad lines holding a newline in a literal were split by the line count (each line is now a JSON array). The runner prints no output for a passing file, so the test's own timing lines are not visible; not a problem for the tier, noted.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1EC4X6R001",
    "text": "**Verification — the skills' v4.5 edit read and checked by the orchestrator; the emergency's end conditions are met** (2026-10-11, orchestrator; skill-only checks, no suite run): the staged skill diff (4 files, +17 −8 against the committed v4.3) matches the Ask: `Status:` is v4.5 in all four role skills; the base skill's Verification Scope names the budgets (`essential` 5 s, `commit` 60 s), the stop-and-report rule reads \"passes its budget\", `--suite demand` runs the corpus checks on a copy of the real mirror (minutes, no budget, no warning) only when the user asks, and the legacy sentences are gone; the orchestrator's section 7 and the Coder's step 2 and Operational Invariants say the same; the Grapher's skill only moved `Status:`; a search finds no `legacy` or `emergency` in any skill except `kg-doc-ingest`'s unrelated \"legacy hand-written concern doc\". `python3 skills/aperas/scripts/skill_drift.py` exits 1 as expected before the delta (4 Status, 11 added unrecorded, 9 dropped unaccounted — the units of the v4.4 delta that v4.5 replaces — and 201 superseded); `aperas check-links` is clean.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R002",
      "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R002:props:StringProp:00D1EC5AJ8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R002:props:StringProp:00D1EC5AJ8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D1EC4X6R002",
    "text": "**Decision — base Step 7 does not name the budget** (orchestrator, answering the Coder's question): it already says `npm run verify` runs `commit`, and the budget is in the Verification Scope it points to.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The emergency's end conditions**",
    "text": "**The emergency's end conditions**: the user's rule was that the emergency ends when a core fix and a rebuilt verification bring `verify` under a minute. The legacy suite is retired (deleted) and `npm run verify`, the `commit` suite, runs 48 cases in 15 files in 8 to 17 s wall; the heavy part is now the `demand` tier, minutes by design, run only when asked. Both conditions the design named hold, so the \"Verify emergency\" issue is resolved. The core defect itself is not: the scans, the reload, the memory and the service's refused commands remain open in their own issues, and the emergency was closed by making the suite independent of them, not by fixing them.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1EC4X6R001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
