    "title": "**v4.5 — verification text that follows the built suites: the budgets of `essential` and `commit`, the `demand` suite, and no mention of the legacy suite**",
    "text": "**v4.5 — verification text that follows the built suites: the budgets of `essential` and `commit`, the `demand` suite, and no mention of the legacy suite**: recorded as [the v4.5 delta](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D1EDH18G001), which replaces in part [the v4.4 delta](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D1DA0Q18001). A code task runs `npm run verify -- --group <area>` and `npm run verify -- --suite essential` (a budget of 5 seconds) after an edit, and `npm run verify`, which runs the `commit` suite (a budget of 60 seconds), before handing a change back and at Step 7; the reporter warns once a run is past its suite's budget, and if the `commit` suite passes its budget the agent stops and reports to the user. `npm run verify -- --suite demand` runs the corpus checks on a copy of the real mirror, takes more than a minute, has no budget and no warning, and runs only when the user asks. The legacy suite and its two commands are gone from the text. The base skill states this in Verification Scope, the Orchestrator's Verification Protocol and the Coder's step 2 and Operational Invariants restate it, and the Grapher's skill moved only its `Status:` line. Skill-only tasks still never run `npm run verify`. The four `Status:` lines move to v4.5. The text follows [Testing — Design](../design/testing.md#id/BlockNode:00D1D5F63R001), and the [Verify emergency](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D1BAT6Q0001) issue is resolved by the suites it describes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CEA5MHD8004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EFQ93G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1EFQ93G001",
    "text": "**Question — `verify` has no `--help`; what does the bare `verify` run, does it look like `essential`?** (2026-10-11, user; answered by the orchestrator from `run.mjs` and the suite files): the bare `npm run verify` runs the `commit` suite (`opts.suite ?? 'commit'`), which lists `essential.suite.mjs` first and then seven more files (`runner/runner.test.ts`, `store/dangling-unfolds.test.ts`, `store/views.test.ts`, `links/backlinks.test.ts`, `linking/collisions.test.ts`, `linking/integrity.test.ts`, `linking/edits.test.ts`): 15 files, 48 cases, against `essential`'s 8 files and 27 cases. It looks like `essential` because the run prints no header: nothing names the suite, its budget or the number of files, and the files appear in completion order, so the first files seen are essential's. `--help` is an unknown argument (the runner prints \"error: unknown argument --help\" and its usage line). Proposed, not decided: `--help` that prints the usage, the suites found in `suites/` with their budgets and the first comment line of each, and the groups; a header line at the start of every run (\"suite commit, budget 60 s, 15 files, concurrency 2\"), and the suite's name in the summary line. A small Coder change; not a go.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EG6N6R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1EG6N6R001",
    "text": "**The same data costs about thirteen times more in a process that has already worked.** A copy of the real mirror (55,968 quads, 0 dangling references) rehydrated in 2.8 s in a fresh process, which then dehydrated it in 24.3 s; a second fresh process rehydrated the dehydrated directory in 2.9 s, and the same directory took 37.8 s to rehydrate in a process that had already rehydrated and dehydrated. The cost is state built up in the process, not the data; the dehydrate, which scans the store, leaves the wrappers that slow the next rehydrate *(measured, one run, for the times; read, for the wrappers)*.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1D5F640005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
