    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EXNME8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1EXNME8001",
    "text": "Every run except `--list` and `--help` prints a header line first, naming the suite or the group with its budget, the file count, the concurrency and the per-file timeout, and the case pattern when `--case` is given: `suite commit (budget 60 s) - 61 files, concurrency 2, timeout 120 s a file`. *(built, measured)*",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1D5F648003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1EYTA10001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1EYTA10001",
    "text": "**Question — why are the open issues about `verify` in `issues/core.md` not updated?** (2026-10-11, user; answered by the orchestrator): because no Ask named them. Every Ask about `verify` targeted the new \"Verify emergency\" issue, which links the older ones, and the older ones were left as they were: the entry \"`npm run verify` costs ~5 minutes, dominated by a process-lifetime slowdown…\" (`BlockNode:00CJ0202MG001`, with its child \"Practical problem resolved, root cause still not\", `00CJ0QG048001`, which records the split into `verify` and `verify:slow` and about 42 s), the entry \"Why isolation helps is still unexplained\" (`00CJ0HX2Y0000`, whose leading guess was a JIT-level degradation and which proposed V8 profiling), and the sibling \"Confirmed at real production scale, 2026-09-20\" (`00CKSAK04R001`, a 40-minute unresponsive service during a corpus-wide re-ingestion). The orchestrator's omission: closing the emergency and retiring the suite left these three describing a state that no longer exists and a mechanism that is now measured. To do in the next Grapher turn: record on the first two what was found (the cost is repeated full collections, 70 to 87% of the slow steps, triggered by wasm memory whose release needs a later callback; a pause or a fresh process cuts it; the suite is replaced by `commit` at 8 to 20 s and `demand` at 73 to 74 s) and resolve them, with the strength the evidence gives (the guess of a JIT-level degradation is replaced by the collection reading; the external-memory trigger remains a reading); add the finding to the 2026-09-20 entry and keep it open, since the service-level stall it records is not closed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1EYTA10002"
    ]
  },
