        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The cap**",
    "text": "**The cap**: the warning's limit is read by `resolveEffectiveThreadSizeCap` (`core/src/graphConfig.ts`): the environment variable `APERAS_THREAD_SIZE_CAP`, then `threadSizeCap` in `aperas.config.json`, then the default 200. `AperasKG/aperas.config.json` (tracked in the graph repo) has no `threadSizeCap`, so the change is one field there: `\"threadSizeCap\": 1000`, by the Coder. Whether the service reads the file on each warning or once at start is for the Coder to check; a restart is the user's. The grapher thread (about 1,190 live blocks) and the coder thread (about 1,100) are still over 1,000 and will warn until pruned a little; noted for the user. Any text that names the number 200 (the design document on threads) is updated by the Grapher afterwards.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1FFV2FR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1FFV2FR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The transcript path**",
    "text": "**The transcript path**: a protocol rule for the skills, so a version bump decided by the orchestrator: **v4.6**. Every subagent records the path of its own transcript in its thread, in the first subturn of its Work, so that a later agent can trace details the Report left out. How a subagent finds its own path is part of the work: in Claude Code the subagent's transcript is a JSONL file under the session's project directory (the orchestrator sees only an `.output` path in `/tmp` that it must not read whole), in Antigravity it is elsewhere; the Coder finds a reliable method on each harness it can test (for example writing a unique marker in a command and searching the candidate directories for the file that holds it) and the skill says what to do when no path can be found (record \"Transcript: not found\" and what was tried, so nobody searches again). The skill also says how a later agent uses the path: search it with `grep` or `jq` for the detail wanted and never read the whole file, because a transcript is large.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1FFV2FR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1FHX5MG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1FHX5MG001",
    "text": "**Verification — the thread cap and the transcript-path rule read and checked by the orchestrator; answers to the Coder's two questions** (2026-10-11, orchestrator; skill-only checks, no suite run): the config diff is one field, `\"threadSizeCap\": 1000`, in `AperasKG/aperas.config.json`; the service reads the cap once at start (`service.ts:173`, compared at `:313`), so a restart is needed before 1,000 applies (the running service still says \"over its cap of 200\"); a scratch process with the real graph root returns 1,000; both threads exceed it (1,206 and 1,128 live blocks by `aperas tree`). The staged skill diff matches the Ask: `Status:` v4.6 in the base, Coder and Grapher skills (the orchestrator's skill stays at v4.5, untouched: the base rule covers how a later agent reads a transcript); the thread-structure template has `**Transcript**: <harness>: <path>` as the first subturn; the base skill's Core Thread Disciplines has \"Record your transcript first\" with the Claude Code command (an `echo \"TRANSCRIPT_$(date +%s%N)\"` in one call, then `grep -lF <marker> ~/.claude/projects/*/\"$CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID\"/subagents/*.jsonl` in the next, because the transcript is written after the call returns) and an Antigravity line (\"No method is recorded yet\"); the Coder and Grapher turn templates get one `**Transcript**` line. The orchestrator confirmed that the Coder's reported file exists (`.../4dd57903-…/subagents/agent-a665b323af9b7fc1a.jsonl`, 406 KB). `skill_drift.py` exits 1 as expected before the delta (3 Status, 7 added unrecorded, 5 dropped unaccounted, 210 superseded) and `aperas check-links` is clean.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1FHX5MG002",
      "BlockNode:00D1FHX5MG003"
