    ],
    "title": "00D1FXJEK8001",
    "text": "**The user commits v4.6 and restarts the service; \"Let's continue with the `free()` work\"** (2026-10-11, user): the v4.6 skills (code commit `b63da71`) and the graph are committed, and the service is restarted, so the thread cap of 10,000 applies. The user's direction is to continue the `free()` work. Orchestrator reading, stated so the user can correct it: this opens the task loop at its survey steps (Grapher survey, then Coder map of every place the store hands out quads and terms, no edits). The production change to `dehydrate.ts`, `node.ts`, `store.ts` and `service.ts` waits for the user's explicit go after the Coder's map, because those files have not been cleared for editing and the map decides the shape of the change. Measurements the plan rests on: [the correction on `free()`](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D1DMKT60001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1G0N8DG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1G0N8DG001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's survey for the `free()` work; what goes to the Coder, what waits** (2026-10-11, orchestrator): the Grapher's report (`BlockNode:00D1G08WVG006`) names the `free()` item (`00D1DP5GG0001`) as the whole recorded plan: decided only the order (first remedy, pause as safety net), not decided the sites, the helper shape or any production edit; no go is on record for `dehydrate.ts`, `node.ts`, `store.ts` or `service.ts`. I read three of its cited nodes and they say what the report says (`00D0X65RB8009` \"No fix has been tried.\" although `removeDanglingUnfolds` landed in `0a7a226`; `00D0Y9HG30002` still names \"type quads\", which the store does not have; `00D1DP5GG0001` the measured item). Six disagreements in the record, the first three being stale statements in `issues/core.md` (fix status, the type-quad premise, the removal-stall issue with its open status and `PLACEHOLDER-*` blocks). **Decision (orchestrator, open to the user's correction)**: the Coder's map comes first and is read-only; the Grapher corrects the three stale statements in the Filing Turn that files the map, since the map settles what the correct text says. The Grapher's second question, whether the landed `removeDanglingUnfolds` covers `unfoldsCollapsed` and `unfoldsNoPreview`, goes to the Coder as part of the map.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1G1YEK8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1G1YEK8001",
    "text": "**The user wonders whether to replace Oxigraph with a store the project builds with three indexes** (2026-10-11, user): the project used Terminus first and migrated to Oxigraph for the basic functionality of a graph, with no query language, only triples (quads in Oxigraph). The JS and wasm quirk now makes the user ask whether it is worth hacking around it (`free()` and the like) when the project could build the graph itself with just three indexes. This is a question, not a decision; nothing is changed. Orchestrator's reading, stated for the user to correct: the three indexes are subject-predicate-object, predicate-object-subject and object-subject-predicate, kept in JS, which answer every match pattern with at least one bound position; the store would stay in memory and still be rehydrated from the mirror, as it is today. Orchestrator's view: the idea is plausible at this size (58,376 quads), and it would remove the cause rather than patch it (no wasm side, so no wrapper objects to free, no external-memory growth to trigger collections, no boundary cost per call). What is not known yet: which Oxigraph calls the code uses (`match` patterns, `has`, `size`, named graphs, any query or load), which is what the Coder's map of the store reads (`BlockNode:00D1G0TQ08001`) partly answers; and whether the 24 s fresh-process dehydrate is only collector thrash or also JS-side work that a new store would not change. A prototype of the used subset on a scratch copy would settle the second. The `free()` work stays on its survey steps until the user decides.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1G3PV30001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1G3PV30001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Coder's map of the store reads (Turn 1, `BlockNode:00D1G3BJCR003`); what the grep shows about the API surface** (2026-10-11, orchestrator): I checked three claims in the code myself and they hold. There is no `.free()` call anywhere under `monorepo/packages`; `unfoldsCollapsed` and `unfoldsNoPreview` appear in no source file (only `unfolds` and `root` are defined, so the landed `removeDanglingUnfolds` covers every field that exists); and the only methods called on a `store` variable are `match` (41 call sites), `add` (17) and `delete` (12), plus the term factories `quad`, `namedNode` and `literal` imported from `oxigraph` (`vocab.ts`, `store.ts`, `node.ts`). The grep matches the name `store.` only, so a store held under another name would not show; the Coder's map lists the sites by file and line. The Coder reports that every production read ends as a string, a number, a boolean or a wrapped node holding only `store` and `id`, so no wasm-backed object outlives its call (the exception is the test-only `referenceDanglingUnfoldQuads`); that a freed shared constant (`PARENT_PRED` and the like) or `store.delete(freedQuad)` throws; and, on a copy of 58,780 quads with `gc()` between calls, that the unchanged `allIdsOfKind` grows about 42 MB per call (570 to 1,140 ms) while the variant that frees the subject term and the quad stays flat at 349 MB after a first-call rise of 32 MB, about 150 ms per call: one site, one corpus size, rss only. Bearing on the user's question about building the graph with three indexes: the surface a replacement must offer is `match`, `add` and `delete` over quads, plus term and quad construction, which is small. Two questions are open for the user: which change comes first if `free()` goes ahead (`allIdsOfKind` alone, or the whole read helper), and whether to keep `free()` at all if the three-index store is chosen. Citation correction from the Coder: `isThreadScopedId` is at `dehydrate.ts:137` and `168`, not `162,169`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1GCWWYR001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1G68Q3G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1G68Q3G001",
    "text": "**The user's decision: `free()` as the best effort on Oxigraph, no more hacks on it; then a pure JS ApeironNgn engine to compare** (2026-10-11, user): the user rejects the orchestrator's \"lost testing\" cost: the store is only a memory reflection of the mirror, the mirror is the source of truth and has been stable from Terminus until now, so the store (Terminus', Oxigraph's or plain JS) is not what holds the truth. The orchestrator's point was wrong and is withdrawn. The user's plan, in the user's words: \"I think we should applied the `free()` patch as our best effort on Oxigraph, but no more hack on Oxigraph. After that, we'll try the pure JS ApeironNgn (as the name originally suggested, a dedicated engine) to compare with the `free()`-patched version.\" **Orchestrator's reading, to be corrected by the user**: this answers the question of which path (the `free()` patch first, the plain JS engine as a later comparison) and is the go for the `free()` change; the user did not choose between `allIdsOfKind` alone and the whole read helper, so the orchestrator takes the Coder's recommendation (the whole read helper, with plain-value reads and write helpers that take strings, `predIri` cached per field name), since \"best effort\" points that way. Out of the `free()` change: the whole-store `free()` on reload (measured to change nothing), the plain JS engine (a later task), and new fields for `removeDanglingUnfolds` (none exist in code, so nothing to sweep beyond `unfolds`). The Grapher files the Coder's map and corrects the three stale statements in `issues/core.md`, and records the plan with the comparison as a later item.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1GCYPGR001",
      "BlockNode:00D21GV8P8001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1GBN3G8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1GBN3G8001",
    "text": "**The user confirms the `free()` scope and will fork a branch for the pure JS version; friction: a killed zoom** (2026-10-11, user): on the orchestrator's stated reading (the `free()` patch as the whole read helper, the Coder's recommendation, out of scope the reload `free()`, the plain JS engine and new `unfolds*` fields) the user said \"Just go like that\", and will fork a new branch for the pure JS version, so the comparison lives on its own branch while this branch carries the `free()` patch. Earlier in the same stretch the service stopped answering for about two minutes after the orchestrator ran `aperas zoom` under `timeout 90`, which killed the command at 90 s; the next commands were refused (\"No ApeironNgn service running\") while the service processes were alive. The user said killing makes a busy service worse and asked to retry without `kill`; both zooms then ran in the background without `timeout` and answered in under 20 s each. Orchestrator's mistake: a `timeout` wrapper on a graph write is a kill; slow `aperas` commands run in the background without `timeout`, polled for at most a minute, and both subagents were told the same.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1GC79B8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1GC79B8000",
    "text": "**The plan of the user, 2026-10-11: the `free()` helper first, then a pure JavaScript engine to compare** (decided as an order; the helper's shape is the orchestrator's reading of \"best effort\", open to the user's correction)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0X65RB8008",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D1GC79B8001",
