  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D1GF7EBG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D1GF7EBG001",
    "text": "**Verification — the `free()` patch (Coder Turn 2, `BlockNode:00D1GENHHR00B`) and the Grapher's filing (Turn 2, `BlockNode:00D1GE12DR008`)** (2026-10-11, orchestrator): I read the diff scope (8 source files changed and 2 new: `storeAccess.ts`, 250 lines, and its test `store/storeAccess.test.ts`, named in the `commit` suite) and read `storeAccess.ts` in full: every read returns plain strings, numbers, booleans or rows, `eachMatch` frees each quad after its callback and the argument terms in a `finally` (also when the callback throws), `deleteMatches` frees a quad only after deleting it, `addQuad` frees the quad and its terms, `predIri` is cached per field name and seeded with `PARENT_PRED` and `SIBLING_INDEX_PRED`, and no shared constant is freed. I ran `npm run verify -- --suite essential` (27 of 27, 3.2 s) and `npm run verify` (the `commit` tier, 61 of 61, 19.5 s), and `tsc --noEmit` on `core` and `cli` printed nothing; I did not run `demand` or `verify:legacy`. The Coder's measurement (production code, a copy of the mirror with 59,275 quads, 3 samples per case, a fresh process each, `gc()` between calls): eight `allIdsOfKind` calls went from 349 to 634 MB (+40 MB per call) to flat at 264.6 MB, and from 0.7 to 1.7 s per call to 0.14 to 0.23 s; dehydrate of both mirrors from 33 to 38 s to 2.5 to 3.8 s, with rss from about 770 to about 350 MB; rehydrate from 3.3 to 3.5 s to 2.0 to 2.9 s; the dehydrated directories are byte-identical before and after in all three repetitions. Not measured: the write paths (ingest, track, tombstone sweeps) in a real service session; tests cover them only. The Grapher's filing: the map is a subtree under the `free()` item (`00D1GCR1XR001`), the plan item `00D1GC79B8000` carries the user's words, the fix status (`00D0X65RB8009`), the type-quad premise (`00D0Y9HG30002`, `00D0Y9HG30003`) and the removal-stall issue (`00CVNX6A3R000`, now in Resolved) are corrected; I read the first three of those and they say what the report says. The code is staged in the code repo, the graph in `AperasKG`. The running service still holds the old code; the new code applies after a restart, which is for the user to approve, and the first restart is also the live check of the write paths. Two Grapher questions go to the user: whether Layers 1 to 3 and the pause stay planned, and the \"above\" wording in `00CVNZXVR8001`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D21794FG001",
      "BlockNode:00D21GKYAR001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2030938001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Live check — the service restarted onto the `free()` patch (2026-10-12, orchestrator)**",
    "text": "**Live check — the service restarted onto the `free()` patch (2026-10-12, orchestrator)**: the user committed the patch and approved the restart. `aperas service restart` found no service running (the previous one was already gone) and started a fresh one; the command's own 5 s ready wait expired, and the service answered 17 s after its process started (08:15:59 to 08:16:16 UTC), at 379 MB resident, against 37.8 s to rehydrate in the earlier measurement and a resident size of 1.0 to 1.8 GB. After that: `aperas tree --view` took 0.57 s; `aperas check-links` (a full scan of 6,900 live blocks, 1,480 with internal link text) took 2.7 s twice, clean, 0 discrepancies, and the service stayed at 375 to 379 MB across both scans. One sample each. This entry's own insert into the Freeflow document is the first timed write; its time and the resident size after it are in the next entry.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D217ABQG001",
      "BlockNode:00D21GN5TR001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2033S00001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Live check, write path (2026-10-12, orchestrator)**",
    "text": "**Live check, write path (2026-10-12, orchestrator)**: the first insert into the Freeflow document (the largest artifact, the entry above) took 5.1 s with the service at 392 MB resident afterwards, against a minute or more for a write into this document before the patch and 2 minutes right after the earlier restart. One sample. The entry recording this one is the second timed write; its time is the first line of the next entry.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2037TRG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Live check, second write and projection (2026-10-12, orchestrator)**",
    "text": "**Live check, second write and projection (2026-10-12, orchestrator)**: the second insert into the Freeflow document took 5.0 s and `aperas project discussion/aperas-skill.md` 2.4 s; resident memory rose from 379 MB at start through 392 and 413 MB after the two inserts to 424 MB after the projection, so it still grows with writes (about 45 MB over five commands, one sample, not yet a trend). The live check of the `free()` patch is done for reads, a full scan and the write path of one artifact; the ingest, `remove` and tombstone-sweep paths were not exercised live. This is the last timing entry; a longer run (an hour of normal agent work) is the next evidence for the memory trend.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
