    ],
    "title": "00D20BZE28001",
    "text": "**The user pauses Oxigraph patching at branch `oxigraph` and starts the JS ApeironNgn on branch `js-apeironngn`** (2026-10-12, user): the `free()` patch is committed as `d794060` on `oxigraph`, which stays as it is; the user says \"pause Oxigraph patching here\" and \"start JS ApeironNgn here in branch `js-apeironngn`\". The code repo is on `js-apeironngn`, clean; `AperasKG` stays on `main` and is shared by both branches. The earlier decisions stand: the store is a memory reflection of the mirror, which is the source of truth, so the engine is compared with the `free()` version on the same mirror ([the decision](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D1G68Q3G001)); Layers 1 to 3 and the pause stay parked on the Oxigraph side. **Orchestrator's reading, to be corrected by the user**: \"start\" opens the survey steps only, a Grapher survey of what the graph records about the engine's contract and its history, and a Coder map of the contract the code asks of the store (which methods and arguments, the term shapes read, the ordering the callers rely on, what the tests and `dehydrate` need to stay byte-identical), ending in a short design proposal with options; no engine code before the user agrees to a design. Questions the survey should settle first: (1) scope: replace only the `Store` behind the call sites that now go through `storeAccess.ts` (about 41 `match`, 17 `add` and 12 `delete` sites in the Oxigraph version), or the term and quad types as well; (2) the three indexes (subject-predicate-object, predicate-object-subject, object-subject-predicate) and whether the graph position is used at all; (3) the order `match` returns, which `dehydrate` and the sorted children may depend on; (4) how to compare with the `free()` version: the same tiers, and the Coder's before and after measurement on the same mirror copy. Risk the orchestrator sees: the running service executes the source on disk, so while this branch is checked out and in progress, a service restart would run unfinished engine code; the engine should be built behind an opt-in, or restarts should wait for the `oxigraph` branch; this goes to the user.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D21GT120001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D20FGGX0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D20FGGX0001",
    "text": "**The user: the JS engine needs neither quads nor triples, only pairs (direct links)** (2026-10-12, user): \"Neither quads like in Oxigraph nor triples like in RDF, we only use pairs (direct links). So the design would be much simpler.\" This changes the options the Coder was asked for (a drop-in class over three indexes of triples, or the `storeAccess.ts` helpers over them), so the data model is settled before the options. **Orchestrator's reading, stated for the user to confirm or correct, nothing built on it yet**: a node is one plain record of its own fields (strings, numbers, booleans) held in a Map by id, with no predicate IRIs and no wasm; a relation between two nodes (`parent`, `unfolds`, `root` and whichever others the code uses with a node as the object) is a named table of pairs, from id to id, kept in both directions (forward and reverse), so \"children of X\" and \"the parent of Y\" are single lookups and a `match` with a bound object is no longer a scan; sibling order is a field of the child and is no longer a separate predicate. Open until the Coder's map answers: which fields are multi-valued (a pair table or a list in the record), whether any field holds a literal on one side and a node on the other, and the order a lookup returns for the dehydrated mirror to stay byte-identical.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D20H79PR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D20H79PR001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's and the Coder's surveys for the JS engine (Turn 1 of `js-apeironngn-survey`; Reports `BlockNode:00D20ETQ28000` and `BlockNode:00D20GSWJG009`)** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): I checked what is cheap to check. The code repo is clean (`git status` empty); outside the tests the only store members used besides `match`, `add` and `delete` are `size` (`store.ts:147`, `verifyDanglingUnfolds.ts:53,55`, `verifyLinkRefusals.ts:61`), as the Coder says; the Coder's scratch copy exists under `/tmp/mono`; and in the Grapher's findings, `Apeiron/README.md` still describes a TerminusDB mirror and the archived ApeironNgn design is at `artifacts/archive/Aperas-apeironngn-design.md`, outside the graph. I did not rerun the Coder's benchmark or its suites on the prototypes; the figures are the Coder's, one run on a 60k-quad copy: rehydrate 2,050 ms (Oxigraph) against 174 ms (JS, layout B), dehydrate 2,500 against 530 ms, heap plus wasm about 107 MB against about 14 MB, 8 dehydrated files byte-identical, the `commit` tier 61 of 61 with both stores and `essential` 27 of 27 on the JS one. Bearing on the user's pairs model (`BlockNode:00D20FGGX0001`): the graph position is never used; the code reads only `termType`, `value` and `datatype.value`; no SPARQL, load or dump; the mirror does not depend on `match` order, but the order of `aperas backlinks`, the profile view list and reads of `links`, `props` and `unfolds` does (Oxigraph returns the newest quad first, which held for about 3,600 lists on the real mirror; 2 of about 2,900 lists mismatched after deletes and re-adds and are not explained). The Grapher's open questions: ingest the archived design or author `design/core.md` from it before the proposal; and correct the stale statements and record the `free()` landing (`d794060`) in `issues/` and `history/`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D20NWKZ0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The user questions the engine switch** (2026-10-12, user)",
    "text": "**The user questions the engine switch** (2026-10-12, user): \"Why do you think we need a hot switch between Oxigraph and JS store? Is it for comparison?\" Orchestrator's answer: the switch (`APERAS_STORE_ENGINE`, read at start) came from the orchestrator's Ask to the Coder (\"say how the engine would be selected so the service can run either\"), for two reasons: to compare the engines on the same tree, and so that a service restart on `js-apeironngn` before the engine is ready would still run Oxigraph. It is a start-time choice, not a hot one. With the two branches the user made (`oxigraph` holds the `free()` version, `js-apeironngn` holds the JS one), the branch is already the selection, so the switch is not needed: the comparison is the same mirror copy measured on each branch (the other branch in a `git worktree` if both are wanted side by side), and the rule for the service is not to restart it on `js-apeironngn` until the engine passes the tiers. A switch costs a second code path, the `oxigraph` import kept alive, and tests that must cover both. Orchestrator's recommendation, open to the user: no switch; the JS branch replaces the import directly and `engine.ts` is not built.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D20RN0EG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D20RN0EG001:links:Link:00D20RNBVG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D20NWKZ0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D20RN0EG001:links:Link:00D20RNBVG000:props:StringProp:00D20RNBVG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "287"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D20RN0EG001:links:Link:00D20RNBVG002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D20H79PR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D20RN0EG001:links:Link:00D20RNBVG002:props:StringProp:00D20RNBVG003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "724"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D20RN0EG001",
    "text": "**The user decides: no engine switch, keep newest-first lookup order, accept the three dropped Oxigraph behaviours; asks whether the drop-in class and the pairs implementation are one route or two** (2026-10-12, user): decided: (1) no switch, the JS branch replaces the import directly ([the question](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D20NWKZ0001)); (2) the lookup order stays Oxigraph's newest-first; (3) the IRI-validation throw, the canonical rewriting of integer literals and the replacement of lone surrogates are given up. The user's question: \"Is the drop-in class more lightweight and the pair impl requires additional mods, or are they different routes?\" Orchestrator's answer, from the Coder's Report ([options (a), (b), (c)](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D20H79PR001) as summarised in the verification): they differ on two separate axes that the survey did not separate. Interface: (a) keeps Oxigraph's `Store` API (`match`, `add`, `delete`, plain term objects), 3 import lines change; (b) has the engine implement the `storeAccess.ts` helpers directly (`objectsOf`, `subjectsWhere`, ...), no quad or term object is built, about 40 files change, mostly mechanical (28 are type-only imports). Storage under either: the Coder's prototype is still triple-shaped (flat per-subject arrays and a predicate-object map); the user's pairs model (a record per node plus named relation tables, both directions) is a different storage and the Coder has not designed it. The drop-in class is lighter but is a bridge: its `Store`/term layer is discarded when moving to pairs, because pairs has no `match(s,p,o)` to emulate; the helper signatures are the natural interface for pairs. Orchestrator's recommendation, open to the user: skip (a) as a goal; next a Coder turn designs the pairs storage against the real mirror (every field with its kind, single or multi-valued, literal or reference, which relations need a reverse table, how newest-first order is kept) and prototypes it in scratch with a differential test against Oxigraph through the helpers; then the build on the branch follows option (b) with Oxigraph kept as a test-only oracle.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D20XX3SG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D20XX3SG001",
    "text": "**The user defines \"pairs\": triples whose predicates are a small fixed set** (2026-10-12, user): \"Technically we're still using triples with the very limited set of predefined 'predicates' like `parent`, `link`, `unfolds`... because these predicates are fixed, not generalized like subjects and objects, I call them 'pairs'. Is my model correct, or missing something?\" Orchestrator's check against `shape.ts` (read by the orchestrator, to assess the claim): the model holds. The fields are declared per class in `SHAPE_BY_KIND` (seven kinds: `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`, `FolderNode`, `Link`, `StringProp`, `Profile`, `TreeView`), each field with a cardinality (`one`, `optional`, `set`) and, for node-valued fields, a `storageKind` (`reference` or `embed`); `parent` and `siblingIndex` are two further structural predicates kept outside the shapes. What the model must also carry, for the user to confirm: (1) `set` fields keep an order (newest first, as decided); (2) `embed` fields own their child nodes (`Link`, `StringProp`, preferences), so deleting the owner removes them; (3) backlinks are generic over any `reference` field (`backlinks(store, id, 'profile')`), so every reference field needs a reverse table; (4) a per-kind list of ids, or the kind read from the id, for the scans that now walk every quad; (5) whether any field outside the shapes appears in the mirror or the code (not checked; the Coder's design turn is to check it); (6) literal values keep their JS types (string, number, boolean). The same model is a table per predicate (column view) or a record per node (row view); the choice is for the design turn.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2107ZQ0001",
