    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D213VSR8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D213VSR8001:links:Link:00D213W4P8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D212HGJ0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D213VSR8001:links:Link:00D213W4P8000:props:StringProp:00D213W4PG000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "144"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The user: update the graph and `Apeiron/README.md` as listed** (2026-10-12, user)",
    "text": "**The user: update the graph and `Apeiron/README.md` as listed** (2026-10-12, user): \"Yep, update the graph & README.md like that.\" The list is [the orchestrator's answer](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D212HGJ0001): correct the stale statements about `free()`, record the landing (`d794060` and its results) in `issues/` and `history/`, and fix the other discrepancies the Grapher found, including `Apeiron/README.md` (which still describes a TerminusDB mirror). Routing decided by the orchestrator: the graph corrections go to the Grapher now; `Apeiron/README.md` is a plain file in the graph repo and not a graph node, so it is a file edit for the Coder, queued after the Coder's running usage census so that the Coder's thread has one Ask at a time. No issue is marked resolved by this turn: the live evidence is one sample and the memory trend over a longer run is unknown.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D215AJ1G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D215AJ1G001",
    "text": "**The user: use the Grapher's correction turn to check performance** (2026-10-12, user): \"Let's use this Grapher turn to check for performance.\" Orchestrator's setup, passive so that the turn itself is unchanged: a sampler outside the agents records the service process (pid 374326, started 08:15:59 UTC on the `free()` code) every 10 s into `/tmp/perf_samples.csv` (time, resident memory, CPU share since start, system load); the Grapher's per-command durations are read afterwards from its transcript timestamps and its thread; the turn's own writes are the load (dozens of `update` and `insert` on `issues/core.md`, `design/testing.md` and `history/`, projections, a link check). Limits stated in advance: the Coder's usage census runs at the same time on the same machine (it does not use the service but takes CPU), so timings include that load; the Freeflow entry recording this is one more write in the window; the figures are one run. Baseline just before the turn's first edits: 399 MB resident at 09:31:18 UTC (424 MB at the end of the earlier live check, 379 MB at start).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Landed** (2026-10-12)",
    "text": "**Landed** (2026-10-12): the patch is commit `d794060`, \"Fix core performance 2\", on branch `oxigraph`: the read helper of the map above, in the form the Coder recommended (plain-value reads, write helpers that take strings, `predIri` cached per field name), with the two rules that every wasm object is freed at the root as soon as its value is read and that no shared constant is freed. The whole-store `free()` on reload, the plain JavaScript engine and new `TreeView` fields stay out, as the user's plan says.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D1DP5GG0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0002",
      "BlockNode:00D216W8Z8000",
      "BlockNode:00D216W8Z8001",
      "BlockNode:00D216W8Z8002",
      "BlockNode:00D216W8Z8003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0002:props:StringProp:00D216WKVR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0002:props:StringProp:00D216WKVR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**What changed**",
    "text": "**What changed**: ten files, two of them new: `storeAccess.ts` in `core` (250 lines) and its test `store/storeAccess.test.ts`, named in the `commit` suite; the converted sites are in `node.ts`, `dehydrate.ts`, `store.ts`, `tree.ts`, `threadScope.ts`, `artifacts.ts` and `vocab.ts`. Every read returns plain strings, numbers, booleans or rows; `eachMatch` frees each quad after its callback and the argument terms in a `finally`; `deleteMatches` frees a quad only after deleting it; `addQuad` frees the quad and its terms. `decodeLiteral` also frees the datatype term it made.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Checked by the orchestrator**",
    "text": "**Checked by the orchestrator**: the diff scope and `storeAccess.ts` read in full; `npm run verify -- --suite essential` 27 of 27 in 3.2 s and `npm run verify` (the `commit` suite) 61 of 61 in 19.5 s; `tsc --noEmit` on `core` and `cli` printed nothing. The `demand` suite and the legacy suite were not run.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D216W8Z8001",
    "text": "**Measured on a copy of the mirror** (the Coder; production code, 59,275 quads, 3 samples per case, a fresh process each, `gc()` between calls): eight `allIdsOfKind` calls went from 349 to 634 MB (+40 MB a call) to flat at about 265 MB (264.6 to 264.7), and from 0.7 to 1.7 s a call to 0.14 to 0.23 s; a dehydrate of both mirrors went from 33 to 38 s to 2.5 to 3.8 s, with resident size from about 770 to about 350 MB; a rehydrate went from 3.3 to 3.5 s to 2.0 to 2.9 s, with resident size from about 300 to about 258 MB. The dehydrated directories are byte-identical before and after in all three repetitions.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Live check** (2026-10-12, the orchestrator; one sample each)",
    "text": "**Live check** (2026-10-12, the orchestrator; one sample each): `aperas service restart` found no service running and started one; the service answered 17 s after its process started (08:15:59 to 08:16:16 UTC) at 379 MB resident, where a fresh service had idled at 1.05 to 1.5 GB. `aperas tree --view` took 0.57 s; `aperas check-links` over 6,900 live blocks took 2.7 s twice, clean, with the service at 375 to 379 MB across both scans. The second insert into the Freeflow document took 5.0 s and a projection of that document 2.4 s; resident size went from 379 MB at start through 392 and 413 MB after the two inserts to 424 MB after the projection.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Not shown**",
    "text": "**Not shown**: the trend of resident memory over a longer run (the rise of about 45 MB over five commands is one sample, not a trend); the write paths of ingest, `remove` and the tombstone sweeps, which tests cover but no measurement or live run exercised; the gain split by site; and whether releasing the old `Store` on reload is a gain or a hazard. The test-only reads in `verifyDanglingUnfolds.ts` and `verifyLinkRefusals.ts` still read raw quads without `free()`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D216W8Z0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
