    ],
    "title": "**The change**",
    "text": "**The change**: ten files, two of them new, `storeAccess.ts` in `core` (250 lines) and its test `store/storeAccess.test.ts`, named in the `commit` suite. Every read returns plain strings, numbers, booleans or rows; each quad is freed after its callback, each argument term in a `finally`, a deleted quad only after its delete, a quad added with its terms after the add; no shared constant is freed; `predIri` is cached per field name. The whole-store `free()` at reload, the plain JavaScript engine and new `TreeView` fields stayed out, as the user's plan says.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Checked by the orchestrator**",
    "text": "**Checked by the orchestrator**: the diff scope and `storeAccess.ts` read in full; `npm run verify -- --suite essential` 27 of 27 in 3.2 s; the `commit` suite 61 of 61 in 19.5 s; `tsc --noEmit` on `core` and `cli` printed nothing. The `demand` and legacy suites were not run.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D21FP4ZG006",
    "text": "**Measured on a copy of the mirror** (the Coder; production code, 59,275 quads, 3 samples per case, a fresh process each, `gc()` between calls): eight `allIdsOfKind` calls went from 349 to 634 MB (+40 MB a call) to flat at about 265 MB, and from 0.7 to 1.7 s a call to 0.14 to 0.23 s; a dehydrate of both mirrors from 33 to 38 s to 2.5 to 3.8 s, with resident size from about 770 to about 350 MB; a rehydrate from 3.3 to 3.5 s to 2.0 to 2.9 s, with resident size from about 300 to about 258 MB. The dehydrated directories were byte-identical before and after in all three repetitions.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Live check** (2026-10-12, one sample each)",
    "text": "**Live check** (2026-10-12, one sample each): the service restarted onto the patch answered 17 s after its process started, at 379 MB resident. `aperas tree --view` took 0.57 s; `aperas check-links` over 6,900 live blocks took 2.7 s twice, clean, with the service at 375 to 379 MB across both scans; the second write into a document took 5.0 s and its projection 2.4 s; resident size went from 379 MB to 424 MB over five commands. In a later run of 12.6 minutes the service went from 390 to 424 MB resident over 63 samples, about 3 MB a minute with no plateau seen, measured from outside the agents; where the time between a write's CPU and its wall time goes, and whether the rise is garbage or retained data, were not decomposed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Not shown**",
    "text": "**Not shown**: the trend of resident memory over a longer run; the write paths of ingest, `remove` and the tombstone sweeps, which the tests cover and no measurement or live run exercised; the gain split by site; whether releasing the old `Store` at reload is a gain or a hazard. The test-only reads in `verifyDanglingUnfolds.ts` and `verifyLinkRefusals.ts` still read raw quads without `free()`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG009:links:Link:00D21FPK98000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D1GC79B8000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG009:links:Link:00D21FPK98000:props:StringProp:00D21FPK98001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "297"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "2026-10-12",
    "text": "2026-10-12: Oxigraph patching paused at branch `oxigraph`, the JavaScript ApeironNgn begun on branch `js-apeironngn`. The user's plan of 2026-10-11 was the `free()` helper as the best effort on Oxigraph and no more hacks on it, then a plain JavaScript engine to compare with the `free()` version; [the plan](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D1GC79B8000) is recorded with the scans issue, and the store being only an in-memory reflection of the mirror (the JSON-LD under `Apeiron/`, the source of truth) is why a replacement store does not hold the truth. The `free()` patch was committed as `d794060` on `oxigraph`, which stays as it is; `js-apeironngn` starts from the same commit, and `AperasKG` stays on `main`, shared by both branches. The two engines are to be compared on the same mirror, by their collections as sets and never list by list; Layers 1 to 3 and the pause stay parked on the Oxigraph side. What exists at this date is a survey of the contract the code asks of the store, with no engine code on the branch; no comparison has been measured.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D21FP4Z8005",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D39EQJY0000",
      "BlockNode:00D39EQJY0001",
      "BlockNode:00D39EQJY0002",
      "BlockNode:00D39EQJY0003"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21FVGF8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D21FVGF8001",
    "text": "**The user questions why the census covers flush hot spots** (2026-10-12, user): \"Hot spots at a flush: I don't understand why this section is surveyed? It looks like about performance, while we're checking the logic and structure of the graph.\" Orchestrator's answer and mistake: the census Ask asked for access patterns per predicate and what each full scan is used for, which is structure; the counts at a flush (7,276 `holder` probes of which 7,274 empty, `pathOf` 14,368 times, the Coder's 46% share of matches) are volume figures the Coder added as support, and the orchestrator then headlined them as \"hot spots\" in the summary and in the answer, which turned a structure survey into a performance one, against the user's \"facts first, layout later\". What the design needs from the census is the structure only: the closed predicate set, the reverse lookups on `parent`, `target`, `links`, `unfolds`, `profile`, `path`, `handle` and `name`, the scans and their purposes, the embed ownership and the order-sensitive reads. The volumes are performance evidence: they stay in the Coder's thread Report, belong to the issues if anywhere, and are not carried into `design/core.md`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
