  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21HY9ZR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D21HY9ZR001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's Turn 3 (`history/core.md`, four statements; Report `BlockNode:00D21HEJKR005`) and the Coder's Turn 3 (`Apeiron/README.md`)** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): `history/core.md` exists with a Current Status and three milestones in date order (`0a7a226` on 2026-10-11, `d794060` and the branch split on 2026-10-12); I read the milestone for `0a7a226`, the tree of the document and one corrected statement (`00D1EZSCH8001`, still \"explained, in part measured, not proved\"), and `aperas check-links` is clean over 7,170 live blocks (1,555 with link text). The README: I read it in full and ran a script over the real `Apeiron/BlockNode.jsonld`: all 4,531 documents follow the key order the README states, all carry `children`, all are sorted by `@id`, and `Apeiron/.state/` is git-ignored (`.gitignore` line 4); the TerminusDB and `kg:export`/`kg:import` text is gone and `design/core.md` is not named; the writers paragraph (the `--flush`, `aperas flush`, timer and shutdown triggers, `reload --discard`, `flush --clobber`) is the Coder's reading of the code and was not re-verified. The Coder could not write its Work and Report: it read \"No ApeironNgn service running\" as a crash, but the service (pid 374326, up 1 h 40 min, 410 MB, running) was busy with the Grapher's writes, the known case in which a live service refuses commands; a 40 s bounded probe did not answer either, and nothing was restarted or killed. Open items from the two reports: (1) the Coder's turn has an Ask and no Work or Report, so a follow-up turn records them and drops \"(an Oxigraph store)\" from README line 3, because the graph repo is shared by both branches and the sentence stops being true if the JS engine lands; (2) the Grapher found two more kinds of stale statement: Layer 2's \"To check first\" (`00D0Y9HG30007`) says the write helpers are not built although `addQuad` and `deleteMatches` exist, and the three Resolved entries of the doubled-anchor issue (`00D1BB69AG001`, `00D1BB9VE8001`, `00D1BBAN3G001`) say the code is \"staged and not committed\" although `0a7a226` carries it. The user's \"update the staled ones\" is taken to cover these two kinds (orchestrator's reading, open to the user's correction); both turns run in parallel.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21JKMR8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D21JKMR8001",
    "text": "**The user: the stall is still there after `free()`; what is the service running for?** (2026-10-12, user, on the 40 s probe that was refused while the Grapher worked): \"What's it running for? Such a stall is still there after `free()` applied ... that's frustrating!\" Orchestrator's answer, in order of what is known. Known: the service handles every request, pings included, through one queue, so a ping waits behind the running operation, and a client whose ping is not answered in time prints \"No ApeironNgn service running\" while the process is alive (the open issue `00CVMPABY8001`); during the Grapher's turn the service had a write queued almost continuously (77 tool calls in 12.6 minutes, each write 3.5 to 3.8 s wall, inserts with a link about 5 s, `check-links` 2.9 s, projections 2 to 3 s), so every probe landed behind one. Not known: what a write spends its 3.5 s on, because `service.log` records only startup sweeps and shutdown flush refusals and the per-operation timing instrumentation planned earlier (queue wait, operation, flush, post-check) was never built. Measured while the service was idle, one sample each: a read (`aperas show`) 0.59 and 0.62 s, an empty `aperas flush` 0.37 to 0.40 s, 0.24 s of service CPU over those five commands, so the CLI round trip is about 0.4 to 0.6 s and is not the cost of a write. Hypothesis to test: a write with `--flush` is the operation plus a dehydrate of the whole class files (measured on a copy at 2.5 to 3.8 s after `free()`), which would match 3.5 s per write. This entry is itself the test: it is inserted without `--flush`, timed, and the flush after it is timed separately; the result is in the next entry.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21JT8BR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D21JT8BR001",
    "text": "**Result of the stall test: the write itself costs about 3.8 s and 4.4 s of CPU; the flush hypothesis is refuted** (2026-10-12, orchestrator, one sample): the entry above was inserted without `--flush` while the service was idle: the insert took 3.84 s wall and the service used 436 CPU ticks (4.36 s; more than the wall time, so the collector's helper threads were working) for it, with the output printed only when it finished. The `aperas flush` that followed 0.7 s later was refused with \"No ApeironNgn service running\", having cost 76 ticks (0.76 s) of CPU: the service went on working after it had answered the insert. So the cost is in the operation and what follows it, not in the dehydrate on `--flush`, and the service is not idle when the command returns. Candidates from the census (item 6) that run on a write and scan every block, none of them verified as the cause: `buildLiveSlugPathIndex` (`node.ts:959`, all `BlockNode` ids on every write that checks path collisions), and the post-write sweeps `tombstoneVacuousContainers` (`node.ts:2218`, at least two passes), `pruneStaleUnfolds` and `pruneUnreachableTombstones`. The next step is a profile of one insert on a copy of the mirror (a CPU profile of the production insert, no source edit), which names the functions; queued as a Coder turn after the README follow-up so that the thread has one Ask at a time. The two Turn 4 agents (Grapher's two stale kinds, Coder's README record) are dispatched now.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21MFFE8000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D21MFFE8000",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's Turn 4 (two further kinds of stale statement; Report `BlockNode:00D21M359R008`)** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): I read three of the corrected entries and they say what the report says: `00D0Y9HG30007` (the write helpers `addQuad` and `deleteMatches` exist and keep no index, so the index and its comparison with a full scan are still not built or run), `00D1BB69AG001` (the doubled-anchor fix is carried by commit `0a7a226` on branch `oxigraph`) and `00D1BBAN3G004` (the fixture for a link to a nonexistent id is carried by the commit as step 5g and `tests/links/refusals.test.ts`; the tombstoned-target case stays uncovered); `aperas check-links` is clean over 7,200 live blocks (1,563 with link text); no issue was resolved; `AperasKG` is staged (eleven files, among them `Apeiron/README.md` and the new `history/core.md`). The Grapher's two questions, answered by the orchestrator and open to the user's correction: (1) `00D1BB69AG007` still says the full `npm run verify` \"waits on the verify emergency\", which was resolved later (`00D1EGQDN8001`): correct it in the next Grapher turn, since it is the same kind of stale statement; (2) `0a7a226` is an ancestor of `main`, `oxigraph` and `js-apeironngn`, so \"on branch `oxigraph`\" is true but not exclusive: harmless, and the commit hash is the durable reference.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
