    ],
    "title": "00D21P0ZP8001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Coder's Turn 4 (README follow-up; Report `BlockNode:00D21NMNCR00A`) and the next step on the stall** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): README line 3 no longer says \"(an Oxigraph store)\"; the Coder re-checked every other sentence against the code and the files (key order with 0 violations over all 4,620 real documents, sort order, indent, `.state/` and its git-ignore, the writers paragraph against `service.ts`) and a rehydrate then dehydrate of a copy of the real mirror in `/tmp` returned all four files byte for byte; the README is staged. It left out of the writers paragraph that `aperas reload` flushes pending changes before it rehydrates (incomplete, not false), and two smaller facts (the timer is 10 s for these files and 3 s for `.state/`; a refused flush at shutdown loses the pending changes); the orchestrator's view is to add the `reload` clause the next time the README is touched, not in a turn of its own. The stall: the user asked what the service runs for ([the test](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D21JT8BR001): one insert 3.84 s wall and 4.36 s of service CPU, then a refused flush 0.7 s later). The orchestrator opens a Coder task `write-cost-profile`: a CPU profile of one insert through the production code on a copy of the mirror, to name the functions behind the 3.8 s and what the service does after it has replied; investigation only, no source edit, no service restart.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21QR118001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D21QR118001",
    "text": "**The user: does the survey confirm \"technically triples, practically pairs\"?** (2026-10-12, user). Orchestrator's answer from the census Report (`BlockNode:00D214DTAR007`; read, with the structure points checked against `shape.ts` and `dehydrate.ts` earlier): yes, with four qualifications. For: the predicate set is closed (27 predicates, each a shape field or `parent`/`__siblingIndex`, none outside them in the mirror or in production code); the fourth position is never used; every shape field is read with its predicate bound and its subject bound (S,P,*); reverse lookups bind the predicate and the object on eight predicates only (`parent`, `target`, `links`, `unfolds`, `profile`, `path`, `handle`, `name`); the forms (*,P,*), (*,*,O) and (S,*,O) never occur in production, so the predicate is a constant at every call site. Qualifications: (1) two access forms leave the predicate unbound: `nodeExists`, an (S,*,*) match at 18 call sites that only takes `.length`, and the full scans (*,*,*) behind `allSubjectIds` and `allObjectRefIds`; both are node-level questions (does a record exist, which ids exist) and become direct in a pairs engine; (2) the closure is a property of the data and of the callers, not enforced: `encodeDoc` and `mintEmbedded` write any key they are given as a predicate, and the tests use made-up predicates (`count`, `flag`) and a foreign-subject quad on scratch stores, so an engine that rejects unknown fields changes behaviour and some tests; (3) pairs are not all single-valued: `set` fields hold several objects in an order (newest first, decided), and the `embed` fields own their children; (4) containment is written twice in the mirror (the owner's `children` and the child's `parent`), so loading must accept a repeated pair. The conclusion is a statement for `design/core.md`: the store holds a closed vocabulary of fields, each with a fixed cardinality and storage kind, and the engine's job is those fields' lookups in both directions.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D21TBXT0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D21TBXT0001",
    "text": "**The user asks what the `set` fields are used for** (2026-10-12, user, on the orchestrator's \"pairs are not all single-valued\"): answered from `shape.ts` (lines 52-54, 107-110, 127-131, 143-146) and a count over the real mirror files (`Apeiron/` and `.state/`, one pass, one moment). `set` fields in the code: `links` and `props` on every node (`BASE_NODE_SHAPE`, embed), `Link.props` (embed), `Profile.preferences` (embed) and `TreeView.unfolds` (reference); the fourth cardinality, `orderedContainment`, is `children`, which the orchestrator left out of its list of three (`one`, `optional`, `set`). In the mirror: `BlockNode.links` 1,565 blocks with 2,551 `Link` nodes (at most 68 on one block); `BlockNode.props` 1,598 blocks with 3,128 `StringProp` nodes (at most 3), whose keys are `orderedList` and `startIndex` (1,518 each, the list-structure facts of list items) and `checked` (92, task-list items); `Link.props` 2,553 links with 2,558 props, all `position` (the offsets of the occurrences of the target in the block's text; at most 2); `ArtifactNode.props` 52 and `FolderNode.props` 6, all `description`; `ArtifactNode.links` 2; `TreeView.unfolds` 17 views with 350 unfolded ids (at most 53); `Profile.preferences` none. So the fields that really hold several values are `BlockNode.links`, `BlockNode.props`, `Link.props` (only two `position` values at most) and `TreeView.unfolds`; every other `set` holds zero or one value in practice.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D220D77R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D220D77R001:links:Link:00D220DK7G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D21QR118001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D220D77R001:links:Link:00D220DK7G000:props:StringProp:00D220DK7G001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "925"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D220D77R001",
    "text": "**The user: a `set` is the objects of one subject, reciprocal with the backlinks of one object; that is the basic of the pair** (2026-10-12, user). Orchestrator's restatement, for the user to confirm or correct: a field is a binary relation between subjects and objects; its forward form is the set of objects per subject and its reverse form is the set of subjects per object (the backlinks), the two being the same pairs seen from either end; `one` and `optional` are the same relation constrained to at most one object per subject, not a different kind of field; `orderedContainment` (`children`) is a relation whose forward set is ordered (stored as `parent` on the child plus its `__siblingIndex`); a literal object (a string, number or boolean) is an object like any other, so a reverse lookup on a literal (`path`, `handle`, `name`) is the same reverse form keyed by the value. The orchestrator's qualification (3) in [the confirmation](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D21QR118001), that pairs are not all single-valued, is withdrawn: many-valued is the general case of a pair, not an exception. What stays open for the design: which relations keep their reverse form stored (the census found reverse lookups on eight: `parent`, `target`, `links`, `unfolds`, `profile`, `path`, `handle`, `name`) and which have none because nothing asks for it; and whether the set is ordered (newest first, as decided) or unordered with the order imposed by its consumers (the order-sensitive reads are `links`, `props`, `unfolds` and the `position` comparison).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2248STR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2248STR001:links:Link:00D22499J0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D20RN0EG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2248STR001:links:Link:00D22499J0000:props:StringProp:00D22499J0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "584"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2248STR001",
    "text": "**The user: the interface is an unordered `set`; \"newest first\" is only the engine's implicit, natural order** (2026-10-12, user): \"The interface is `set`, i.e. unordered. That's the standard, the user must order themselves, not rely on the 'natural order' provided by the graph engine. What we agree for the 'newest first' order is only the implicit and natural order within the engine, isn't it? I think the unorderedness is due to the nature of map, isn't it?\" Orchestrator's reading and answer, for the user to confirm: the earlier decision (\"keep Oxigraph's newest-first order\", [the decisions](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D20RN0EG001)) is amended from a contract to a description: the engine's lookups return an unordered collection; newest first is what Oxigraph happens to return and the JS engine need not reproduce it; any reader that needs an order sorts for itself. On \"the nature of map\": partly. The unordered contract is a choice of the interface; the structures underneath do have an order (a JS `Map` or `Set` iterates in insertion order, which is oldest first, the opposite of Oxigraph's newest first), so without an explicit sort the two engines would differ, which is the reason for the contract. Consequences, from the census: `children` is already ordered by data (`__siblingIndex`); `dehydrate` sorts documents and set fields itself, so the mirror does not depend on the order; the readers that do depend on it and must sort are the `aperas backlinks` output, the profile views list, the accessors for `links`, `props` and `unfolds`, and the `position` comparison (a different order there only triggers a rewrite of those props, never a wrong value); node ids are issued in increasing order in time (observed in this session, not verified as a guarantee), so sorting by id gives a stable order that does not depend on any engine. The differential test against Oxigraph then compares collections as sets and ignores the 2 of about 2,900 lists whose order the Coder could not explain.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D227G9CG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D227G9CG001:links:Link:00D227GMNG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D21JT8BR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D227G9CG001:links:Link:00D227GMNG000:props:StringProp:00D227GMNG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "2272"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D227G9CG001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Coder's profile of one insert (`write-cost-profile` Turn 1, Report `BlockNode:00D2270QXG000`): what the service runs for** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): I checked in the code that `rejectSlugPathCollisions` is called on the write path (`node.ts:822`), that `buildLiveSlugPathIndex` (`node.ts:957`) is documented as \"a fresh full scan\" with no cheaper index, that `toPath` (`node.ts:430`) walks the `parent` chain of each block without a cache, and that `service.ts:443` sets a `flushTimer` by `setInterval`; I did not rerun the profile. The Coder's figures (3 fresh processes, 6 inserts per cell, scratch copy of the mirror, one inserted paragraph of about 600 characters, no `--flush`): the handler blocks the service for 3.2 s on `discussion/aperas-skill.md` (CPU 3.85 s) and 1.6 s on `planning/core.md`: `rejectSlugPathCollisions` 1.56 s on both (it follows the store, not the artifact: `toPath` for all 7,433 live blocks, about 170,000 `store.match` lookups and 72,000 `wrap` calls), and two link-integrity sweeps of the target artifact, 0.83 s before and 0.78 s after the write on the big one and 24 and 18 ms on the small (they follow the artifact: every block's text parsed with remark each time); parse, hydrate and place cost 0 to 3 ms. After the reply the 10 s `flushTimer` finds `dirty` and runs `dehydrateToJsonLd`: 4 full scans, 334,000 quads, 1.40 s wall (15 flushes), also synchronous; the 3 s state timer does nothing for an `insert`. Why a request is refused meanwhile: not the queue: the handlers and the flush are fully synchronous, so the socket's `data` event is not delivered while they run, and a pinger saw one slow ping per busy window in all six runs. `tombstoneVacuousContainers`, `pruneStaleUnfolds` and `pruneUnreachableTombstones` do not run on a write (startup, `reload`, `flush --clobber`, shutdown only), which corrects the orchestrator's earlier candidate list. The `free()` patch is not a cause of the remaining cost (98 ms of 3,034 ms); with it disabled the second insert in one process took 31 to 33 s after 180 to 195 major collections and resident memory reached 1.1 to 1.3 GB, so `free()` removed the catastrophic stall and what is left is the access pattern of a write. The flush-hypothesis of the orchestrator ([the test](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D21JT8BR001)) was half right: the timed flush is a real second block of 1.4 s, but it is not the cost of the write, and it falls within 10 s of the write at a point set by the timer, which is why the refused flush came 0.7 s after the insert. The Coder's proposals, none done: do not rebuild the slug-path index on every write, make `toPath` cheap in bulk, sweep only the written subtree or cache the per-text parse, dehydrate only the changed class files, and add per-task timing. Its two questions: the thread-write gap (an insert under `.threads/coder.md` followed by `--flush` and `aperas flush` wrote nothing to `.state`, because only `dirty` is set and `stateDirty` is not; one scratch sample, the known open gap) and the live service version (it started at 08:15 UTC, after the `free()` commit, so it runs the patch; the later fingerprint is only edits and the branch checkout since).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22KKP7G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22KKP7G001",
    "text": "**The user: keep JS's natural oldest-first order; no one-to-one comparison with Oxigraph; the engine supports reversal in general** (2026-10-12, user): the user learnt that a JS `Map` or `Set` iterates in insertion order, which is the reverse of Oxigraph's; says \"all the order-sensitive fields have been sorted already\", so the engine keeps JS's natural oldest-first order; the JS engine will not be compared one to one with the Oxigraph version (or, if needed, sorted before comparing); and on which reverse views are stored: \"the engine should support reversal in general, then the choice is on the user side only\". Orchestrator's check of the first claim against the census, item 7 (`BlockNode:00D214DTB000Y` and its children): it does not show that all order-sensitive reads sort. Sorted already: `children` (`orderedChildIds` by `__siblingIndex`), the mirror (`dehydrate.ts:58,106` sort what they write), `project.ts:68-70` (by key; ties in match order). Reading in match order, so the order of today's output would flip with the engine's order: the rendering of `links` (`node.ts:1745,1882`, `kgUnfold.ts:109-115`), `toReconcileShape` (`node.ts:527`), `kgLink.ts:54` (writes back in the same order), the order of `view.unfolds` in `buildRenderTree` (`node.ts:1976`) and in `kgProfile.ts:115`, the printed `backlinks` (`kgBacklinks.ts:32`, `kgProfile.ts:69`) and the `getProps(link,'position')` comparison (`positionsEqual`, only a rewrite). And a semantic case, not only display: first-match-wins lookups on `path`, `handle` and `name` (`tree.ts:21`, `node.ts:1397, 2013, 2022`) where duplicates exist: one path is used today by a tombstoned entry and a live one; newest first tends to return the live (newer) one, oldest first would return the tombstoned one unless the lookup filters live (not checked for each caller). Orchestrator's restatement of the second claim: every field is a relation with its forward and its reverse view by construction, the engine keeps no list of fields that have a reverse, and callers choose which they use; the cost (a second index per relation) is small at 60,000 quads, and building a reverse lazily on first use is an option for the design. Open for the user: for the readers above, accept the flipped order where it is only display, and add an explicit order or a live-first rule where the choice of node depends on it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22RPRK0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22RPRK0001",
    "text": "**The user questions three items of the order check: link rendering, the order of backlinks, and the first-match lookups** (2026-10-12, user): (1) \"the list of links below the `text` follows the exact order in the `text`, so they are sorted by link position, aren't they?\"; (2) \"I don't see any order [in printed lists], simply assuming unordered now. What's the meaning for 'order of backlinks'?\"; (3) the first-match lookups on `path`, `handle` and `name`: elaborate on what they are for, without code lines. Orchestrator's checks and answers. (1) Tested on a real block with three links (`BlockNode:00CDBZ7698002`, whose text names its three targets in the order `00CDBYV4T8002`, `00CDBYV4T8007`, `00CDBYV5A8002`): the tree view (`aperas unfold`) lists them in the reverse order (`...JR003`, `...JR001`, `...JG000`), the newest link first, so the display follows the engine's order and not the text; the stored mirror lists them oldest first. One block, one view; other views (the projected markdown, the web view) were not checked. The `position` prop that every wikilink carries (the offset of its occurrence in the text) would give the text order. (2) The order of backlinks is only the sequence of lines `aperas backlinks` prints; nothing reads it, so it carries no meaning and the engine's order only shuffles the lines. (3) Lookups by exact value, taking the first match: `path` finds the artifact or folder at a file path (resolving `design/linking.md` to its node; checking whether a live document already exists at a path before ingest or track), `handle` finds a profile (`aperas profile list claude`), `name` finds a view (`aperas tree --view claude-orchestrator-coder`), and the owner of a link is found the same way. `handle` and `name` are unique by design (the code refuses a duplicate view name) and the real mirror has no duplicates (17 views, none repeated; no repeated handle). `path` is unique among live nodes only, because a removed (tombstoned) node keeps its path; the mirror has exactly one repeated path, `issues/cli.md`: a tombstoned artifact (`ArtifactNode:00CF1TZQV8000`) and a live one (`ArtifactNode:00CF2AEA78000`, the newer). One of the lookups, `findLiveByKindAndPath`, skips the tombstoned; the plain one used by path resolution (`findByExactPath`) takes the first, which today is the newer (live) one under newest-first and would be the old tombstoned one under oldest-first, so that lookup needs a live-first rule when the engine changes its order.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22XK11R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22XK11R001:links:Link:00D22XKC48000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D22RPRK0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D22XK11R001:links:Link:00D22XKC48000:props:StringProp:00D22XKC48001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "161"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D22XK11R001",
    "text": "**The user: the link order is reverse; a lookup that returns the removed node is a bug; filing is a must, fixing waits for the design** (2026-10-12, user, after [the tested answers](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D22RPRK0001)): \"Oh, the link order is actually reverse!\" (confirming the tree view lists a block's links newest first, against the text order); \"Under oldest-first it would be the old removed one. If so, that's a bug! File is the must, always, but fixing anything is not now, we're designing.\" Orchestrator's reading: (1) everything found goes to `issues/` now, none of it is fixed during the design; (2) the lookup case is filed as a latent defect, not a visible one: today `findByExactPath` returns the newer live artifact for `issues/cli.md` only because the engine returns the newest first, so correct behaviour depends on the engine's order and nothing in the lookup says so; which callers can meet a tombstoned duplicate is not checked; (3) what is filed in the next Grapher turn: the profile of one insert (the cost per write and per timed flush, and that refused pings come from synchronous work and not from the queue), the thread-write gap (one sample), the correction of the orchestrator's earlier list of sweeps on a write, the reversed link display, the order-dependent path lookup, the stale smoke-test script `apeironNgnSmokeTest.ts` (asks for the dead predicate `__parent`, referenced by nothing), the two write paths that accept any key as a predicate, and the stale statement `00D1BB69AG007` that still says the full `npm run verify` waits on the verify emergency.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**One `insert` blocks the service for 1.6 to 3.2 s before its reply and for 1.4 s more after it, and none of it is the parse, the write or the dehydrate on `--flush`: it is a path index rebuilt over every live block, two link sweeps of the target artifact, and the timed dehydrate of all four content classes**",
    "text": "**One `insert` blocks the service for 1.6 to 3.2 s before its reply and for 1.4 s more after it, and none of it is the parse, the write or the dehydrate on `--flush`: it is a path index rebuilt over every live block, two link sweeps of the target artifact, and the timed dehydrate of all four content classes**: profiled 2026-10-12 by the Coder (`write-cost-profile`, Turn 1) on a scratch copy of the mirror, after the `free()` patch (commit `d794060`). Filed 2026-10-12.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8002",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG003",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG009",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00C",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR003",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR00A",
      "BlockNode:00D457M2F8001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8002:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XT0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8002:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XT0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D22Z1HJ8002",
    "text": "**Symptom**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8003",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG000",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG001",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8003:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XT0002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8003:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XT0003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D22Z1HJ8003",
    "text": "One inserted paragraph of about 600 characters, no `--flush`, on a copy of the mirror of 7,433 live blocks and 62,792 quads. The handler wall time before the reply was a median of 3,234 ms (3,096 to 3,295) on the 5,200-line `discussion/aperas-skill.md` and 1,572 ms (1,519 to 1,755) on the 37-line `planning/core.md`, with CPU of 3,849 and 1,697 ms. The CLI process wall was 3.79 s and 2.10 s, so the client's own start, stdin, ping, connect and print take about 0.55 s of it. Cells: 6 inserts in 3 fresh processes for the big artifact, 6 in 3 for the small one.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG000",
    "text": "The single sample of the stall test (3.84 s wall, 4.36 s of service CPU) reproduces: 3.52 to 3.63 s unprofiled and 3.71 to 3.76 s profiled, 2 inserts per cell, one process per cell.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG001",
    "text": "The reply is sent only after the second link sweep, because the handler defers nothing; the reply is 167 bytes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG002",
    "text": "After the reply the 10 s timer finds the store dirty and runs `dehydrateToJsonLd`: 1,401 ms wall (1,367 to 1,469, 6 flushes after big inserts), 1,383 ms after small inserts and 1,399 ms after inserts with a link. It comes 0 to 10 s after the write, by the timer's phase. Service CPU over the 14 s after an insert was 158 to 218 ticks (median 177; 19 inserts in 11 processes unprofiled), which is this flush.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG003",
    "text": "**Root cause** (profile and counts of the Coder; line numbers are declaration lines in the source, because the profiler reports line 1 or 2 for TypeScript run through `tsx`)",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG004",
      "BlockNode:00D39EVFH8001",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG005",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG006",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG007",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG008"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG004:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XT0004",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG004:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XT0005",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The path index follows the store, not the artifact**",
    "text": "**The path index follows the store, not the artifact**: `rejectSlugPathCollisions` (`node.ts:986`) calls `buildLiveSlugPathIndex` (`node.ts:957`), which the code itself documents as a fresh full scan, on every write. It took a median of 1,556 ms on the big artifact and 1,527 ms on the small one. Inside it: `liveBlockNames` 1,247 ms, `toPath` (`node.ts:430`) 1,125 ms, which walks the `parent` chain of each block up to its artifact with no cache (about 8.7 `parent` reads per live block), and `allIdsOfKind` 144 ms. Counts for one insert: one full scan (62,792 quads materialised), about 170 thousand bound `store.match` lookups at about 9 microseconds each, and 91,224 `wrap` calls on the big artifact (72,088 on the small one).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG005:links:Link:00D22Z1XVG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CVQHBP0R001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG005:links:Link:00D22Z1XVG000:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XVG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "436"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The two link sweeps follow the artifact, not the store**",
    "text": "**The two link sweeps follow the artifact, not the store**: `checkArtifactLinkIntegrity` (`linkIntegrity.ts:131`) ran 831 ms before and 780 ms after the operation on the big artifact, and 24 and 18 ms on the small one. About 1,070 ms of the 1,574 ms in the profile is `collectLinkCodesFromText` (`astParser.ts:517`): the remark parse of every block's text, run for every block of the artifact twice, with no cache. This is the cost the [per-write re-parse issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHBP0R001) names.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The timed flush follows the store**",
    "text": "**The timed flush follows the store**: 1.4 s for both artifacts. In the 14 s window after an insert (3 profiles, 42 s sampled) 88.7 percent of the samples were idle and `flushIfDirty` was 3,988 ms inclusive, i.e. nothing else; inside it `allIdsOfKind` 1,651 ms (4 full scans, 334,075 quads materialised), `serializeDoc` 1,331 ms and `isThreadScopedId` 847 ms (per block `owningArtifactOrFolderId` and `firstRefIriOf`). It dehydrates all four content classes after an insert, which changes `BlockNode` and at most `ArtifactNode`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG007:links:Link:00D22Z1XVR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CVNZXVR8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG007:links:Link:00D22Z1XVR000:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XVR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "269"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG007",
    "text": "The parse of the new text, the hydration, `resolveBlockLinks`, the placement and the line numbering cost 0 to 3 ms each; a link in the text adds 1 to 2 ms. The `setInterval` of 3 s (`flushStateIfDirty`) does nothing after an insert, as an insert sets only `dirty` (see [the thread-write issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVNZXVR8001)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG008",
    "text": "Garbage collection is 3 to 5 percent of the wall time (156 collections in an insert on the big artifact, 114 ms of pause); the CPU time above the wall time is V8's helper threads.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG009",
    "text": "**What it is not**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00A",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00B"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00A:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XT8000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00A:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XT8001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "The `free()` patch is not the cause",
    "text": "The `free()` patch is not the cause: `freeWasm` costs 98 ms of the 3,034 ms handler (3.2 percent). With `freeWasm` made a no-op, the first insert in a fresh process was about the same (3,566 to 3,635 ms big, 1,840 to 1,953 ms small), but the second insert in the same process took 31.5 to 33.3 s on the big artifact (up to 12 s on the small one) after 180 to 195 major collections, with 1.12 to 1.29 GB resident against 432 to 462 MB with the patch, and the timer flush after it 19 to 25 s. So the patch holds the cost at the level above and what is left is the access pattern it cannot touch: one `store.match` and one new wasm term for every field read, repeated about 170 thousand times, plus 7,433 uncached ancestor walks.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG009",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG00B",
    "text": "The timer flush is not the cost of the write, but a second block of its own that falls within 10 s of it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG009",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00C",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG00C",
    "text": "**Not proven**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00D",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00E",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR000",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR001",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR002"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00D",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00D:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XTG000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00D:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XTG001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "That the index build is linear in the store",
    "text": "That the index build is linear in the store: the store size was not varied, so the claim is read from the code (one `wrap` and one ancestor walk per live block).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJG00E",
    "text": "The 0.55 s of the CLI process was not split further (one sample set; an earlier read of 0.6 s agrees).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "The live service was not profiled",
    "text": "The live service was not profiled: it reported \"Running code is stale\" and the Coder did not touch it. Its resident size after a day near 450 MB (patched) or above 1 GB would show which code it runs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJR001",
    "text": "Only `insert` was profiled. `update`, `remove` and `retype` share the two sweeps, and whether they share the path index was not measured.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJR002",
    "text": "Counters and timers added about 6 percent to the wall time, the profiler about 4 percent.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJG00C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJR003",
    "text": "**Fix directions**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XTR000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XTR001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D22Z1HJR004",
    "text": "None decided. The Coder's proposals, each of which changes behaviour, listed as candidates and none tried:",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR003",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR005",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR006",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR007",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR008",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR009"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR005:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XV0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR005:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XV0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "Do not rebuild the index on every write",
    "text": "Do not rebuild the index on every write: look up the candidate names of the new subtree directly (an index query by path), or keep the index across writes and invalidate it on the write itself. The name set is the path plus embedded anchors, and whether anchors are global, which decides whether a per-artifact index is enough, could not be told from the code.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "Make `toPath` cheap in bulk",
    "text": "Make `toPath` cheap in bulk: memoise the ancestor title chain, or compute the index in one top-down walk instead of one bottom-up walk per block.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "For the link sweeps",
    "text": "For the link sweeps: sweep only the written subtree, or cache `collectLinkCodesFromText` per block text, since the before and after sweeps parse the same unchanged text twice.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR008:links:Link:00D22Z1XVR002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CVMPABY8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR008:links:Link:00D22Z1XVR002:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XVR003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "217"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "Do not run `dehydrateToJsonLd` on the loop in one piece",
    "text": "Do not run `dehydrateToJsonLd` on the loop in one piece: write only the class files a write changed, or chunk it so the loop can answer; and let `ping` answer while the service is busy (see the refusal measured under [the spurious \"not running\" issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVMPABY8001)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR009:links:Link:00D22Z1XVR004",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00CVQHD7CR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR009:links:Link:00D22Z1XVR004:props:StringProp:00D22Z1XVR005",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "139"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D22Z1HJR009",
    "text": "If none of that is wanted, log the per-task timing; the Coder's instrumented `enqueue` is a model of 15 lines. This is the coverage gap of [the no-coverage issue](#id/BlockNode:00CVQHD7CR001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR00A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z1HJR00A",
    "text": "**Related**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR00B",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR00C"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR00B",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJR00B:links:Link:00D22Z1XVR006",
