    ],
    "title": "**Since measured (2026-10-12), the mechanism refined, not resolved**",
    "text": "**Since measured (2026-10-12), the mechanism refined, not resolved**: the Coder's profile of one insert ([the cost-of-a-write issue](#id/BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001)) tested the mechanism under Root cause on an instrumented scratch service, with a pinger sending a ping every 50 ms (the client's own timeout is 300 ms).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVMPABY8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0002",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0003",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0004",
      "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0005"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0002:props:StringProp:00D22Z53V0000",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0002:props:StringProp:00D22Z53V0001",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Measured**",
    "text": "**Measured**: in the two runs whose events were logged (12 tasks over 1 s) no request's `data` event was logged inside any task window; a ping sent in a window was logged as received 0 to 60 ms after the window ended and answered 0.4 to 2.2 ms after receipt. In all six runs (3 on a big artifact, 3 on a small one) the pinger saw exactly one slow ping per busy window, of 1.3 to 4.7 s.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**What it shows**",
    "text": "**What it shows**: a request is refused while the service is busy not because of the queue but because the work is synchronous. `runInsert`, `checkArtifactLinkIntegrity` and `dehydrateToJsonLd` never yield to the event loop, so the socket is not read at all while one runs, and a ping never reaches the queue. The queue would add ordering only if the handlers yielded, and they do not. This answers the first item under Not proven: a ping does not wait in the queue for part of its 300 ms, it is not read until the work ends.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Windows in which the service is unreachable to a ping**",
    "text": "**Windows in which the service is unreachable to a ping**: the whole handler of an insert (1.6 s on a small artifact, 3.2 s on a 5,200-line one), and again the timer's dehydrate (1.4 s) at a point set by the 10 s timer within 10 s of any write. The orchestrator's refused `aperas flush` 0.7 s after an insert (a stall test, one sample) fits such a window.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Not shown**",
    "text": "**Not shown**: the mechanism was shown for an insert and the timer flush on scratch services; the other verbs and the live service were not measured. Whether the retry of the \"Fix to try\" below would have to outlast a window of 1.4 to 3.2 s to help is a reading, not tested.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D22Z4RF0001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22Z96GG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D22Z96GG001",
    "text": "**The user asks for the remaining wrinkles of the order decision** (2026-10-12, user: \"Back to the order. What's the remaining wrinkles?\"). Orchestrator's list, from the census item 7 and the tests of today (verified where stated, inferred otherwise): (A) the readers whose visible output follows the engine's order: the tree view lists a block's links newest first today (tested on one block), the `view.unfolds` order in the render and in the profile print, and the printed backlinks; each needs a decision, accept the change or sort at the reader (`position` for links, id otherwise). (B) the engine's natural order is not durable: `dehydrate` writes set fields sorted by id, so after a restart the load order is id order, which for `unfolds` (plain node ids) is lexical and not the order of unfolding, while inside a session new entries append; inferred from the census (dehydrate sorts set fields), not tested after a restart. (C) first-match lookups: path (live-first rule; one reused path, `issues/cli.md`) and every single-valued read that takes the first match, which is safe only while cardinality holds (0 violations now, not enforced). (D) ties: children with equal `__siblingIndex` (0 now) and several props with the same key (4 Links carry two `position` values) have no defined order and need an id tie-break. (E) tests: any test that asserts today's newest-first order is unknown until the commit suite runs on the new engine; and `positionsEqual` compares a Link's `position` values index by index against an ascending list, so with newest-first storage the two-position Links may be rewritten on every ingest today (inferred, not tested).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D22ZC858001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Since sampled (2026-10-12), one sample, the gap is present on the current code**",
    "text": "**Since sampled (2026-10-12), one sample, the gap is present on the current code**: the Coder, while profiling an insert, used a plain scratch service on a copy of the mirror and ran `echo text | aperas insert BlockNode:00CR9ME608000 --flush` under a heading of `.threads/coder.md`, then `aperas flush`. `.state/BlockNode.jsonld` and `BlockNode.jsonld` were both unchanged (the string was in neither file after 6 s), although `aperas show` returned the new text. So neither `--flush` nor `aperas flush` wrote the thread node to `.state/`, which the agreed fix would make them do (part 1 above). The code reading agrees with Root cause: `insert` sets only `dirty`, `flushIfDirty` excludes thread-scoped nodes, and `flushStateIfDirty` runs only when `stateDirty` is set, which `unfold`, `fold`, `zoom` and the sweeps set. The sample was not repeated, and no other verb or thread file was tried.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CVNZXVR8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
