    ],
    "title": "Corrected in [the verification of the Coder's profile](#id/BlockNode:00D227G9CG001)",
    "text": "Corrected in [the verification of the Coder's profile](#id/BlockNode:00D227G9CG001): `tombstoneVacuousContainers`, `pruneStaleUnfolds` and `pruneUnreachableTombstones` do not run on a write (they run at startup, reload, `flush --clobber` and shutdown only), so the candidates listed above for what runs on a write were not the cost; the profile names the path index, the two link sweeps and the timed dehydrate instead.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2318FM8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2318FM8001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's filing of the profile and survey findings (`write-cost-profile` Turn 1, Report `BlockNode:00D230WVYG009`)** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): I read three of the filed issues and they say what the report says: the cost of a write (`00D22Z1HJ8001`: 1.6 to 3.2 s before the reply and 1.4 s after, a path index over every live block, two link sweeps and the timed dehydrate, profiled by the Coder after the `free()` patch), the reversed link display (`00D22ZK7C8001`: newest first, not by `position`) and the first-match path lookup (`00D22ZMTKG001`, filed as latent: it returns the live node only because the store lists the newest first). Five new issues in `issues/core.md` (the two above, the stale smoke test `00D22ZV0FG001`, the unenforced closed vocabulary `00D22ZWZMR001`, and the write cost), three children on existing issues (refused pings under `00CVMPABY8001`, the thread-write gap under `00CVNZXVR8001`, the per-write re-parse under `00CVQHBP0R001`) and the correction of `00D1BB69AG007` in `issues/linking.md`; nothing fixed or resolved; `aperas check-links` is clean over 7,381 live blocks; the code repo has nothing staged and the graph repo is staged. The Grapher's two questions, answered by the orchestrator and open to the user's correction: (1) repeating the thread-write sample with more runs is a Coder turn that changes nothing; the gap is already filed as one sample, so it waits until the fix is scheduled and is not needed for the design; (2) the display-order and lookup issues stay in `issues/`: when `design/core.md` records the order decisions (unordered set, explicit order at the readers, live-first lookups), the issues will cite it, because citations point up, and no issue moves under a design topic.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D235EAQR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D235EAQR001:links:Link:00D235EPD8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D22Z96GG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D235EAQR001:links:Link:00D235EPD8000:props:StringProp:00D235EPD8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "83"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D235EAQR001",
    "text": "**The user's decisions on the order wrinkles A to E** (2026-10-12, user, answering [the list](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D22Z96GG001)): (A) a link sorts by `position`, anything else by id: \"OK\". (B) the user doubts there is a way to list all `unfolds` pointers, and says the projected tree is constrained by the order of its branches (blocks and links). Orchestrator's check in the code: one command does list them, `aperas profile list-view <name>`, which prints one `unfolds: <id>` line per entry in the engine's order (diagnostic; sorting by id there is free); and `buildRenderTree` reads the unfolds only to learn which nodes and links are open, so the user's point holds for the order of the rendered tree, which follows the blocks and the links. Not checked: the render keeps a \"canonical\" record for a node reached through several unfolded links; if the choice of the canonical place follows the order of the unfolds list, that is an order dependence the census did not list, to be checked in the build, not now. (C) first-match lookups: \"only `path` is there and its current behavior is a bug! Path is meant to be unique, not first-match\": the filed issue `00D22ZMTKG001` was filed as latent and is a defect by this decision; the invariant is that a path names one live node; a tombstoned node keeps its path forever, so the rule must be in the lookup (live only) or in tombstoning (release the path): a design question for `design/core.md`. `handle` and `name` stay as they are (unique by design). (D) ties: \"just add them\": an id tie-break wherever equal keys occur (children with equal `__siblingIndex`, props with the same key). (E) tests: \"if anything emerge, check if it's a true bug or a wrong assumption, then fix it\": the rule for the build, when the commit suite runs on the new engine.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2392JYG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2392JYG001",
    "text": "**The user: `list-view` sorts by id; how does a tombstoned node keep its path, when the live set must be unique anyway?** (2026-10-12, user): `aperas profile list-view <name>` prints its `unfolds` sorted by id (decided). On the path: \"How does a tombstoned node keep its path? The live set must be unique anyway.\" Orchestrator's check in the code (`folders.ts:155-160`, `node.ts:850-879`): tombstoning a node only sets `tombstonedAt` and empties `children`, `links` and `props` (for a block also keeps its type, title and text); nothing clears `path` (or the hashes), so the old path stays on the dead node simply because no step removes it; the comment at `artifacts.ts:184-186` records it as a known consequence. The user's reasoning holds: the invariant is \"a path names one live node\", the tombstoned copy is harmless provided every lookup asks for the live node; the defect is the one lookup that does not (`findByExactPath`), as filed. Two ways to make it structural, for `design/core.md` to choose: (a) one primitive \"the live node at this path\" used by every caller, `findByExactPath` retired or made live-only; (b) tombstoning clears the path, which loses the record of where the removed node was (whether anything reads a removed node's path, for example in the removed-node display, was not checked). Orchestrator's leaning: (a), because (b) changes stored data and the engine stays free of any notion of \"live\".",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D23A9S48001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D23A9S48001",
    "text": "**The user is surprised that folders store their full path; had thought everything was unified to deep-path semantics** (2026-10-12, user). Orchestrator's check on the real mirror (`Apeiron/FolderNode.jsonld`, 16 folders; `ArtifactNode.jsonld`, 54 artifacts, one pass) and the code read earlier: a folder or an artifact stores its own segment as `title` (`design`, `linking.md`), its `parent`, and also the full `path` (`archive/design`, `design/linking.md`); the root folder stores `.`; of the 15 folders below the root, the 9 below `archive` or another subfolder have a path equal to the parent's path plus `/` plus the title, and the other 6 are the root's children, whose path is just their title, so every stored path equals the join of the titles along the parent chain. A block stores no path: `toPath` builds it from the nearest artifact's stored path plus the slugs of the titles above it. So the deep-path semantics are unified for resolving references (one grammar, `resolveDeepPath`), and for blocks in storage, but not for artifacts and folders, whose stored `path` is a redundant copy of what the parent chain already says; the copy serves as the key of the \"node at this path\" lookup and as the tie to the file on disk. Consequences for the design: dropping the stored path would make the lookup a walk down from the root by title, which is the resolver's own walk, and would make \"a path names one live node\" the same statement as \"live siblings under one parent have distinct titles\", moving the tombstone question to whether a removed node stays among its folder's children (not checked); keeping it leaves a second copy to rewrite for every descendant when a folder is renamed (not checked how rename is handled). Both go to `design/core.md` as an open point; nothing is changed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D23CR0MG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D23CR0MG001",
    "text": "**The user asks what the stored path is used for now** (2026-10-12, user). Orchestrator's answer from the callers in the code (`findByExactPath`: `resolve.ts:69`, `resolveCreate.ts:98`, `linkIntegrity.ts:323`, `kgProject.ts:46`; `findLiveArtifactByPath` and `findLiveByKindAndPath`: `artifacts.ts:67,194,210,311,736,789`, `kgIngest.ts:48`; `threadScope.ts:20`; `toPath`, `node.ts:430`) and the census: (1) finding the artifact or folder for a path typed by the user or found on disk: ingest and track ask \"is there a live artifact for this file?\" to choose between creating and updating; `aperas project` finds the artifact to project; link integrity finds the artifact of a link's path; the reference resolver anchors at the root folder (`.`) and checks how much of a `--create-holder` prefix chain exists; (2) the thread-scope test at every flush: a node is kept out of the content mirror and written to `.state/` when its owning artifact or folder has a path equal to `.threads` or starting with `.threads/`; for every block it climbs to the owner and reads that path (14,368 reads in one flush); (3) the deep path of a block, which starts from the stored path of its artifact and appends the slugs of the titles above it (display, link rendering, the collision check on write); (4) the tie to the file on disk: ingest reads, and projection writes, the file named by the path; (5) messages that name the node (for example \"would be tombstoned as removed\"); (6) a large part of the tests, which get the id of the artifact they built through the path. Of these, (1), (2) and (3) could be answered from the parent chain and the titles instead of a stored copy; (4) needs the path text, which the chain also gives; nothing found needs the stored copy itself. Not verified: every caller of the readers in the commands `kgTrack`, `kgRetype`, `kgUpdate`, `kgRemove` and `kgInsert`, which only count as readers in a grep.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D23JGNSR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D23JGNSR001",
    "text": "**The user: no duplicated stored path, to be refactored later; and how will the pairs be indexed?** (2026-10-12, user): \"I prefer not to store such a duped path, but it can be refactored later on. Now, after the order, I'm wondering about the index. How will the pairs be indexed?\" The path is decided as: derive it from the parent chain, as a later refactor, not part of the first engine. Orchestrator's proposal for the index, for discussion, nothing built (figures from the Coder's survey, `BlockNode:00D20GSWJG00F`, and the census, `BlockNode:00D214DTAR007`): (1) forward, per subject: all the pairs of one subject in one small list (a subject has 4 on average and 73 at most), answering \"the objects of field F of subject S\" by a scan of that list and \"all fields of S\" (the flush, `wrap`) by reading it whole; (2) reverse, per field: object to subjects, a plain list that becomes a set past about 32 entries (the largest bucket is 5,462), built lazily the first time a field is asked in reverse and kept up to date afterwards, so the engine supports reversal for every field without storing eight or twenty-seven of them up front; keys keep their JS type, so the string \"1\" and the number 1 stay different; (3) per kind, a set of ids, which answers `allIdsOfKind` (16 callers, each a full scan today) and existence; (4) writes: a field is cleared and rewritten (as `writeField` does today), a repeated add is ignored (containment is written twice in the mirror), and the reverse buckets of the old objects are updated; (5) no ordering promised, ids as the tie-break. Memory, from the Coder's prototypes of a triple-shaped store on 60,000 quads: flat lists cost about 149 bytes per quad (8.5 MB), nested maps and sets about 663 bytes (37.9 MB), so small lists, not a map and a set per subject and field. Open: eager or lazy reverse; whether a node exists by being in a registry or by having at least one pair (today: at least one); whether the engine enforces the cardinality and the closed vocabulary from the shapes or the layer above does; the choice between one list per subject (record) and one table per field, which the user postponed and which does not change the interface.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D23T2E50001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D23T2E50001:links:Link:00D23T2SN0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D23JGNSR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D23T2E50001:links:Link:00D23T2SN0000:props:StringProp:00D23T2SN0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "107"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D23T2E50001",
    "text": "**The user asks where the numbers in the index proposal come from, and why so many** (2026-10-12, user, on [the proposal](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D23JGNSR001): \"73 at most\", \"27\", \"about 32 entries\", \"5,462\"). Orchestrator's account, all from the Coder's survey entry for option (a) (`BlockNode:00D20GSWJG00F`) and the census, repeated without re-measuring: \"4 on average and 73 at most\" is the number of pairs per subject on the real data (mean 4.0, maximum 73), measured by the Coder on a copy of the mirror; \"5,462\" is the size of the largest reverse bucket on the same data (the bucket's key was not recorded in the entry); \"27\" is the number of predicates in the store in the census (the orchestrator's own count of the field names in `shape.ts` gave 26, not reproduced exactly); \"149 and 663 bytes per quad\" are the Coder's measurements of its two scratch prototypes. \"About 32\" is different: it is the Coder's threshold in its prototype for switching a list to a set, a tuning constant it chose, not a finding, and the orchestrator wrote it as if it were one. Mistake and proposal: the numbers are evidence about today's data and belong in the survey, not in the design; `design/core.md` should state rules (the forward pairs of a subject in one small list, because a subject has few; a reverse bucket in a set, because a bucket can hold thousands) and not carry the figures; the 32 threshold is dropped from the proposal (a reverse bucket is always a set, a forward list always a list), unless a measurement shows it is needed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D23XPKTG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D23XPKTG001",
    "text": "**The user asks what the largest reverse bucket (the \"5k\") is** (2026-10-12, user). Orchestrator's check on the real mirror files (`Apeiron/` and `.state/`, a quick script that counts, for each field and object, the subjects pointing at it; it counted 71,727 pairs against the Coder's 60,810 quads, so it does not reproduce the Coder's figure exactly: different moment, and the script does not merge repeats): the largest bucket is `type` = `listItem`, 5,881 subjects (the Coder's 5,462 is the same kind of bucket at its moment): every list-item block, most of them the bullets of the threads. Next: `key` = `position` (2,605 props), `predicate` = `[[wikilink]]` (2,600 links), `__siblingIndex` = 0 (1,826, every first child), `key` = `orderedList` and `startIndex` (1,556 each), `value` = `1` and `false` (about 1,550 each). All the big buckets belong to fields with few distinct values (a type, a key, a flag, an index) and none of them is among the eight fields the census found read in reverse (`parent`, `target`, `links`, `unfolds`, `profile`, `path`, `handle`, `name`). Consequence for the design: an eager reverse for every field would build and maintain thousands-strong buckets that nobody queries; a reverse built the first time a field is asked in reverse (lazy) never creates them. This supports the lazy choice; it is evidence, and the decision is the user's.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D23ZRYE8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D23ZRYE8001:links:Link:00D23ZS9S0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D23XPKTG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D23ZRYE8001:links:Link:00D23ZS9S0000:props:StringProp:00D23ZS9S0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "84"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D23ZRYE8001",
    "text": "**The user asks whether the 5.4k bucket is only theoretical** (2026-10-12, user, on [the bucket check](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D23XPKTG001)). Orchestrator's answer: the data is real (about 5,500 to 5,900 blocks of type `listItem` in the mirror); the bucket is an index structure, and it exists only where such an index is built. It was real in the Coder's prototype, which kept an object-first index for every predicate (the entry says the largest bucket \"is 5462, so a set matters for deletes\"), and Oxigraph keeps the same kind of entry in its own predicate-object index; in the design with a reverse built on first use it would not exist unless someone asked for all blocks of type `listItem` in reverse, which no production code does.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D241MMC0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D241MMC0001:links:Link:00D241MZTR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D23JGNSR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D241MMC0001:links:Link:00D241MZTR000:props:StringProp:00D241MZTR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "316"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D241MMC0001",
    "text": "**The user asks whether a bucket that large can be real for `allIdsOfKind`** (2026-10-12, user). Orchestrator's answer: yes, and it is the one large structure the design wants. A node's kind is not a stored pair; it is read from the prefix of its id (`nodeKindFromId`), so the per-kind set of ids (proposal point 3, [the index proposal](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D23JGNSR001)) is maintained when a node is created or removed, not derived from a field. The `BlockNode` set holds every block: `aperas check-links` scans about 7,400 live blocks (7,381 at the last run), more with the tombstoned ones; the `ArtifactNode`, `FolderNode`, `TreeView` sets are small (54, 16, 17 in the mirror). It replaces the 16 full scans that `allIdsOfKind` callers make today. It is a different thing from the `type = listItem` bucket: that one is a value of a stored field, queried by nobody; `allIdsOfKind` asks by kind (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`, ...) and never by block type. Not checked: how many of the callers filter out the tombstoned ids after the call, which decides whether a live-only set would also be worth keeping.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D244CZXG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D244CZXG001",
    "text": "**The user asks why `allIdsOfKind` exists when each kind is already stored in its own JSON-LD file** (2026-10-12, user, asking whether it is because Oxigraph has no notion of that separation). Orchestrator's answer: yes. The files separate the kinds on disk (`ArtifactNode.jsonld`, `FolderNode.jsonld`, `BlockNode.jsonld`, `Profile.jsonld`, and under `.state/` `TreeView.jsonld` plus the thread-scoped blocks, artifacts and folders); at start `rehydrateStore` reads every file into one store of quads, and `encodeDoc` skips the `@type` of each document (read in `store.ts:70-111`), so the store holds no trace of which file or kind a node came from; the kind survives only in the prefix of the id. `allIdsOfKind` rebuilds the lists by passing over all quads and filtering the subjects by that prefix, and `dehydrate` needs those lists to write the files back (8 passes per flush pair, from the census). Two points: `rehydrate` knows each document's file and kind at the moment it reads it (it already keeps the set of ids it has seen), so the per-kind sets can be filled for free at load and kept up to date by creation and removal; and the files are not strictly one per kind, since the blocks, artifacts and folders under `.threads/` are written to `.state/`, so the write-back is by kind and by scope (the thread-scope test of the path), which the engine need not know if the layer above splits them.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
