    "title": "### The data model",
    "text": "The engine stores pairs. Each field is a fixed relation, and a pair (s, v) is one link of that relation from a subject s to a value v; in RDF terms these are triples (subject, field, value) whose predicate, the field, never varies. The field is fixed first, and pairs are managed field by field. On a node n each field has two sides: the forward side holds the pairs n → v, where n is the subject, and the reverse side holds the pairs s → n, where n is the value. Each pair sits on two records, on its subject's forward side and on its value's reverse side. Only reference and embed fields hold pairs (see [Literal fields are properties of the record](#id/BlockNode:00D39DRYB0001)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHJXK0001",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D4MYZ6Q0000",
      "BlockNode:00D24N87SR00E",
