    ],
    "title": "**Records by kind and by graph**",
    "text": "**Records by kind and by graph**: the kind of a node is not a pair; it is read from the prefix of its id, and its graph is held in two folds. In memory the graph is a field: the record carries its `graph` and is filed in the collection of its graph and kind, with the master map from id to record across graphs beside the tree, so `graphOf` reads the field of the record that the master map finds. In the mirror the graph is file containment: a document has no `graph` field, the file for its graph and kind holds it, the load gives each record the graph of the file it reads, and the dump writes each record to the file of its graph and kind (see [Written by graph and kind](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01Y)). The engine keeps one collection for each graph and each kind of the schema, a set of the records themselves, and no separate set of ids. Creating a node adds its record to its collection and deleting the node removes it; a dump or a sweep iterates the collection for all the nodes of a kind in a graph. The natural order of a collection is the order of insertion, which follows the increasing codes of the ids and so makes the shell's sort at a dump cheap (see [Orders in the mirror](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01G)); it is promised to no reader.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR00D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR019",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR019:links:Link:00D3EM9WQG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D255WVK0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR019:links:Link:00D3EM9WQG000:props:StringProp:00D3WMZQPR000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "230"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Writes**",
    "text": "**Writes**: writing a field of a node updates that node's pairs of that field: its new values replace its old ones, and the reverse entries of the old values are updated; a write of an ordered field is one splice of its list (see [Ordered fields](#id/BlockNode:00D255WVK0001)). Adding a pair that is already present changes nothing; this is a property of the structure and not a promise the shell relies on. Removing a node removes all its pairs, its record from its kind's collection and its entries in every reverse side that was built.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR015",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR01A",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR01A:links:Link:00D3EMBKE0000",
