    "title": "**Ids**",
    "text": "**Ids**: a node id is the kind and a snowflake, `Kind:snowflake`, unique across all graphs; an embedded node's id carries its owner and field in front, `owner:field:Kind:snowflake`, and nests further for the props of a link. The kind of any id is read from its last `field:Kind:` segment, or from its prefix when it has none. The grammar is the engine's; the shell mints the ids and passes them in, and the engine checks the grammar and the uniqueness of each, and refuses an id that fails the grammar. The engine keeps the bare id, with no IRI or term encoding of its own, and the mirror stores the bare id as `@id`. The canonical address of a node is `aperas://id/` and the id, which belongs to the shell's addressing (see [Addressing](#id/BlockNode:00D5AYBPWR001) and [node-ID addressing](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T800B)). The resolvers of an address count a string as an id only if a valid snowflake follows `Kind:`, so a folder or an artifact named after a kind is never taken for an id; whether the load does the same is an Open point (see [An id-like string at load](#id/BlockNode:00D256098G001)). The address `aperas://graph.domain/` and a deep path (`aperas://tree/` for the main graph) is resolved at lookup time and never stored ([linking](../design/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CDBYV4T0001)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR01M",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
