    "title": "**The path is derived from the parent chain**",
    "text": "**The path is derived from the parent chain**: the path of an artifact or a folder is, in the end, derived and stored nowhere; the stored `path` that the Aperas schema keeps for now is transitional, and it goes with the batch that removes it (see [Deriving the path](#id/BlockNode:00D25698B0001)). The path is the chain of the node's `title`s (an artifact's or a folder's is its name, see [The title of an artifact or a folder is its name](#id/BlockNode:00D4RSAZ3G001)) along its parent chain, from the root folder down to the node, and the shell derives it over the engine's reads of `parent` and `title` (the root folder is `.`: its place is the directory that `graph.artifacts` in the configuration names). A block's path is built from its nearest artifact's path and the slugs of the titles above it. The derived path serves the lookup of a node for a path (ingest, track, project, link integrity, the anchor of reference resolution), the deep path of a block and the tie to the file on disk; the scope test of a node does not need it, since it is the graph the node belongs to (see [Written by graph and kind](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01Y)). A lookup by path is served by a map that the shell keeps, holding only the live nodes' ids (see [How a path names one node](#id/BlockNode:00D25686RR001)), keyed by the stored `path` while it is stored and by the derived path once it is not (see [Literal fields are properties of the record](#id/BlockNode:00D39DRYB0001)). A path names one node as long as live siblings under one parent have distinct titles.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHMYQG004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
