    "title": "**Tombstones**",
    "text": "**Tombstones**: the engine manages tombstones, since a tombstone is part of the meaning of a reference. Tombstoning is the engine's operation: a tombstoned node gets a timestamp, a string that the shell passes in so that the engine needs no clock, in the field to which the schema assigns the role of the tombstone marker (`tombstonedAt` in the Aperas schema), and a node is live when that field is unset. A tombstone loses all its references, its parent included, and keeps its literal properties, so that stale incoming links can check its content to update their reference. The engine tombstones one node, or a subtree over containment; the rule that a removed artifact tombstones its whole subtree is the shell's. A tombstone stays only to catch incoming links, so that they do not dangle. Which commands show a tombstoned node, and how they mark it, belongs to the commands and not to the engine; the shell's rules for a removed node are under [A removed node leaves its container](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000R). Two gaps remain open: [no operation revives a tombstoned node](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00CN1PT1B0001) and [a block that is unreachable and was never tombstoned is never collected](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D1A2AY68001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
