            "@id": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000Q:links:Link:00D76ARX70000:props:StringProp:00D76ARX70001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "200"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The canonical place in the render**",
    "text": "**The canonical place in the render**: the render keeps one canonical place for a node reached through several unfolded links, and that place is the first in code-point order of the unfolded ids (see [Ids give a stable order](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01F)), so the rendered tree depends on no order that is only listed by id.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR01C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000R",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A removed node leaves its container**",
    "text": "**A removed node leaves its container**: a tombstoned node loses its parent, so it is taken out of its container's list, and a container lists live nodes only. Once the path is derived, a tombstone is in no path, and the titles of live siblings alone decide which node a path names. In the shell this has three consequences. First, `tree --tombstoned` shows nothing for a removed child in its place, since its container no longer lists it; it keeps the `(tombstoned)` tag on a link whose target is tombstoned, and the commands keep a way to show a tombstone by its id. Second, a removal that empties a textless `listItem` or a `list` tombstones that container in the same write, a rule applied at the time of the removal; the boot sweep `tombstoneVacuousContainers` keeps the same rule over a container that has no live child. Third, `toPath()` of a tombstone is `null`, since it has no parent to derive a path from.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000S",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
