  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D24QZNW0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D24QZNW0001",
    "text": "**Verification — the draft `design/core.md` (`design-core-draft` Turn 1, Report `BlockNode:00D24QGR7G009`) and the next steps** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): the document exists in the mirror with a Context, a Status and the eight sections of the Ask (Purpose and scope, The data model, The index, Ordering, Identity and uniqueness, The mirror, The engine's interface, Open points), about 100 blocks; a scan of its text found no code fence, no source line reference and no figure, as the Ask required; `aperas check-links` is clean over 7,564 live blocks; I did not read the content against the decisions, which the user does on the artifact. The Grapher's questions, answered by the orchestrator and open to the user's correction: (1) Context should list its Discussion as `design/documentation.md` says (the base skill allows a doc's Context as a normative singular reference downward): add the link to `discussion/core.md`; (2) the path rule: the draft writes option (a), the live-node-at-path primitive; the user has not chosen between (a) and (b), only that the stored copy is to be refactored away, so (a) stays as the orchestrator's leaning and the user is asked to confirm; (3) the Coder fact-checks the draft against the code and the mirror, read-only, opened now as the task `design-core-fact-check`; (4) the unplaced archive content (service mechanics, ingest ordering and reconciliation, the wikilink grammar, the fold classification) stays in the archive for now and `design/service.md` and an ingest design wait for the user; (5) the issue `00D0W07DRR000` is marked resolved after the fact-check and the user's reading.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D24ZD0QR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D24ZD0QR001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Coder's fact-check of `design/core.md` (`design-core-fact-check` Turn 1, Report `BlockNode:00D24Z0JP8007`)** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): the Coder read all 99 blocks against the code and a copy of the real mirror and reports 8 statements false as stated, 7 partly true and the rest true (the fields per class and their cardinalities, the key order, the mirror files per kind and scope, the exact round trip with the 8 files byte-identical, the helper list). I re-checked three counts on the real files: of 207 tombstoned nodes 52 still hold `children`, `links` or `props` (the Coder's figure, confirmed); the live artifacts without a `parent` are exactly seven, six `README.md` files and `concepts.md` (confirmed); live artifacts without a `type`: I count 6, the Coder 7 (probably a different set, such as a tombstoned one; not reconciled). The false statements, in plain terms: a duplicate id is detected at load and logged, not silent; `tombstoneVacuousContainers` sets only `tombstonedAt`, so tombstoned nodes can keep their content; sibling indexes are not always dense (a move across parents leaves a gap and the next append can repeat an index) and \"at most one parent\" is not enforced (a child can end with two `parent` pairs), and a tombstoned node stays in its container's `children`; the kind of an id is read only from the `props` and `links` segments, so a title such as \"BlockNode: hello\" aborts the load with an invalid IRI; `nodeExists` and the size are read from the store directly, not only through the helpers; the loader writes `parent` from its own pair and not from the lister (the invariant holds in the mirror, 0 violations); `allSubjectIds` scans the whole store on every `allIdsOfKind` call; `findByExactPath` is first-match (the filed defect). Today nothing sorts links by position, unfolds by id or ties by id; only `dehydrate` sorts, with `localeCompare`. Four points are unclear (why some ingested artifacts have no `type`; whether a reconcile move can drop a child's sibling index; what produced 12 tombstoned nodes that hold content; whether the design states each false rule as the rule plus today's gap or holds the rule and files the gap). The orchestrator's answer to the last one, open to the user: the design states the rule the engine guarantees; today's gaps are filed as issues (filing is a must), and the design says in its Status that the draft was fact-checked and that the code differs in the listed places.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
