          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**How a path names one node**",
    "text": "**How a path names one node**: the path map holds only live nodes' ids, and every lookup by path goes through one primitive, \"the live node at this path\", with an optional kind and an optional graph, so that the answer does not depend on the order of the store (see [A lookup does not depend on first match](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01H)). While the path is stored, a removed node keeps it, as a tombstone keeps its literal properties (see [Tombstones](#id/BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8000)), so two nodes may hold one path and only one of them is live; the tombstone is never in the map. Once the path is derived, a tombstone, which has no parent, is in no path.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHMYQG004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D25698B0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
