    "title": "**Deriving the path**",
    "text": "**Deriving the path**: not settled. The target is decided (2026-10-16): the shell derives the path of an artifact or a folder from the parent chain, as [The path is derived from the parent chain](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01S) states, a path that cannot be derived is refused ([A path that cannot be derived](#id/BlockNode:00D4R9VPGR001)), the root folder needs no parent (its place is the directory that `graph.artifacts` in the configuration names), and the `README.md` files are not artifacts (the content of a README is the sub-blocks of its folder, and the migration deletes the stray README artifacts). The stored `path` stays for now, for the technical reasons below, and is transitional ([Fields per class](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR00M)); its removal is a later batch of the plan, after the engine is in place. What is open is how that removal handles five holes, which the count of the readers of the stored `path` found, undecided: (1) a tracked artifact has a path and no parent until the next folder ingest (`trackArtifact` creates the node, and the folder ingest finds it by a map by path to give it a parent), so with a derived path a second `aperas track` of the same file mints a duplicate and a match by title alone is ambiguous (`a/x.md` and `b/x.md` are both titled `x.md`); the ways out are that track attaches the node at once, creating the folder chain as the creation of a holder does, that something stored keeps the identity of an unattached node (a second field, which defeats removing `path`), or that track always runs the folder ingest next and the artifact ingest takes the node and not the path; (2) the tombstoned nodes keep their `path` today, and `staleOnDisk` and the reuse of a path after a removal rely on it, while a tombstone loses its `parent` and so has no derived path; (3) the thread scope reads the path in a module of raw quads that cannot import the node classes, so it needs its own walk over `parent` and `title`, and the answer for an undeterminable chain (a throw, or a quiet default) decides whether a thread artifact that lost its parent leaks into the tracked mirror instead of `.state/`; (4) the mirror holds 7 live artifacts with no parent (`concepts.md` and the six `README.md` artifacts, which the migration deletes), whose derived path fails, and `concepts.md` stays as the test of the refusal; (5) the root folder is the one folder with no parent in this mirror, but a folder holder or a ghost could lack one too, so whether \"the live folder with no parent\" is the definition of the root, or a marker is, is open.",
