    "title": "00D256HK30001",
    "text": "Promoted to [Status](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR005) (the fact-check and the list of the differences), [Containment](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR00Z), [Containment in the file](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T0000), [Ids](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01N) and [Tombstones](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01V) (the corrected rules), and to the Open points [The density of sibling indexes](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D255WVK0001), [A removed node among its container's children](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000R), [An id-like string at load](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D256098G001), [Where a node's parent comes from](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D2562RC8001), [How a path names one node](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D25686RR001) and [Deriving the path](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D25698B0001). The gaps between the code and the rules are filed as issues: [tombstoning leaves content on the node](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D252W7VG001), [sibling indexes are not kept dense](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D252Y7WR001), [a child can end with two `parent` pairs](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D2532JJR001), [the kind of an id and an id-like string](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D2534RSG001), [the store is read outside the helpers](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D2536E1R001), [the loader writes `parent` from its own pair](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D2538VKR001), [artifacts ingested without a `type`](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D253D680001), and the scans, as a new finding under [the full-store scans](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D0W02KPG001).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D257KG40001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D257KG40001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's correction of `design/core.md` and filing of the gaps (`design-core-draft` Turn 2, Report `BlockNode:00D256ZA40008`)** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): the design has 106 blocks and the same eleven headings; a scan found no code fence and no figure (only the date of the fact-check); `aperas check-links` is clean over 7,791 live blocks; I read the Ask, both Reports and the first new issue (`00D252W7VG001`, tombstoning leaves content: 52 of 207 tombstoned nodes). The Grapher used `aperas reload --discard` once to recover from a mistyped anchor; the thread entries of the earlier turns are all present, so nothing was lost; since a reload discards what the service has not flushed and thread writes are the known gap in flushing, the use is recorded here as a risk, not as an error. Seven new issues in `issues/core.md` (tombstoning leaves content; sibling indexes not dense; two `parent` pairs; the kind of an id read from `props` and `links` only; direct store reads outside the helpers; the loader writes `parent` from its own pair; artifacts ingested without a `type`) and one finding added under the scans issue. The Grapher's three questions, answered by the orchestrator and open to the user's correction: (1) the drafted rule that a removed node is emptied stays, with the 52 nodes filed as the gap against it; the user confirms when reading the design; (2) the four findings not on the user's list are filed, since filing is a must: the service never reads `danglingRefs`; the thread-scope test sits inside `core/apeironNgn`; `ownerOfLink` is first-match; a duplicate id is logged but the load merges the quads; (3) the README repeats the sentence that a node's `parent` is derived from the container that lists it, which the fact-check found false for the loader: the Coder corrects that one sentence in `Apeiron/README.md` to the wording of the design.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D258W098001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The load computes `danglingRefs` and the service never reads it, so nobody is told at run time of an id that is referenced and never defined**",
    "text": "**The load computes `danglingRefs` and the service never reads it, so nobody is told at run time of an id that is referenced and never defined**: found 2026-10-12 by the Coder in the fact-check of the draft `design/core.md`, among the findings the orchestrator asked to file. Filed 2026-10-12.",
