  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D259WMYG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D259WMYG001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's filing of the four extra findings (`design-core-draft` Turn 3, Report `BlockNode:00D259KAV8005`)** (2026-10-12, orchestrator): four issues in `issues/core.md` (`danglingRefs` computed and not read by the service, `00D258W098001`; the thread-scope test inside `core/apeironNgn`, `00D2590B2R001`; `ownerOfLink` first-match, `00D2593220001`; a repeated `@id` logged and merged, `00D2595QAG001`), each with Symptom, Root cause, Not proven and no fix decided; I read the first and `aperas check-links` is clean; nothing fixed or resolved; the Grapher did not use `reload --discard`. Its question: yes to both, an Open point in `design/core.md` for whether the load goes on or refuses on a repeated `@id` (the design lists none) and a link of the four new entries from Status; they are small, so they ride with the next Grapher turn on the user's comments about the design and cost no turn of their own.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2HCD6WR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2HCD6WR001",
    "text": "**The service was found stopped; started with the user's approval; an allow rule for edits under `AperasKG/`** (2026-10-13, orchestrator): after the Coder's README turn every `aperas` call was refused and no service process existed; the service log has no shutdown line (last entry: the 2026-10-12 14:13Z reload of the Grapher's `reload --discard`), so the cause is not known; the user says the machine had been on throughout. The user approved `aperas service start`; it started at 04:24:33 UTC, rehydrated 68,394 quads at 04:24:35 (about 2.4 s of load) and answered within about 30 s (the CLI's own 5 s ready wait expired, as at the earlier start). Nothing was lost: the last Freeflow entry, both Grapher Reports, both Asks of the last turns, the Coder's fact-check Report and the last new issue are present, and both views still zoom on their last Asks. The Coder's README turn (`design-core-fact-check` Turn 2, Ask `BlockNode:00D257R5D0001`) has the edit (line 27 of `Apeiron/README.md`, staged) and no Work or Report, since the service was down at the time; a follow-up turn records them. The user asked why edit prompts appear in Auto mode with no file name; the cause is that edits under `AperasKG/` resolve outside the working directory (a symlink to the sibling repo) and no allow rule existed; `/add-dir` is not available here, so at the user's request the orchestrator created `.claude/settings.local.json` (git-ignored) with `Edit(//home/will/source/AperasKG/**)` and `Edit(//home/will/source/Aperas/AperasKG/**)`; whether it stops the prompts is not yet observed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2HD3DZR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2HD3DZR001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Coder's record of the README turn (`design-core-fact-check` Turn 3, Report `BlockNode:00D2HCWPGG007`)** (2026-10-13, orchestrator): line 27 of `Apeiron/README.md` matches the design's \"Containment\" and \"Where a node's parent comes from\" (the container's `children` and each child's `parent` state containment twice; the load reads `parent` as written; the data keeps the two in agreement), so the Coder changed nothing; the previous Coder's edit stays staged; the Report is in the thread and `aperas check-links` is clean. The README turn is closed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2HFED2G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2HFED2G001:links:Link:00D2HFESG0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2HCD6WR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2HFED2G001:links:Link:00D2HFESG0000:props:StringProp:00D2HFESG0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "384"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2HFED2G001",
    "text": "**The user explains the stopped service: it shuts itself down after 30 minutes idle, and a permission prompt stalled the Coder** (2026-10-13, user: \"the service will be automatically down after an idle time (half an hour or so). Maybe that ask for write permission has stalled the process making the service down.\"). Orchestrator's check, which fits and replaces \"cause not known\" in [the restart entry](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2HCD6WR001): `service.ts` sets an idle timer of 30 minutes (`IDLE_TIMEOUT_MS`) that ends the service by a plain shutdown, which writes no log line; the Coder's README turn began about 14:15Z on 2026-10-12, its first `aperas` calls worked, then its `Edit` of `Apeiron/README.md` waited for a permission answer (the Coder's turn lasted about 13.7 hours of wall time), with no `aperas` command in that time; the run directory, where the service removes its socket and lock when it stops, was last changed at 21:51 local (14:51Z), and the log's last line is 14:13Z, so the stop came about 30 minutes after the last command; after the answer every call was refused, which is what a stopped service gives. So the stall was the permission prompt, the stop was normal, and the allow rule created for edits under `AperasKG/` removes the cause. Gap to file (filing is a must): a stopped service and a busy one give the same message (\"No ApeironNgn service running\"), so agents report a crash or wait for a service that is not there; a distinct message (stopped after idle, start it) would tell them apart; the CLI also does not start the service by itself. It joins the next Grapher turn.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2NAMXKG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2NAMXKG001",
    "text": "**The user, reading `design/core.md`, asks for an example of \"reverse, for each field, built on first use\"** (2026-10-13, user). Orchestrator's example, given in chat and offered for the design's \"The index\": `aperas backlinks <block>` asks the engine which subjects have `target` equal to that block; the field `target` has no reverse yet, so the engine passes once over the pairs of the store, builds the map from each target to the links that point at it, keeps it and answers; the next `backlinks` call, for any block, is a lookup; a link created or removed afterwards updates both views of its pair, because the reverse of `target` now exists; a field nobody asks in reverse (`type`) never gets one; the reverse is derived, never written to the mirror, and is gone at restart and rebuilt by the next ask. Cost to state honestly: with the forward pairs held per subject, the first ask on each field is one pass over all subjects' lists, not only over that field; with a table per field it would be a pass over that field's table only, which ties the example to the open point on layout.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2NEXPVR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2NEXPVR001",
    "text": "**The user asks why a set is better than a list for a large reverse bucket** (2026-10-13, user, from the design: \"a bucket is always a set, because a bucket can hold thousands of subjects\"). Orchestrator's answer: membership, adding without a repeat and removing cost one step in a set and a pass over the bucket in a list (removing from a list also shifts everything after it); at thousands of entries, removing a task of a hundred or more blocks, or loading a file that states each containment twice, would make thousands of such passes; a set does not promise an order, which matches the unordered contract. The price: a set takes more memory per entry than a list slot, which is why a bucket of one to three entries (the usual case for `target` or `profile`) is cheaper as a list; the Coder's prototype kept a list until a size threshold and then switched, and the design dropped the threshold for simplicity (\"always a set\"); if memory shows to matter with many tiny buckets, it is reconsidered by measurement, not by the design.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2NMRN48001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2NMRN48001",
    "text": "**The user asks about multiplicity: forward supports it, so the reverse should keep it to be reciprocal; are there cases where it is unintentional?** (2026-10-13, user, on \"adding without a repeat\"). Orchestrator's answer, with a question back: \"multiplicity\" can mean two things. (1) Several objects for one subject, and so several subjects for one object: supported in both directions by the design (the forward list of a subject, the reverse bucket of an object); each forward pair appears exactly once as a reverse entry, which is the reciprocity. (2) The identical pair (same subject, field and object) occurring several times, a bag: not supported in either direction; the store is a set of pairs, as Oxigraph's is, and a repeated add is ignored; \"adding without a repeat\" meant only this. Where (2) occurs today: the mirror states each containment twice (the owner's `children` and the child's `parent`), which is redundancy to collapse, not a count (68,035 adds make 60,810 quads in the census); a block that cites one target twice in its text has one `Link` carrying two `position` props (the `shape.ts` comment calls this \"a real multiset\"), so the repeated occurrence is carried by two distinct embedded nodes, not by a repeated pair; no other repeated pair was found. Where it would be unintentional: none found; if a count per pair were wanted, the occurrences would stay distinct nodes as they are. The orchestrator asks the user which meaning was intended.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2NVRNJ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
