            "value": "156"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2NVRNJ8001",
    "text": "**The user: the `Link` is the subject, not the block; the set is useful only if the same link can be added twice by accident** (2026-10-13, user, answering [the question on multiplicity](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2NMRN48001)): the multiplicity meant is that a source block has several `Link` subjects that point at one target; \"I cannot imagine how that accident can be\". Orchestrator's check in the code: the user's reading is right: each `Link` is its own subject with a fresh id, so a block citing a target twice has two links, both reached from the block through `links` and both pointing at the target through `target` (the reverse of `target` holds both links; the reverse of `links` gives the block twice, once per link); an identical pair can only repeat if one subject receives the same field and object twice, and the write paths already guard that: `writeField` clears the field before adding, `appendOrderedChild` removes the child's old `parent` and index first, `unfold` returns when the id is already unfolded (`node.ts:1250-1252`), and new `Link` and `StringProp` nodes have fresh ids so their pairs are unique by construction; no accidental double add was found. The one real repeat is the mirror, which states each containment twice (the owner's `children` and the child's `parent`), so the idempotent add serves the load and is otherwise a cheap safety net; the fact-check's finding of a child with two `parent` pairs is a pair with two different objects, which a set does not prevent. Two ways, for the user to choose: (i) the engine's add ignores an identical pair (a documented contract, cheap), and the loader may feed both statements; (ii) the engine adds blindly and the caller guarantees uniqueness, and the loader reads each containment once (from the owner's `children`) and only checks the child's `parent` copy, which is also the open point on where a node's parent comes from. The orchestrator leans to (i): the engine stays tolerant of the mirror's redundancy at a small cost, and the redundancy of the mirror is a separate decision.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2NYY1FR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2NYY1FR001",
    "text": "**The user asks whether the current core stores a JS `children` list in RAM** (2026-10-13, user). Orchestrator's answer, from `encodeDoc` (`store.ts`), `childrenOf` and `orderedChildIds` as read earlier: no. The store holds no forward `children` pair at all: each child carries a `parent` pair and a `__siblingIndex` pair, and `.children` is computed at every access (`childrenOf`, `node.ts:71`): the reverse lookup of `parent` for the container, each child's index read, a sort by index; a node's wrapper object holds only the store and the id, so no list is kept in memory between accesses; the lists that exist are transient (the parsed tree during an ingest, the arrays `dehydrate` builds for the file). The mirror file does hold `children` arrays, and loading turns them into the `parent` and index pairs. Implication for the engine, offered as an option and not a decision: the engine could hold containment as the ordered list in the container (the mirror's own form) and derive `parent` as its reverse; that would make the index implicit in the position, remove the density gap and the two-parent gap by construction, and need the reverse of `parent` always built (the flush and `toPath` climb it constantly), where the present form needs it for every `.children`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P2SG10001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P2SG10001:links:Link:00D2P2SW0R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2NVRNJ8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P2SG10001:links:Link:00D2P2SW0R000:props:StringProp:00D2P2SW0R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "194"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P2SG10001:links:Link:00D2P2SW0R002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2NYY1FR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P2SG10001:links:Link:00D2P2SW0R002:props:StringProp:00D2P2SW0R003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "290"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2P2SG10001",
    "text": "**The user: `children` and `parent` are separate fields; feeding both repeats no pair, so no idempotent add; \"parent is the reverse of children\" is semantics only** (2026-10-13, user, answering [the choice between (i) and (ii)](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2NVRNJ8001) and [the children-list question](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2NYY1FR001)). Orchestrator's reading and consequences, for the user to confirm: the engine treats `children` and `parent` as two unrelated fields; the mirror's double statement of containment is then two different pairs in two fields (the container's `children`, the child's `parent`), never one pair twice, so the load needs no repeat to be ignored; the engine's add is blind and its contract is that a caller never adds an identical pair (no write path was found to do so); keeping the two fields in agreement is the job of the layer above, which today does it in the `children` setter and `appendOrderedChild`, and which a check can verify; this settles the open point \"where a node's parent comes from\" in the design (a pair of its own, as the code reads it today) and removes \"a repeated add is ignored\" from the design's index; the reverse bucket stays a set for the cost of removal and membership, a reason of speed and not of repeats. One follow-up the orchestrator asks the user, because it decides what the `children` field is: whether `children` is an ordered list (the position is the order, no `__siblingIndex`, which removes the density gap) or keeps the `__siblingIndex` pairs next to it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P98RCG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P98RCG001:links:Link:00D2P994Q0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2P2SG10001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P98RCG001:links:Link:00D2P994Q0000:props:StringProp:00D2P994Q0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "157"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P98RCG001:links:Link:00D2P994Q0002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2HFED2G001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2P98RCG001:links:Link:00D2P994Q0002:props:StringProp:00D2P994Q8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1656"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2P98RCG001",
    "text": "**The user: `children` stays as the backlinks of `parent`; sets stay for removal; the idempotent add is a welcome side effect** (2026-10-13, user, answering [the question on `children`](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2P2SG10001)): \"Keep it as backlinks of `parent` as current. I just analyze the worst case to see if the add of identical pair occurs. And we keep `set` for removal, OK. The idempotent add turns out to be a side effect, which is also nice.\" Decisions, which amend the previous entry: (1) containment stays as today: each child holds a `parent` pair and a `__siblingIndex` pair, and a container's `children` is the backlinks of `parent`, ordered by the index; the engine has no `children` field of its own, and the arrays in the mirror are the file form only; (2) a reverse bucket is a set, for removal and membership; (3) adding a pair that is already present changes nothing, a property of the structure and not a promise callers rely on: in this form the load does meet it (the mirror states containment twice, so the `parent` pair of a child arrives twice), and the property absorbs it; the previous entry's option (ii), a blind add and a loader that reads each containment once, is dropped, and \"where a node's parent comes from\" keeps its answer of today (the child's `parent` pair is the stored fact). To record in the design: the index section says that adding a present pair changes nothing; the data model says `children` is derived; a block citing one target twice has two `Link` subjects (the multiplicity is of subjects); and the Grapher files the gap that a stopped service and a busy one give the same message ([the cause](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2HFED2G001)). The lazy-reverse example is not added until the user says so.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PC3ZG8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A repeated `@id` at load**",
    "text": "**A repeated `@id` at load**: the load rejects every document that carries a repeated `@id`, keeps none of them, reports the id, and goes on with the other documents.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHMYQG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PCFDBR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A stopped service and a busy one give the same refusal, \"No ApeironNgn service running\", so an agent reports a crash or waits for a service that is not there**",
    "text": "**A stopped service and a busy one give the same refusal, \"No ApeironNgn service running\", so an agent reports a crash or waits for a service that is not there**: found 2026-10-13 by the orchestrator in the check of the Coder's stalled turn of 2026-10-12, on the user's explanation that the service stops itself after an idle time. Filed 2026-10-13.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D2PCFDBR002",
      "BlockNode:00D2PCFDC0000",
      "BlockNode:00D2PCFDC0004",
      "BlockNode:00D2PCFDC0006",
      "BlockNode:00D2PCFDC0008"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PCFDBR002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PCFDBR002:props:StringProp:00D2PCFSQR000",
