  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PDYN4G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2PDYN4G001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's update of `design/core.md` and the stopped-service issue (`design-core-draft` Turn 4, Report `BlockNode:00D2PDC080008`)** (2026-10-13, orchestrator): the design has 107 blocks, no code fence and no figure (only the date); `aperas check-links` is clean over 7,936 live blocks; the new issue `00D2PCFDBR001` (a stopped and a busy service give the same refusal) is filed with the idle timer, the missing log line and the refusal message as root cause; the Report shows the writes (a present pair added again changes nothing, as a property), the reverse bucket as a set, `children` derived, multiplicity of subjects, \"where a node's parent comes from\" closed and moved under Containment, an Open point on a repeated `@id`, and the Status links to the four issues of Turn 3; no stray `aperas` process is left from the Grapher's blocked move. The Grapher's questions, answered by the orchestrator and open to the user's correction: (1) the closed point under Containment in the data model is right; (2) marking `children` derived in the `BlockNode` and `FolderNode` entries keeps the design consistent; (3) an Open point for a file whose `parent` and `children` disagree is added, with the options that the load goes on as it stands (today), refuses, or reports; (4) the \"Promoted to\" paragraphs as siblings follow the entries above them. A tool gap from its friction, to be filed: `aperas insert <id> --after <anchor>` with no body (a move) waits on standard input when it is an open pipe, which the mechanics reference does not say; run with `< /dev/null`. Both ride with the next Grapher turn.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PGSFCG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2PGSFCG001",
    "text": "**The user asks for the pros of tables (one table per field) in the layout open point** (2026-10-13, user). Orchestrator's answer: pros of a table per field: (1) it is the pairs model itself, a field is a relation held as one unit with its forward map and, once asked, its reverse map; (2) a lookup of one field of one subject is a direct lookup in that field's table, and an absent field is one miss, which matters because most probes at a flush find nothing; (3) building the lazy reverse of a field passes over that field's table only, not over every subject; (4) cardinality, storage kind and any uniqueness are attributes of the relation and sit in one place, and a single-valued field can hold its object directly, without a list per subject; (5) whole-field operations (all pairs of a field, clearing a field, checking a field's invariants) are cheap. Cons, for balance: reading all fields of one node (the flush, `wrap`) probes every table, mostly misses, where a record per subject reads one list; removing a node visits every table. Neither effect is measured; both layouts sit behind the same interface, so the choice can wait for the design to settle and be made by a measurement on the real mirror.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PS7ZB8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PS7ZB8001:links:Link:00D2PS8BX8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2PGSFCG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PS7ZB8001:links:Link:00D2PS8BX8000:props:StringProp:00D2PS8BX8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "177"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2PS7ZB8001",
    "text": "**The user chooses a record per subject, for modularity: nodes partitioned into the main graph, the threads in `.state`, and many external graphs** (2026-10-13, user, answering [the pros of tables](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2PGSFCG001)): \"I prefer record per subject for modularity. E.g.: the nodes should be partitioned into main graph vs threads in `.state`, and many external graphs.\" Orchestrator's reading, to be corrected by the user: (1) the layout is a record per subject (all the pairs of one subject held together), decided; the reverse stays lazy; (2) the engine holds several graphs, each node belonging to exactly one: the main graph, the threads graph (today's `.state` content, the nodes under `.threads/`), and further external graphs; the records of a graph live in that graph's own collection, so a graph can be loaded, written and dropped on its own; (3) consequences: the thread-scope test, which today climbs the parent chain of every block at each flush to read a path (the filed issue about the thread-scope test and the cost of a flush), becomes a lookup of which graph holds the id; the files per kind and scope of the mirror become files per graph and kind; (4) not decided and listed as Open points: references and reverse lookups that cross graphs (a thread cites main nodes; does the backlinks of a main node include the threads, or does the caller name the graphs to search?), whether an id is unique across graphs or within one, which graph a new node joins, what an external graph is (its source, whether it is read-only, when it is loaded and dropped), and how the mirror files map to graphs. The Grapher records this in the design, with the pending Open point on a file whose `parent` and `children` disagree, and files the tool gap that a move with `aperas insert --after` and no body waits on standard input.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PTHM9R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-10-13T10:48:49.573Z",
    "title": "### Graphs and partitions",
    "text": "The engine holds several graphs, and each node belongs to exactly one of them.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR00D",
    "type": "heading",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PTZ7C8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-10-13T10:48:19.624Z",
    "title": "**References and reverse lookups across graphs**",
    "text": "**References and reverse lookups across graphs**: a node of one graph can refer to a node of another, as a thread cites the nodes of the main graph, and the backlinks of a node are the `Link`s whose `target` is the node. The options are (a) a reverse lookup searches every graph, so the backlinks of a main node include the links of the threads and the answer is the one a single store would give, at the cost of reverse tables kept or built across graphs; (b) the caller names the graphs to search, and the default is the graph of the node, so the threads that cite a main node are found only when the threads graph is named; (c) a graph refers only to its own nodes and to those of a graph it depends on, the threads on the main graph and never the reverse, which makes the direction one-way as the citation rule of the documents does, where nothing above the thread tier cites into `.threads/`. The same question stands for a `parent` that names a node of another graph, for the list of ids referenced and never defined that the load returns (it must know which graphs were loaded), for \"all ids of a kind\", and for whether the reverse tables and the sets of ids of each kind are kept for each graph or across graphs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PV1D5R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-10-13T10:48:21.836Z",
    "title": "**Ids across graphs**",
    "text": "**Ids across graphs**: whether an id is unique across graphs or within one graph. The options are (a) across graphs: an `@id` names one document in the whole engine, as [An `@id` names one document](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01P) says now, so an id alone addresses a node and a lookup by id needs no graph; (b) within a graph: two graphs may hold one id, so a lookup by id names its graph and a reference carries it, which changes the form of an id and of a reference in the mirror. The answer also decides what a repeated `@id` at load is when its two documents are in different graphs (see A repeated `@id` at load).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PV2YNR000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-10-13T10:48:24.112Z",
    "title": "**Which graph a new node joins**",
    "text": "**Which graph a new node joins**: the options are (a) the graph of its parent, so a subtree is never split and a child needs no declaration, and a new artifact or folder takes the graph of the folder that holds it; (b) the graph the caller names when it creates the node; (c) a rule on the path at creation, as the path `.threads` is the rule of the thread scope today, with the result stored as the node's membership from then on. Whether a node can change graph afterwards, and what happens then to its subtree and to the references into it, is not decided either.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PV4RT0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-10-13T10:48:26.337Z",
    "title": "**What an external graph is**",
    "text": "**What an external graph is**: its source (a directory of the mirror, a file elsewhere, another repository, a generated set), whether it is read-only, and when it is loaded and dropped. For writing the options are (a) read-only: it is never written back and is replaced when it is reloaded; (b) writable, with a mirror of its own that a flush writes. For its life they are (a) loaded when the service starts and dropped when it stops; (b) loaded on first use and dropped on request or when idle. Whether the nodes of the main graph can refer to its nodes is the question of References and reverse lookups across graphs. None of it is decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PV6KAG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-10-13T10:48:28.702Z",
    "title": "**How the files map to graphs**",
    "text": "**How the files map to graphs**: today the tracked mirror is one file for each class and `.state/` holds the thread scope (see Files in The mirror). The options are (a) a directory for each graph holding one file for each class, the layout of `.state/` taken for every graph, with the main graph at the top of `Apeiron/`; (b) one file for each graph holding all of its classes; (c) the two places kept and a place added for each external graph. Whichever is chosen, the write of each file keeps the round trip exact, and which files git tracks is decided for each graph.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PV8GC0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PV8GC0001:links:Link:00D5C1AJTR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D4MZ7DZG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PV8GC0001:links:Link:00D5C1AJTR000:props:StringProp:00D5C5WYX0000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "610"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**A file whose `parent` and `children` disagree**",
    "text": "**A file whose `parent` and `children` disagree**: when the `parent` of a node and the `children` array of its container state different containments, `parent` wins: since it carries no place, the load appends the node at the end of the `children` of the container that its `parent` names, drops its entry from the `children` of the other container, and warns, naming both containers. A `parent` that names an id with no document leaves the node with no container, and the load warns. The load returns that id in a list of its own, together with the ids referenced and never defined that the engine gives (see [Persistence](#id/BlockNode:00D4MZ7DZG001)): the engine's `danglingRefs` does not see it, because the `parent` that a build supplies is an assertion of the shell and never a stored pair, and a derived entry is not read as a reference. A node that a container lists and that has no `parent` of its own stays in that container: the list is the source.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHMYQG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PVH8PR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PVH8PR001:links:Link:00D2PVHN6R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2PS7ZB8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PVH8PR001:links:Link:00D2PVHN6R000:props:StringProp:00D2PVHN6R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "147"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2PVH8PR001",
    "text": "**The user: the multi-graph system deserves a completely different design; here it is only the rationale for modularity** (2026-10-13, user, after [the layout decision](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2PS7ZB8001)): \"The multi-graph system worth a completely different design which is not simple to be mentioned here. I just give it for the rationale of modularity.\" Orchestrator's correction of its own Ask: the Ask told the Grapher to add a graphs-and-partitions subsection and five Open points about graphs to `design/core.md`; that is out of scope for this document; a message to the running Grapher turn withdrew it: the design keeps the record-per-subject decision with one sentence of rationale (the engine is meant to serve several graphs; that design is separate), without the subsection and without the graph Open points; anything already written is removed. The multi-graph design is a future document, not opened. Mistake recorded: the orchestrator turned an example given as a reason into a section of the design.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PVVMWG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A move, `aperas insert <id> --after <anchor>` with no body, waits on standard input when it is an open pipe**",
    "text": "**A move, `aperas insert <id> --after <anchor>` with no body, waits on standard input when it is an open pipe**: found 2026-10-13 by the Grapher when it ran a move from a harness in the update of `design/core.md`, and agreed by the orchestrator to be filed. Filed 2026-10-13. The skills and the mechanics reference carry the workaround from v4.7; the fix in the code is not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CF1WZ4JG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D2PVVMWG002",
      "BlockNode:00D4PMMZY0001",
