          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2PW4JE8001",
    "text": "**The user refines the rule: update the design only when something is settled** (2026-10-13, user: \"Only update when there are something settled.\"). Orchestrator's reading, replacing \"only when the user asks\" in [the previous entry](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2PVH8PR001) and the memory: explanations, examples, analysis and questions never trigger a design update; a decision the user has made does; settled decisions are kept in the list and put into the design together in one Grapher turn when several have accumulated or when the user says \"update\", not one turn per decision. The memory was corrected to say so.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PWBHKR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PWBHKR001:links:Link:00D2PWBXX0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2PVH8PR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PWBHKR001:links:Link:00D2PWBXX0000:props:StringProp:00D2PWBXX0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "117"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2PWBHKR001",
    "text": "**The user: what is settled cannot be decided by the orchestrator; it must be a consensus** (2026-10-13, user, after [the rule on settled decisions](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2PVH8PR001)). Orchestrator's account: its list of \"settled decisions waiting\" counted as settled the Open point on a file whose `parent` and `children` disagree, which was the orchestrator's own suggestion; the same happened earlier, when the orchestrator answered the Grapher's questions (\"yes\" to an Open point on a repeated `@id` and to Status links to four issues) and had them written into the design in Turn 4 without the user's agreement. Settled means the user decided it in their own words, or agreed to a proposal explicitly. Settled by the user so far, by their words: lazy reverse; the pairs model and a set as the objects of one subject with its reciprocal backlinks; unordered sets with no promised order, JS's natural order, an explicit order at the readers (links by `position`, anything else by id), ties added, test surprises judged bug or wrong assumption and then fixed; `list-view` sorted by id; reversal supported in general, the choice on the caller's side; the stored path not duplicated, refactored away later, and the live set unique; sets kept for removal, adding a present pair a side effect that changes nothing, `children` as the backlinks of `parent`; record per subject for modularity, the multi-graph design separate; the design states the rule the engine guarantees, today's gaps filed as issues, Status noting the fact-check. Proposals of the orchestrator not yet agreed: the path rule (a), the removal rule, the Open point on a repeated `@id`, the Status links to the four issues, the Open point on a file whose `parent` and `children` disagree, existence by registry or by a pair, enforcement of the vocabulary, per-kind id sets filled at load, \"rules not figures\", the lazy-reverse example. The memory was corrected.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2PXJPQ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2PXJPQ8001",
    "text": "**Verification — the Grapher's Turn 5 (`design-core-draft`, Report `BlockNode:00D2PX0NVG008`)** (2026-10-13, orchestrator): the layout item under \"The index\" now says a record per subject, chosen for modularity because the engine is meant to serve several graphs, a design separate from this document (the user's decision); the Open point on the layout is tombstoned; the graphs subsection, its items and the five graph Open points that the Grapher had written before the correction arrived are tombstoned and three edited blocks restored; the Grapher made no design edit after the stop instruction; a new Open point \"A file whose `parent` and `children` disagree\" (`00D2PV8GC0001`) stands in the design, which was the orchestrator's proposal and not the user's decision, and so is listed with the other unagreed items; the tool gap (a move waits on standard input) is filed as `00D2PVVMWG001`; `aperas check-links` is clean over 7,984 live blocks. The Grapher's question (leave the thread-scope issue untouched while \"Written by kind and scope\" says the scope test belongs above the engine): yes, untouched.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2Q1WA28001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2Q1WA28001",
    "text": "**The user proposes partitioning the reverse buckets into a tree of \"ports\" mirroring the forward tree of blocks** (2026-10-13, user, on modularity): the forward and backward directions are totally reciprocal; a block A links to a block B through its children A1, A2, A3 (the macro-link A to B is reified by A1 to B, A2 to B, A3 to B, and deeper descendants), and each smallest block can hold many `Link`s at various positions; in reverse, the crowd of backlinks to B should be partitioned into a similar backward tree of ports B1, B2, B3 mirroring the forward tree (A1, A2, A3), so that reverse buckets stay at bite size. Orchestrator's reading, for the user to confirm before any discussion of merit: the reverse of `target` for a block B is not one flat bucket of every `Link` that points at B but a tree of ports under B, whose shape follows the forward tree of the blocks that hold the links (a port per source block or per level of the source's ancestry); a backlink lands in the port that corresponds to its source's place in the forward tree; the aim is buckets of bounded size. Questions to the user: is a port a stored node or only an in-memory partition of the reverse index; is the partition keyed by the source's ancestry, so that \"backlinks of B from within section A\" is a lookup of one port; does the idea apply to `target` only or to every reverse. Data point, not an argument: on the real mirror (2,840 links to 1,264 targets, links under `links` fields of the content and the threads) the median target has 1 link, 24 targets have more than 10, and the largest reverse buckets of `target` hold 59, 42, 37, 36 and 34 links, each from a different source block (the biggest are the issue entries of the performance work); the very large buckets of the earlier check were values of low-variety fields (`type` and the like), not link targets. Nothing is changed in the design: this is a proposal under discussion.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2Q908ER001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2Q908ER001:links:Link:00D2Q90N88000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2Q1WA28001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2Q908ER001:links:Link:00D2Q90N88000:props:StringProp:00D2Q90N88001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "167"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2Q908ER001",
    "text": "**The user on ports: dense future linking, partition by any hierarchy chosen by the caller; asks whether a port should be a stored node** (2026-10-13, user, answering [the reading and questions on ports](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2Q1WA28001)): the current links are sparse (object links); the future dense linking of `concepts.md` will make huge buckets of meta-links (\"type\" is itself a node, a concept, that gathers links); the simplest port tree mirrors the source's geography at some levels, not all; in general it can be any hierarchy, the next candidate being the IS-A hierarchy of tags and supertags (against the HAS-A hierarchy of blocks); the semantics are defined by the caller and the engine only supports the capability of partitioning ports into a tree. The user asks what the orchestrator thinks about a port being a stored node. Orchestrator's view, offered as a proposal and not as a settled point: a port need not be a stored node. Reasons: the buckets are derived data and the mirror is the source of truth, so a stored port is a second fact that must be kept consistent with the links it groups; a port can be identified by nodes that already exist, as the pair (target, an ancestor in the chosen hierarchy): \"the links to B whose source lies under A1\" or \"under the tag T\"; both hierarchies are already data (the `parent` tree of blocks, the IS-A tree of tags), so no new node is needed; the engine's capability is then \"build the reverse of a field partitioned along a hierarchy relation named by the caller (and to which levels)\", and a grouping the user wants to keep and name (an inbox, a curated set) is an ordinary node the caller creates. Against, honestly: a derived port is not addressable and costs a rebuild after a restart, as every lazy reverse does; a stored port could be linked and shown. In the same lazy spirit the partition could be made only when a bucket needs it. Not decided: the user has not agreed to any of this.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QDJ3T8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QDJ3T8001:links:Link:00D2QDJG78000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2Q908ER001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QDJ3T8001:links:Link:00D2QDJG78000:props:StringProp:00D2QDJG78001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "145"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2QDJ3T8001",
    "text": "**The user: \"lazy\" means when needed, not ephemeral; a partition, once a bucket has grown, should be kept across reloads** (2026-10-13, user, on [the view that a port is derived](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2Q908ER001) and \"the partition could be made only when a bucket needs it\"): \"Yep, that's the point. But by 'lazy' I mean 'when needed', not necessarily ephemeral. When a bucket has grown enough, it usually won't shrink and the partition should be kept across reloads.\" Orchestrator's reading and options, for the user to choose: the earlier reading that a reverse and its partition are derived and rebuilt flat after a restart is withdrawn for partitions; the decision to partition a bucket is taken once, when it outgrows, and must survive a reload. Two things could persist: (1) the decision only, a small record that the reverse of field F for target B is partitioned along hierarchy H to level L, while the bucket contents are still derived from the links at load and arranged as recorded; (2) the ports themselves as stored nodes, with their links. The orchestrator's view, a proposal: (1), because it keeps the mirror as the source of truth for the facts and the buckets as derived data, and the record is small; it could live as ordinary pairs on the target node (visible in the mirror and in git) or in a derived side file; (2) makes ports facts to keep consistent with the links and grows the mirror. Open for the user: which of the two, where the record of (1) lives, and whether a partition is ever undone (the user expects buckets not to shrink, so probably only by hand).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QFZENG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QFZENG001:links:Link:00D2QFZTX8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2QDJ3T8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QFZENG001:links:Link:00D2QFZTX8000:props:StringProp:00D2QFZTX8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "73"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2QFZENG001",
    "text": "**The user asks to elaborate \"the decision only\"** (2026-10-13, user, on [the two persistence options](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2QDJ3T8001)). Orchestrator's elaboration, an example and not a design: a block T (say the issue entry with 59 backlinks) gathers links; its reverse bucket for `target` is one flat set at first; when it has grown enough (a bound chosen by the caller or by a policy, not fixed here) the partition is made along a hierarchy relation named for it, for example the `parent` chain of the sources, to a given level, so that a port is the pair (T, an ancestor of the source) and a link goes to the port of its source's ancestor at that level; what is stored is only the decision, four values: the field (`target`), the object it concerns (T), the hierarchy relation (`parent`), the level; what is not stored is which link sits in which port, since that is computed from the links and the current hierarchy; at the next load the engine rebuilds the reverse for T from the links and arranges it as recorded, and a link added later goes straight to its port; if a source block moves, its port changes by itself, because ports are computed from the current chain, so no content goes stale; if the recorded hierarchy no longer exists, the bucket falls back to flat and nothing breaks; the record could live as ordinary pairs on T (new shape fields; visible in the mirror and in git; a write to T when its bucket grows, which is a problem if T belongs to a read-only or other graph) or in a derived side file under `.state/` (not in git, kept across reloads on the machine, lost on a fresh checkout and then re-made when the bucket needs it); the difference from storing the ports as nodes is that nothing but the four values is a fact. Open: who sets the bound, and where the record lives.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QM1YW8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QM1YW8001:links:Link:00D2QM2BAR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2QFZENG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QM1YW8001:links:Link:00D2QM2BAR000:props:StringProp:00D2QM2BB0000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "183"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2QM1YW8001",
    "text": "**The user's analogy: a stored port is a mailbox with the mail inside; a stored decision is only the shape of the port, fixed to receive whatever pairs fit it** (2026-10-13, user, on [the elaboration](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2QFZENG001)). Orchestrator: yes, with one refinement: in both cases the mail itself, the links, is already stored once as forward pairs on the sources; a stored port keeps a second copy of that mail in the box, which must be updated whenever a link is added, removed or its source moves; a stored decision keeps only the pigeonhole layout on the wall, and the mail is sorted into it from the forward pairs at each load and on each write, so a letter whose sender moved lands in its new pigeonhole by itself.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QNT1BR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QNT1BR001:links:Link:00D2QNTDJ0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2QM1YW8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QNT1BR001:links:Link:00D2QNTDJ0000:props:StringProp:00D2QNTDJ0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "84"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2QNT1BR001",
    "text": "**The user: the industry does cache indexes for fast reload** (2026-10-13, user, on [the mailbox analogy](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2QM1YW8001)). Orchestrator: yes; databases keep their indexes on disk (B-trees in SQLite and PostgreSQL, Lucene's segments, Oxigraph's own persistent backend keeps its index orderings on disk) and in-memory stores write snapshots so they need not rebuild at start. The distinction that matters here is between a fact and a cache: a cached index is derived data written for speed, with a rule to detect that it is stale (a version or a hash of the source it was built from) and to rebuild it then; it is never the source of truth. So caching does not change the choice between storing the ports and storing the decision: the decision is the fact; the built buckets, ports included, can be cached beside the mirror (a side file under `.state/`, keyed by the hashes of the mirror files, which the service already computes to detect a changed file) and rebuilt when the hashes differ. The cost is a second thing to keep right (stale-cache bugs); the gain is the time of a rebuild, which is small at today's size (the Coder's scratch JS store rehydrated the whole mirror in a fraction of a second, one run) and may grow with dense linking. The orchestrator's view, a proposal: design the cache as an optional layer behind the same interface, and build it when a measured rebuild time asks for it.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QVB430001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2QVB430001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: the stored decision (the port's shape, not the mailbox) is kept as metadata in `.state`; next question: how the caller sets the source hierarchy that the ports mirror** (2026-10-13, user: \"OK, nail down the stored decision (the port shape, not the mailbox) in a metadata in `.state`.\"). Settled, for the next design batch: when a reverse bucket is partitioned, only its shape (which field, which object, which hierarchy, which levels) is recorded, in a metadata file under `.state/` (not in the mirror and not in git); the contents of the ports are derived from the forward pairs at load and on each write. Orchestrator's options for the next question, a proposal for discussion: (A) the caller names a relation to climb from the source (`parent` for the HAS-A tree of blocks; a `broader`-like field for the IS-A tree of tags), and the levels to mirror, counted from the root of that hierarchy so that sources at different depths fall into the same ports (the artifact, then a top section, ...); (B) the caller registers a function from a source to its chain of port keys, the most general form, but a function is code and cannot be recorded as data, so the metadata would hold only its name and the code must exist at load; (C) a small declarative spec: where to start from the source (the source itself, or a field of it such as its tags), the relation to climb, and the levels. Two points the orchestrator raises: an IS-A hierarchy is usually not a tree (a tag can have several supertags), so a link would fall into several ports unless the spec chooses one path, which decides whether ports are a partition or overlapping groups; and the split can itself be lazy and recursive: a bucket splits at the first level, and a port that grows big splits at the next level, so the recorded shape is a tree of split decisions, which is the port tree. The orchestrator leans to (A) declared per field once by the layer above, with the per-bucket shape recorded when the split happens; not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QZ6FYG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QZ6FYG001:links:Link:00D2QZ6WQ0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2QVB430001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2QZ6FYG001:links:Link:00D2QZ6WQ0000:props:StringProp:00D2QZ6WQ0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "178"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2QZ6FYG001",
    "text": "**The user picks (A) and asks whether the caller can set a port size (a cap on the number of pairs) and let the engine choose the levels by itself** (2026-10-13, user, answering [the hierarchy options](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2QVB430001)). Orchestrator: yes; it is the recursive lazy split: when a bucket holds more pairs than the cap, the engine splits it by the sources' ancestors at the next level of the named hierarchy, counted from its root; any resulting port still over the cap splits at the level below; so each branch goes as deep as its own crowd needs, and a lopsided bucket (most links from one artifact) splits deep on that side only. Conditions to settle, a proposal: (1) the cap is soft: pairs whose sources share the same deepest ancestor, or have no place in the hierarchy (a source with no `parent`, such as the seven parentless artifacts), cannot be told apart by it and stay together in one port even above the cap; for the reverse of `target` this ends at one link per source block, so the split always ends; (2) splits are sticky: a port that has been split stays split when it shrinks, which is what the user expects (\"it usually won't shrink\") and is why the shape must be stored; without stickiness the shape would be a plain function of the data and the cap and could be recomputed at load instead of stored; (3) on an IS-A hierarchy with several supertags the climb needs one chosen path, or ports become overlapping groups instead of a partition; (4) the cap is set per field by the caller (or one value for all). Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2R93ZB8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2R93ZB8001:links:Link:00D2R94BM0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2QZ6FYG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2R93ZB8001:links:Link:00D2R94BM0000:props:StringProp:00D2R94BM0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "181"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2R93ZB8001:links:Link:00D2R94BM8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2QVB430001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2R93ZB8001:links:Link:00D2R94BM8000:props:StringProp:00D2R94BM8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1238"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2R93ZB8001",
    "text": "**The user: non-sticky splits need no stored decision; the root rationale of ports is top-down traversal of backlinks, to handle them in bulk within a scope** (2026-10-13, user, on [the cap conditions](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2QZ6FYG001)): \"If the splits are not sticky, we don't have to store any decision. Back to the root rationale for such a partition: the caller needs a way to traverse the backlinks top-down so that it can handle them in bulk. E.g.: when dealing with an issue about linking performance, we need only to check blocks pointing to the 'performance' concept that are scoped to {issues,discussion}/linking.md.\" Orchestrator's reading, for the user to confirm: what the caller needs is a scoped reverse query (the backlinks of T whose sources lie under given subtrees) and a top-down walk of the backlinks grouped by the sources' place in the hierarchy; ports are one way to make that fast; the answer of the query does not depend on the shape of the ports, so the shape is an internal detail of the engine; if so, non-sticky splits (shape a function of the data and the cap, recomputed at load) are enough and nothing need be stored, which would replace the stored-shape decision settled earlier ([that decision](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2QVB430001)) and needs the user's agreement to change. The example walked through: the pairs whose object is the \"performance\" concept are the bucket; a link's place in the tree is its owner block's place (the owner is found through the reverse of `links`); the engine keeps only the pairs whose owner lies under `issues/linking.md` or `discussion/linking.md`; with ports keyed at the artifact level this is two port lookups; without ports it is a filter that climbs each source's ancestors. That climb is the same one the thread-scope test makes for every block at a flush, so a derived record of each node's place (its artifact and its chain of ancestors), kept by the engine, would serve the scoped query, the thread scope and the derivation of the path alike; an observation, not a proposal yet.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RDF208001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RDF208001:links:Link:00D2RDFEM0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2R93ZB8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RDF208001:links:Link:00D2RDFEM0000:props:StringProp:00D2RDFEM0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "170"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2RDF208001",
    "text": "**The user is torn: ports should be stable within a task, not flicker mid-task, yet a dynamic partition following growth and shrink is attractive** (2026-10-13, user, on [the choice](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2R93ZB8001)). Orchestrator's two ways to have both, proposals: (1) separate what the caller sees from what the engine keeps: the caller asks for backlinks grouped or scoped by named places in the hierarchy (by artifact, by section, under these subtrees), which are stable for as long as the data is, and the engine's ports stay internal, dynamic and invisible, used only to answer fast; a split or merge then changes no answer and nothing flickers; (2) if the caller wants the engine's automatic grouping itself (the level chosen by the cap), make it dynamic with hysteresis: a port splits when it passes the cap and merges back only when it falls well below it, and the shape is recomputed at load; in practice it stays still within a task and needs no stored decision. The orchestrator leans to (1), which keeps the cap a matter of speed and the grouping a matter of meaning chosen by the caller. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RFASFG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RFASFG001:links:Link:00D2RFB5PG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2RDF208001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RFASFG001:links:Link:00D2RFB5PG000:props:StringProp:00D2RFB5PG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "145"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2RFASFG001",
    "text": "**The user asks whether option (1) means the engine does a secondary split and merge from its ports to the caller's view** (2026-10-13, user, on [the two options](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2RDF208001)). Orchestrator: yes, at query time and without storing a second structure: the caller names the level or the subtrees it wants (for example the backlinks of the \"performance\" concept grouped by artifact); because the ports follow the same hierarchy, every port boundary is a node of it, so the regrouping is only of two kinds: where the ports are finer than the asked level, the engine merges the child ports under that node; where a port is coarser (or the bucket was never split), the engine sorts that port's pairs on the fly by climbing each source to the asked level. The cap is what makes the second kind cheap: no port the engine has to sort holds more pairs than the cap, apart from the soft-cap residue of pairs the hierarchy cannot tell apart. So the cap bounds the work of a query, and the caller's grouping never depends on where the splits happen to be.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RJBE9G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2RJBE9G001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: option (1), separation of concerns between the caller's view and the engine's ports; next: how to keep the partitioning relation free of overlap** (2026-10-13, user: \"That SOC is a nice resolution! The remaining problem is how to constrain the partitioning relation to have no overlap (like the supertag issue you posed).\"). Settled, for the next design batch: the caller asks for backlinks scoped or grouped by named places of a hierarchy, which are stable as long as the data is; the engine's ports are internal, split and merged dynamically by a cap on their size, never stored, and the engine regroups from its ports to the asked view at query time (merging finer ports, sorting a coarser port on the fly); the cap bounds the work of a query. This replaces the earlier stored-shape decision. Orchestrator's proposal on overlap, not decided: (a) a relation can serve as a partition hierarchy only if each node has at most one value in it (a field of cardinality `one` or `optional`) and it has no cycle, so the hierarchy is a forest and every pair falls into exactly one port; `parent` is such a relation by its shape; (b) the engine checks both when a caller names the relation (it refuses a many-valued field) and keeps them as invariants while the relation is in use (a write that would give a node a second value or close a cycle is refused), which settles, for these relations at least, the open point on whether the engine enforces cardinality; (c) for IS-A, where a tag may have several supertags, the data keeps the many-valued relation for meaning and adds a single-valued field naming one primary supertag, which is the tree used for partitioning; (d) a question over the whole DAG (everything under T by any path) stays possible as a query that walks it and removes repeats, not as a partition. Rejected alternatives: the engine choosing one path by itself (implicit and surprising), and overlapping ports (a pair counted in several groups, so merges count it twice).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RMQ6J8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RMQ6J8001:links:Link:00D2RMQJK8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2RJBE9G001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RMQ6J8001:links:Link:00D2RMQJK8000:props:StringProp:00D2RMQJK8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "137"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2RMQ6J8001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: points 1 to 3 of the no-overlap proposal; point 4 to be elaborated with an example** (2026-10-13, user, answering [the proposal](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2RJBE9G001): \"1-3: clear, OK. 4: let's elaborate on it with example.\"). Settled, for the next design batch: a relation can serve as a partition hierarchy only if it is single-valued and acyclic; the engine checks both when the relation is named and refuses a write that would break them while it is in use; for IS-A the data keeps the many-valued relation for meaning and adds a single-valued primary-supertag field as the tree for partitioning. Orchestrator's example for point 4, offered for discussion: tags `quality`, `performance`, `user-experience`, `memory`, `latency`; primary supertags (the tree): `performance` and `user-experience` under `quality`, `memory` and `latency` under `performance`; one secondary supertag: `latency` is also under `user-experience`. A block B1 links to `memory`, a block B2 to `latency`, a block B3 to `user-experience`, and a block B4 to both `latency` and `user-experience`. Grouping by the partition, the backlinks under `quality` fall into the port of `performance` (B1, B2, B4 through `latency`) and the port of `user-experience` (B3, B4 through its own link); each pair is in one port, and `latency`'s links sit only under `performance`, its primary path. The question \"everything under `user-experience` by any path\" is answered as a query instead: the engine walks the many-valued IS-A downward from `user-experience` (its subtags by any supertag edge: `latency`), keeping a set of visited tags so that a cycle or a second path is walked once, collects the backlinks of each tag reached (B3 and B4 for `user-experience`, B2 and B4 for `latency`) and removes repeats (B4 once): B2, B3, B4. The partition alone would have given only B3 and B4 under `user-experience`. What the engine provides for this: the reverse of the IS-A relation, built on first use like any reverse, and a generic walk of a relation from a node with a visited set; the caller composes the walk and the backlink lookups, and each lookup is served by the ports.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RV7GM0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RV7GM0001:links:Link:00D2RV7WM8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2RMQ6J8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RV7GM0001:links:Link:00D2RV7WM8000:props:StringProp:00D2RV7WM8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "178"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2RV7GM0001",
    "text": "**The user asks whether the engine trims `latency` to `performance` to make a tree before partitioning, and whether the any-path query uses the partition** (2026-10-13, user, on [the example](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2RMQ6J8001)). Orchestrator's answer and a correction of its own example: (1) the engine trims nothing: the tree is a separate single-valued field in the data (the primary supertag), chosen by whoever writes the tags; the many-valued IS-A stays whole; in the example the tree keeps `latency` under `performance` (its primary) and the partition simply does not look at `latency` under `user-experience` (its secondary). (2) The example mixed two different things, which is the orchestrator's mistake: it grouped the links to the tags under `quality`, which is a walk over the hierarchy of the targets, the same thing as point 4's query, and not a partition. A port partitions the backlinks of one target by where their sources sit in a hierarchy: for example the backlinks of the concept `performance`, split by the primary tag of each source block (`issue`, `decision`, `measurement` under their supertags), or by the source's place in the tree of blocks; a source's secondary tags play no part in its port. (3) The any-path query does not use the partition for its walk, which follows the many-valued IS-A over the tags; for each tag it reaches it reads that tag's backlinks, and those buckets may be partitioned by their sources; the ports serve the query only when it also has a scope on the sources (for example \"under `user-experience` by any path, only in `issues/`\"); without a scope it takes each bucket whole, the union of its ports.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RXQ438001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RXQ438001:links:Link:00D2RXQH6R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2RV7GM0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RXQ438001:links:Link:00D2RXQH6R000:props:StringProp:00D2RXQH6R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "178"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2RXQ438001",
    "text": "**The user corrects the edge in the question: `latency` to `user-experience` is the one declared secondary from the start and excluded from the partition** (2026-10-13, user, on [the answer](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2RV7GM0001)): \"I stared at the edge to be kept and wrote it down instead of the other edge.\" Orchestrator: agreed, and nothing is trimmed: the secondary edge is marked as such in the data when it is written, and the partition reads only the primary field.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2RZHTV8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2RZHTV8001",
    "text": "**The user: the design is elegant, but the hierarchical port system should not live in a flat table, should it?** (2026-10-13, user). Orchestrator: right; a flat map from an object to its set of subjects cannot hold ports. A sketch of the implementation, a proposal and not a decision: (1) the reverse of a field stays a map from each object to its bucket, but a bucket is a small tree: either a leaf (a set of subjects) or a split node that records the level it splits at, a map from the ancestor at that level to a child bucket, a residue leaf for pairs whose source has no ancestor at that level, and a count of the pairs beneath it (the count drives splitting and merging and answers \"how many\" without a walk); (2) to place a pair the engine needs the source's ancestor at a given level, which is fast with a derived record per node of its depth and its chain of ancestors (the same record that would serve the thread scope and the path), kept up to date when a node moves; (3) for links the source is the link's owner block, found through the reverse of `links`; (4) a leaf over the cap becomes a split node by grouping its subjects by the next level; a split node whose count falls well below the cap folds back into a leaf (the hysteresis of option (2) applied inside the engine); (5) the costly case is a move in the hierarchy: when a node changes parent, every pair whose source lies under it may change port, in every partitioned bucket where it appears, so a move costs the size of the moved subtree; moves are rare (reconcile, a move with `insert --after`), and an alternative is to mark the touched buckets for rebuild on next use; (6) the forward side stays flat (a record per subject); only the reverse buckets that grow become trees. Whether this level belongs in `design/core.md` or in a later implementation note is for the user.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2SBMNC0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2SBMNC0001:links:Link:00D2SBN2BR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2RZHTV8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2SBMNC0001:links:Link:00D2SBN2BR000:props:StringProp:00D2SBN2BR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "178"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2SBMNC0001",
    "text": "**The user: the forward side is a tree too, by containment; records follow that topology; the split node is a record kept on the object, to be reciprocal** (2026-10-13, user, on [the implementation sketch](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2RZHTV8001), point 6 \"the forward side stays flat\"): \"No, it's a tree by the nature of containment, and the 'record per subject' follows this topology instead of staying in a flat table. The only diff is the manual hierarchy on the forward side vs the automatic hierarchy on the reverse side. And the 'split node', as you said, is actually a record, which should stay on the object to be reciprocal.\" Orchestrator's reading, for the user to confirm before anything is built on it: (1) the records are arranged in the containment tree, a node's record held under its container's, as the documents are in the mirror, and not in one flat table of all subjects; (2) the forward hierarchy is authored by hand (containment), the reverse hierarchy is made by the engine (split by the cap); (3) a node's record holds both sides: its outgoing pairs by field, and, once a field has been asked in reverse, its incoming pairs by field, as a leaf or as a split record of ports; so the bucket of T lives on T's record, reciprocal to the forward pairs living on the subject's record; point 6 of the sketch is withdrawn. Questions the orchestrator asks: (a) is there still a direct map from an id to its record, used to follow a link by id, beside the tree (an index of references, the data staying in the tree), or is every access a walk from a root; (b) when a subtree is dropped (the modularity reason), the incoming pairs held on its records go with it, but its outgoing pairs into nodes that stay must be removed from those nodes' reverse records: is that clean-up the engine's job.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2SJEZBR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2SJEZBR001:links:Link:00D2SJFBM0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2SBMNC0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2SJEZBR001:links:Link:00D2SJFBM0000:props:StringProp:00D2SJFBM0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "237"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2SJEZBR001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: a master map from id to record aids the tree and does not replace it; a droppable graph holds no incoming pairs from the remaining graph, and the engine cleans up its outgoing pairs** (2026-10-13, user, confirming [the reading](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2SBMNC0001) with \"Yep\" and answering its two questions: \"there's a master map from ID to record, but it aids the tree, not to replace the tree. The graph modularity is another long story, but in brief, the droppable graph should contain no incoming pairs from the remaining graph, and its outgoing pairs should be cleaned up by the engine.\"). Settled, for the next design batch: (1) records are arranged in the containment tree; a node's record holds its outgoing pairs by field and, once a field is asked in reverse, its incoming pairs as a leaf or a split record of ports, so a bucket lives on its object's record; the forward hierarchy is authored, the reverse hierarchy is made by the engine; (2) a master map from id to record sits beside the tree as an aid for direct access; (3) a graph can be dropped only if no pair from the remaining graph points into it, and on dropping it the engine removes its outgoing pairs from the reverse records of the nodes that remain; the rest of multi-graph modularity belongs to its own design. Orchestrator's observation: the first rule is already the citation rule of the threads (nothing above the thread tier links into `.threads/`), so the threads graph is droppable by construction, and the incoming records on its nodes are where the engine can check it before a drop.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2SRDGV0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The user checks the batch summary** (2026-10-13, user)",
    "text": "**The user checks the batch summary** (2026-10-13, user): item 7 confirmed (\"not stored\": nothing about ports is stored, which replaces the earlier stored-shape decision); hysteresis on merge, the soft cap and its residue: yes; the cap is set per field. Settled, added to the batch. The user asks whether \"named places of a hierarchy\" means specific levels. Orchestrator's answer and question: two things were meant: a scope names nodes of the hierarchy (the subtrees to search, such as `issues/linking.md` and `discussion/linking.md`), and a grouping names a level; a level can be counted as a depth from the root, or as the nearest ancestor of a kind (group by artifact, by top heading); the two differ because nodes of one kind sit at different depths (a `README.md` at the root, `design/core.md` under a folder), so a depth groups unlike things together and a kind does not; the engine's ports can answer either, since every port boundary is a node. Asked: is a grouping level a depth, a kind, or both. Still not settled: point 4 (the any-path walk), the path rule (a), the removal rule, the lazy-reverse example, the per-node place record, keep or remove for the three unagreed items in the design (the repeated-`@id` Open point, the disagreeing `parent`/`children` Open point, the Status links), and whether the implementation-level items go into `design/core.md` or a separate note.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2SZ0SX0001",
