    ],
    "title": "00D2SZ0SX0001",
    "text": "**The user replaces the separation of concerns with capturing the native partition as a view** (2026-10-13, user, after [the named-places question](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2SRDGV0001)): \"So, the SoC sounds nice but not practical! I think we can simply capture the partition instead: let the engine show its native port tree, capture it as a view and use it later on.\" Orchestrator's reading, for the user to confirm: (1) the caller sees the engine's own port tree of a bucket as it currently is (the dynamic split by the per-field cap); (2) when a stable grouping is needed, for a task, the caller captures that port tree into a view, which stays still while the engine's partition keeps changing; (3) later work goes through the captured view; the engine answers through it by the same regrouping as before (merging its finer ports, sorting a coarser one on the fly), with the captured view instead of a named level as the target, so the question of depth or kind for a level falls away; (4) the engine itself still stores nothing about ports; what is stored is the caller's view. Questions: (a) does a captured view keep only the shape (the boundary nodes of each port), so its contents are recomputed from the current pairs when used, or also the pairs it held at capture; (b) is it an ordinary `TreeView`, a lens over the graph like the views kept in `.state`, or a new kind of view; (c) how long does it live: for a task, removed with it, or until removed by hand.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2T2GBZ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D2T2GBZ8001:links:Link:00D2T2GRAR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2SZ0SX0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D2T2GBZ8001:links:Link:00D2T2GRAR000:props:StringProp:00D2T2GRAR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "210"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D2T2GBZ8001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: a captured port view keeps only the shape, is a kind of view of its own (like a `TreeView` but distinct), and is removed by hand as it was captured by hand** (2026-10-13, user, answering [the reading and questions](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D2SZ0SX0001)). Settled, for the batch, replacing option (1) and the question of named levels: the engine shows its own port tree of a bucket as it currently is (split by the per-field cap, with hysteresis on merge and a soft cap with its residue); the caller may capture that tree as a view that keeps only the shape (the boundary node of each port), so its contents are recomputed from the current pairs when the view is used; the engine answers through a captured view by merging its finer ports or sorting a coarser one on the fly; the engine stores nothing about ports; the captured view is a kind of its own, removed only by hand. Open, not settled: where captured views are kept (by analogy with views, under `.state/`) and what they are called.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2T3MN4G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2T3MN4G001",
    "text": "**The user: captured port views live under `.state`; write the batch down** (2026-10-13, user: \"Yeah, the view should be under `.state`. Let's write them down.\"). Settled: captured port views are kept under `.state/`. The batch goes to the Grapher in one turn, into `design/core.md`. What the user has not settled stays out or stays marked: the name of the captured view (an Open point), the any-path walk, the path rule (a), the removal rule, the lazy-reverse example, the per-node place record, and the three items already in the design that the user never agreed to (the Open point on a repeated `@id`, the Open point on a file whose `parent` and `children` disagree, the Status links to four issues). The orchestrator's addition, for the user to correct: the Grapher marks in the design every statement that came from an orchestrator proposal the user has not agreed to, as \"proposal, not agreed\", so the document does not present them as decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
