    "title": "**Records follow containment**",
    "text": "**Records follow containment**: the records are arranged in the containment tree, a node's record under its container's, as the documents are in the mirror, and not in one flat table of all subjects. The forward hierarchy is authored (containment); the reverse hierarchy, the ports, is made by the engine (see [Ports](#id/BlockNode:00D2T5VG08001)). The engine aims to keep a node's record as close as possible to its document in the mirror, to reduce the shell's work: its literal fields as plain properties, the values of its reference fields, and its embedded nodes nested in the fields that own them. This is an aim and not a guarantee, and the shell always bridges the gap between a record and its document. A record also carries what the file does not hold: the reverse sides of its fields, with their ports, each built the first time the field is asked in reverse (see [Reverse, for each field, built on first use](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR017)). One master map from id to record sits beside the tree as an aid for direct access and does not replace the tree; it serves every graph, since an id is unique across all graphs (see [Ids](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01N)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR00D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2T5VG08001",
