    ],
    "title": "**Ports**",
    "text": "**Ports**: a bucket is split into incoming ports (see [Ports, the two ends of a pair](#id/BlockNode:00D3CJCK10001)) when it outgrows the cap set for its field. The shell sets the cap of each field and names the [partitioning field](#id/BlockNode:00D3CJDGH8001) of the sources that the split follows, such as containment for the tree of blocks; the engine chooses the levels: a bucket over the cap is split by the ancestors of its sources at the next level of that hierarchy, and a port still over the cap is split at the level below, so each branch goes only as deep as its own pairs need. A split merges back only when its count falls well below the cap, so the ports do not change back and forth around it. The cap is soft: pairs that the hierarchy cannot tell apart (sources with the same deepest ancestor, or with no place in the hierarchy) stay together in one port, the residue, even above the cap. Nothing about ports is stored: their shape follows from the current pairs and the caps.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR015",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2T5XBH0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
