  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D2T8J308001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D2T8J308001",
    "text": "**Verification — the batch written into `design/core.md` (`design-core-draft` Turn 6, Report `BlockNode:00D2T86Y2R008`)** (2026-10-13, orchestrator): the design has 113 live blocks, 11 \"(proposal, not agreed)\" marks, no code fence and no figure (only the date); the any-path walk, the lazy-reverse example, the per-node place record and the multi-graph content are absent, as the Ask required; `aperas check-links` is clean; added: records follow containment (data model), ports, a partition hierarchy and captured port views (the index), ports and captured views in the interface, and an Open point on the captured view's name; replaced: the reverse, the layout (now with the droppable-graph rule), the files (captured views under `.state/`) and enforcement (decided for partition relations). The Grapher's four questions go to the user: where the reverse bucket of a literal value lives (a literal has no record); whether \"outgoing pairs by field\" changes the forward one-list-per-subject rule; whether to audit two more statements that may be proposals (the scope test above the engine, the mark-and-sweep deletion); whether the captured view should appear in the fields per class and the format.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D38VHG0R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D38VHG0R001",
    "text": "**Exchanges of 2026-10-14 while the service was stopped (idle shutdown overnight; started by the user), recorded together** (user and orchestrator): (1) the user, reading `design/core.md`, found that \"The data model\" introduces triples and then speaks of \"pairs\" without saying where they come from, and mentions \"quad\" from nowhere; the opening reads \"The engine stores triples (a subject, a field, a value) ... The fourth position of a quad is not used.\" and the next item says \"every pair\"; \"quad\" is a leftover of Oxigraph (also in \"never a pair or a quad\", \"never a term or a quad\", and the helper name `addQuad`). The orchestrator proposed a new opening; the user corrected it: \"in RDF terms these are triples (subject, field, value) whose predicate (field) never varies\", and rejected the asymmetric \"a record holds its pairs by field, the reverse groups them by value\": the symmetric model is, on a node, the forward side of a field holds the pairs this → value and the reverse side holds the pairs subject → this; grouping by field is a meta-grouping, the fixing of the predicate, done before the pairs are managed. Orchestrator's rewording, not yet confirmed by the user: \"The engine stores pairs. Each field is a fixed relation, and a pair (s, v) is one link of that relation from a subject s to a value v; in RDF terms these are triples (subject, field, value) whose predicate (field) never varies. Fixing the field comes first: pairs are managed field by field. On a node n, each field has two sides: the forward side holds the pairs n → v, where n is the subject; the reverse side holds the pairs s → n, where n is the value. Each pair sits on two records: its subject's forward side and its value's reverse side.\" (2) The user asked whether non-reference fields are worth managing by the engine at all, or are plain JS properties with no index. Orchestrator, from the census: most literal fields (`title`, `text`, `type`, the hashes, `tombstonedAt`, `holder`, the sibling index, a `StringProp`'s `key` and `value`) are read only forward and can be plain properties of the record (an absent one is `undefined`); three are read by value: `path` (to become a walk down by title once the path is derived from the parent chain, as decided), `handle` and `name` (unique by design; a small unique-key map per such field could serve them instead of the generic reverse); `type` may turn into a reference to a concept node later. Not decided. (3) The user asked whether the current system does every lookup through Oxigraph's quads: yes; `encodeDoc` turns every field of every document into quads, a node object holds only the store and its id, every field read is a `match` and a decode (about 170,000 at one flush), reverse lookups use the predicate-object index, and `allIdsOfKind` is a full `match`. (4) The user: \"the pure RDF thinking! My model shifts it from that edge-based graph (RDF) to node-based graph (LPG) ... Using RDF for node-heavy ops is such a waste.\" Orchestrator: mostly yes for this workload, with the measured cost split into three causes: the edge layout (a lookup per property, most of them probes for absent fields), the wasm boundary (the Coder's triple-shaped plain JS store rehydrated in 174 ms against 2,050 ms, one run) and algorithms (the write cost). (5) The user: the wasm boundary is not RDF but amplifies its mismatch with this workload, and asks to elaborate on the algorithms; answered next.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D38VNQX0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D38VNQX0001:links:Link:00D38VP588000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D38VNQX0001:links:Link:00D38VP588000:props:StringProp:00D38VP588001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "563"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D38VNQX0001",
    "text": "**The wasm boundary as an amplifier, and the algorithmic costs of a write** (2026-10-14, user and orchestrator): the user: the wasm boundary is not RDF but amplifies the mismatch of RDF with this workload; orchestrator: yes: the boundary charges per crossing (wrapper objects for a quad and its terms, decoding, freeing), the edge layout sets the number of crossings (one per property read), so the cost is the product and RDF inflates the second factor; a node layout behind the same boundary would cross once per node. The algorithms, from the Coder's profile ([the filed cost of a write](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001); 3 fresh processes, 6 inserts per case, a copy of the mirror): a small local change recomputes everything: (1) the path-collision check rebuilds the slug path of every live block (about 7,400), climbing each to its artifact, for one inserted paragraph: about 170,000 lookups, 1.56 s, whatever the artifact; a new block can collide only with its siblings, so a local check would do; (2) two link sweeps of the target artifact around each write parse every block's text (0.83 s and 0.78 s on the big Freeflow document, milliseconds on a small one); only the written blocks changed; (3) the timed flush within 10 s of a write rewrites all four content files: four full scans, 1.4 s, synchronous; a write usually touches one file; (4) the thread-scope test at each flush climbs every block to its owner (14,368 path reads in one flush); (5) `allIdsOfKind` scans the whole store for each of its 16 callers. These costs grow with the corpus, not with the change; a faster engine makes each lookup cheaper but not fewer, so they need fixing in the code whatever the engine; all five are filed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D39083TG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D39083TG001:links:Link:00D3908FW8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D38VNQX0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D39083TG001:links:Link:00D3908FW8000:props:StringProp:00D3908FW8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "189"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D39083TG001",
    "text": "**The user on two of the algorithmic costs: the path check rebuilds shared ancestors; a flush could be a plain dump if the records in memory have the mirror's shape** (2026-10-14, user, on [the algorithms](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D38VNQX0001)): \"path-collision check: why rebuilding the common ancestors while we're all sharing them!?\"; \"The flush: if we set the objects in mem the same as the mirror, a flush then will be just a dump, isn't it? (The same for threads and other graphs.) Or else, we must do the harder work of partitioning the huge BlockNode.jsonld.\" Orchestrator: (1) yes: `toPath` climbs from each block on its own (read in `node.ts`), so the slug path of a shared ancestor is recomputed once for every block below it; building the paths top-down, each node's path from its parent's, would compute each once, and a new block can only collide with its siblings, so no full rebuild is needed at all. (2) Yes, with conditions: if a node's record in memory has the shape of its document in the mirror (the same fields in the shape's order, embedded `Link` and `StringProp` nested in their owner, as records already follow containment), a flush is a serialization of the records of each file in id order, with no reconstruction from pairs; what remains to do at dump: the documents sorted by id and set fields sorted as the format requires (or kept sorted), and the `children` arrays written out from the reverse of `parent`, ordered by sibling index, since `children` is derived; the threads, held as their own records, are dumped to `.state/` without the scope test. Dumping and partitioning are separate choices: a dump still writes the whole `BlockNode.jsonld` at each flush, which is cheap at today's size (not measured for a plain JS dump; the Coder's scratch JS store dehydrated both mirrors in about half a second, one run); partitioning the file (by artifact or by graph) would make a flush write only what changed and shrink git diffs, but changes the mirror's format, the source of truth. Orchestrator's question: is a record shaped as its mirror document the intent, and is the dump first and the partitioning later the order.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3936PG8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3936PG8001:links:Link:00D393735R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D39083TG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3936PG8001:links:Link:00D393735R000:props:StringProp:00D393735R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "238"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3936PG8001",
    "text": "**The user: `children` comes from the reverse map that is already there; the order is dump first, partition later; keep things sorted so each add is an insertion paid at operation time, not at load or flush** (2026-10-14, user, answering [the flush questions](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D39083TG001)). Settled: dump first and partition later; keep the orders the mirror needs maintained as each write happens, so a flush only writes. Orchestrator: (1) yes, the reverse of `parent` is always present (every `.children` reads it), so the `children` arrays at a dump come from it; what remains is their order by sibling index, which keeping each container's children ordered on insert removes; this sits beside the earlier decision that a reverse bucket is a set for removal, so the children of a container would be an ordered bucket, a case of its own; (2) the other orders: documents by id within a file, where new ids have so far been issued in increasing order (observed, not guaranteed), so an insert is mostly an append; set fields sorted as the format requires; (3) a finding to file, since filing is a must: the census reports that `dehydrate` sorts set fields with `localeCompare`, whose order can depend on the runtime's locale, so the byte-for-byte round trip could differ between machines; a plain code-point order would not, but changing it reorders the existing files once; not checked beyond the census. Still to confirm by the user: that a node's record has the shape of its document in the mirror.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D39668M8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D39668M8001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: code-point order for the mirror's sorting; a node's record as close as possible to its mirror document** (2026-10-14, user: \"Yeah, it should be code-point-sorted. A node's record should have as similar shape as possible to the mirror. It cannot have the exact shape due to additional derived structures like reverse fields with port partitioning, though.\"). Settled, for the next design batch: the mirror's orders (documents by id, set fields) use plain code-point comparison, not a locale-dependent one; a node's record in memory follows the shape of its document in the mirror as closely as possible, so a flush is a dump, and carries in addition the derived structures that the file does not hold (the reverse sides of fields, with their ports). With the earlier points of this exchange (dump first, partition later; orders kept on each write so a flush only writes; `children` written from the reverse of `parent`, kept ordered by sibling index). Gap to file, since today's `dehydrate` sorts set fields with `localeCompare` (from the census), against the decided code-point order.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D399KSRG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D399KSRG001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: lookup maps by a literal value are the caller's, not the engine's; the user asks where to record the rationale of the move from RDF to LPG and the analysis of Oxigraph's mismatch** (2026-10-14, user: \"So the key maps are additional thing added by the caller, not managed by the engine.\"). Settled, for the next design batch: the engine manages pairs for reference and embed fields only; literal fields are plain properties of the record, with no reverse side; a lookup by a literal value (a profile by `handle`, a view by `name`) is served by a map that the caller adds and keeps. Orchestrator's proposal for the placement, following the concern taxonomy: (1) the rationale, which lasts, goes into `design/core.md` under Purpose and scope as a short \"why a node-based engine\": the workload is node-heavy (whole-node reads, tree rendering, a dump per flush), so properties live on the node and only relations between nodes are pairs; RDF's edge layout pays a lookup per property, which suits arbitrary pattern queries over an open vocabulary that Aperas does not have; rules only, no figures, linking the issues that hold the evidence; (2) the measured analysis of the mismatch, with its figures and sample sizes, goes into `history/core.md`, extending the milestone of 2026-10-12 (Oxigraph patching paused, the JS engine begun): the three causes (the edge layout, the wasm boundary that multiplies its cost per crossing, and the algorithms of a write, which no engine fixes) with links to the issues; (3) the algorithmic costs stay where they are filed, in `issues/core.md`. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D39ASRR0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D39ASRR0001:links:Link:00D39AT58R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D399KSRG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D39ASRR0001:links:Link:00D39AT58R000:props:StringProp:00D39AT58R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "84"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**The user: write them** (2026-10-14, user",
    "text": "**The user: write them** (2026-10-14, user: \"Nice! Let's write them.\"), agreeing to [the placement](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D399KSRG001): the rationale of a node-based engine in `design/core.md`, the measured analysis of Oxigraph's mismatch in `history/core.md`, the algorithmic costs where they are filed. The batch for the Grapher, all settled in the user's words: the data model's opening in the user's terms (triples (subject, field, value) whose predicate (field) never varies; grouping by field is the fixing of the predicate, done first; on a node, the forward side of a field holds the pairs this → value and the reverse side the pairs subject → this; no mention of quads); literal fields as plain properties of the record with no reverse side, lookups by a literal value served by maps the caller adds and keeps; a record as close as possible to its mirror document, plus the derived reverse sides with their ports; a flush as a dump; dump first, partition later; the orders kept on each write (documents by id, set fields, a container's children by sibling index, written out from the reverse of `parent`); code-point order for the mirror's sorting. Two findings to file: today's `dehydrate` sorts set fields with `localeCompare` against the decided code-point order; and the path-collision check recomputes the slug path of every shared ancestor once per block below it (`toPath` climbs from each block on its own), an addition to the filed cost of a write.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D39DRYB0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D39DRYB0001:links:Link:00D4RADWFG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR01S",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D39DRYB0001:links:Link:00D4RADWFG000:props:StringProp:00D4RMNHY8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "564"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Literal fields are properties of the record**",
    "text": "**Literal fields are properties of the record**: a literal field is a plain property of its node's record, read and written there, with no pairs and no reverse side. The engine manages pairs for reference and embed fields only. A lookup by the value of a literal field (a profile by its `handle`, a view by its `name`, an artifact or a folder by its stored `path` as long as it is stored) is served by a map that the shell adds and keeps; the engine keeps no such map. Once the path is derived, the same map is keyed by the derived path, which no field holds (see [The path is derived from the parent chain](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01S)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR00D",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D39EAKW0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D39EAKW0001:links:Link:00D3EK364R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D2T5P9Y8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D39EAKW0001:links:Link:00D3EK364R000:props:StringProp:00D3EK364R001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "90"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D39EAKW0001:links:Link:00D3EK364R002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR01G",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D39EAKW0001:links:Link:00D3EK364R002:props:StringProp:00D4N0F9FG000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "535"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**A flush is a dump**",
    "text": "**A flush is a dump**: the engine aims to keep a node's record close to its document (see [Records follow containment](#id/BlockNode:00D2T5P9Y8001)), so a flush serializes the engine's records of each file and rebuilds nothing from pairs. The aim is not a guarantee, and the dump bridges whatever differs between a record and its document, the orders of the file among it: the shell sorts the documents of a file and the embedded entries of each set field by id, and writes the children of each container in the order of its list (see [Orders in the mirror](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01G)). Each graph is flushed at an interval of its own. A flush still writes each class file whole; partitioning the files, so that a flush writes only what changed, comes later, and it changes the mirror's format.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHMYQG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
