    "title": "**Why a node-based engine**",
    "text": "**Why a node-based engine**: the workload is node-heavy: commands read whole nodes, render trees of them, and write the mirror by a dump at each flush. So the properties of a node live on its record, and only the relations between nodes are pairs (see [The data model](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR00D)). An edge layout, which holds every property as a statement of its own, pays a lookup for each property read; it suits arbitrary pattern queries over an open vocabulary, and Aperas has neither: its vocabulary is closed and the shell asks no such query (see [Closed vocabulary](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR00E) and [Not offered](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000H)). The evidence is filed in [the full-store scans](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D0W02KPG001) and [the cost of a write](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D22Z1HJ8001), and the move away from the edge layout is recorded in [the history](../history/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D21FP4ZG009).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87SR006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
