  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D39GD568001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D39GD568001",
    "text": "**Verification — the second batch (`design-core-draft` Turn 7, Report `BlockNode:00D39G1H98002`)** (2026-10-14, orchestrator): `design/core.md` has 116 live blocks, the same 11 \"(proposal, not agreed)\" marks, no figure (only the date), and \"quad\" only in the marked Open point on a repeated `@id`, which the Grapher was told not to touch; \"Why a node-based engine\" is under Purpose and scope; `history/core.md` has four new entries under the milestone of 2026-10-12; the `localeCompare` issue is `00D39EY5B0001` and the shared-ancestor finding is added to the cost of a write (`00D39EVFH8001`); `aperas check-links` is clean over 8,101 live blocks. The Grapher also adjusted the statements that literal fields as plain properties contradict (the sibling index is a literal, a field-relation covers reference and embed fields only, no literal key in a reverse), which answers its earlier question on a literal's reverse bucket. Its questions, for the user: the `localeCompare` issue covers the order of documents as well as of set fields, since the code sorts both that way and the decision covers both; \"Not offered\" still says the engine has no dump of the whole store, against \"a flush is a dump\"; the interface's writes do not name setting a literal property. In parallel the orchestrator listed for the user the eleven marked proposals ([the list is in the chat]), with the conflict between the marked path rule and literal `path` as a plain property.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D39K30TR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D39K30TR001",
    "text": "**The user decides on three of the marked proposals and asks for the others to be explained** (2026-10-14, user, on the list of the eleven \"(proposal, not agreed)\" marks). (1) Items 2 and 3, the per-kind id sets: \"What do we need it for? With the new design, we will store the records by kinds and even by location (main, threads, other graphs) already.\" Orchestrator: agreed: if the records are kept by kind (and by graph), \"all ids of kind K\" is an iteration over that collection and needs no set of its own; the callers of `allIdsOfKind` (the dump per kind, the link sweep, the tombstone sweeps, the view audit, track and rename) iterate the collection; the per-kind set is dropped as a structure, and removing a node removes it from its kind's collection. (2) Items 4 and 5, tombstones: the user chooses option (c) of the Open point \"A removed node among its container's children\" with a purpose: a tombstoned node loses all its references, `parent` included, so it is in no path (once the duplicated stored path is removed from the implementation); it stays only to catch the incoming links, so they do not dangle. Orchestrator: this needs one rule the design's option (c) text did not have: the sweep that deletes tombstones reached by no mark from the roots would delete such a node and leave its incoming links dangling; so a tombstone is kept while any pair points at it (its record's reverse side shows its incoming pairs) and deleted only when none does. Asked: whether the tombstone keeps its literal properties (`title`, `type`, `text`) so a dead link can still show what it pointed at. (3) The user asks for the other points to be explained: given in the chat (the Status links to four issues, whether the engine knows \"live\", the Open points on a repeated `@id` and on a disagreeing `parent` and `children`, and the two unmarked statements: the scope test above the engine, and the mark-and-sweep deletion).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
