    ],
    "title": "00D3AJYX38001",
    "text": "**The user corrects the tombstone sweep rule and decides containment and the disagreement rule** (2026-10-14, user, on [the decisions on items 2 to 5](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D39K30TR001)). (1) The sweep: a tombstone is removed when no incoming reference from a node reachable from the root points at it; once no reachable link points at it, the tombstone has done its role and goes; \"kept while any pair points at it\" is wrong, because an island of mutually referencing nodes unreachable from the root would keep itself. Orchestrator's correction of its own statement, after reading `pruneUnreachableTombstones` (`node.ts`): today's sweep is already this rule: the mark starts from the live artifacts and folders and walks the structural children and the `target` of each visited node's links, so a tombstone linked from a reachable node is marked and kept, and a dead island is deleted (the code's comment records that counting referrers was rejected for exactly the cycle case); the orchestrator's claim that the sweep would delete a tombstone taken out of its container and leave links dangling was wrong. (2) Settled: a tombstone keeps its literal properties, so stale incoming links can check its content to update their reference. (3) Settled, on `BlockNode:00D24N87SR00Z` (Containment) and `BlockNode:00D2562RC8001` (where a node's parent comes from): `children` is not purely derived, it carries the order; in theory `children` equals the reverse of `parent` plus the sibling index, so either `children` or `parent` plus the index would be enough; in practice both `children` and `parent` are stored, redundantly, for clarity, instead of the sibling index; when they disagree, `parent` wins (as today), and since it carries no position the node is appended at the end of its parent's `children` with a warning. This replaces the design's statements that `children` is derived from the reverse of `parent`, that the engine has no `children` field, that the load absorbs a repeated `parent` pair, that the sibling index is a literal property, and the Open point on a file whose `parent` and `children` disagree (item 7, now decided); for the next batch.",
