    ],
    "title": "00D3AK09Y0001",
    "text": "**The user decides the remaining explained points, the rewording of containment, multiple graphs, and who starts a stopped service** (2026-10-14, user, answering [the sweep correction and containment](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3AJYX38001)). (1) \"Whoever sees the service down should start it, don't ask me all the time\": a standing rule for every role; the service was found stopped again (idle timer) and the orchestrator started it (another start had already begun; it answered at 09:47Z); the skills today tell a subagent not to restart the service, which this rule changes (a skill change, for a later Coder turn with the user's go); saved as a memory. (2) `BlockNode:00D24N87SR00Z` and `BlockNode:00D2562RC8001` are to be reworded: the current text reads as if `children` were purely derived, which is untrue; the sibling index is hidden in the stored `children` (its position); today the index is extracted from the stored `children` first, and only then is `children` derived; with the decision just recorded, the new engine stores both `children` (ordered) and `parent` and keeps no separate index. (3) The Status links to the four issues: agreed. (4) The engine manages only references, and a tombstone is an intrinsic semantics of a reference, so the engine manages tombstones too (item 4b decided: the engine knows a tombstone). (5) Nodes with a repeated `@id` are rejected at load, and the load goes on with the other nodes (item 6 decided); orchestrator's reading, for the user to correct: every document carrying the repeated id is rejected, none of them is kept, and the repeat is reported. (6) The engine supports multiple graphs, like the stores of Oxigraph, each with its own mirror and its own settings (such as the flush interval); the rest of multi-graph modularity stays in its own design; orchestrator's reading: the threads become a graph with its mirror in `.state/`, so the scope test of a node becomes the graph it belongs to (the unmarked point on the scope test), for the user to confirm. Note: the first insert of this entry lost its code spans to a shell quoting mistake of the orchestrator (an unquoted heredoc ran the backticked words as commands, harmlessly) and was rewritten.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
