    ],
    "title": "00D3APPWP0000",
    "text": "**The user clarifies the restart rule, confirms two readings, and asks whether stored `children` and `parent` make special fields or call for an order feature** (2026-10-14, user, on [the decisions](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3AK09Y0001)). (1) The skills' \"do not restart the service\" is about a restart, not about starting a stopped one: it is there not to disturb a busy service, which usually makes things worse; so no skill change is needed, and the memory was corrected. (2) Confirmed: every document carrying a repeated `@id` is rejected at load, none is kept, and the repeat is reported; the scope test of a node becomes the graph it belongs to. (3) The user's question: storing both `children` and `parent` with no separate index would make them special fields; or should the engine have an order feature? Orchestrator's two options, a proposal: (a) containment is the engine's one built-in relation: `children` an ordered list of references on the container, `parent` its declared inverse, both stored, kept consistent by the engine, with the load rule already decided (`parent` wins, the node goes to the end of `children` with a warning); containment is already special in this design, since records follow it, ports climb it, the sweep marks along it and the dump writes it; (b) a general feature: an ordered cardinality for any reference field, plus a declared inverse between two fields that the engine keeps consistent, with containment as one instance (today's shapes already name `orderedContainment` as a cardinality of its own). The orchestrator leans to (a): no other field needs an order kept by the engine (the order of links, props and unfolds is imposed by their readers, as decided), and (b) can come later with (a) as its first instance. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3ATVG7G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3ATVG7G001:links:Link:00D3ATVX48000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3APPWP0000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3ATVG7G001:links:Link:00D3ATVX48000:props:StringProp:00D3ATVX48001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "239"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3ATVG7G001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: option (b), a general order and inverse feature; the engine manages only forward and reverse links in memory and leaves dehydrating and rehydrating to the caller; multiple graphs in two folds** (2026-10-14, user, on [the order question](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3APPWP0000)): \"I prefer (b), pushing the boundary of the engine back to only manage the forward & reverse links in memory, leaving the de/rehydrate to the caller. Then, the 'multiple graphs' feature is actually 2 folds: of the engine in memory and of the caller on disk.\" (The user also noted with surprise that today's shapes name `orderedContainment` as a cardinality of its own.) Orchestrator's restatement, for the next batch and for the user to correct: the engine, in memory: records of nodes with literal properties; reference fields with a forward and a reverse side (the reverse built on first use, with ports); field declarations with a cardinality that may be ordered and an optional declared inverse that the engine keeps consistent (containment is one instance: `children` ordered, `parent` its inverse); tombstones; several in-memory graphs (stores) with the rule for dropping one. The caller, on disk: the mirror's files per graph and kind, the load (rejecting every document of a repeated `@id`; when `parent` and `children` disagree, `parent` wins and the node goes to the end of `children` with a warning), the flush as a dump with its interval per graph, code-point order, dump first and partitioning later, maps for lookups by a literal value, any cache. Questions the orchestrator asks: (1) with the boundary moved, does \"a record as close as possible to its mirror document\" stay, as a contract that lets the caller dump the engine's records cheaply, or is the shape of a record now the engine's own business and the caller converts; (2) should `design/core.md` keep the mirror as a section marked as the caller's layer, or should the persistence move to a document of its own; (3) when the loader feeds both `children` and `parent` of a declared inverse pair, the engine would derive one from the other: the orchestrator's reading is that the caller feeds one side (the `parent`, which wins, with the order taken from `children`) and only checks the other.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3B0C460000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3B0C460000:links:Link:00D3B0CHG8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3ATVG7G001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3B0C460000:links:Link:00D3B0CHG8000:props:StringProp:00D3B0CHG8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "365"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3B0C460000",
    "text": "**The user: refactor the design so the boundary between engine and caller is clear, with persistence as the caller's layer; the engine's general features: marking and ensuring a field as \"partitioning\", and defining and managing ordered fields, the latter to be discussed deeper (\"a pair of fields ... interesting ... but I feel a wrinkle\")** (2026-10-14, user, on [the moved boundary](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3ATVG7G001)). Settled: the design is to be refactored around the boundary, persistence being the caller's layer (a design update for a batch, when the user says); the engine offers a partitioning mark on a field, which it ensures (each node has at most one value on the side being climbed, no cycle), used by the ports. Orchestrator's reading of the wrinkle, for the user to confirm or correct: in this model every reference field already has a reverse side, so `parent` is the reverse of `children`; declaring `parent` as a second field and keeping the two consistent brings back inside the engine the redundancy the model had removed; the redundancy belongs to the mirror, which the caller writes for clarity. Proposal: one field, `children`, declared ordered and partitioning: ordered means the forward side is a list whose order the engine keeps; partitioning means each node appears in at most one container's list and the relation has no cycle, so the reverse side of `children` is single-valued and `parent` is that reverse, always built because climbing needs it; the caller's loader reads `parent` and orders by the file's `children`, appending a node with a warning when they disagree, and its dump writes `parent` from the reverse. Two further points for the deeper discussion: order lives on the forward side only (the authored list), the reverse side stays a set or ports, which is an asymmetry inside the otherwise symmetric model; and an ordered field keeps set semantics (no identical pair twice), so a reference repeated at two positions needs two subjects, as links do. Open: the operations of an ordered field (insert before or after an anchor, move, remove) and how positions are named.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BFNYYR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BFNYYR001:links:Link:00D3BFPC48000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3B0C460000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BFNYYR001:links:Link:00D3BFPC48000:props:StringProp:00D3BFPC48001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "313"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3BFNYYR001",
    "text": "**The user on the wrinkle of ordered fields: the full reverse of `children` is `parent` plus the sibling index; a unique list is a set plus order; taking only `parent` as the reverse cuts the order off; asks how `Link`, which carries even more (positions in the text), is dealt with today** (2026-10-14, user, on [the ordered-field discussion](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3B0C460000)): the wrinkle dates from the TerminusDB era (worked around with cons cells); leaving RDF removes the need for the workaround but not the question, which is one of semantics and isomorphism, not of implementation; the set semantics stay and order is added: a (unique) List = Set + Order, not the general List = Multiset + Order. Orchestrator's answer, from `shape.ts` and the census: a block's `links` field holds embedded `Link` nodes; each `Link` has `target` (a reference), `predicate` (a literal such as `[[wikilink]]`) and `props`, embedded `StringProp` nodes, one per occurrence of the target in the block's text, each with key `position` and the offset as its value; a block has one `Link` per target, and a target cited twice has two `position` props on that one `Link` (four such links in the mirror). So the relation from a block to a target is reified as a node of its own, and everything about it (the predicate, the positions) sits on that node, reachable from both ends: the block reaches it through `links`, the target through the reverse of `target`; nothing is cut off on either side. Containment is the opposite case: it is not reified, and its extra information (the order) lives on one side, in the container's list (in today's code as a literal sibling index on the child). Ways to make an ordered relation isomorphic, for discussion: (1) reify it as links are (a node per containment, heavy); (2) let a pair carry properties, an order key among them, stored once and seen from both sides, with keys chosen so an insertion between two neighbours needs no renumbering (which also removes the density question); (3) accept the cut: order on the forward side only, with the position of a child found from its container's list. An aside for later: in the new model a link's positions could be a literal list property of the `Link` instead of one `StringProp` node per occurrence. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BSXFN0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BSXFN0001:links:Link:00D3BSXWCR000",
