            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BSXFN0001:links:Link:00D3BSXWCR000:props:StringProp:00D3BSXWCR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "344"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3BSXFN0001",
    "text": "**The user: a `Link` is an outgoing port on its block, and a list item is a pair whose key, its position, is an outgoing port on its host; ports extend the deep path (`folder/artifact/section/block:Link`, `folder/artifact/section/block:order`), as a block is a port of its section; so the correct reverse is to the port** (2026-10-14, user, on [the Link answer](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3BFNYYR001)): in `Link` two things were done: the source moved from the block to the `Link`, each `Link` being an outgoing port launched from instead of the block itself; and the position was factored out into a prop. Option (2) reads as: a list item is itself a pair whose subject (key) is its position, a port on the host. Orchestrator's restatement and evidence, for the user to confirm: every relation leaves its host through a port, a sub-address of the host that continues its deep path; the reverse of a relation points at the port, which knows its host and its key, so nothing is cut off: the reverse of `children` for a child is the slot `container:key`, giving the parent and the order together; the reverse of `target` is the `Link`, giving the block and the positions. Evidence that the id scheme already has this shape: embedded nodes carry their owner in their id, for example `BlockNode:00CDBZ7698002:links:Link:00CDBZ76JG000` (host, field, kind, id). Two points raised: (a) what keys a port: if the key of a containment slot is its order, inserting a sibling changes the address of every later slot and every reverse entry pointing at them; if the slot has a stable key and its order is a property of the port, as a `Link` has a stable id and its position as a prop, addresses survive reordering; (b) the word \"port\" now names two things: the outgoing port on a host (a `Link`, a slot) and the incoming partition of a reverse bucket from earlier in the discussion; the two may be one idea (incoming ports partition by the prefix of the sources' deep paths, which the outgoing ports extend) or need two names. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BWFPHR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BWFPHR001:links:Link:00D3BWG3J0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3BSXFN0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3BWFPHR001:links:Link:00D3BWG3J0000:props:StringProp:00D3BWG3J0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "91"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3BWFPHR001",
    "text": "**The user: a network address has both incoming and outgoing ports** (2026-10-14, user, on [the two uses of \"port\"](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3BSXFN0001)). Orchestrator: not a hallucination; in TCP and UDP a port is a number under a host's address, one notion for both directions; a connection is named by both ends (source address and port, destination address and port); a server listens on a known port for incoming connections and a client sends from a short-lived port, and firewalls speak of inbound and outbound traffic, but the port itself is the same kind of thing either way. Applied here, a proposal: \"port\" stays one notion, a sub-address of a node that continues its deep path; a pair connects an outgoing port of its subject (a `Link`, a slot of `children`) to an incoming port of its object (the partition of its reverse side where the entry lands), as a connection joins two ports; the two uses found earlier are then the two ends of the same thing. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3C253YR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3C253YR001:links:Link:00D3C25GCG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3BSXFN0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3C253YR001:links:Link:00D3C25GCG000:props:StringProp:00D3C25GCG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "251"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3C253YR001",
    "text": "**The user: renumbering on list updates is an age-old problem (from section numbers to ordered lists); a stable key comes from a node, the closest being the child block's own id; is there a problem in using it as the port key?** (2026-10-14, user, on [the port key question](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3BSXFN0001)). Orchestrator: no problem in principle, under the decided semantics: a list is a set plus order, so a child appears at most once in its container's list and the slot `container:child` is unique and stable; with containment partitioning, a child is in at most one container, so the child's id alone already names its slot; a move to another container changes the slot's host, which is right since the relation changed. What the child's id does not carry is the order, so the order becomes a property of the port, as a `Link` carries its position as a prop. To avoid the renumbering the user describes, that property can be an order key chosen between its neighbours' keys on each insertion (fractional or lexical keys), so an insertion touches no other slot; keys only grow longer after many insertions at the same place, which an occasional respacing fixes. The keys need not reach the mirror: the file's `children` array already states the order, so the caller's loader can assign evenly spaced keys at load, and the dump writes the plain array again. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3C8YBVG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3C8YBVG001:links:Link:00D3C8YRB0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3C253YR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3C8YBVG001:links:Link:00D3C8YRB0000:props:StringProp:00D3C8YRB0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "182"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3C8YBVG001",
    "text": "**The user: the port encapsulates the order of the list; no fractional keys; the reverse never updates an order, it unpacks it from the live list when needed** (2026-10-14, user, on [the child-id key](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3C253YR001)): \"The port encapsulates the normal order in the list, no need for such a hack like fractional number. Because it's encapsulated, not exposed, the reverse refs don't have to update the order, but can still get it when needed: whenever needed, just unpack the order from the port, which is not stored but dynamically retrieved from the live list.\" Orchestrator's restatement, settled in the user's words: an ordered field is a list on its host; each item's port is `host:item`, keyed by the item's own id; the reverse of an item points at that port; the order is not stored anywhere but in the list itself, and a reader that needs an item's position asks the port, which finds it in the host's live list; an insertion or a move changes the list only, and no reverse entry changes; the sibling index and any order key disappear from the engine. Cost, stated honestly: finding a position in the live list is a pass over that list, which is cheap for lists of ordinary length; a cache of positions per list could be kept and rebuilt on first use after a change, if a measurement ever asks for it. Still open: whether \"port\" is one notion with an outgoing and an incoming end.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3CAC668001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3CAC668001:links:Link:00D3CACJ7R000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3C8YBVG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3CAC668001:links:Link:00D3CACJ7R000:props:StringProp:00D3CACJ80000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "79"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3CAC668001",
    "text": "**The user asks when a child's position is ever needed** (2026-10-14, user, on [the encapsulated order](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3C8YBVG001)). Orchestrator, from the code (`node.ts:106-127`, the readers of the sibling index): only in one place today: an insert or a move placed before or after an anchor (`insert --before` and `--after`, and the move with no body) needs the anchor's position in its parent's list, found by one pass over that list (`insertOrderedChild`); every other reader takes the children in order from the container (rendering, the dump, the path), and nothing reads a position from the child's side. The same function shows the old renumbering in code: it removes and rewrites the `parent` and index pairs of every sibling for one insertion; with the order kept only in the list, an insertion becomes one splice of the list and touches no sibling.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3CEAKAG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D3CEAKAG001",
    "text": "**The user: \"port\" is one notion with two ends; go update the design** (2026-10-14, user: \"Yep, 'port' is one notion with two ends, and go update the design.\"). Settled: a port is a sub-address of a node that continues its deep path; a pair joins an outgoing port of its subject (a `Link`, an item's place in an ordered list) to an incoming port of its object (the partition of the reverse side where the entry lands). The batch for the Grapher, a refactor of `design/core.md` around the boundary between the engine and its caller, persistence being the caller's layer, carrying every decision settled since the last batch: the boundary itself (the engine manages forward and reverse links in memory; dehydrating and rehydrating are the caller's); records kept by kind and by graph, so \"all of a kind\" is an iteration and the per-kind sets go; tombstones managed by the engine: a tombstone loses all its references, `parent` included, keeps its literal properties so stale incoming links can check its content, and stays only to catch incoming links; the sweep removes a tombstone that no reference from a node reachable from the roots points at, so a dead island goes too (today's sweep already works so); ordered fields as a general feature (a list is a set plus order, on its host; the reverse of an item points at its port `host:item`, keyed by the item's own id; the order is kept only in the live list and read from it on demand; no sibling index, no order key; an insertion is one splice); a partitioning mark on a field, ensured by the engine and used by the ports; containment as `children`, ordered and partitioning, whose reverse at an item is its port, giving its parent and its place; the two containment blocks reworded so they no longer read as if `children` were purely derived (today the index is extracted from the stored `children` and `children` is derived after); several graphs in memory with their own settings, and, on the caller's side, a mirror per graph, the threads being a graph whose mirror is `.state/`, so the scope test of a node is its graph; the caller's loader rejects every document of a repeated `@id` and goes on, and when `parent` and `children` disagree `parent` wins and the node goes to the end of `children` with a warning; the Status links to the four issues agreed; \"port\" as one notion with two ends.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
