    "title": "**Graphs in memory**",
    "text": "**Graphs in memory**: the engine holds several graphs in memory, in a map keyed by the graph's name, its `graph.domain`. The engine holds no settings of a graph: the persistence settings (where the mirror is, the flush interval) are the shell's, a table keyed by graph name, the `graphs` key of the shell's configuration (see [Addressing](#id/BlockNode:00D5AYBPWR001)). A graph has two folds: the engine's, in memory, and the shell's, on disk, which is its mirror (see [Files](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01X)). The grammar of a `graph.domain` is that of the host of a URL authority, normally a (sub)domain name such as `my.graph.myhost`: lowercase DNS labels joined by dots, with letters, digits and hyphens inside a label, and no hyphen at either end of a label and no dot at either end of the name. The engine enforces the grammar and refuses a name that fails it, and it refuses the name `id`, the one reserved authority, since `aperas://id/` gives it a fixed meaning (see [Addressing](#id/BlockNode:00D5AYBPWR001)). It gives no other name a special case: the graph that exists at the start is a normal graph, with no default graph. The engine does not refuse the name `tree`. The shell warns, once, when its table of settings has an entry keyed `tree`, because the main graph has no identifier of its own in the configuration and is named `tree` today, and it uses the entry for the main graph. The rest of multi-graph modularity is a design of its own, outside this document.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
