  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3CQ83DG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D3CQ83DG001",
    "text": "**Verification — the design refactored around the engine/caller boundary (`design-core-draft` Turn 8, Report `BlockNode:00D3CPTVFG004`)** (2026-10-14, orchestrator): `design/core.md` has 130 live blocks in four parts (Purpose and scope with \"The boundary\"; The engine, in memory, with the data model, the index, tombstones, several graphs, ordering, identity and the interface; The caller, on disk, with the mirror, the load and the flush, and lookups by a literal value; Open points); one \"(proposal, not agreed)\" mark is left (the path rule (a), moved to the caller's lookups by a literal value with its words unchanged); no figure (only the date), no \"quad\"; \"sibling index\" appears only in the Status link to the filed issue, in \"there is no sibling index\" under ordered fields, and in the description of today's code; earlier blocks kept their ids (moved, not recreated); `aperas check-links` is clean over 8,168 live blocks; thirteen \"Promoted to\" notes. The Grapher's judgment calls, for the user to check: (1) it removed \"the engine has no notion of live\", reading the decision that the engine manages tombstones as contradicting it; (2) three blocks still say a record keeps the shape of its mirror document, since the orchestrator's question whether that stays after the boundary moved was not answered; (3) it dropped option (b) (tombstoning clears the path) from the Open point on how a path names one node, since a tombstone keeps its literal properties; (4) it added two writes to the interface, the splice of an ordered field and the tombstoning of a node, restating the batch.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3CYTVE8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3CYTVE8001:links:Link:00D3CYV8M0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3936PG8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3CYTVE8001:links:Link:00D3CYV8M0000:props:StringProp:00D3CYV8M0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "435"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3CYTVE8001",
    "text": "**The user's comments on the refactored design text** (2026-10-14, user, reading `design/core.md`). (1) On `BlockNode:00D24N87SR01G` (\"The mirror does not depend on the engine's order: documents and the embedded entries of a set field are written sorted by id, and the children of a container in the order of its list (see A flush is a dump)\"): \"I remember that we've settled on keeping the list sorted.\" Orchestrator: yes, settled in [the sorted-on-insert entry](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3936PG8001): the orders the file needs are kept on each write, so a flush only writes, with code-point comparison; the block states the result (sorted files) but not when the sorting happens nor the code-point order; to be corrected. (2) \"There are many places saying '(see ...)', why no links there?\" Orchestrator: the Grapher wrote the cross-references as plain section names; a scan found 50 unlinked \"(see ...)\" in the document; each can be a link to the block it names, inside the document. (3) On `BlockNode:00D24N87SR012` (\"The key is derived: the part of an id after its kind is the node's `key`. It is derived on read and never stored, so it cannot drift from the id.\"): \"Isn't the node's `key` a snowflake code? I don't know why and what is 'never stored'.\" Orchestrator: yes, the key is the snowflake code of the id (`BlockNode:00D24N87SR012` has the key `00D24N87SR012`, a code issued in increasing order in time); \"never stored\" refers to the old schema's fields `blockId`, `artifactId` and `folderId`, which copied that code into a field of its own and were removed (`shape.ts` says so); that is history, not a rule, and the sentence should simply say that an id is its kind and its snowflake code and that nothing else holds the code; it also leaves unclear what the key of an embedded node is, whose id carries its host (`BlockNode:...:links:Link:...`). The three corrections are proposed for the next batch, at the user's word.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3D40G28001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3D40G28001:links:Link:00D3D40WY8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3CYTVE8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3D40G28001:links:Link:00D3D40WY8000:props:StringProp:00D3D40WY8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "153"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3D40G28001",
    "text": "**The user: the Coder fact-checked the document, so leaving an obsolete legacy statement in it looks like the verifier's mistake** (2026-10-14, user, on [the key wording](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3CYTVE8001)). Orchestrator's account: the miss is mainly the orchestrator's: its fact-check Ask (`design-core-fact-check`, Turn 1) asked whether each statement is true against the code and the mirror, and \"the key is derived on read and never stored\" is true of today's code, so a check for truth passes it; the Ask did not ask whether a statement belongs in a design at all (a rule of the engine against a description of today's code, history, or a remark tied to one session); the Grapher wrote a historical aside into the design, and the orchestrator's own checks after each batch looked at structure and hygiene (figures, quads, marks, links), not at that. The Coder's verdict on that block was not re-read. Proposed: a review turn whose criterion is belonging, not truth: flag every statement in `design/core.md` that describes today's code, history or a session rather than a rule, and say where it should go (history, an issue, or nowhere); the review lists, the user decides, the Grapher moves.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3DA7K80001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D3DA7K80001",
    "text": "**The user on the old meaning of \"derived, not stored\"** (2026-10-14, user): \"'Not stored' is clearly not a replacement of 'no other field repeating it'. The reading of 'not stored' in that old time was completely different: in the context of the main direction being ingestion instead of projection, 'derived, not stored' meant not in the artifact!\" Orchestrator: the user's account of the intent stands; the archived passage the orchestrator quoted (\"never its own triple, so nothing to keep in sync\") speaks of the store, so the archive's wording and the user's intent differ, and the orchestrator's rewording (\"no other field repeats the code\") captured neither. In either reading the sentence is obsolete today: projection now writes ids into the artifacts (links carry `#id/BlockNode:...` anchors), and the store keeps the code inside every id. Proposal: remove the sentence from the design; record in `history/core.md` what \"derived, not stored\" meant when ingestion was the main direction (the user's account) and how it changed; state in the design only the current rule (an id is its kind and a time-ordered code), with the naming question of \"key\" against `StringProp.key` still open.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3DMW2J8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3DMW2J8001:links:Link:00D3DMWEZ8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3DA7K80001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3DMW2J8001:links:Link:00D3DMWEZ8000:props:StringProp:00D3DMWEZ8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "237"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3DMW2J8001",
    "text": "**The user rereads the archive: the field `key`, unifying `blockId`, `artifactId` and `folderId`, was derived from `@id` and not stored as a field of that name; asks whether `key` still exists in the code** (2026-10-14, user, correcting [the earlier reading](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3DA7K80001): the orchestrator's \"no other field repeats it\" was partly right, but the point was that the very field `key` is not stored under that name). Orchestrator, from the code: yes; `TreeNode` has a getter `key` (`node.ts:387`) that returns the part of the id after the first `:`, so it is computed on every read and never a field of the record; it is read in about a dozen places, among them the folder ingest (`folders.ts:37,72,78,140`, as `folderId`) and the block reconcile (`node.ts:530,772,790,1032,1070`, as `blockId`), so the old names survive inside the ingest and reconcile structures in memory, fed by `.key`, but not in the mirror; it applies only to the tree nodes (blocks, artifacts, folders), not to embedded nodes, whose ids carry their host. So the design's sentence describes a real rule of the code (the code of an id is read from the id, never kept as a field), and its fault is the wording \"never stored\", which reads as if the code were not in the mirror. Proposal, for the user: keep the rule and reword it (\"a node's code is the part of its id after its kind; it is read from the id and never kept as a field of its own\"), with the naming question of \"key\" against `StringProp.key` still open.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3DT2RG8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3DT2RG8001:links:Link:00D3DT35NR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3DMW2J8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3DT2RG8001:links:Link:00D3DT35NR000:props:StringProp:00D3DT35NR001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
