        "target": "BlockNode:00D3DVKXP8000",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3DYNPH8001:links:Link:00D3DYP398000:props:StringProp:00D3DYP398001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "67"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3DYNPH8001",
    "text": "**The user answers the four judgment calls** (2026-10-14, user, on [the explanation](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3DVKXP8000)): (1) keep \"the engine has no notion of live\" removed; (2) \"the engine is intentionally designed to keep the records closest to the mirror, to reduce the work of the caller, but not a guarantee; so the caller must bridge the gap anyway\": the three blocks that state the record's shape are to be reworded so: an aim of the engine, not a contract, and the caller always converts what differs; (3) \"`path` is a literal but to be removed, isn't it?\"; (4) keep the two interface writes. Orchestrator on (3): yes: the stored `path` is to be derived from the parent chain later (settled earlier); once it is, a tombstone, which loses its `parent` (decided), has no derived path at all, so option (b) is moot and dropping it stands; what remains of the Open point on how a path names one node is the time until the stored path is removed, while a tombstone still holds the literal `path`, and for that time the caller looks up the live node at a path (the marked rule (a)). Asked: close that Open point with this account and decide rule (a) as the caller's interim rule, or keep it open. For the next batch, all settled: the `key` block removed; the record-shape blocks reworded as the user said.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
