    ],
    "title": "00D3E623VR001",
    "text": "**The user: the title \"The mirror does not depend on the engine's order\" needs updating too; how would the engine keep the order, and is that the engine's duty or the caller's?** (2026-10-14, user, on [the sorting wording](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3E2D530001)). Orchestrator, a proposal: three orders are involved and they split along the boundary. (1) The children of a container: the order of an ordered field, kept by the engine as part of its semantics (decided). (2) Documents in id order within a file, and (3) the embedded entries of a set field in id order: these are requirements of the mirror's format, so by the boundary they are the caller's; the engine's collections and sets promise no order (decided). How the caller meets them: sort at dump; the cost is small, sorting the ids of one file is a matter of milliseconds, and the measured cost of a flush today (about 1.4 s) came from full passes over the quads and the rebuilding of documents, not from sorting; ids are plain ASCII, so the default string comparison of JavaScript, which compares code units, gives the code-point order. This revises the earlier \"keep the orders on each write so a flush only writes\", which the orchestrator had framed as a cost to move away from the flush; for (2) and (3) that cost is negligible, while keeping them in the engine would make it promise an order it otherwise does not. Proposed title: \"Orders in the mirror\". Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3E837T0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3E837T0001:links:Link:00D3E83MD8000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3E623VR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3E837T0001:links:Link:00D3E83MD8000:props:StringProp:00D3E83MD8001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "232"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3E837T0001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: the engine keeps only the order of ordered fields; the caller sorts documents and set entries at dump (\"Deal\"); the user asks how documents are placed in memory, accessed and traversed** (2026-10-14, user, on [the order duty](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3E623VR001)). Settled for the next batch: the title \"Orders in the mirror\"; the children of a container in their list's order (the engine's ordered field); documents and the embedded entries of a set field sorted by id by the caller at dump, by code-point comparison. Orchestrator's answer to the question, from the decisions so far, marking what is not decided: placement: one object per node, its record, never copied, reached by three structures that hold references to it: the containment tree (a container's record holds its ordered `children` list of child records; embedded nodes such as `Link` and `StringProp` sit inside their owner's record), the master map from id to record, and the collections by kind (and by graph); access: by id through the master map; all nodes of a kind through its collection, in no promised order; a node's fields on its record (literal properties directly, reference fields as forward lists); who points at a node through the reverse side of a field, built on first use, with its ports; the parent and the place of an item through its port; a node by a literal value through the caller's maps; traversal: top-down from a graph's roots along `children` in list order; bottom-up through the port to the parent; across along `links` to a `Link` and its `target`, and back along the reverse of `target`; whole kinds for the dump and the sweeps. Not decided: whether the master map and the collections are per graph or shared by all graphs, which depends on whether an id is unique across graphs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EAQMK8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EAQMK8001:links:Link:00D3EAR1S0000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3E837T0001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EAQMK8001:links:Link:00D3EAR1S0000:props:StringProp:00D3EAR1S0001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "261"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3EAQMK8001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: an id is unique across graphs, so there is one master map and many collections; the user asks who is in charge of the collections by kind and graph, and whether they are lists that keep an order from load to flush** (2026-10-14, user, on [the placement answer](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3E837T0001)). Settled: ids are unique across all graphs; one master map from id to record serves every graph; collections are many, one per graph and kind. Orchestrator's proposal for the question: the engine is in charge of them, since graphs are an engine feature and membership in a graph decides dropping it, the sweep and the caller's dump per graph; a collection needs fast membership and removal more than positions, so a set; a JavaScript `Set` keeps the order of insertion by the language's rules, so a collection filled at load from the file (whose documents are in id order) and given new nodes at its end keeps id order as long as new ids are larger than the old ones, which they are when one process issues them in increasing time (observed, not guaranteed: a clock set back, or nodes brought in from elsewhere, would break it); so at a dump the order is normally already right, and the caller's sort by id (decided) becomes a cheap check over an already sorted list rather than real work; the collection itself promises no order to its readers, so nothing depends on that natural order. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3ECQGPG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3ECQGPG001:links:Link:00D3ECQY7G000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3EAQMK8001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3ECQGPG001:links:Link:00D3ECQY7G000:props:StringProp:00D3ECQY7G001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "93"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3ECQGPG001",
    "text": "**The user asks whether a collection, if it is a set, is keyed by id** (2026-10-14, user, on [the collection proposal](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3EAQMK8001)). Orchestrator, a proposal: a set holds its members by identity, so the choice is what the members are: (a) the ids, so membership is by the id string, and every use of a member (the dump, the sweep) goes through the master map to reach its record; (b) the records themselves, so membership is by the record object, which corresponds one to one with its id through the master map, and iteration hands out records directly; a map from id to record per collection would repeat what the master map already holds. Leaning (b): the operations on a collection (add on create, remove on delete, iterate for a dump or a sweep) all start from or end at a record, and a test by id goes through the one master map first anyway. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EDBV6G001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EDBV6G001:links:Link:00D3EDC7PG000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3ECQGPG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EDBV6G001:links:Link:00D3EDC7PG000:props:StringProp:00D3EDC7PG001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "102"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3EDBV6G001",
    "text": "**The user asks the difference between a set of records and a list of records** (2026-10-14, user, on [the membership question](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3ECQGPG001)). Orchestrator: both hold references to the same records and both iterate in insertion order in JavaScript; they differ in what they promise and what is cheap: a set holds each record at most once and has no positions, so adding a record already there does nothing and testing or removing a record takes one step; a list (an array) has positions, can hold a record twice unless checked, gives an item by position, can insert at a place and be sorted in place, but finding or removing a given record means a pass over it and a shift of what follows. A collection by kind and graph is used by adding on create, removing on delete and iterating whole, with no meaning in a position, so a set fits; a list fits where positions mean something, which is why `children` is an ordered list. A list takes less memory per entry, and sorting a set for the dump means copying it into a list first, which is cheap.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EFHTX8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EFHTX8001:links:Link:00D3EFJ8DR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3EDBV6G001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EFHTX8001:links:Link:00D3EFJ8DR000:props:StringProp:00D3EFJ8E0000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "230"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3EFHTX8001",
    "text": "**Settled by the user: collections are sets; their natural order, from snowflake codes that keep increasing, reduces the work of the sort at a flush, in line with \"closest to the mirror but not guaranteed\"** (2026-10-14, user, on [set against list](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3EDBV6G001)): the main reason for a set is removal; adding the same record twice is not a realistic case. Settled for the next batch: each collection (one per graph and kind) is a set kept by the engine; its iteration order is the order of insertion, which follows the increasing codes, so the caller's sort by id at a dump is normally cheap; nothing promises that order. The orchestrator takes the members to be the records themselves (its option (b)), the user having chosen a set without naming the members.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EGC560001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D3EGC560001",
    "text": "**The user: do the batch, with the links for the \"(see ...)\" references and the belonging review** (2026-10-14, user: \"Do the batch, including the 2 points you're waiting.\"). The Grapher's next turn: the settled batch (the `key` block removed; the record-shape blocks reworded as an aim, not a guarantee, the caller bridging the gap; \"Orders in the mirror\": the engine keeps only the order of ordered fields, the caller sorts documents and set entries by id at a dump by code-point comparison; one master map, ids unique across graphs; one set of records per graph and kind, kept by the engine, its natural order from the increasing codes not promised); every unlinked \"(see ...)\" turned into a link to the block it names; then, read-only, the belonging review: a list of every statement in `design/core.md` that describes today's code, history or a session rather than a rule of the design, each with where it should go (history, an issue, or nowhere), for the user to decide; the review edits nothing.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
