  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EQKVJ8001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D3EQKVJ8001",
    "text": "**Verification — the fourth batch, the links and the belonging review (`design-core-draft` Turn 9, Report `BlockNode:00D3EQ3FYR008`)** (2026-10-14, orchestrator): written as settled: \"Orders in the mirror\"; the record shape as an aim, not a guarantee (three blocks); ids unique across graphs, one master map, one set of records per graph and kind; 37 blocks had their \"(see ...)\" turned into links, the four \"(see Tombstones)\" left because a heading and a block share that name; `aperas check-links` clean; staged. Not done: the removal of the `key` block (`BlockNode:00D24N87SR012`): the permission system refused the Grapher's `aperas remove BlockNode:00D24N87SR012 --flush`; the orchestrator does not run a command refused to a subagent; the user is asked to run it or allow it. The belonging review lists 23 statements that describe today's code, today's data, history or a session, with a destination for each: most are \"today the load / today's code\" sentences whose issues are already filed (nowhere or the issue), others the dated fact-check in Status, \"as Oxigraph holds several stores\", the \"earlier engine\" framing, today's function names in the interface's reads and writes, and \"the key of a node is not written\". The Grapher's further questions: whether that last sentence goes with the `key` block; how to settle the \"(see Tombstones)\" ambiguity; whether the values of a reference set field (such as `unfolds`) are sorted at a dump and by whom; that dropping a graph must also remove its records from the one master map; that the interface says \"a repeated add is ignored\" while the index calls it \"not a promise\"; and whether Turn 8's go note should move back beside its origin.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3ERJXBG001",
