    ],
    "title": "00D3ERJXBG001",
    "text": "**The user: `aperas remove` is a graph command, not a file deletion, so the refusal looks like the same quirk of the permission check met earlier; the orchestrator retried it once** (2026-10-14, user, on [the Turn 9 verification](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3EQKVJ8001)). The removal was the user's decision ([the decision](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3DT2RG8001)); with the user's word that the refusal was a quirk, the orchestrator ran `aperas remove BlockNode:00D24N87SR012 --flush` once: \"Tombstoned 'BlockNode:00D24N87SR012'\"; `aperas check-links` clean; staged. The \"Promoted to\" note for that decision is left to the next Grapher turn. Still open for the user: the default destinations of the belonging review, and the Grapher's six questions.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3ET5M88001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D3ET5M88001",
    "text": "**Why `aperas remove` was refused for the Grapher, from its transcript** (2026-10-14, orchestrator, at the user's request). In the Grapher's Turn 9 transcript (`agent-a5fced17efcf90ab6.jsonl`, 14:26:51Z) the call `aperas remove BlockNode:00D24N87SR012 --flush < /dev/null` returned, 12 s later: \"Permission for this action was denied by the Claude Code auto mode classifier. Reason: [Modify Shared Resources].\" It is the same category as the refusal of the orchestrator's batch edit earlier in this session. The command never reached `aperas`: the decision was the classifier's, made from the command and its context; a removal with `--flush` writes the shared graph, which the classifier counts as modifying a shared resource; the same command passed in the orchestrator's session right after the user said the removal was decided and the refusal a quirk, which is the likely difference: there the user's words were in the context, while the Grapher saw them only as citations in its Ask. Options, for the user: (a) Asks quote the user's decision in its own words when they ask for a removal or another destructive graph command; (b) an allow rule for the command in `.claude/settings.local.json` (for example `Bash(aperas remove:*)`), the user's settings to change; (c) the orchestrator runs such removals itself after the user's go, as it just did. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EWHKNG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "00D3EWHKNG001",
    "text": "**The orchestrator's own refused command, from its transcript** (2026-10-14, at the user's request; the user notes the \"Modify Shared Resources\" refusal is not limited to `aperas remove`, so an allow rule for that one command is pointless). The refused call, 2026-10-11 06:20Z: a loop over every block under an Ask (`for id in $(aperas tree BlockNode:00D19TBVBG001 ... )`), which piped each block's text through `sed` to replace two wrong ids and wrote it back with `aperas update $id --text-only --flush`; refused by the auto mode classifier, \"[Modify Shared Resources]\"; after the user said to try again, two separate single-block updates passed. The two refusals share a shape: a write to the shared graph whose scope the command does not state plainly (a loop rewriting whatever matches a pattern; a removal that tombstones a subtree with `--flush`), issued by an agent; the many single, explicit `insert` and `update --flush` calls of every turn pass. Practice to follow, a proposal: graph writes one explicit node at a time, never a loop that rewrites matches in bulk; for a removal, the Ask quotes the user's decision in the user's words. Not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EYSCVG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EYSCVG001:links:Link:00D3EYST90000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3EWHKNG001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EYSCVG001:links:Link:00D3EYST90000:props:StringProp:00D3EYST90001",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "130"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D3EYSCVG001",
    "text": "**The user's trial rule: retry after a short wait when the harness refuses with \"Modify Shared Resources\"** (2026-10-14, user, on [the two refusals](../discussion/aperas-skill.md#id/BlockNode:00D3EWHKNG001)): \"I think the workaround is simply to try again after a small sleep, whenever the harness shows that restriction! Let's add that to memory to try and see if it's effective. If it's effective, we will update the skill later.\" Saved as a memory, scoped by the orchestrator so it stays a retry and not a way around the check: only for `aperas` commands that carry out approved work; one retry, unchanged, after a few seconds; if refused again, stop and report; never rephrase the command or switch tools to get past the refusal; each case is noted in Freeflow so the user can judge the rule; subagents do not read the memory, so an Ask that needs it says it. The skills change later only if the trial works.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0V83SF8001",
    "type": "paragraph",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EZWV70001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3EZWV70001:links:Link:00D3EZX880000",
        "@type": "Link",
