    "title": "**1. Records and literal fields**",
    "text": "**1. Records and literal fields**: a record for each subject; a literal field is a plain property of the record, with no pair and no reverse side ([Literal fields are properties of the record](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D39DRYB0001)); the engine reads and writes a field as the schema declares it for the node's kind ([One schema, handed in at construction](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR008)). Literals are kept as JavaScript strings, integers and booleans, so the string \"1\" and the number 1 differ, and a string that looks like a date stays a string ([Literal types](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR00J)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR008:props:StringProp:00D3XQQHHR003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "checked",
        "value": "false"
      }
    ],
    "title": "**2. Cardinalities**",
    "text": "**2. Cardinalities**: `one`, `optional` and `set`, declared and never inferred from the data; the values of a set come back in no promised order; the first or only value of a single-valued field is read without a scan.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR006",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR009",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
