    "title": "**3. The load**",
    "text": "**3. The load**: the shell's load builds the engine's graphs from the files through the persistence API: it creates each node with its id and kind in the graph of the file it reads (a document holds no `graph` field) and sets its fields, a reference to a node not yet built being accepted ([Persistence](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D4MZ7DZG001)). No two documents share an `@id`: the load rejects every document that carries a repeated one, keeps none of them, reports the id, and goes on ([An `@id` names one document](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01P), [A repeated `@id` at load](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D2PC3ZG8001)). When the `parent` of a node and the `children` array of its container disagree, `parent` wins: the load appends the node at the end of its parent's `children`, drops its entry from the `children` of the other container and warns, naming both; a `parent` that names an id with no document leaves the node with no container, returns the id in a list of its own and warns; a node that a container lists and that has no `parent` stays in that container ([A file whose `parent` and `children` disagree](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D2PV8GC0001)). Once a graph is built, the load asks the engine for the ids that are referenced and never defined, and returns them together with the ids that a `parent` names with no document: the engine's list does not see those, since a `parent` that a build supplies is never a stored pair.",
