    "title": "**1. The maps the shell keeps**",
    "text": "**1. The maps the shell keeps**: a profile by its `handle`, a view by its `name` (the `name` field is shared by profiles and views, so the lookup filters the kind), and an artifact or a folder by its stored `path`; the maps are the shell's, in `src/shell/`; each is updated by the shell's writes and rebuilt by the load ([Lookups by a literal value](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3CHMYQG004), [Handles and names](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01T)). Built in `src/shell/lookups.ts`: `createLookups(engine, graphs)` gives an object with `profileByHandle`, `viewByName`, `liveAtPath`, `duplicates`, `size`, `rebuild` (one pass over `idsOfKind` of `Profile`, `TreeView`, `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` in the graphs given, reading `record`, a node with the tombstone marker left out) and an update function for each write that changes a map (`built`, `fieldWritten`, `tombstoned`, `subtreeTombstoned`, `removed`, `swept`, `graphDropped`); each update reads the engine for the node it is told of and files what the engine holds, so a write the engine did not take leaves the maps as the engine is. The shell has no write layer before Batch 11, so nothing calls the update functions yet and the model test drives them.",
