    "title": "**1. The seam**",
    "text": "**1. The seam**: `storeAccess.ts`, `vocab.ts`, `store.ts` and `dehydrate.ts` of the reference copy are the places where the code reads and writes the store; they are replaced by the engine's interface and the layer of Batch 9, so that the `Store` of Oxigraph is no longer a type in the code. In production only `storeAccess.ts`, `vocab.ts` (`nodeExists`, `namedNode`, `literal` and `Term`) and `store.ts` (`new Store()`) use Oxigraph values; 30 other files import the `Store` type and three tests and tools build a `Store`. `vocab.ts` splits: `NODE_BASE` builds only the IRIs of the codec and goes with it; `isNodeRef` and `nodeKindFromId` (8 command uses) have nothing of Oxigraph and stay in the shell and read ids by the grammar they import from the engine ([Ids](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR01N)); the shell keeps no copy of the grammar. The seam also takes `threadScope.ts` (it reads through `firstLiteralOf` and `firstRefIriOf` and walks `parent` itself), `tree.ts` (`findByExactPath`) and `kgInit.ts` (lines 43 to 55, which writes the empty `Apeiron/*.jsonld` files itself and so holds the mirror's file names and format a second time, with `service.ts` lines 171 and 172 and `store.ts` lines 25 to 37). Steps 1 and 2 are one change: `node.ts` makes about 45 calls into `storeAccess` and `wrap(store, …)` is the entry of everything else, so the seam cannot be swapped before the node classes without leaving the tree red. Inside the change the 12 helpers of `storeAccess.ts` go first, then the five low-level functions of `node.ts`, and `commit` runs at the end of both.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W000C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W000E",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
