      }
    ],
    "title": "**Tombstones leave their container**",
    "text": "**Tombstones leave their container**: settled: the design wins. [The design](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000R) says a tombstoned node loses its `parent` and leaves the `children` of its container, and the code relies on the opposite in four places. (1) `tombstoneLiveSubtree` (the path of `aperas remove` and of the removal of an artifact) clears the removed node's own `children`, `links` and `props` and leaves the node in its parent's `children` (`node.ts:870-878`, `kgRemove.ts:42`), which is why `tree --tombstoned` and the `(tombstoned)` tag show a removed child (`node.ts:1342,1738-1744`); only the ingest path (`applyTombstone`) leaves the node out of the list. (2) `tombstoneVacuousContainers` calls a textless `listItem` vacuous when `treeChildren.length > 0 && every(tombstoned)` (`node.ts:2209-2210`); once the tombstones are out of the list, such an item has no children and the rule is false for it, and a `list` with none is vacuous at once (`node.ts:2209`). (3) `pruneUnreachableTombstones` marks from the live roots through `treeChildren` (`node.ts:2165`), so a removed block is never pruned while it is in its parent's list. (4) `toPath()` of a tombstone becomes `null` with no `parent` (13 sites in core and 15 in the cli; the fallbacks at `node.ts:1876,1900` already handle `null`). Settled (2026-10-20): a tombstone loses its `parent` and leaves the `children` of its container, as [the design](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000R) states with its consequences in the shell. A removed block is in no list; `tree --tombstoned` shows nothing for a removed child in its place, keeps the `(tombstoned)` tag on a link whose target is tombstoned, and the commands keep a way to show a tombstone by id; a removal that empties a textless `listItem` or a `list` tombstones that container in the same write (a rule at the time of the removal, not a flag), and `tombstoneVacuousContainers` keeps the rule over a container with no live child as the boot sweep; `toPath()` of a tombstone is `null`. Gates steps 4 and 7, which pieces 9 and 10 carry; they no longer wait for the user.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3YG50B0000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
