    "title": "**Which order differences are accepted and which are fixed in the batch**",
    "text": "**Which order differences are accepted and which are fixed in the batch**: settled. [Three behaviours depend on an unordered read and five listings change order with the engine](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3YFXDC800D). Of the three, `findByExactPath` by asking for the live node is settled: [How a path names one node](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D25686RR001) says every lookup by path goes through the one primitive of Batch 10, and the readers move onto it (see the readers of the old lookups, above). The two others can be fixed in the shell (`positionsEqual` by comparing sorted lists, the canonical place by a rule that does not follow the order of `unfolds`). Settled (2026-10-20): the five listings (`check-links` and `linkWarning`, `track --reverse`, the profile and view lists, `backlinks`, the links of a block) are sorted in the shell, so that the layer-4 output compare is exact and none of them is a deliberate difference: the first four by id, and the links of a block by `position`, then id, a link with no position after those with one ([The order of links without a position](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000P)). The canonical place of a node reached through several unfolded links is the first in code-point order of the unfolded ids ([The canonical place in the render](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000Q)), and the engine keeps no set of live ids, the shell's `liveIdsOfKind` filtering ([No set of live ids](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000X)). Gates steps 3, 5 and 6, which pieces 7 and 8 carry.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3YG50B0000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D3YG50B0004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Regrouping the batch**",
    "text": "**Regrouping the batch**: settled. The eight groups follow the commands, but the commands hold almost no store code (no command calls a helper of `storeAccess`); the work is in `node.ts`, `artifacts.ts`, `folders.ts`, `resolve.ts`, `resolveCreate.ts` and `linkIntegrity.ts`. A cut by file would be: A, the seam with the node classes (steps 1 and 2); B, `tree.ts`, `threadScope.ts`, `resolve.ts` and `resolveCreate.ts` on the Batch 10 primitives (step 3 without the render); C, the render, `node.ts` lines 1325 to 2007 (the rest of step 3, gated); D, `artifacts.ts`, `folders.ts` and the tombstone calls (steps 4 and 5); E, `linkIntegrity.ts` (step 6); F, the sweeps (step 7); G, the service (step 8). Settled (2026-10-20): neither. The batch is cut by dependency into ten pieces of one coder turn each, listed at the head of the batch: the commands hold almost no store code and the seam is `node.ts`, one file, so the pieces follow what must exist before what, with the tests first, the additive handle before the flip and the semantic moves last.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3YG50B0000",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
