    ],
    "title": "**Cause found, and the fix decided (2026-10-15, the user)**",
    "text": "**Cause found, and the fix decided (2026-10-15, the user)**: the Coder found the folder called `null`: it is a ghost `FolderNode`, the id of the `.threads` folder, which the tracked mirror names in the `children` of the root and whose document lives only in `.state/`, so that without `.state/` any `ingest` lists it for removal and the prompt waits on standard input ([the ghost folder](#id/BlockNode:00D417WK80001)). The root cause that the entry above does not find is this one, and the wording of this issue, a corpus-wide ingest, is incomplete: any `ingest` reaches the prompt, with paths or without and with `--track` or without. With standard input closed the answer is no and the rest of the ingest lands; with it open and silent the prompt blocks for good. The user decided to fix it now, in the working engine on branch `oxigraph`: a flag that answers every removal prompt of `ingest` and `track` with no, and no refusal on a missing terminal, together with the filter that keeps the ghost out of the removals. A coder is making it, and the issue stays open until the coder reports.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CNX1T4XG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4198VGG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Since observed (2026-10-15), a second `service start`**",
    "text": "**Since observed (2026-10-15), a second `service start`**: a second `aperas service start` while a service is running replaces the socket, the lock and the token, and can leave the first service running and unreachable, so that every command answers \"No ApeironNgn service running\". It is not a defect of the agents' use of `service start`. Whether `service start` should refuse when a service process is alive is not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D0W05P08001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D419V27R001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
