      }
    ],
    "title": "**A folder's `text`**",
    "text": "**A folder's `text`**: a folder's `text` is its frontmatter `description`, and its `README.md` only adds sub-blocks to the folder. So the design's \"The artifact is its own root document\" is not stale. The earlier lines of the plan and of this Freeflow that say the folders \"keep their README content as their own text\" are inexact: the README's content is the folder's children, and the folder's text stays the description.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Design edits, batch 2**",
    "text": "**Design edits, batch 2**: edit \"Deriving the path\" so that it no longer calls the `README.md` files artifacts (they are the folder's sub-blocks, and the migration deletes the stray artifacts); extend \"References across graphs\" to the root folder's `children` that names the `.threads` folder (the ghost-folder case, known and worked around, the multi-graph design being out of scope); correct the Status dates. No Open point is decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Plan wording**",
    "text": "**Plan wording**: step 6 of Batch 9 says a stripped tombstone keeps its literal properties (`title`, `text`, `tombstonedAt`, and `path`, `holder`, `type` where the document has them).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Script loose ends**",
    "text": "**Script loose ends**: `migrate-mirror.py` with no argument prints its header and exits 2 (it exited 1), and `verify-migration.py` makes a `--rules` with no value, or with a value that is not a list of rule numbers, a usage error with exit 2 as `migrate-mirror.py` does, with the headers updated to match.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**A rule for the Asks**",
    "text": "**A rule for the Asks**: never restore a graph file with `git checkout` while the service runs, and always `aperas update --text-only` on a block that has children.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4KA0VRR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4KCPB70001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Where the cross-graph reference goes in the design, 2026-10-16**",
    "text": "**Where the cross-graph reference goes in the design, 2026-10-16**: the node meant to carry it, \"References and reverse lookups across graphs\" (`BlockNode:00D2PTZ7C8001`), was tombstoned on 2026-10-13, so the live design has no such text to extend. The orchestrator chose, within the user's approval of the second batch of design edits, a new bullet at the end of \"Several graphs\", beside \"Dropping a graph\", which already speaks of pairs that point into another graph. The text: the root folder's `children` in the tracked mirror names the `.threads` folder, whose document is in the threads graph; this is a reference from the main graph into another graph that exists today, is known, and is worked around in the working engine by a filter on documents without a `path`; the multi-graph design that would remove it is out of scope. It decides no Open point.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
