    ],
    "title": "**The code structure: `src/apeironNgn` and `src/shell`, 2026-10-16**",
    "text": "**The code structure: `src/apeironNgn` and `src/shell`, 2026-10-16**: the [map of the layout](../discussion/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D4KSHCD8001) (the coder's survey) showed that `src/apeironNgn/` holds the Oxigraph engine and its callers on disk together (ingest, projection, dehydrate/rehydrate, resolve, link integrity, sweeps), so the new engine cannot take the directory while they are in it. The user agreed the clash comes from the change of the boundary, and asked whether a `legacy/` directory would stay forever; the orchestrator answered that the plan deletes the old engine files (Batch 11 steps 1 and 2, Batch 12) but never says where the callers end up. The user decided the names: `src/apeironNgn` is the engine in memory and `src/shell` is the caller on disk. Their proposal, as the orchestrator reads it: if all the current code of `src/apeironNgn/` can either move into the shell or dissolve into the new engine, it moves to `src/shell/` as it is, with no `legacy/` directory, and the old engine files in it are deleted as the batches replace them. The code structure is to be designed with its dependencies checked file by file and mapped onto the batches of the plan, updating the batches where needed; a detailed plan is drawn from it. Nothing is moved or edited before that plan is agreed.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
