    "title": "**Addresses and a generic engine, 2026-10-16**",
    "text": "**Addresses and a generic engine, 2026-10-16**: on the orchestrator's proposals for persistence and ids (the engine advertises shapes as data, a build API and a read-out API; the id grammar in the engine, minting in the shell, the Oxigraph IRIs dropped), the user raised two points. (1) If the IRIs die, who takes charge of the canonical address? The separation of addresses (canon used inside the graph, compat used only in the artifacts, converted at ingest and projection) is designed and not built (the [issue](../issues/linking.md#id/BlockNode:00CVQHG5JR001)). (2) Shapes as data should be given by the shell, with the engine requesting them, for the genericness of the engine; the user asked whether that is too big a refactor. The orchestrator's answer: only the Oxigraph term encoding dies; `aperas://id/<id>` is the canonical address, which is addressing and belongs to the shell, and the design's sentence that calls it the engine's IRI needs an edit; a generic engine is no refactor of code, since the engine is not written yet, but a design pass over every engine rule that names a concrete class or field. Not settled.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
