    "title": "**The generic engine and the edits, decided 2026-10-16**",
    "text": "**The generic engine and the edits, decided 2026-10-16**: the user decided that \"the engine requests the shapes\" means the shell passes the schema in when it constructs the engine, fixed for the engine's life (closed at construction), and said \"Go doc it\". Taken together with [the decisions on the survey](#id/BlockNode:00D4MJBY98001) and [the addresses](#id/BlockNode:00D4MRS7JR001), the batch of edits is: (1) the engine is generic: its design states a meta-model (kinds and inheritance, storage kinds, cardinalities, reference and embed pairs with their reverse names, ordered and partitioning fields, containment), and the Aperas schema (`BaseNode`, `TreeNode`, `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`, `FolderNode`, `Link`, `StringProp`, `Profile`, `TreeView`) is the shell's, handed to the engine at construction; every engine rule that names a concrete class or field becomes a role the schema assigns; (2) \"the caller\" is renamed \"the shell\", the part that calls the engine, holding `wrap`, the class hierarchy and every caller; (3) the engine advertises the API that the shell's persistence uses: build (create a node with a given id, kind and graph, set its fields, references to nodes not yet built accepted), read out (all ids of a kind in a graph, the record of a node in shape order) and the ids referenced and never defined; the engine has no load or dump; (4) ids: the grammar (`Kind:snowflake`, embedded `owner:field:Kind:snowflake`) is the engine's, the shell mints and passes ids in, the engine checks grammar and uniqueness; the Oxigraph term encoding is dropped, and `aperas://id/<id>` is the canonical address, owned by the shell's addressing; the canon/compat conversion stays in its issue, outside this plan; (5) the engine imports no file system and no store library; (6) the plan: a new batch for the move of `src/apeironNgn/` to `src/shell/` before Batch 3, with the uncovered code as decided, and the batches updated for the location, the schema, persistence in the shell and the profile and view operations; the code-versus-rule difference filed as an issue closed by the move.",
