    "title": "## v4.7 additions — delta on the v4 snapshot",
    "text": "Records the v4.7 edits to the skills: the stopgap for [the move's stdin hang, a third time](../discussion/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D4PDAWZ8001), decided by the user, with [the coder's three questions, decided](../discussion/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D4PHZBV8001), against [the move that waits on standard input](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D2PVVMWG001). Every move recipe ends in `< /dev/null`, with the reason: a move reads standard input to its end, and a harness can leave it open. The base skill carries it in its quick-reference row and in a new move line in its command patterns, and the Grapher skill in its two move recipes (step 3 of the edit loop and *Move nodes cleanly*). The `Status:` lines of all four skills (the base, Orchestrator, Coder and Grapher skills) move to v4.7, with four `Status` entries, one per skill; the Coder and Orchestrator skills have no move recipe, so only their `Status:` lines moved. `skills/aperas/references/mechanics.md` goes from v4.2 to v4.7, the skill release it ships with, and gains a section, *Moving a Node (`insert` with No Body)*. The drift check reads only the `SKILL.md` files, and a recorded block absent from them reads as dropped, so the reference's text is recorded here, in the delta's commentary, as it has been since its extraction in v4.2: `aperas insert <node-id> --after <anchor-id>` (or `--before <anchor-id>`) with no body moves the existing node next to the anchor, keeping its ID, backlinks and history, and the anchor's parent becomes the node's new parent; the recipe is `aperas insert <node-id> --after <anchor-id> --flush < /dev/null`; and its one rule, **Close standard input**, says that a move reads standard input to its end before acting, and a harness can leave standard input open, so a move without `< /dev/null` can wait on it indefinitely. No in-place promote recipe appears in the skills or the reference, so none changed. Each entry that rewrites text recorded in an earlier delta or the v4 snapshot carries a `Supersedes:` line naming the blocks it replaces.",
