  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4R7KBJ0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The class hierarchy straightened, `removePair`, and `path`, decided 2026-10-16**",
    "text": "**The class hierarchy straightened, `removePair`, and `path`, decided 2026-10-16**: the user explained that the archived class hierarchy diagram had `BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` as siblings under `TreeNode`, that `children` was an oversight of the diagram and belongs to `TreeNode`, and that the gap came from the old `root` node of `ArtifactNode` merged into it later; and said to straighten it now. They then understood the embed problem (a `Link` is a node with its own id that can be passed around, so it differs from a literal; clearing an embed field would destroy a node that the new value keeps) and said \"yes to all\" to the orchestrator's proposals: (1) `TreeNode` holds `title`, `text`, `children` (ordered and partitioning) and the derived `parent`; `BlockNode` adds `type` (one); `ArtifactNode` adds `path`'s replacement (see 3) and the five hash and timestamp fields and has no `type`; `FolderNode` adds `projectedHash`; the restated-field rule and `redeclares` go; (2) a sixth migration rule strips `type: \"root\"` from the artifacts; (3) `path` is removed from the schema now: it is the chain of `title`s from the root folder (the directory `graph.artifacts` names) down, derived by the shell over the engine's `parent` and `title` reads; a node whose chain does not reach the root is refused when its path is asked (ingest and project throw \"path undeterminable: no parent\") and the load warns once listing them; `concepts.md` is such a case, kept as a test; a seventh migration rule strips `path` from artifacts and folders, and the checker verifies that the title chain equals the old stored path for every artifact and folder except `concepts.md`; Batch 11 gets a step to derive the path and replace every read of `.path`, and Batch 10's lookup by path is a map over derived paths; (4) `removePair(graph, subject, field, value)` is added to the writes, removing the pair and, on an embed field, the embedded node recursively; it replaces the `setEmbeds` proposed earlier, the orchestrator deciding that a field write for embeds is `removePair` for the dropped values and `addPair` for the new ones, so the nodes kept keep their ids; (5) the design records why a write of a field is clear then add: a `one` or `optional` field refuses a second value, so changing it means clearing it first, and it is the plain operation for a caller that holds the new value and not the old; the reverse side is kept in step by every pair the engine adds or removes, so it is not the reason. The orchestrator first has the coder count the readers of `.path` for the plan's Batch 11 step.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
