    "title": "**The readers of `path` and the decisions applied, 2026-10-16**",
    "text": "**The readers of `path` and the decisions applied, 2026-10-16**: the grapher applied [the decisions](#id/BlockNode:00D4R7KBJ0001) to the design and the plan (the hierarchy, `removePair` and the rationale of clear then add, the derived path and \"A path that cannot be derived\", Batch 9 with seven rules, Batch 10, Batch 11 step 12). The coder counted the readers of the stored `path`: 47 lines (38 in 8 files of `core`, 9 in 4 of `cli`, none in `web`), 27 can take the derived path as it is, 8 can but throw for a node with no chain, 9 need something else, 3 writes go away. It raised holes in removing `path`, put to the user: a tracked artifact has a path and no parent until the next folder ingest (`trackArtifact`, found again through a map by path), so with a derived path a second `aperas track` mints a duplicate; the mirror holds 7 live artifacts with no parent (`concepts.md` and the six `README.md` artifacts, which the migration deletes); the tombstoned nodes keep their `path` today and `staleOnDisk` and the reuse of a path after a removal rely on it, while a tombstone loses its `parent`; the thread scope and the root folder (path `.`, title `Artifacts`). Decided by the orchestrator as not about the design, to go on: the Aperas schema and the shell keep the stored `path` for now, until the user decides, and the coder reworks the rest of the schema (the hierarchy, `children` in `TreeNode`, `type` only on `BlockNode`, no restated-field rule) and types `removePair` with the oracle; a kind may declare the explicit order of all its fields (own and inherited) when the Format requires an order that inheritance does not give, as for `BlockNode` (`parent`, `type`, `children`); the stale places the grapher listed are brought up to date once the user decides on `path`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4RFM278001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The schema reworked, checked by the orchestrator, 2026-10-16**",
    "text": "**The schema reworked, checked by the orchestrator, 2026-10-16**: in `Aperas.dev/monorepo` (`js-apeironngn`, staged, not committed) the coder reworked the Aperas schema (`TreeNode` holds `children`, `BlockNode` alone has `type`, `ArtifactNode` and `FolderNode` are siblings that keep `path` for now, `BlockNode` declares its explicit `order`), removed the `redeclares` rule, and typed `removePair` with the oracle. The orchestrator ran the suites: `commit` 325 of 325 in 24 s, `essential` 51 of 51, `--group engine` 255 of 255. The coder's three questions, decided as not about the design: `removePair` of a pair that is absent changes nothing, as `addPair` of a pair already present; `aperasSchema.test.ts` puts `path` after `children` in the artifact and folder orders until the user decides on `path` (the design's Format lists none, since the grapher removed it, and is restored if `path` stays); `Stub` stays a kind with no field of its own for the five test files that use it.",
