    "title": "**A tracked artifact with no parent**",
    "text": "**A tracked artifact with no parent**: `trackArtifact` creates a node that has a path and no parent until the next folder ingest, which finds it by a map by path to give it one; with a derived path a second `aperas track` of the same file mints a duplicate, a match by title alone is ambiguous (`a/x.md` and `b/x.md` are both titled `x.md`) and 12 track-then-ingest sites of the tests break. The ways out: track attaches the node at once, creating the folder chain as the creation of a holder does; something stored keeps the identity of an unattached node (a second field, which defeats removing `path`); or track always runs the folder ingest next and the artifact ingest takes the node and not the path.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4RNDQVG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4RNDQVG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The tombstoned nodes**",
    "text": "**The tombstoned nodes**: a tombstone keeps its `path` today, and `staleOnDisk` (a tombstoned artifact whose path still names a file on disk) and the reuse of a path after a removal rely on it, while a tombstone loses its `parent` and so has no derived path. Whether the migration keeps the last path somewhere is open.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4RNDQVG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4RNDQVG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The thread scope**",
    "text": "**The thread scope**: the scope of a node is read through its path in a module of raw quads that cannot import the node classes, so it needs its own walk over `parent` and `title`, and the answer for a chain that cannot be walked, a throw or a quiet default, decides whether a thread artifact that lost its parent leaks into the tracked mirror instead of `.state/`. The same question stands for the flush and the reverse track.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4RNDQVG002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
