    "title": "**An open point of Batch 3, built**",
    "text": "**An open point of Batch 3, built**: clearing an embed field that keeps some of its nodes is `removePair` for each value the write drops and `addPair` for each it adds, so the nodes kept keep their ids; `removePair` and `clearField` on an embed field build the reverse of each field dropped with its target, and only when a pair is there to remove ([Batch 3's open points](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D4QHPDV8004)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4RWTPGR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4RWTPGR006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Decided before the batch**",
    "text": "**Decided before the batch**: the reverse of a node with no record is held in a map keyed by its id, which moves onto the record when the node is created or built and back at its removal; `addPair` accepts any string as the value of a reference field and, for an embed field, an id whose owner, field and kind match; `removeNode` on an embedded node keeps the behaviour of the oracle. The map is called `waiting` in the code: `holder` is a field of `BaseNode` in the Aperas schema, and the test that no file of the engine's directory names a kind or a distinctive field of it failed on the word.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4RWTPGR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4RWTPGR007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Choices of the batch, reported by the Coder**",
    "text": "**Choices of the batch, reported by the Coder**: the forward side keeps an array for a reference or embed field, as Batch 4 does for the literals, and no `Set`, so a value's membership is a scan of that one field; `build` checks the whole plan before the first change and keeps no undo log; `removePair` checks the embedded id only through the shape (a string), the owner, field and kind rule being that of `addPair` and `build`, so a pair that does not match is not there; the reverse of a field is by its name over every kind that declares it as a reference or embed field, and a name that is also a literal field in another kind builds only over the reference kinds; `subjectsOf` of a graph the engine does not have is empty (Batch 8 completes graphs); `inspectEngine(engine)` is exported from `engine.ts` for the tests and is not on the `Engine` interface. In the model-based test the field `pinned` cannot be \"asked after writes\" as the others are, since the first removal builds it by design, and the test allows that one exception.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4RWTPGR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4RWTPGR008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Changes outside the engine's new code**",
    "text": "**Changes outside the engine's new code**: `cli/src/tests/support/oracle.ts` gained the embed rule, the `subjectsOf` guard on a literal or unknown field, and `oracleOwnerOfId` and `oracleFieldOfId`. The `Rec` comment in `records.ts` and the header of `engine.ts` now say Batch 5.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4RWTPGR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
