    "title": "**Batch 6: the grapher's two questions, 2026-10-16** (the [brief](#id/BlockNode:00D4S3MST0000))",
    "text": "**Batch 6: the grapher's two questions, 2026-10-16** (the [brief](#id/BlockNode:00D4S3MST0000)): decided by the orchestrator to go on, none blocking: (1) the climbed side of a partitioning mark: Batch 6 does what the oracle does (an object has at most one subject in the field, and no cycle), which containment needs, and tests only such fields; the design's IS-A example climbs the forward side of a single-valued field and is Batch 14's; the direction, if the mark must name it, is a design question put to the user at the next pause, not decided; (2) records follow containment: the design's aim stands as written, records in the containment tree beside the master map, embedded nodes nested in the fields that own them, an aim and not a guarantee; if Batch 5's representation makes the nesting costly the coder does not nest and says why in the Report. The grapher's reading of \"Questions only for what the design settles and the Freeflow does not hold\" as \"does not settle\" is right.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SGY6JR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Batch 6 checked by the orchestrator, 2026-10-16**",
    "text": "**Batch 6 checked by the orchestrator, 2026-10-16**: in `Aperas.dev/monorepo` (`js-apeironngn`, staged, not committed) the coder built ordered fields, partitioning fields, containment and the records that follow containment, with the two reproductions of the open issues written first, failing on the old engine in `Aperas/` and passing as engine tests in `containment.test.ts`. The orchestrator ran the suites: `commit` 530 of 530 in 24 s, `essential` 51 of 51 in 3.0 s, `--group engine` 460 of 460. The coder's implementation choices are to be filed by the grapher (one place in the tree for a node: beneath the node that embeds it, else its container; the reverse of a partitioning field built by the first write; the climb bounded by the number of records; the tree re-placed in the attach, detach and release paths). Two gaps are left to the user as design and are not decided: a node that is both embedded and listed has no sentence in the design (the coder put it beneath its owner and the container keeps the entry); the order of the checks of `addPair` on an optional partitioning field (cardinality, then the rule) is the oracle's. Stale: Batch 5's Done record and comments that said a removal from an ordered field throws. The partitioning mark's direction stays the question put to the user. Next: the grapher files Batch 6, then surveys Batch 7.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SK2BN0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-10-17T12:50:11.130Z",
    "title": "**A node both embedded and listed**",
    "text": "**A node both embedded and listed**: which place in the tree a node takes when one node embeds it (the embed field holds its id) and another node lists it in its containment field (see [Records follow containment](#id/BlockNode:00D2T5P9Y8001), [Containment](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87SR00Z)). The design says a node's record sits under its container's and that embedded nodes are nested in the fields that own them, and has no sentence for the node that is both. The engine places it beneath the node that embeds it, and the container keeps the list entry; this is the engine's reading and is not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SK2BN0002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "tombstonedAt": "2026-10-17T12:50:13.952Z",
    "title": "**The order of the checks on a partitioning field of cardinality `one` or `optional`**",
    "text": "**The order of the checks on a partitioning field of cardinality `one` or `optional`**: which refusal comes first when one write breaks both the cardinality of the field and the partitioning rule: the host already holds a value in the field (see [Enforcement](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000M)), and the item written already has another subject on the climbed side or would close a cycle (see [Partitioning fields](#id/BlockNode:00D3CJDGH8001)). The engine checks the cardinality first and then the rule, so such a write is reported as the cardinality's `Undecided` and not as the rule's `Refused`; a test holds that order, and the design does not state it. It is not decided.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000J",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
