    "title": "**Decided before the batch**",
    "text": "**Decided before the batch**: the side of a partitioning mark that is climbed is the oracle's reading, an object has at most one subject in the field and the relation has no cycle, which containment needs; the batch tests only such fields, the design's IS-A example (the forward side of a single-valued field) and the primary-supertag use are [Batch 14](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W0019)'s, and the direction, if the mark must name it, is a design question for the user, not decided. The records follow containment as the design states the aim, in the containment tree beside the master map, with embedded nodes nested in the fields that own them.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4SKPNR8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SKPNR8007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Choices of the batch, reported by the Coder**",
    "text": "**Choices of the batch, reported by the Coder**: (1) a node has one place in the tree: the node that embeds it when that node holds its id in the embed field its id names, else its container, else none; a node both embedded and listed is beneath its owner and the container keeps the entry. (2) The reverse of a partitioning field is built by the first write of it, a refused write builds nothing, and a read of a derived entry builds the reverse of its field only when some declaration of that field is not partitioning. (3) `clearField` and `removeNode` take a field out in one pass; `build` dedupes the strings of an array with a `Set` (other values by `===`, so `NaN` twice is kept twice as the oracle keeps it). (4) The climb of `partition` is bounded by the number of records. (5) The tree is re-placed in `attach`, `detach` and `release`, so every write path gets it without a call of its own. `BUILT_BY` still names Batch 6 for the five operations built, as it names Batch 4 for `values`, because it names the batch that builds an operation.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4SKPNR8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
