    ],
    "title": "00D4SRWNPR002",
    "text": "**Symptom**",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNPR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D4SRWNPR003",
      "BlockNode:00D4SRWNQ0000"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNPR003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "props": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNPR003:props:StringProp:00D4SRX520002",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "orderedList",
        "value": "false"
      },
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNPR003:props:StringProp:00D4SRX520003",
        "@type": "StringProp",
        "key": "startIndex",
        "value": "1"
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D4SRWNPR003",
    "text": "Read from the code, not run. Ids are unique across all graphs, so the bucket of an object for a field (the subjects whose field holds it) can hold subjects of several graphs. Some reads and rules filter to the graph they name and some do not. In the oracle: `values` of a derived entry and `subjectsOf` filter by graph; `holds` (the read of `mustPartition`, the second-container and cycle rule), the dropped-with-target filter of `drop` and the removal from a container in `tombstone` do not. In the engine: the derived entry (`valuesOf`) and the tree (`parentOf`) look at the subject's own graph; `partition`, `drop` (the fields dropped with their target), and `subjectsOf`, `firstSubjectOf` and `countSubjectsOf` read the bucket whole, and those three only check that the graph exists.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNPR002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNQ0000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNQ0000:links:Link:00D4SRX52G002",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR00V",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNQ0000:links:Link:00D4SRX52G002:props:StringProp:00D4SRX52G003",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "143"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D4SRWNQ0000",
    "text": "So the engine's `subjectsOf` and the oracle's differ as soon as a subject in another graph holds the object. With one graph, which is all that [Batch 6](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR00V) and the batches before Batch 8 have (`createGraph` is not built), no test can tell any of these apart.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNPR002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4SRWNQ0001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
