    ],
    "title": "Sweep a graph, `sweep(graph)`",
    "text": "Sweep a graph, `sweep(graph)`: it deletes outright every tombstone that the mark from the live nodes of the schema's root kinds, through the schema's followed fields, does not reach, and gives the ids removed, sorted by code point; when it runs belongs to the service lifecycle (see [The sweep](#id/BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8001)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T000C",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D4T85FNG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Done, 2026-10-17**",
    "text": "**Done, 2026-10-17**: in `Aperas.dev/monorepo` on branch `js-apeironngn`, staged and not committed. The tombstone, the tombstone of a subtree, the removal of embedded nodes with their owner, and the sweep. Step 1: `tombstone(graph, id, at)` checks in this order the schema's marker (else `Refused`), the record (else `Undecided('Existence')`) and the node's kind declaring the marker (else `Refused`, which is also how an embedded node is refused), does nothing when the marker is set and keeps the first stamp, and otherwise makes one operation that cannot fail: the node leaves the list of every container that holds it (`detach` from the subjects in its bucket of the containment field, which the first write of that field built), every non-literal field it holds is taken out in one pass (`release`), its embedded nodes are dropped, and the marker is set to the string the shell passed in. Its literal fields stay with their types, and so do the references that name it, its entries in the fields dropped with their target (removal's) and its record in the master map and in the collection of its kind; `up` reads empty by itself. The tombstone of a container takes its own list, and the former children stay live as roots. `tombstoneSubtree(graph, id, at)` checks the role, the record and the kind of every member before its first change, so a member that holds no marker refuses the whole with nothing changed, and walks the subtree in pre-order with an explicit stack and a visited set (a chain of 5,000 containers is tombstoned from the top). Step 2: no kind of an embedded node holds the marker, in either schema, so an embedded node is never tombstoned; the tombstone of its owner drops it and the nodes it embeds, with their entries in the reverse. Step 3: `sweep(graph): Id[]` is typed in the interface and built in the engine and the oracle; it checks that the graph exists, that the schema assigns a sweep and that it assigns a marker (each `Refused`), marks from the live records of the root kinds and of the kinds below them through the followed fields, going through a tombstone it reaches, deletes outright every tombstone left unmarked, an island of tombstones included, with the entries of the swept in the fields dropped with their target, and gives the ids sorted by code point (a second sweep gives `[]`). Files: `core/src/apeironNgn/interface.ts` (+5), `engine.ts` (616 lines, was 497: `markerOf`, `isTombstone`, `makeTombstone`, `tombstone`, `tombstoneSubtree`, `sweep`), `records.ts` (+5, `recordsOf(graph, kind)`); in `cli/src/tests/`: `support/oracle.ts` (`tombstoneSubtree` all-or-nothing and iterative, `sweep` by scan, the header), `engine/tombstone.test.ts` (27 cases), `sweep.test.ts` (15), `tombstone.model.test.ts` (the model-based test, 21 cases) and `oracle.test.ts` (11 cases more), all named in `commit.suite.mjs` and none in `essential`; `schema.test.ts` takes `danglingRefs` and `dropGraph` as the unbuilt examples. `exists`, `size`, the generic reads and writes, `createGraph`, `dropGraph`, `danglingRefs` and the ports are unchanged, and the boundary test passes.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR014",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D4T85FNG002",
      "BlockNode:00D4T85FNG003",
      "BlockNode:00D4T85FNG004",
