    "title": "00D5AHW09G001",
    "text": "**Batch 8: the user's answers on several graphs, 2026-10-17** (the grapher's [three questions](#id/BlockNode:00D4T3JTP8001) and its survey): (1) a graph's \"own settings\" are not designed yet, but until now all are the shell's (the persistence settings in `aperas.config.json`, the flush interval): the engine holds none, and the design's sentence \"each with its own settings\" is reworded; (2) addressing across graphs has two forms in the user's sketch, `aperas://id/${universal ID}` and `aperas://graph.domain/${deep_path}`; whichever side holds the addressing, the engine holds a map of graphs keyed by `graph.domain`; the sweep deals within one graph only: a graph's tombstone is never kept alive by another graph, because another graph pointing to it may not be loaded (yet) and cannot be known, and a universal tombstone would stay forever and could never be cleaned up, so a reference across graphs may dangle after the sweep of the graph it points into; (3) every graph has a `graph.domain` identifier that the engine enforces, and the default graph is a normal one, with no special case. The user asked the orchestrator to elaborate on the three older points (the partitioning mark's direction, a node both embedded and listed, the order of the checks of `addPair` on an optional partitioning field). The orchestrator's reading, for the user to confirm: addressing (reading `aperas://` strings, resolving a deep path) is the shell's, over the engine's reads; the engine knows the graph names (`graph.domain`) and, through its master map of ids, which graph holds a node, so an `aperas://id/` reference resolves through the engine and a deep path through the shell inside the graph named by the domain; the reads that give a node's relations are filtered to the graph they name, and the rules that guard an invariant of the whole store (one container, no cycle, `dropGraph`'s check) read across graphs, which the user did not object to. Not settled: the grammar of a `graph.domain` and the names of the main and the threads graphs.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D5B1P14G001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D5AWYG40001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Graph names, `graphOf` and the older points, decided 2026-10-17**",
    "text": "**Graph names, `graphOf` and the older points, decided 2026-10-17**: the user asked how an id says which graph holds a node. Correction by the orchestrator: it does not (`Kind:snowflake` carries no graph); the master map maps an id to its record, a record belongs to one graph, and the engine already files records by graph and kind, so the answer is a read from an id to its record's graph. Proposed by the orchestrator, awaiting the user's confirmation: `graphOf(id)` joins the reads in Batch 8, so that `aperas://id/<ID>` resolves through the engine without naming the graph first. The user decided the names: the grammar of a `graph.domain` is that of the host of a URL authority, normally a (sub)domain name such as `my.graph.myhost`; today the main graph is `tree`, and the threads graph, which has no identifier of its own today, becomes a subdomain of the main graph, `threads.${main}`, that is `threads.tree` today. The engine enforces the grammar (lowercase DNS labels, dots, hyphens inside labels, none at the ends) and gives `default` no special case; the shell refuses the name `id`, which `aperas://id/` reserves. The user was OK with the orchestrator's reading of the three older points: the partitioning mark names no direction, the engine derives it from the cardinality (a single-valued partitioning field climbs forward, its cardinality bounding it; a many-valued one climbs the reverse side, where the engine checks one subject per object; the cycle check follows the climb); an embedded-shaped id cannot be listed in a containment field (a node is owned or contained, never both, which also closes the gap of `insertItem` into an ordered embed field); the order of the checks of `addPair` on an optional partitioning field is not a design point.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D5B1Q9W8001"
    ]
  },
