    "title": "**Addressing**",
    "text": "**Addressing**: the shell reads an `aperas://` string and resolves it; the engine does not. There are two forms. `aperas://id/<ID>` names a node by its id, and the shell resolves it through the engine, which finds the record by the master map and answers the graph that holds it (see [Reads](#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T0004)), so the reference need not name the graph. `aperas://graph.domain/<deep_path>` names a node by a deep path inside the graph that the domain names, and the shell resolves the path inside that graph. The first segment is the graph's name, so the name `id` is reserved for the first form and the engine refuses it as the name of a graph (see [Graphs in memory](#id/BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8003)). The shell has three graphs. The main graph, `tree`, has no identifier of its own yet and is called `tree` today, so its addresses begin `aperas://tree/`; the shell warns, once, when the table of settings has an entry keyed `tree`, because the main graph has no identifier of its own in the configuration and `tree` is its name today, and it uses the entry for the main graph; a fourth graph named `tree` is not a case, since the shell has three graphs. The threads graph is a subdomain of the main one, `threads.${main}`, that is `threads.tree` today. The graph of the views is `treeviews`, a name of one label at the top level and not under `tree`, because a view can index many graphs and not only `tree`. In the order of the linking topology (design, then issues, planning and history, then discussions, then threads, then views) the graphs are `tree`, `threads.tree` and `treeviews`: a graph points only at the graphs before it, and the one pair that goes the other way, the root folder's reference to the `.threads` folder, is kept (see [A reference across graphs](#id/BlockNode:00D4KD44M8001)). `treeviews` points at every other graph, through a view's profile, the nodes it unfolds and its root, and nothing points into it, so it can be dropped freely (see [Dropping a graph](#id/BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8004)). The shell keeps the settings of each graph (the mirror's location, the flush interval) in a table that is the `graphs` key of `aperas.config.json`, keyed by graph name; where the key is absent, or an entry lacks a setting, the existing `graph` keys give the main graph's location (and `APERAS_APEIRON_ROOT`, where it is set, gives the main graph's directory over any entry) and the shell's defaults give the rest: `tree` in the tracked directory every 10 s, and `threads.tree` and `treeviews` in its `.state/` directory every 3 s. At start the shell creates the three graphs in that order and drops the engine's first graph, `default` (see [Graphs in memory](#id/BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8003)).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHMYQG001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
