    "title": "**Reads and rules across graphs**",
    "text": "**Reads and rules across graphs**: an id is unique across all graphs, a pair may join a node of one graph to a node of another, and every read and write names the graph it acts on. The reads that give a node's relations are filtered to the graph they name: the subjects of a reverse side, their first one and their count, the derived entry of a node and the tree. The rules that guard an invariant of the whole store read across graphs: a node has one container and containment has no cycle (the same second-value and cycle rule of any partitioning field), and the check of dropping a graph. The drop of a value with its target (see [The roles it assigns](#id/BlockNode:00D4NB5FSR001)) reads across graphs too, so a node removed from one graph leaves the `unfolds` of the views of every graph. A sweep deals within one graph and never keeps another graph's tombstone alive (see [The sweep](#id/BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8001)): another graph that points at the tombstone may not be loaded, so it cannot be known, and a tombstone kept for it would stay for ever and could never be cleaned up. So a reference across graphs may dangle after the sweep of the graph it points into.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3CHJXK8002",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D5AYTCJR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D5AYTCJR001:links:Link:00D5AYTYJR000",
        "@type": "Link",
        "target": "BlockNode:00D5AYBPWR001",
        "predicate": "[[wikilink]]",
        "props": [
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D5AYTCJR001:links:Link:00D5AYTYJR000:props:StringProp:00D5B1FD5G000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "454"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "The graph that holds a node, `graphOf(id)`",
    "text": "The graph that holds a node, `graphOf(id)`: it answers the graph whose record has the id, through the master map, or nothing when no graph holds one; it reads the `graph` field of the engine's record (`Rec.graph` in `records.ts`), which already exists. An id does not say its graph (`Kind:snowflake` carries none), so the read is from an id to its record's graph; it lets `aperas://id/<ID>` resolve through the engine without naming the graph first (see [Addressing](#id/BlockNode:00D5AYBPWR001)). Confirmed by the user on 2026-10-20.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D24N87T0004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D5B0SBZG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "links": [
      {
        "@id": "BlockNode:00D5B0SBZG001:links:Link:00D5B0SXR8001",
