    ],
    "title": "**The Done when, checked**",
    "text": "**The Done when, checked**: the Coder's run for the batch: `tsc --noEmit` of `core` and `cli` clean (it found one unused variable in the model test, which `npm run verify` does not run), group `engine` 632 of 632 in 16.4 s, `essential` 51 of 51 in 3.5 s, `commit` 702 of 702 in 34.9 s; and after the follow-up: group `engine` 638 of 638 (20.5 s, then 24.1 s), `essential` 51 of 51 (6.6 s on its first run, over its 5 s budget, then 3.7 s and 4.3 s), `commit` 708 of 708 in 49.6 s. The orchestrator's run after the follow-up: `commit` 708 of 708 in 41.6 s of its 60 s budget, `--suite essential` 51 of 51 in 4.4 s and 3.9 s of its 5 s budget; the engine's files import only files of their own directory. The Coder's 6.6 s was taken with a grapher and the service working at the same time, a load average of 3 to 4: a timing carries its load ([Layer 7](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D457HDWG001)). The budgets are near and are watched at each batch: `essential` is within its budget on a quiet machine (about 4 s) and takes no new file, Batch 9 adding nothing to it beyond one second, and `commit` has about 18 s (Batch 7: 605 cases in 26 s; Batch 8 and its follow-up: 708 cases in 42 s). The model-based test, `graphs.model.test.ts`, runs 20 seeds (821 to 840) of 250 operations over four graph names (`default`, `a.example`, `b-2`, `c.d.e`) plus eleven names that fail the grammar and `nowhere`, two graphs made at the start; the operations of the earlier models are issued in the graph that holds the node (6% in another), with `createGraph`, `dropGraph` (a failing name, a taken name, a graph not there, a refused drop, one that goes), `sweep` of a named graph, `graphOf`, and an embedded id offered to a list (`insert`, `move`, `pair` of `items`, `build`). Engine and oracle are compared by value, or by the class and the open point of the error, after every operation, and the whole state every 12th. Checks that do not rest on the oracle: an id is in one graph and `graphOf` names it; a refused `createGraph`, `dropGraph` or sweep changes nothing in any graph; after a drop that went through, the graph has no record and no kind and every other graph reads as before; a sweep returns only ids of its graph and leaves the other graphs' tombstones; no bucket names a subject with no record; the reverse of the six fields equals a scan of the forward sides in each graph; `checkTree` holds for each graph. A coverage case holds what the sequences reach (graphs made 78, names refused 23, graphs taken 57, drops 51 of which 41 had nodes, drops refused by a pair 25, references across graphs 80, containers listing a node of another graph 73, reverse reads of an object with a subject in another graph 14, sweeps that removed something 108, embedded ids refused 33, `graphOf` answered 42 and nothing 93, graphs made again after a drop 24); the file takes 8 s. Mutations of the engine caught by this file: reads unfiltered (19 failures), `partition` limited to a graph (9), the refusal of the drop removed (10), the bucket cleanup of the drop removed (12), `graphOf` wrong (20), the grammar weakened (13), the sweep marking from every graph (4), the entries dropped with the target limited to a graph (4), the embedded refusal removed from `insertItem` (12), the tombstone leaving only same-graph containers (11). Of the Coder's mutations of the batch all were caught but one, the `moveItem` refusal of an embedded id, which the follow-up covers (see step 5).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DB8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DBG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The first cross-graph run**",
    "text": "**The first cross-graph run**: the cases of `graphReads.test.ts` (13, a subject in each of two graphs) were written first and run with only `createGraph` and `dropGraph` built: 9 of 13 passed. `subjectsOf` differed between the oracle and the engine for a `likes` object, an object with no record and `mate` (the engine returned both subjects, the oracle the one of the named graph). `firstSubjectOf` and `countSubjectsOf` read the same unfiltered bucket and their cases stopped at the first failing assertion, so the Coder did not see them fail on their own and shows them from the code (one line of the same bucket). The fourth failure was the Coder's setup (a tombstone leaves the list, so the island case never reached the node). Already agreeing, so nothing changed: the derived entry and the tree, the second-container and cycle rule (`partition` and the oracle's `holds` both read the bucket whole), the entries dropped with their target, the removal of a tombstone from the containers, the mark and the sweep (both inside the named graph). The oracle needed no change for the reads. The fix is `subjectsIn(graph, name, value)` in the engine, behind `subjectsOf`, `firstSubjectOf` and `countSubjectsOf`; it keeps the subjects whose record is in the named graph and is skipped while only one graph exists (`hasSeveralGraphs`), so a single-graph count stays the bucket's size.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DB8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DBG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**1 and 2, the graph and its dropping**",
    "text": "**1 and 2, the graph and its dropping**: `graphName.ts` holds `isGraphName` (the string of 1 to 253 characters, split on dots, each label `^[a-z0-9]([a-z0-9-]{0,61}[a-z0-9])?$`, the name `id` refused); `createGraph` is `Refused` for a name that fails it and for a name taken, otherwise `Registry.addGraph`; the oracle's `createGraph` has the grammar written apart (`oracleIsGraphName`), and `graphs.test.ts` runs a table of 37 names on both grammars and through `createGraph`. `dropGraph` is `Refused` when the graph is not there, then scans the forward sides (`rec.fields`) of the records of the other graphs for a non-literal value that has a record in the graph (the oracle's way), `Refused` naming the subject, its graph and the field before any change; then each record's non-literal values leave the buckets they sit in, and `Registry.removeGraph` deletes the records from the master map, the collections and the roots. The drop of the graph that exists at the start is allowed (no special case) and tested. Mutations: the refusal removed gave 2 more failures, the bucket cleanup removed 2 more, the refusal limited to reference fields (an embed field skipped) 1 more.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DB8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DBG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**4, `graphOf`, built and unconfirmed by the user**",
    "text": "**4, `graphOf`, built and unconfirmed by the user**: `graphOf(id): GraphName | undefined` joined `EngineReads` in `interface.ts`, is built in the engine as `registry.byId(id)?.graph` (`BUILT_BY`: 'Batch 8') and in the oracle by a scan of the nodes; the cases are an id of each graph, an embedded node made in a graph other than its owner's, an id nobody holds, the empty string, a node removed and a graph dropped. The design says it is proposed by the orchestrator and awaits the user's confirmation. If the user does not confirm it, remove the line of `interface.ts`, the entries of the engine and the oracle, the `BUILT_BY` entry, and the `graphOf` cases in `graphs.test.ts` and in the model test (the operation, its read in the sequences and its three checks).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DB8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DBG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**5, an embedded id in a containment field**",
    "text": "**5, an embedded id in a containment field**: `notOwned(field, value)` in the engine (the field is the schema's containment role and `ownerOfId(value)` is defined, so `Refused`) is called right after `inShape` in `addPair`, `build` and `insertItem`, and after `listOf` in `moveItem`; the oracle has the same in the same places (`notOwned`, `oracleOwnerOfId`), so the two meet the same error in the same order. `embeddedId.test.ts` (9 cases over the test schema, `items`, and the Aperas schema, `children`): refused with or without a record, in another graph, by `build` (which creates nothing), a top-level id and a string that is no id as before, the embedded node keeps its place under its owner, and a reference field that is not the containment field (`likes`) takes the id. Mutations: removed from `addPair` 2 failures, from `build` 2, from `insertItem` 4, from `moveItem` none. The `moveItem` case could not be seen as it stands: an embedded-shaped id is never in a list, so the refusal \"not in the list\" comes first with the same class; the follow-up below added a case that fails without the check. The old case of `containment.test.ts` that held \"a node that is embedded and also listed by a container is beneath its owner, and the container keeps the entry\" (the point settled on 2026-10-17) was rewritten to assert the refusal and that the node stays beneath its owner.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D5BD3DB8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
