    "title": "**Resolved (2026-10-17)**",
    "text": "**Resolved (2026-10-17)**: [Batch 8](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR01D) built several graphs in the new engine in `Aperas.dev` (branch `js-apeironngn`), staged and not committed, and made the oracle and the engine say the decided rules for each read and rule that touches the bucket of an object. The reads that give a node's relations (`subjectsOf`, `firstSubjectOf`, `countSubjectsOf`, the derived entry and the tree) are filtered to the graph they name; the rules that guard an invariant of the whole store (one container, no cycle, the second-container and cycle rule of a partitioning field, the check of `dropGraph`) read across graphs; the entries dropped with their target, the removal of a tombstone from its containers and the mark and delete of the sweep are tested too. The reproduction is the test: the 13 cases of `graphReads.test.ts`, each with a subject in each of two graphs, were run first with only `createGraph` and `dropGraph` built, and 9 of 13 passed; `subjectsOf` differed for a `likes` object, an object with no record and `mate` (the engine returned the subjects of both graphs, the oracle those of the named graph), and `firstSubjectOf` and `countSubjectsOf` read the same unfiltered bucket. The fix is `subjectsIn(graph, name, value)` behind the three reads, skipped while only one graph exists; the 13 cases pass, and the model test `graphs.model` holds the engine equal to the oracle for 20 seeds over four graphs. With several graphs `countSubjectsOf` is O(bucket), which is accepted. The reference copy under `core/src/shell/` is not changed by the batch and keeps its own reads until [Batch 11](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W000C) moves the shell onto the engine and [Batch 12](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W000S) deletes it.",
