    "title": "**Resolved (2026-10-17)**",
    "text": "**Resolved (2026-10-17)**: the follow-up of [Batch 8](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR01D), checked by the orchestrator the same day ([the done record](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D5BD3DB8001)), passed the host to the shape check in `insertItem` (`engine.ts` and the oracle's `insertItem`, one line each), so that an ordered embed field takes only an id that the host embeds in that field, and a node cannot be held by two owners through a list; a reference field is unchanged. Step 5 of Batch 8, which refuses an embedded id in the containment field, did not close it: the issue is the other way round. The reproduction is the test: `engine/embeddedOrdered.test.ts` (6 cases, in `commit` only) runs over a test schema with two ordered embed fields (`Pack`, with `parts` and `spares`, embedding `Part`, `Big extends Part` and `Other`), on the engine and the oracle: `insertItem` takes an id the host embeds (at the end, at a place, a descendant kind); refuses the embedded node of another owner (the reverse `subjectsOf` stays one subject), an id of the other field, a top-level id, a non-id and an id of another owner, leaving the list unchanged; is `Undecided('Enforcement')` for an embedded node of another kind; and gives the outcome `addPair` gives for seven values on fresh stores. Without the host argument, in the engine or in the oracle, 4 cases fail. No schema of the project declares an ordered embed field, so only a test schema reaches the case. The same follow-up added a case for the `moveItem` refusal of an embedded id (it reads the message \"embedded node\" against \"is not in items\", which a rewording of either breaks), and removing the check from either side fails it. The reference copy under `core/src/shell/` is not changed by the batch and keeps its own behaviour until [Batch 11](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W000C) and [Batch 12](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W000S).",
