            "value": "76"
          },
          {
            "@id": "BlockNode:00D5BM7928001:links:Link:00D5BM7SFR000:props:StringProp:00D5BM7SFR002",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "229"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "**Batch 8 filed, Batch 9 surveyed, 2026-10-17**",
    "text": "**Batch 8 filed, Batch 9 surveyed, 2026-10-17**: the grapher filed Batch 8 ([the Report](#id/BlockNode:00D5BKMV0G005) is the check) and resolved the cross-graph reads issue and the `insertItem` issue. Of the six questions of the [Batch 9 brief](#id/BlockNode:00D5BKMV0G005) the orchestrator decided four, taking the grapher's defaults: (2) the load: where `parent` and `children` disagree `parent` wins, the entry in the other container is dropped with a warning that names both, a `parent` naming an id with no document leaves the node with no container and `danglingRefs` returns the id, with a warning, and a node listed by a container with no `parent` stays in that container; the real mirror has none of the three, so only fixtures reach them; (3) the shell, at start, creates the graphs `tree` and `threads.tree` and drops `default`, and the engine is unchanged; (4) `danglingRefs` counts an id defined in any graph as defined, as the oracle does, and the design says so (the root folder's `children` names the `.threads` folder, defined in the threads graph); (6) the new fixture round trip goes into `essential` if it measures under one second on a quiet machine, the old `store/roundtrip.test.ts` moves to `commit` and stays until Batch 12. Two are the user's, as design: (1) which graph holds the `TreeView` nodes (and so what the table of settings is keyed by), and (5) where the table of settings lives in `aperas.config.json`. The batch builds what they do not gate: the dump (step 1), the load (step 3), the round trip (step 4), the writers (step 5) and the two missing rules of the migration scripts (step 6), with the function from (graph, kind) to a file taking its mapping as an argument; step 2's mapping and the table wait for the user.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D5C24NMR001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D5BVXASG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Views are in the engine, in a graph of their own, 2026-10-17**",
    "text": "**Views are in the engine, in a graph of their own, 2026-10-17**: asked whether the engine needs to deal with `TreeView` when the shell renders it, the orchestrator pointed to the two uses of the reverse side that the user had missed: the `unfolds` field has the drop-with-target role (a removed node leaves the views that unfold it, which is why `pruneStaleUnfolds` left Batch 11 and the mirror's 443 `unfolds` entries stay clean) and `profile` and `root` are reference fields whose reverse lists a profile's views and feeds `backlinks`. The user decided: `TreeView` stays in the engine, as a separate graph lower than threads, because it references everything and no other graph references it. The orchestrator checked the code: the drop-with-target already reads across graphs (the Batch 8 test \"a removed node leaves the fields dropped with their target in every graph\"), so a view in its own graph still loses its entry; and `dropGraph` of the views graph is never refused by a pointer into it, since nothing points into it. Proposed by the orchestrator, awaiting the user's confirmation: the name of that graph follows the nesting of the others, `views.threads.tree` (a subdomain of `threads.tree`, which is a subdomain of `tree`); the table of settings is then keyed by graph alone: `tree` the tracked kinds (`BlockNode`, `ArtifactNode`, `FolderNode`, `Profile`) in `Apeiron/` at 10 s, `threads.tree` the thread-scoped tree kinds in `Apeiron/.state/` at 3 s, `views.threads.tree` the `TreeView` kind in `Apeiron/.state/` at 3 s. The location of the table in `aperas.config.json` is still the user's.",
