    "title": "**Since (2026-10-17), the computing half built in the new engine, the reading not resolved**",
    "text": "**Since (2026-10-17), the computing half built in the new engine, the reading not resolved**: [Batch 9](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2VR01M) built `danglingRefs(graph)` in `Aperas.dev` (`engine.ts`, branch `js-apeironngn`, staged and not committed): for each record of the graph, each non-literal field and each value that no graph holds a record for, the ids sorted with `compareIds` and listed once; an id defined in any graph counts as defined, so the root folder's entry for the `.threads` folder is not returned for the main graph ([A reference across graphs](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D4KD44M8001)); a graph that does not exist gives an empty list, as the oracle does. `engine/danglingRefs.test.ts` has 28 cases (8 by hand and 20 seeded sequences of builds, removals, `addPair` and `insertItem` over two graphs, the engine against the oracle after every step), and mutations that define an id by the named graph only (5 or more failures), count literals (1) or leave the list unsorted (5 or more) fail them. The load returns `dangling`: the engine's list for each graph plus the ids that a `parent` names with no document, which the engine cannot see since a `parent` that a build supplies is never a stored pair; the ghost-folder fixture is a case of `load.test.ts`. This entry stays open for the reading: what the service does with the list belongs to [the service lifecycle](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000W), which gates it in [Batch 11](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W000C) step 8.",
