    "title": "**Batch 11 surveyed, the first answers, 2026-10-20**",
    "text": "**Batch 11 surveyed, the first answers, 2026-10-20**: the grapher split Batch 11 into ten coder turns ([the Report](#id/BlockNode:00D75ERCG0008): 1 manual links removed; 2, 3, 4 tests before the reads, the writes and the service; 5 the additive engine-side handle; 6 the flip, the one piece that moves what the shell runs on; 7 reads, link integrity, profiles and views; 8 ingest, track and project; 9 writes and tombstones; 10 sweeps and the service lifecycle). Decided by the orchestrator, as not about the design: the ten pieces with piece 5 apart; the waiver (only `runLinkCandidates`); the split of `commit` into `commit`, `models` and `tools`; the reword of Batch 12 by the grapher when it files piece 6. The user asked what the flip is, what `tree --tombstoned` shows now, the last sentence of the order question and the load's rejection, and the orchestrator explained them. The user's answers: (a) the engine keeps no live set because no engine operation needs one (the sweep walks reachability from the roots, the dump needs every id, the live lookups are the shell's maps), which the user restated as \"no bulk task of the engine with live nodes or with tombstones\" and which the orchestrator corrected in one point: the engine does bulk work with tombstones (the sweep, the subtree tombstone), none of it a flat set of live ids; (b) when the load rejects a document the service keeps the default of refusing to start and a reload refuses to replace the graph, and a start-anyway option is added so that the user can work on the unaffected parts and fix the affected ones in memory. The orchestrator's reading of the option, a safety measure it implies and the user may change: the option is explicit (a flag of `service start` and of `reload`, named by the coder), the log and the response name every rejected file and id, and before the first flush overwrites a file that had a rejected document the service copies the raw rejected documents to a side file next to the mirror (nothing is lost, in the gitignored `.state/` files too); the unaffected files flush as usual. Still open for the user, as design: Existence and `graphOf` for the flip, the tombstones and their container, the order of the listings.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D766N4RR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Batch 11: the four design answers, 2026-10-20**",
    "text": "**Batch 11: the four design answers, 2026-10-20**: the user said yes to all four, including the backup of the raw rejected documents: (1) Existence and `graphOf`: `graphOf` is confirmed, the shell's `nodeExists(id)` is `graphOf(id) !== undefined` (a node exists when a document stands for it, a ghost named only by its parent's `children` does not exist), the engine's `exists` and `size` stay undecided for a node with incoming pairs and no record, which the shell never asks; (2) tombstones and their container: the design wins: a tombstone loses its `parent` and leaves the `children` of its container; a removed block is in no list; `tree --tombstoned` shows nothing for a removed child in place, keeps the `(tombstoned)` tag on a link whose target is tombstoned and has a way to show a tombstone by id; a removal that empties a textless `listItem` or a `list` tombstones that container in the same write; the boot sweep `tombstoneVacuousContainers` keeps the rule over a container with no live child; `toPath()` of a tombstone is `null`; (3) order: the five listings are sorted in the shell by id; the links of a block by `position`, then id, a link with no position after those with one; the canonical place of a node reached through several unfolded links is the first in code-point order of the unfolded ids; no engine set of live ids, the shell's `liveIdsOfKind` filters; (4) the load: when it returns `rejected` or `errors` the service refuses to start and a reload refuses to replace the graph, naming the files and ids; an explicit start-anyway option (a flag of `service start` and of `reload`) lets the user work on the unaffected parts and fix the affected ones in memory; before the first flush overwrites a file that had a rejected document the service copies the raw rejected documents to a side file next to the mirror; `warnings` are logged and the service starts. To go into the design and the plan.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D3XJ7GTR001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": [
      "BlockNode:00D76DS038001"
    ]
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D767MWXG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Piece 1 checked, the corpus case, 2026-10-20**",
    "text": "**Piece 1 checked, the corpus case, 2026-10-20**: in `Aperas.dev/monorepo` (`js-apeironngn`, staged, not committed) the coder removed the manual links (the verbs `link` and `unlink`, the `predicate` of a `Link` in the old code, the render and JSON field, the cases that used a manual link). The orchestrator ran the suites: `commit` 904 of 904 in 40 s, `essential` 52 of 52 in 3.2 s; the remaining mentions of `predicate` are in the frozen Oxigraph files and the RDF names, not the field of a `Link`. The coder found one failing case of the `demand` suite: `corpus/roundtrip.test.ts` case 7, the round trip of the real, unmigrated mirror through the old engine, short by 5,906 quads, exactly the mirror's `predicate` quads that the old dump no longer writes. Decided by the orchestrator, as not about the design: the case is made exact, not weakened: it asserts that the difference is exactly the `predicate` quads of the mirror (5,906 today, counted from the mirror, not a constant) and nothing else, and says that the migration at the cutover removes the difference; `predicate` does not come back into the old shape.",
