            "@id": "BlockNode:00D76B1ZTR001:links:Link:00D76B2J00000:props:StringProp:00D80MMSN8000",
            "@type": "StringProp",
            "key": "position",
            "value": "1137"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "title": "00D76B1ZTR001",
    "text": "A load that rejects a document is not served silently. When the load of the mirror returns `rejected` documents (for instance a repeated `@id`) or `errors`, `aperas service start` refuses to start and `aperas reload` refuses to replace the graph in memory, and both name every rejected file and id in the log and the response; the load's `warnings` are logged and the service starts. An explicit start-anyway option, the flag `--start-anyway` of `aperas service start`, `aperas service restart` and `aperas reload`, lets the user work on the unaffected parts and fix the affected ones in memory. When the service accepts the load with the option, and so before any flush can overwrite a file that held a rejected document, it copies the raw documents the load skipped to a side file next to that mirror file, `<file>.rejected-<UTC stamp>.json` (next to the `.state/` file too, which git ignores), so nothing is lost, and the unaffected files flush as usual; a copy equal to one already there is not written again. A refused start says why to the client that started it. The engine's design leaves the service's lifecycle outside itself ([The service lifecycle](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000W)), so this is where the rules of the service are kept.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00CDY7T61R001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
