    "title": "**The order, and what runs in parallel**",
    "text": "**The order, and what runs in parallel**: 1 manual links removed (step 9); 2, 3 and 4 tests before the reads, the writes and the service (step 10); 5 the engine-side handle (steps 1 and 2, the additive half); 6 the flip (steps 1 and 2, the half that moves; the boot and flush of step 8; the wiring of the maps); 7 reads, link integrity, profiles and views (steps 3, 6 and 11); 8 ingest, track and project (step 5); 9 writes and tombstones (step 4, gated); 10 sweeps and the service lifecycle (steps 7 and 8, gated). Piece 1 comes first because the tests of 2 and 3 pin the text of `tree` and `unfold`, which loses the predicate. Pieces 2, 3, 4 and 5 touch different files and may run in any order; all four come before 6, because 6 moves every group at once and the plan wants each group's tests written before it moves. The order puts 8 before 9, against the plan's step numbers, so that the work that does not touch the tombstone functions comes first ([Order](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQM4X0002)); 8 calls the tombstone functions and does not change them. This answers the open point [Regrouping the batch](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3YG50B0004) with a cut by dependency, not the plan's eight groups and not its A to G: the commands hold almost no store code (no command calls a `storeAccess` helper), the seam is `node.ts`, and `node.ts` is one file.",
