    "title": "**The rule for the order held**",
    "text": "**The rule for the order held**: no case pins the order of the siblings of a folder, the links of a block, the children of an unfolded node, the profiles, the views or the `unfolds` of a view; each compares sets or the invariant. The one place that depends on the text is the pointer format of a node reached through two links (`[*]` and `[*see <id> (link)]`), asserted as exactly one canonical and one pointer naming it, with no pin on which link is canonical. The tombstone and order rules of the design ([A removed node leaves its container](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000R), [The canonical place in the render](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000Q), [The order of links without a position](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000P)) change the assertions only at the pieces that apply them (7 and 9).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D76KP0D8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D76KP0D8006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The reproduction**",
    "text": "**The reproduction**: `resolve.test.ts` builds two holders of one path with `wrap` (`title`, `path`, `tombstonedAt`) in both orders and asserts on the reference reader `findByExactPath(fx.store, path)` that it returns the tombstone when the live node was stored first. No sequence of commands shows the defect: 24 removal-and-reingest cycles of one path always left the live node stored second, so the reader returned the live node every time. The command-level case (a path reused after a removal resolves to the live node) is separate and passes on both engines. Its title says it goes with the reference reader at Batch 12; see the decision below.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D76KP0D8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D76KP0D8007",
