    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG000",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The Done when, checked**",
    "text": "**The Done when, checked**: the tests pass with 23 of 24 mutations caught (the brief asked for 8 or more), the one missed an equivalent mutant (below); `tsc --noEmit` of `packages/core` and of `packages/cli` clean; the boundary test passes (`--group engine` 666 of 666 with `engine/boundary.test.ts`); the old files are untouched. The checks: `--group shell` 57 of 57 (1.8 s); `--suite models` 165 of 165 (12.9 s of 30 s by the Coder, 13.7 s by the orchestrator); `essential` 74 of 74 (3.8 s by the Coder, 3.5 s by the orchestrator, of 5 s); `commit` 975 of 975 (18.9 s by the Coder, 18.1 s by the orchestrator, of 60 s), no warning; it was 938 of 938 in 18.2 s before, so +37 cases from `db.test.ts`.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The handle (`db.ts`)**",
    "text": "**The handle (`db.ts`)**: `Db` is `engine`, `maps`, `mapping`, `settings`, `graphs` and `write`. `defaultSettings(root)` builds today's layout from the main graph's directory (`root`, `root/.state`, flush intervals 10 s and 3 s). `openDb(root, settings = defaultSettings(root))` gives `{ db, load, warnings, duplicates }`: it builds the mapping, runs `loadMirror` and `rebuild`, and prints nothing, throws nothing; `load.rejected` and `load.errors` are returned, `warnings` is the load's then one string for each duplicated key. `newDb()` is for tests (`createMirrorGraphs`, a mapping with the relative directories `Apeiron/` and `Apeiron/.state/`, maps rebuilt). The reads: `graphOfId`, `nodeExists` (`graphOf(id) !== undefined`), `allIdsOfKind` (the union over the graphs of the mapping, sorted by `compareIds`), `liveIdsOfKind` (the same without the nodes whose tombstone marker is set), `backlinksOf(db, id, field)` (the union of `subjectsOf` of each graph, sorted) and `isThreadScoped` (`graphOfId === 'threads.tree'`).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG002",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The write layer (`writeLayer.ts`)**",
    "text": "**The write layer (`writeLayer.ts`)**: `createWriteLayer(engine, maps)` gives `createNode(id, NewNode)` (the graph by `graphOfNewNode`, and it gives the graph back), `addPair`, `clearField`, `removePair`, `insertItem`, `moveItem`, `removeItem`, `tombstone`, `tombstoneSubtree`, `removeNode` and `sweep`. Each calls the engine, and tells the maps only after the engine accepted (`built`, `fieldWritten`, `tombstoned`, `removed`, `swept`); the list writes tell no map. `tombstoneSubtree` collects the ids with `subtreeIds` before the write (explicit stack, visited set, the `values` of the containment field, a child kept only when `graphOf(child) === graph`, as the engine's own walk) and tells the maps by id for each member in the graph of the write; a threads folder listed by the root of the main graph is not in the subtree, and telling it would have unfiled a live node.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG003",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The six implementation choices (the Coder's, pure implementation, noted for piece 6)**",
    "text": "**The six implementation choices (the Coder's, pure implementation, noted for piece 6)**: (1) the write functions keep the engine's signatures, the graph first, because the shell finds the graph with `graphOfId` where it needs it; only `createNode` differs (it takes the `NewNode` and gives the graph). (2) `openDb(root, settings?)` takes the main graph's directory as `root`; the service passes the table of `resolveGraphSettings` as `settings` at pieces 6 and 10. (3) `openDb` prints nothing: the logging, the refusal to start and the side copy are the service's (piece 10). (4) `Db` carries `settings` (for the flush intervals of piece 10) and `write`. (5) `allIdsOfKind` is sorted by id over the union, not graph by graph, which is the design answer (the five listings are sorted by id in the shell) and makes the order independent of the order of the graphs. (6) `newDb()` has a mapping with relative directories so a test can dump it to any root.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG004",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**What the tests cover**",
    "text": "**What the tests cover**: `db.test.ts` (37 cases): `newDb`, `defaultSettings`, `openDb` on the fixture mirror with no settings, with a duplicated handle (warning, first by id), with a load warning plus a duplicate (the order), with a repeated `@id` and a refused document (returned, the rest built) and with no files; a ghost by a list and by a pair is not an existing node; the sorted listing across the graphs (the engine's own order is insertion); `liveIdsOfKind` drops a tombstone in each graph; `backlinksOf` across the tree and threads graphs; `isThreadScoped` for an artifact, its block and its link; each write function; `tombstoneSubtree` of a folder tree and of a root that lists a threads folder; `sweep`; and the refusals (a second value of a one-valued field `Undecided('Enforcement')`, a missing node `Undecided('Existence')`, a kind without the marker `Refused`, an id taken, a kind not the id's, a path or `under` missing, an item twice), each leaving the maps as they were. `writeLayer.model.test.ts`: the real `newDb()` driven through `db.write` against the oracle driven directly; after every operation the two answers are equal (ok, error and open point, the graph of a creation, the ids of a sweep), the three maps equal the scan of the oracle, and `allIdsOfKind`, `liveIdsOfKind` over seven kinds, `nodeExists`, `graphOfId`, `isThreadScoped` over the whole universe with two ghosts, and `backlinksOf` of five ids for five reference fields equal the oracle's; each sequence asserts that it took most writes, met refusals, held duplicates, created in the threads graph, made links and tombstoned. 20 of 20 on the first run.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG005",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**The mutations**",
    "text": "**The mutations**: 24, each applied by a scratch script that runs the two files under `timeout 100` and restores the source (`git status` showed nothing unstaged after); 23 caught, 1 missed. Caught: `addPair` and `tombstone` told before the engine answers (`db.test` and the model); the subtree told only for its top (`db.test`, and the model after the weights of `insert` and `subtree` were raised from 10 and 5 to 14 and 7); the subtree collected after the write; the subtree told for members of other graphs; `removeNode`, `clearField`, `removePair`, `addPair` and `tombstone` not told; `createNode` always in the main graph; `allIdsOfKind` skipping a graph or unsorted; `nodeExists` true for a node with a pair or children naming it, and always true; `backlinksOf` unsorted or main graph only; `liveIdsOfKind` keeping tombstones or dropping the threads graph; `isThreadScoped` on the wrong graph; `openDb` without `rebuild` or without the duplicate warnings; `newDb` without the graphs. **Missed, an equivalent mutant**: `sweep` not telling the maps (`maps.swept`). A map holds only live nodes and `sync` unfiles a tombstone as soon as it is tombstoned, so a swept id is never in a map and the call cannot be seen to matter; only a test that makes the maps stale on purpose could catch it. The write layer tells it anyway, as the brief lists it, and the mutant is left as it is (decided by the orchestrator).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG006",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Added time**",
    "text": "**Added time**: `commit` goes from 938 cases in 18.2 s to 975 in 18.1 to 18.9 s, 56 files; `models` from 145 cases to 165 (+20 seeds, 12.9 to 13.7 s of 30 s), 8 files; `essential` is unchanged, 74 of 74 in 3.5 to 3.8 s.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG007",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Left to piece 6 (decided by the orchestrator)**",
    "text": "**Left to piece 6 (decided by the orchestrator)**: `Lookups.subtreeTombstoned` and its scan of the whole `filed` map are no longer called by the write layer (it tells each member by id), so they are used only by `lookups/maps.test.ts` and `lookups/lookups.model.test.ts`; they stay until the flip, and piece 6 removes them with those two test uses (it is in the entry of piece 6).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JRVHG008",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Findings of the Coder along the way**",
    "text": "**Findings of the Coder along the way**: the first run of `db.test.ts` was 36 of 37, the Coder's expected order of two link ids being wrong (an id sorts by its owner first), fixed in the test; the first model run passed at once, so the weights of `insert` and `subtree` were raised until the model caught the mutation of the subtree told only for its top.",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D79JRVH8001",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
  },
  {
    "@id": "BlockNode:00D79JW9AR001",
    "@type": "BlockNode",
    "title": "**Set by piece 5, 2026-10-20**",
    "text": "**Set by piece 5, 2026-10-20**: this piece removes `Lookups.subtreeTombstoned` (with its scan of the whole `filed` map) and its two test uses, in `lookups/maps.test.ts` and `lookups/lookups.model.test.ts`. The write layer of piece 5 no longer calls it: `tombstoneSubtree` collects the ids of the subtree before the write and tells the maps for each member by id. Piece 5 was told not to touch the Batch 10 files, so the function stays until the flip. The service of this piece opens the handle with `openDb(root, settings?)` of `db.ts`, passing the table of `resolveGraphSettings` as `settings` (the flush intervals come at piece 10).",
    "parent": "BlockNode:00D76BQ2N0004",
    "type": "listItem",
    "children": []
