    "title": "**Resolved (2026-10-21)**",
    "text": "**Resolved (2026-10-21)**: [Batch 11](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3XQQ2W000C) piece 6, the flip, ran the shell on the engine in `Aperas.dev` (branch `js-apeironngn`), staged and not committed, so an ordered insertion reaches the engine's `insertItem` and `moveItem`, where the no-cycle rule of the partitioning field `children` refuses a move after an anchor inside the node's own subtree ([Partitioning fields](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3CJDGH8001)). The reproduction is the test: `commands/moveIntoOwnSubtree.test.ts` ran on the old code in a child process killed after 6 s (`outcome: moved`) and is now a regular test in `commit`; it ends by itself with the refusal `cannot be moved into its own subtree`, which names the engine's no-cycle rule, with the service answering. The shell puts the child back when the engine refuses, so a refused move changes nothing (a case of the test checks it, and a mutation that does not put the child back is caught). `commit` 999 of 999. The working service and the reference copy in `Aperas/` run the old code until the cutover and keep the loop, so the workaround above stays in force for them: read the id of the node to move and the id of the anchor twice, and check that the anchor is not inside the node.",
