    "title": "**Piece 7 checked, 2026-10-21**",
    "text": "**Piece 7 checked, 2026-10-21**: in `Aperas.dev/monorepo` (`js-apeironngn`, 27 files staged, not committed) the coder moved the callers of the old path lookup onto `liveAtPath` and deleted `findByExactPath`, built the order rules (the unfolds order and the first-escaping-link rule replaced; a block's links by `position` then id with a link without a position last, closing the newest-first issue; the other four listings sorted by id), gave `runPath` of a node without a path a refusal of its own, turned the set assertions of pieces 2 and 3 into exact orders where the shell now promises one, and added `commands/order.test.ts` and a `check-links` fixture; `removeProfile` and `removeTreeViewByName` already went through `removeNode` since the flip. 21 of 21 mutations on the sorts and the canonical rule caught (three survived at first and each got a case). The orchestrator ran: `tsc` clean for core, cli and web; `essential` 74 of 74 in 3.1 s, `commit` 1018 of 1018 in 18.9 s, `models` 165 of 165, `tools` 14 of 14, `--group corpus` 17 of 17 (load 1.5). Decided on the coder's questions: (1) nested cones: the rule of the canonical place is exact within one cone and, with a nested cone, the first link met in the depth-first visit wins, not necessarily the lowest id: left as built and filed as an issue for the user to decide later (the edge was never specified); (2) `aperas show` keeps listing a block's links in id order, the stored form, as the mirror. The engine's `compareByKey` no longer throws `Undecided` on a mixed pair (a link with no position sorts last): the user's answer on the order of links without a position; the design's open point [The order of links without a position](../design/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D24N87T000P) is settled by it.",
