    "title": "**Decided on the Coder's questions** (by the orchestrator, 2026-10-21)",
    "text": "**Decided on the Coder's questions** (by the orchestrator, 2026-10-21): (1) the four commands reach `insertItem`, `moveItem` and `removeItem` through the shell's `insertChild`, `appendChild` and the `children` setter, one place that also leaves the old container first, words the cycle refusal and puts the child back; calling the engine from each command would copy that logic into four files: the clause \"onto the write layer directly\" is read as satisfied in effect, and no literal form is wanted. (2) The engine's `insertItem` accepts a node that is already a tombstone, so a tombstone can be listed again (the model found it, seeds 2009 to 2011); no command does it (`kgInsert` moves only live blocks): left as it is and recorded as a dated entry on [the issue of the plain write on a tombstone](../issues/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D4T7JDAR001), which waits for Enforcement. (3) A move that empties a list and a reconcile (`update`) that drops its items leave the list live and empty until the boot sweep: this is the boot rule the user confirmed ([Tombstones leave their container](../planning/core.md#id/BlockNode:00D3YG50B0001)), the rule at the time of the removal belonging to a removal only; the move case of `containers.test.ts` pins it.",
